\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Onslaught of new  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  restrictions looms in reddest of states\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,415 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2022\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New state legislative sessions likely to bring fresh efforts to restrict, penalize or altogether ban the procedure\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska, a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban could be on the horizon. In Florida, the gestational limit for abortions could drop from 15 weeks to 12. Elsewhere, lawmakers have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in their sights.\par \par \uc2 When Roe v Wade fell, most states were no longer in legislative session, meaning the term during which they usually write and pass bills had ended. In January, state legislatures will reconvene in an entirely new reality, one where conservative lawmakers are no longer constrained by the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once assured by Roe.\par \par \uc2 The midterm elections brought victories for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a number of states. But in others, politics are on the side of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates. In those reddest of states, the new state legislative sessions are likely to bring a fresh onslaught of efforts to restrict, penalize or altogether ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Katie Glenn, the state policy director at Susan B Anthony Pro-Life America, confirms the group\u8217\'20\'19s top priority in 2023 will be reducing the gestational age for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , alongside bringing new outright bans. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently banned in 13 states.\par \par \uc2 Exactly how restrictive those bans will be remains to be seen, with conservatives across the country embroiled in conflicts over which exceptions \u8211\'20\'13 if any \u8211\'20\'13 should be allowed for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CExceptions in the case of rape and incest, we realise, are sometimes a necessary political reality. And we would not block a bill or oppose a bill that would prevent 95% of abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D explains Glenn.\par \par \uc2 In some states, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates previously stymied by Democrats now have room to maneuver since the midterms, which brought some conservative wins. In 2022, progressive members of Nebraska\u8217\'20\'19s legislature filibustered a ban proposed by Republicans, effectively {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclunebraska.org/en/press-releases/victory-nebraska-state-senators-filibuster-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 killing it}}}\uc2 . But lawmakers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.3newsnow.com/news/political/democrats-block-filibuster-proof-majority-in-nebraska-no-guarantee-they-can-stop-republican-bills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say}}}\uc2  that the party no longer has the votes to block an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have been mired in lengthy court proceedings, Republican majorities could pass more stringent laws when the session starts.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, for example, a six-week ban has been held up in court since 2019. With the legislature reconvening on 9 January, it could choose to pass a new ban rather than waiting for the courts. That would be helped by the fact that, just before Roe fell, Iowa\u8217\'20\'19s state supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/2022/06/17/iowa-supreme-court-abortion-law-ruling-planned-parenthood-kim-reynolds/7646049001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled there is no constitutional}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/2022/06/17/iowa-supreme-court-abortion-law-ruling-planned-parenthood-kim-reynolds/7646049001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 right to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  in the state}}}\uc2 . With the midterms solidifying conservative majorities in both chambers, that clears the path toward a tougher ban (though Republicans in the state have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/11/14/will-iowa-abortion-laws-change-after-midterm-election-2022/69626994007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said they will discuss next steps only after}}}\uc2  the court resolves the lawsuit over the six-week ban).\par \par \uc2 Similarly, a six-week ban in Georgia that was recently reinstated by the state supreme court could pave the way for new restrictions when the legislature convenes , considering that Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s governor, state house and state senate are all under Republican control. And in Florida, where the GOP clinched supermajorities in both chambers, legislators have indicated an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/11/11/incoming-senate-leader-supports-12-week-abortion-ban-while-house-stays-quiet-00066549"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interest}}}\uc2  in further limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lowering the gestational limit from 15 to 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Any legislation in Florida ultimately depends on its Republican governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . DeSantis has grown quiet on the issue as bans have increasingly proven unpopular, and since he is weighing up a 2024 presidential bid , he may hold off.\par \par \uc2 Nor does Republican control over state governments elsewhere necessarily guarantee new restrictions . In some states, consensus has been hard to come by in a GOP increasingly mired by internal divisions.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, for example, several attempts to pass an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in special session in 2022 failed despite a strong Republican majority.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers were at odds over how far a ban should go, with some supporting an exception for young rape victims, or in cases where there would be no chance of the fetus {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.live5news.com/2022/09/08/exceptions-split-republicans-south-carolina-abortion-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surviving outside the womb}}}\uc2 . Ultimately, those differences proved insurmountable: neither side budged, and none of the proposed bans moved forward. A separate six-week ban is making its way through state courts, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state remains legal up until 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Targeting medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Since Roe fell, requests for medication that can induce a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/2797883"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 miscarriage have shot up, }}}\uc2  and medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now accounts for more than half of all abortions, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2022/02/medication-abortion-now-accounts-more-half-all-us-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Conservatives are increasingly concerned with how to enforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in a climate where people can access pills online and manage their own abortions. Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , and considered very safe in the first trimester. In Oklahoma, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oklahoman.com/story/news/politics/government/2022/11/25/oklahoma-lawmakers-turn-focus-to-medication-abortion/69668379007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lawmakers}}}\uc2  have asked the state attorney general to clarify whether self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through pills violates the law.\par \par \uc2 Introducing in-person screening requirements is another way to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication harder to access, especially in states without bans. For example, a Kansas law tried to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/kansas-court-says-abortion-clinics-can-use-telemedicine/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban providers from prescribing for medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  through}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/kansas-court-says-abortion-clinics-can-use-telemedicine/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 telehealth}}}\uc2 . That law was shot down by a judge last month.\par \par \uc2 Restricting telemedicine is one route anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates will take to target medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, says Glenn, of SBA Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 Students for Life America , another anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, intends to go after medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through environmental laws, through bills that would require fetal tissue to be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/11/23/abortion-pills-opponents-environmental-laws-00070603"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 treated as medical waste}}}\uc2 , curtailing the ability for people to manage their abortions at home. A petition to that effect has already been filed at the federal level with the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 States that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically impose criminal penalties on providers who violate bans, but exempt \u8211\'20\'13 at least formally \u8211\'20\'13 the person actually seeking the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Far-right groups have advocated for an end to that exemption, but their efforts have so far proved politically untenable: in Louisiana, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.laaclu.org/en/news/recapping-2022-louisiana-legislative-session#:~:text=The%20bill%20heightens%20criminal%20penalties,out%20of%20state%2C%20a%20crime."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a bill looking to bring murder charges}}}\uc2  against people who end their own pregnancies failed to pass over the summer, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsweek.com/abortion-murder-bill-get-rewrite-after-failing-louisiana-house-1706280"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with most Republicans finding it too extreme}}}\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are bracing themselves for further such efforts, including bills to criminalize out-of-state travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 an effort attempted in 2022 by Missouri, without success.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver a dozen states that put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in effect in 2022 are states with trifectas that are hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. In those states that have been the most rabidly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we expect to see a next generation of measures that either remove the exemptions in the current law, or increase the penalties or the enforcement mechanisms\u8221\'20\'1D to ban abortions, says \uc2 Jessica Arons\uc2 , senior policy counsel for the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They are also watching efforts to widen the net to penalize those providing assistance to people seeking abortions, including employers.\par \par \uc2 Other legislation already filed in Texas ahead of the new legislative sessionincludes a bill that would count a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/88R/billtext/pdf/HB00521I.pdf#navpanes=0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fetus as a person in the HOV lane}}}\uc2  ; another that would limit tax subsidies for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/88R/billtext/pdf/HB00787I.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 businesses providing support for employees seeking abortions}}}\uc2  ; and legislation that would make it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/88R/billtext/html/HB00819I.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 harder for prosecutors to refuse to enforce \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bolstered protections in blue states\par \par \uc2 Amid the barrage of restrictions, other states have made moves to bolster protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. In the midterms, Michigan, Vermont and California protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions. And throughout the country, there are moves to pass and strengthen so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cshield laws\u8221\'20\'1D to protect providers caring for patients from states with bans.\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund in California, and you give money to somebody from Texas... what kind of protections do we need?\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are looking at those shield laws to see if there are any protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, for example,\u8221\'20\'1D explains the Guttmacher Institutes\u8217\'20\'19 policy expert \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund in California, and you give money to somebody from Texas to come to California for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , what kind of protections do we need [to make sure they\u8217\'20\'19re not legally liable]?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since Roe fell, states like California, Maryland and Delaware have expanded access, including to those from out of state, by passing laws enabling nurses to perform abortions. Meanwhile, in New York, the governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , has allocated millions of dollars to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and the state is also pursuing efforts to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution.\par \par \uc2 These types of efforts are what states hoping to bolster protections will be looking to. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are seeing where there are gaps are in these laws, and trying to fill them basically,\u8221\'20\'1D Nash says.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221213eicd001jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
