\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re doubling down\u8217\'20\'19: how  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  advocates are building on midterm wins\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,668 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2022\par 07:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pro-choice activists are focusing on expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, voter registration and education, and shield laws for providers\par \par \uc2 Renee Bracey Sherman answers the phone and apologizes \u8211\'20\'13 is it OK if we speak while she drives? Like many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates, she tends to keep a packed schedule and talk at lightning speed \u8211\'20\'13 the next initiative, the next law, the next policy on the horizon. Ask advocates how they felt in June after the Dobbs decision sharply curtailed reproductive rights across the US, or in November after wins in the midterm elections signaled strong public support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they\u8217\'20\'19ll answer immediately: We knew this was coming; but the fight\u8217\'20\'19s not over.\par \par \uc2 What Bracey Sherman \u8211\'20\'13 founder and executive director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wetestify.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 We Testify}}}\uc2 , a group focused on the leadership and representation of people who have abortions \u8211\'20\'13 and her colleagues in the pro-choice movement don\u8217\'20\'19t spend much time doing is elaborating on the past, or how they mourned or celebrated, because it\u8217\'20\'19s already in the rear-view window. Their eyes are laser-focused on the future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re doubling down,\u8221\'20\'1D Bracey Sherman said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/11/abortion-midterm-elections-republicans-women-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 central issue}}}\uc2  in a midterm election that saw Democrats retain the Senate and relinquish only a narrow majority in the House. In Kentucky and Montana, voters rejected anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiatives on the ballot; and in Michigan, California and Vermont, voters chose to establish reproductive rights in state law. Over the summer, Kansas voters similarly rejected a ballot measure to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from its constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on the ballot, it won, so that was fantastic,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elisa Wells, co-founder and co-director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plancpills.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Plan C}}}\uc2 , an organization that helps access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication. Those wins \u8220\'20\'1Creally demonstrate that legislators are out of touch with what the majority of Americans want. They support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and understand that it\u8217\'20\'19s basic, common medical care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So pro-choice advocates are taking the fight to new areas, principally access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, which is now heavily restricted in many places, and support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers in states that have criminalized it.\par \par \uc2 The focus is squarely on the states. For the next two years, with Congress divided, it\u8217\'20\'19s understood that little will get done at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe state level is probably where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates will need to work, and have had some success in the last year,\u8221\'20\'1D said Shana Kushner Gadarian, professor of political science at \uc2 Syracuse University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ballot initiatives were one of those real successes. It\u8217\'20\'19s important for organizers \u8220\'20\'1Cto get things directly in front of voters, because they seem to be winning on that side\u8221\'20\'1D, she said.\par \par \uc2 They were successful in two ways. First, reproductive health can be an issue that stretches across partisan lines, with Democrats, Republicans and Independents alike voicing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2022/07/06/majority-of-public-disapproves-of-supreme-courts-decision-to-overturn-roe-v-wade/#americans-views-of-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some level of support}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 especially when it comes to opposition to total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 But the Dobbs decision also drove a surge in voter registration, especially for Democrats, and it made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a more salient voting issue, even beyond ballot initiatives, Gadarian noted.\par \par \uc2 That translated into results. Voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/interactive/2022/abortion-rights-election/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposed}}}\uc2  anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  candidates in several states: Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin elected Democratic governors who could veto anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation, while Republicans in Arizona, North Carolina and Wisconsin failed to reach a supermajority in both the state senate and house, making it impossible to override such a veto.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a strong message to candidates in future elections. \u8220\'20\'1CPoliticians care about re-election,\u8221\'20\'1D Gadarian said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey want to make sure that they\u8217\'20\'19re not going so far ahead of the public that they are going to get punished during the next election cycle.\u8221\'20\'1D That could mean instituting less-encompassing laws in conservative states, such as Florida\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week restrictions, she said.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates went too far by creating state-level bans, which are extremely unpopular, and even pushing for a nationwide ban, said Bracey Sherman. \u8220\'20\'1CThey overplayed their hand on everything.\u8221\'20\'1D There was also an unprecedented number of candidates and elected officials who were open about their own abortions \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Ca historic moment\u8221\'20\'1D for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  support, she said.\par \par \uc2 Advocates in at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewtrusts.org/en/research-and-analysis/blogs/stateline/2022/11/23/buoyed-by-midterm-successes-abortion-rights-advocates-prepare-2024-ballot-measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10 states}}}\uc2  with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies are now considering ballot initiatives. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Category:Abortion,_Oklahoma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/South_Dakota_Right_to_Abortion_Amendment_(2024)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South Dakota}}}\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have asked to add initiatives to 2024 ballots; while several key leaders in Missouri, which has a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, are up for re-election in two years, and the state could also see ballot initiatives in 2024.\par \par \uc2 New ballot initiatives could enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into state law in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/01/nyregion/abortion-ny-state-law.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/where-abortion-will-be-on-the-ballot-in-2024/2022/11/27/9e6a7388-6e5c-11ed-867c-8ec695e4afcd_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Jersey}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/news/2022/10/majority-of-ohioans-would-support-enshrining-abortion-rights-in-the-ohio-constitution-new-poll-says.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio}}}\uc2 , a proactive move that several states have taken. In some states, citizens can put forth the proposals to be added to the ballot, or they may ask their state lawmakers to add them.\par \par \uc2 Next in Kentucky is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2022/11/15/kentucky-abortion-ban-supreme-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal challenge}}}\uc2  to the near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after six weeks that has no exceptions for rape or incest and that only allows abortions under a licensed physician\u8217\'20\'19s care and if life-saving organs are threatened. Even if that challenge is successful, the next ban in place is on pregnancies after 15 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 so the fight would need to continue, advocates say.\par \par \uc2 One important takeaway from places like Kansas, the first state to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to a vote this summer, is that long-term community organizing and education was highly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bmj.com/content/378/bmj.o2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 effective}}}\uc2 . Other grassroots organizations are taking note. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theafiyacenter.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Afiya Center}}}\uc2 , a reproductive justice organization in Texas that had to stop its work as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund due to new state law, is focusing more on community mobilization, voter registration and education campaigns.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are actually launching a voter engagement campaign at the top of the year, called \u8216\'20\'18I am a reproductive justice voter,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Cerita Burrell, director of programs at the Afiya Center. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to educate folks more on the electoral process. It really comes down to policy and lawmakers \u8211\'20\'13 getting the right people in office that understand the right to full bodily autonomy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the pro-choice movement is not just entrenching but pushing as many laws as they can, \u8220\'20\'1Cand they\u8217\'20\'19re sticking\u8221\'20\'1D, Wells said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo why don\u8217\'20\'19t we go on the offensive?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some groups are focusing on shield laws to protect providers who prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication via telehealth. In July, Massachusetts passed a sweeping law that shields providers who offer care to residents of states where it\u8217\'20\'19s restricted. \u8220\'20\'1CWith this new law in place, we\u8217\'20\'19re helping get a group together of providers who would then be able to legally provide telehealth care into states that restrict access,\u8221\'20\'1D Wells said. Other states should do the same, she said.\par \par \uc2 Plan C advocates for exactly that, as well as pushing to remove federal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/07/us-mail-order-abortions-oklahoma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 regulatory limitations}}}\uc2  on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some international organizations like AidAccess already ship to places where state law restricts \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are \u8220\'20\'1Csafer than Tylenol, safer than Viagra \u8211\'20\'13 it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t need to be as highly regulated and medicalized as it is\u8221\'20\'1D. Wells said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy are these restrictions still standing on this extremely safe and effective technology? It\u8217\'20\'19s politics.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She would like to see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications available over the counter. \u8220\'20\'1COur philosophy has always been you really need to push the envelope and try these things, because they might stand up,\u8221\'20\'1D Wells said.\par \par \uc2 Courtrooms, not coat hangers\par \par \uc2 With medication and self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , safety is not the concern it was in pre-Roe America. The current challenges are not about back-alley abortions and coat hangers, advocates say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSelf-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now does not look like what it looked like in the 70s,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jennifer Lim, the communications and media director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://linktr.ee/IWRising"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Indigenous Women Rising}}}\uc2 , a reproductive justice organization that runs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t lean in towards, \u8216\'20\'18We won\u8217\'20\'19t go back\u8217\'20\'19, because we don\u8217\'20\'19t have to \u8211\'20\'13 the future looks very different than what it used to. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are very safe, they\u8217\'20\'19re very effective. But the criminalization aspect is a whole different part of it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Criminalizing pregnancy was a rising threat even before Roe was overturned, and the Dobbs decision could see prosecutions jump.\par \par \uc2 So legal assistance in a rapidly changing and often confusing landscape has become a huge new area of focus.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to stop criminalizing people for their pregnancy outcomes,\u8221\'20\'1D Wells said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to not have criminal charges falling on a person who needs healthcare in our country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And those who have been marginalized \u8211\'20\'13 people of color, people living in poverty, gender-variant folks, younger people, immigrants \u8211\'20\'13 are the most likely to experience challenges like these. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s people who lack access and resources and face obstacles who are left with fewer options,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jill Adams, executive director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ifwhenhow.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 If/When/How}}}\uc2 , a legal resource for reproductive justice.\par \par \uc2 The organization\u8217\'20\'19s Repro Legal Helpline has seen a 14-fold increase in inquiries since the Dobbs decision. \u8220\'20\'1CWe expect that higher level of interest to maintain, and we have been staffing up,\u8221\'20\'1D Adams said. The jumble of reproductive laws in the US is puzzling to anyone attempting to navigate care, Adams said, and many callers at first \u8220\'20\'1Cneed to understand what the legal landscape is \u8211\'20\'13 what, if any, legal risks they face so that they can make the decision that\u8217\'20\'19s best for them,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 If/When/How also has a Repro Legal Defense Fund to provide financial assistance to people who have been criminalized for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including partners, friends and family members. The fund supports pre-trial release, with bail bonds and bail alternatives, as well as the full costs of legal defense. More money is going into that, too, Adams said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a \u8220\'20\'1Chot topic\u8221\'20\'1D in the midterm elections, with politicians relying on the issue to turn out voters, Lim said. \u8220\'20\'1CHow do we get folks to stay engaged and understand that this is a long-term battle?\u8221\'20\'1D she asked. \u8220\'20\'1C??We don\u8217\'20\'19t want to lose momentum.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221207eic7000xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
