\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How Democratic wins in key toss-up seats helped stave off the \u8216\'20\'18red wave\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam \par \uc2 875 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 November 2022\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The expected Republican steamroll in the midterms never materialized, due to concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and a mobilized Democratic base\par \par \uc2 When Republicans narrowly clinched control of the \uc2 US House of Representatives\uc2 , it was clear the \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D failed to materialize and Democrats avoided what many feared would be a hefty defeat.\par \par \uc2 Considering the midterm elections historically tend to favor the president\u8217\'20\'19s opposing party, Democrats performed well. Despite Biden\u8217\'20\'19s low approval rating, his administration had the best midterm performance of any president in decades.\par \par \uc2 That strong performance, especially in battleground seats, came about because the party was able to mobilize its base \u8211\'20\'13 greatly helped by the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 while in many areas Republicans were hampered because extremist candidates, often espousing unpopular views around election denial, failed to deliver enthusiastic support.\par \par \uc2 Examining certain toss-up seats reveals the pattern that helped the Democrats over-perform expectations. A competitive House race is often defined by how close the results appear to be, as indicated by early polling and the district\u8217\'20\'19s past performance in previous elections.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Koning, the director of Rutgers University\u8217\'20\'19s Eagleton Center for Public Interest Polling, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIf we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about numbers, competitiveness would be something within the margin of error or a very small margin between two candidates in pre-election polling. Substantively, a competitive race would be where either candidate has a shot of winning and are both polling equal support throughout the election cycle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democrats performed better in competitive races, and they certainly performed better where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on the ballot\par \par \uc2 Ashley Koning\par \par \uc2 Koning added: \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats performed better in competitive races, and they certainly performed better where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on the ballot either directly or indirectly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Exit polls showed issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were top of mind for voters, particularly women, as seen by the ballot measures passed to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in all five states that held such votes.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, often referred to as a bellwether state because its election results often match that of the nation, incumbent Democrats like \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  and \uc2 Jennifer Wexton\uc2  fended off their Republican challengers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat was a key indicator that the House would be in better shape for Democrats,\u8221\'20\'1D Koning said.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, a swing state, not only did incumbent governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  hold on to her seat, reproductive rights won big, too. Michigan voters opted to establish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. Before the election, Democratic House candidate \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  predicted that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measure passed, she\u8217\'20\'19d win her election. Slotkin ultimately won in the highly competitive seventh district.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re seeing success for Democrats in such a swing state and such a competitive state time and time again in election cycles,\u8221\'20\'1D Koning said.\par \par \uc2 Another factor is the dismal performance by Republican candidates with extreme far-right views who aligned themselves with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a trend also seen in the losses of election deniers in nearly every statewide race.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe saw that Republican candidates actually took quite a hit if they were extreme and/or backed by Trump,\u8221\'20\'1D Koning said.\par \par \uc2 In south Texas, Democrats re-flipped a seat in the 34th district when \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2  defeated far-right incumbent Mayra Flores, who won a special election earlier this year. Another Democrat, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , held on to his seat in the state\u8217\'20\'19s 28th district.\par \par \uc2 In Washington\u8217\'20\'19s third congressional district, progressive Marie Gluesenkamp Perez didn\u8217\'20\'19t just win her race against Trump ally Joe Kent, she also flipped the red seat blue.\par \par \uc2 Redistricting also played a crucial role in the nail-biting race for House control and could be responsible for some of the few Democrat reversals during the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had a strong night\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\par \par \uc2 In states like Michigan, California, Pennsylvania, North Carolina and New York, independent commissions and courts drew competitive maps that gave both parties a chance to compete for seats. In other states, like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gerrymander.princeton.edu/redistricting-report-card?planId=recL5EF85h0ILukMA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gerrymander.princeton.edu/redistricting-report-card?planId=recGXGgCh8REltOnL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gerrymander.princeton.edu/redistricting-report-card?planId=receAu6OJuYEkxKjG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Illinois}}}\uc2 , lawmakers drew maps that were heavily distorted to favor one party over the other.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn the whole, maps looked slightly better for Democrats than they did previously. Of course, it still did favor Republicans across the country, but really that\u8217\'20\'19s state-by-state dependent. There were a number of gerrymandering court cases that came in the final weeks and months before the election,\u8221\'20\'1D Koning said.\par \par \uc2 One such court case was in New York, often thought of as a predictably blue state.\par \par \uc2 New York Democrats tried to gerrymander the state map in 2022 in favor of their party, but the map was thrown out by a state judge. The state then saw a wave of Republican House wins, like in district 17, where the incumbent representative and chair of the House Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 campaign arm, \uc2 Sean Maloney\uc2 , lost to Republican Mike Lawler.\par \par \uc2 New York\u8217\'20\'19s fourth district, which was expected to lean Democrat, was won by Republican Anthony D\u8217\'20\'19Esposito.\par \par \uc2 In a press conference before the House was officially called for Republicans, Biden said: \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats had a strong night. And we lost fewer seats in the House of Representatives than any Democratic president\u8217\'20\'19s first midterm election in the last 40 years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221120eibk00108\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
