\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Why the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 biggest wins of the midterms weren\u8217\'20\'19t in Washington DC\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine in New York \par \uc2 1,887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2022\par 17:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Breaking Republican strangleholds over state capitols gives Democrats an advantage in the fight for voting, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and LGBTQ+ rights\par \par \uc2 While Democrats staved off a red wave in Washington during the midterm elections, the party\u8217\'20\'19s most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/07/republican-state-legislature-races-power-control-midterms-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 significant victories came}}}\uc2  far away from the US Capitol. They were in state legislatures across the country with consequences that will be felt for years to come.\par \par \uc2 Over the last decade, Republicans have quietly amassed power in state capitols, investing in races for state legislatures that can be decided by just a few hundred votes. It\u8217\'20\'19s an investment that has paid off wildly. Since state legislatures draw electoral districts in many places, Republicans have used that advantage to entrench their power, drawing district lines that further guaranteed their majorities. They have also used those majorities to pass measures that make it harder to vote, strip LGBTQ+ protections, loosen gun laws and restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the midterms, however, Democrats flipped at least three state legislative chambers and held on to their majorities in several states where they were in jeopardy. The victories ended years of Democratic defeat and disappointment and caught even some Democrats off guard. It marked the first midterm election since at least 1934 in which the president\u8217\'20\'19s party didn\u8217\'20\'19t lose control of a single legislative chamber, according to the Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee (DLCC), which focuses on state legislative races.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe went into this cycle very clear-eyed. Knowing it was a presidential midterm and frankly expecting to lose seats,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessica Post, president of the DLCC. \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans had everything in their favor. By all accounts, this election should have been a landslide for the Republicans. Instead, their so-called red wave looks more like a puddle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Democrats took control of both chambers of the legislature for the first time in nearly 40 years. Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, was also re-elected to a second term, giving the party complete control of state government in one of America\u8217\'20\'19s most politically competitive states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI felt pretty confident we were gonna get one chamber and be looking at potentially picking up the chamber in two years. But it was surprising for me that we flipped both houses,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mallory McMorrow, a Democratic Michigan state senator who worked on flipping state legislative seats.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also flipped control of the Minnesota senate, giving them complete control of state government there. In Pennsylvania, ballots are still being counted in two razor-thin state house races that will determine control of the state house.\par \par \uc2 They also prevented Republicans from gaining supermajorities in the Wisconsin and North Carolina legislature, an extremely significant development that will prevent GOP-controlled bodies from overriding vetoes from Democratic governors there. Democrats held their majorities in state legislative chambers in Colorado, Maine, Oregon, Washington and New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe felt those goals were certainly long shots. Many thought they were unattainable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daniel Squadron, a former New York State senator who co-founded the States Project, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/23/us/politics/state-legislature-races.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent $60m}}}\uc2  towards electing Democrats in state legislative races. \u8220\'20\'1CIn every chamber, we\u8217\'20\'19ve either met or exceeded our electoral goals.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Those victories could play an important role in ensuring a free and fair election in two years. In 2020, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to lean on GOP state legislative majorities in key battlegrounds like Wisconsin, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Arizona to try to overturn the results. The Democratic wins could also offer a critical safeguard against the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , which could soon {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/30/us-supreme-court-state-elections-legislatures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 endorse}}}\uc2  a fringe legal theory, backed by Republicans, that says state legislatures have virtually unchecked power to set rules over federal elections.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and allies pointed to a combination of factors behind the wins.\par \par \uc2 There were more competitive maps in place in Michigan and Pennsylvania this cycle, replacing districts Republicans drew a decade ago. Concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and competitive statewide races also drove voters to the polls. Strong candidate recruitment allowed Democrats to connect with voters. And deep investments allowed Democratic candidates to get out on the campaign trail early and launch an offense against Republican opponents.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Democrats failed to flip any of the chambers targeted to try to get a seat at the table in the redistricting process. In 2010, amid a juggernaut GOP effort, Democrats lost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2020/08/07/897831713/with-focus-on-redistricting-democrats-place-new-emphasis-on-statehouses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 control of 20 chambers in a single night}}}\uc2 . In the 2014 midterms, 11 chambers flipped to Republican control.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats can, in fact, win at the state legislative level. When we invest our dollars appropriately. When we utilize evidence-based tactics, and when we have candidates who can meet voters where they are, we proved that this year,\u8221\'20\'1D said Adam Pritzker, another co-founder of the States Project. \u8220\'20\'1CState legislative politics is not the minor leagues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans were massively outspent in state legislative races, Dee Duncan, the president of the Republican State Leadership Committee (RSLC), wrote in a memo to donors last week. Four outside left-leaning groups alone spent $125m on the races, dwarfing the $30m the RSLC invested. Duncan noted that the groups, including the DLCC, the States Project and Forward Majority, spent more in three states \u8211\'20\'13 Michigan, Minnesota and Pennsylvania \u8211\'20\'13 than the RSLC spent across the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis influx in funding drove significant spending disparities in each state that were hard to overcome,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CIn every state where we lost a chamber or have potential to lose a chamber (Pennsylvania), Republicans did not win a single statewide election.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Christina Polizzi, a DLCC spokesperson, disputed that the RSLC was outspent. \u8220\'20\'1CThe RSLC has absolutely no credibility on this. They have a long history of selective memory and flat out lying about their spending numbers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CAlthough they have nothing to show for it, the reality is they outraised and outspent the DLCC this cycle while still being only a fraction of the overall GOP spending in the states. They can\u8217\'20\'19t blame their losses on the money \u8211\'20\'13 they lost because Republicans have bad policies and flawed candidates that the American people didn\u8217\'20\'19t support.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans did earn a few key wins in state races. In North Carolina, they flipped partisan control of the state supreme court. And in Ohio, they won a seat being vacated by a retiring GOP member who had been a swing vote. Those majorities could allow Republicans in those states to pass more aggressively gerrymandered maps, as well as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Beyond local politics\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Kristen McDonald Rivet won one of the key state senate races that helped Democrats flip control of the chamber. From the moment she launched her campaign until just a few days before the election, she said, there were pundits who said she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t win the seat, which borders the Saginaw Bay. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  narrowly carried the district by three points in 2020; McDonald Rivet wound up winning by six points.\par \par \uc2 When she spoke with voters, McDonald Rivet would mention democracy and voting rights \u8220\'20\'1Call of these things that Democrats hold dear\u8221\'20\'1D, she said in an interview. But she also recognized the sense of economic unease in her district; families used to be able to work in a plant, raise their families and live \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Michigan dream to go to Disney World in the winter\u8221\'20\'1D. She and her husband have raised their six children in the district and her husband\u8217\'20\'19s family has lived there for five generations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not about local politics. It was about people\u8217\'20\'19s day-to-day. Local is not quite the right word,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI started a lot of my stump speeches with \u8216\'20\'18you know politics doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be this way.\u8217\'20\'19 And it resonated, probably because I actually believe it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her campaign was also boosted by a strong party infrastructure, millions spent on media, and a massive grassroots effort. Her campaign knocked on 72,000 doors and had 7,500 individual donations, including hundreds of people in her district who had set up recurring donations, some for as little as $3.\par \par \uc2 She was aided by Michigan Democrats like McMorrow, a state senator who gained national attention after a viral speech earlier this year and raised more than $2m to her Pac to help Democrats running for state legislative seats. In 2018, when she was a first-time candidate, McMorrow remembered being brushed off by donors who were more interested in statewide and federal races. That money, now, she said, allowed Democrats to hire paid field organizers and start campaigning earlier than Republican counterparts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think people really get it. And I think we were able to really make the case to people to say in a year when the Dobbs decision came down, really highlighted how important state legislatures are in a very tangible way. Because this is an issue that\u8217\'20\'19s coming back to the states,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Brian Munroe, a Democrat, knocked on 10,000 doors on his way to flipping a state house seat in Bucks county, Pennsylvania, a competitive area outside Philadelphia. \u8220\'20\'1CI heard from a lot of people, Republican, Democrat, independent, pro-Trump, not pro-Trump,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, recalling many conversations about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody was tired of the divisiveness. I\u8217\'20\'19m even talking about people with the Let\u8217\'20\'19s Go Brandon flag out front.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Munroe initially was met with some skepticism from big donors, but won them over by pointing out that the district had been redrawn to include a township where he served on the board of supervisors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTaking a risk can pay off. Not taking a risk will never pay off,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CIf all you do is focus on keeping the seats that you have and you\u8217\'20\'19re not in the majority, then you\u8217\'20\'19re always going to be relegated in the minority. That changed to a degree this cycle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Lori Palmeri, the mayor of \uc2 Oshkosh\uc2 , won a competitive race for the state assembly that blocked Republicans from gaining a supermajority in the legislature that would have allowed them to override any vetoes from \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a Democrat who was elected to a second term.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the things I heard the most at the doors was folks were fed up with the political polarization,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey wanted to see their legislators showing up and doing that work \u8230\'20\'26 The message is really from the voters. They really are not going to tolerate one-party rule.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Inspiring long shots\par \par \uc2 Democrats and allies hope that the success they saw in 2022 will lead more Democrats to take state legislative races seriously.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy great hope is that our party will really start to understand that everything that affects people\u8217\'20\'19s daily lives, including voting rights and the certification of US presidential elections, happens in states with Democratic trifecta control,\u8221\'20\'1D said Post of the DLCC.\par \par \uc2 In the 48 hours after election day, more than 500 people expressed interest in running for office, said Amanda Litman, the executive director of Run for Something, which focuses on recruiting people to run for office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInspiring wins in long shot races inspire more people to take on long-shot races. It\u8217\'20\'19s especially true when the candidates in those are young people, women, people of color. It compounds on itself,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221118eibi0028z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
