\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Marijuana,  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 , climate: how the US voted down the ballot in midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Victoria Bekiempis in New York, Kari Paul in Oakland, Miranda Bryant and agencies \par \uc2 639 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2022\par 12:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Green wave\u8217\'20\'19 expands to Maryland with voters opting for recreational cannabis while California votes to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rightsUS midterm election results 2022: liveUS midterm elections 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 latest live news updates\par \par \uc2 Voters across the US weighed in on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/state-ballot-initiatives-us-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 variety of ballot measures}}}\uc2  during the US midterms on Tuesday, passing judgement on everything from recreational drugs to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, to sports betting, prison labour and the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 Multiple states voted on whether or not to legalize recreational marijuana, part of a growing \u8220\'20\'1Cgreen wave\u8221\'20\'1D that has already led to many relaxing their laws on cannabis use.\par \par \uc2 In Maryland, voters green-lighted a constitutional amendment to legalize recreational marijuana for adults over 21, making it the 20th state to take that step. The constitutional amendment in Maryland does not mean that adults can simply go into a shop and lawfully buy marijuana; rather it opens the door for Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s state legislators to establish a legal industry.\par \par \uc2 That industry could start \u8220\'20\'1Cas soon as\u8221\'20\'1D 1 July, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/elections/bs-md-pol-recreational-cannabis-legalization-question-20221108-gdf5yjmpwvfbzcdgmtdfxvbo6a-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Baltimore Sun}}}\uc2 . The vote also means that adults 21 and over can legally raise up to two marijuana plants at their homes starting 1 July.\par \par \uc2 Voters in four other states \u8211\'20\'13 including Arkansas, Missouri, North Dakota and South Dakota \u8211\'20\'13 were considering similar marijuana measures in elections seen as a test of legalization\u8217\'20\'19s support in more conservative parts of the country. As of late Tuesday night, the results were mixed, with recreation marijuana measures rejected in Arkansas and North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Another ballot measure on legal drug use was in Colorado, where voters weighed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/state-ballot-initiatives-us-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot measure}}}\uc2  that, if passed, would decriminalize psychedelic mushrooms. The result of that proposition was still too close to call late on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, voters weighed up measures on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade. Reliably, Democratic California and Vermont voted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state constitutions, while in Michigan \u8211\'20\'13 a closely watched presidential battleground \u8211\'20\'13 voters approved a ballot measure affirming the right to make personal reproductive decisions without interference.\par \par \uc2 California also rejected a ballot measure developed by a coalition of environmental and labor groups that would have raised taxes on the richest residents to address global heating by helping put more electric vehicles on the roads. The proposal, which was supported by \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , became highly contentious after governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and other opponents characterized it as a corporate carve-out for the ride-hailing platform.\par \par \uc2 A pair of competing measures that would have legalized sports betting in the state \u8211\'20\'13 and became the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/21/california-sports-betting-prop-26-27"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most expensive ballot initiative campaign}}}\uc2  in US history \u8211\'20\'13 failed by a landslide. Sports betting has been legalized in 30 states in the US but not in California, making it a large untapped market for gambling interests.\par \par \uc2 The two propositions would have expanded gambling access in the state in different ways, with one allowing sports betting online and the other limiting it to Native American casinos and the state\u8217\'20\'19s four horse tracks. Californians overwhelmingly rejected both propositions, with 84% voting against sports betting online and 70% voting no on allowing it at Native American casinos.\par \par \uc2 Separately, California voted to ban flavored tobacco sales in the state, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/11/09/californians-back-flavored-tobacco-ban-00065824#:~:text=This%20makes%20California%20the%20fifth,(D%2DSan%20Mateo)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 marking}}}\uc2  the fifth state in the US to do so.\par \par \uc2 In three states, Alabama, Tennessee and Vermont, voters approved measures to ban slavery and involuntary servitude as a punishment for crime, while Oregon remained too close to call. But Louisiana rejected a ballot question asking voters whether they supported a constitutional amendment to ban involuntary servitude in the criminal justice system.\par \par \uc2 While such changes will not force state prisons to immediately change, it could encourage legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 Gabrielle Canon contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221114eibe002ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoa