\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas man faces charges for allegedly slipping  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  drug in wife\u8217\'20\'19s drink\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 633 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2022\par 18:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Grand jury indicted Mason Herring on two felony counts earlier this month, including assaulting a pregnant person\par \par \uc2 A Texas man faces criminal charges for allegedly trying to end his wife\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy without her knowledge by slipping medication used to induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into her drinks.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, a grand jury indicted the man, a 38-year-old attorney from Houston named Mason Herring, on two felony counts, including assaulting a pregnant person. The second charge Herring faces is \u8220\'20\'1Cassault \u8211\'20\'13 forced induced to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, according to court records.\par \par \uc2 Texas has one of the most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/08/25/texas-trigger-law-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restrictive \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  laws}}}\uc2  in the country, allowing abortions only when a pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s life is at serious risk, with no exceptions for rape or incest. The law not only makes it a felony to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure \u8211\'20\'13 it also gives civilians the ability to sue any person who helps a person get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Herring\u8217\'20\'19s wife alleges that he began lecturing her in March on the importance of hydration during pregnancy and offered her glasses of water. After drinking from one of the glasses, she noticed the water was cloudy. Herring told her the cloudiness could be from dirty pipes.\par \par \uc2 She started cramping half an hour later and eventually experienced severe bleeding, leading to an emergency room visit. After experiencing these symptoms, she started to believe that her drink had been tampered with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing medication.\par \par \uc2 An affidavit written by the Houston police department, described by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2022/11/14/texas-husband-abortion-drug-wife/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post}}}\uc2 , said that the Herrings had been married for 11 years, and over time, their relationship became strained. Herring in February expressed wishes to separate from his wife and moved out of their home, though he agreed to couples\u8217\'20\'19 therapy. The couple have two children, ages two and six.\par \par \uc2 Herring\u8217\'20\'19s wife learned that she was pregnant and told her husband during a couples\u8217\'20\'19 counseling session on 8 March. She told officials that her husband\u8217\'20\'19s reaction to the news was negative.\par \par \uc2 After Herring\u8217\'20\'19s wife returned home from the emergency room, he allegedly gave her four more drinks over the next week. She noticed three had \u8220\'20\'1Can unknown substance\u8221\'20\'1D in them, and the fourth was a bottle of orange juice with the seal broken.\par \par \uc2 Herring was scheduled to visit his wife on 20 April. His wife invited two people over to potentially witness any of his suspicious behavior. During that scheduled visit, Herring tried to give her drink, which his wife and the two people noticed had \u8220\'20\'1Can unknown substance\u8221\'20\'1D inside.\par \par \uc2 The next week, Herring\u8217\'20\'19s wife saw him slip the contents of a small plastic bag into a drink that he later gave to her. She also inspected trash that he had taken out and saw open packs of Cyrux, which contains misoprostol, an ingredient known to induce abortions. She showed law enforcement videos of both instances, according to the affidavit.\par \par \uc2 An assistant district attorney prosecuting the case, Anthony Osso, told local news station \uc2 KTRK\uc2  that Herring\u8217\'20\'19s alleged actions were \u8220\'20\'1Cmanipulative\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s premeditated,\u8221\'20\'1D Osso said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we are alleging Mr Herring did, which we believe the evidence supports, is a pretty heinous act. To do that to someone who trusts you, it\u8217\'20\'19s taking advantage of that trust.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Herring is scheduled to appear in court again on 2 December. He is out on $30,000 bond. His lawyers did not immediately respond to comment though said in a statement: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are aware that the grand jury has returned these charges. That said, we very much look forward to our day in court and are thoroughly convinced that we will prevail in a court of law when our time comes to defend these allegations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221114eibe0025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
