\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US midterm elections: Democrats retain control of Senate as House race still undecided \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Luscombe \par \uc2 5,895 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2022\par 21:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  hail achievement after Nevada victory, while Georgia Senate heads to runoffUS midterm elections results 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 live\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re closing our US midterms blog now after a day of rancor and recriminations among senior Republicans following the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 success in retaining control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The party seems split into two factions, those keen to move on from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as party leader and kingmaker after the failure of many of his endorsed candidates, and those who insist others, such as Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , are to blame for a lack of messaging.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, jubilant Democrats were looking ahead with renewed enthusiasm, even with control of the House of Representatives yet to be determined.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Warren, senator for Massachusetts, said it was \u8220\'20\'1C \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s victory\u8221\'20\'1D, while the president himself tweeted en route to Indonesia that he was always an optimist and \u8220\'20\'1Cnot surprised\u8221\'20\'1D his party had recaptured the Senate.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve also been watching the heated race for governor in Arizona between extremist Republican Kari Lake and Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . Lake has repeated unfounded allegations that the count is somehow improper as it approaches its sixth day.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you any updates in news reports tonight, and please join us again on Monday. Meanwhile, take a read of Oliver Laughland \u8217\'20\'19s report on where things stand:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/us-midterms-democrats-retain-senate-power-house-undecided"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats celebrate retaining control of Senate as Republicans take stock}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Younger candidates suggest a generational change is under way in the US political landscape. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/edpilkington"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ed Pilkington}}}\uc2  takes a look:\par \par \uc2 We are in the early hours of Wednesday morning, 6 November 2024, and after a nail-biting night two men are preparing to give their respective victory and concession speeches in the US presidential election. One of the men is days away from his 82nd birthday, the other is 78.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of a possible rematch between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in two years\u8217\'20\'19 time is instilling trepidation in both main parties. It is not just the political perils that go with either individual, it\u8217\'20\'19s also the simple matter of their age.\par \par \uc2 What happened to the America of the new world, the young country?\par \par \uc2 But in the wake of this week\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections there is a stirring in the air. The Democratic party may remain heavily dominated by the old guard \u8211\'20\'13 the House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , is 82 and the top senator, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , is 69 \u8211\'20\'13 yet there are strong signs of fresh beginnings.\par \par \uc2 From the first openly lesbian governors in the US and first Black governor of Maryland, to the first Gen Z member of Congress, as well as battle-hardened young politicians in critical swing states like Michigan and Pennsylvania, a new slate of Democratic leaders is coming into view after Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s elections. They may be too new to reshape the 2024 presidential race, but they carry much promise for the years to come.\par \par \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  of Massachusetts (right), the first openly gay governor-elect of Massachusetts, celebrates her win with running mate Kim Driscoll at an election night party on Tuesday. Photograph: Joseph Prezioso/AFP/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a generational change happening of the kind you see every few decades,\u8221\'20\'1D said Joe Trippi, a Democratic strategist who has worked on state and congressional campaigns. \u8220\'20\'1CA younger generation is emerging with different ideas who aren\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily wedded to the old way of doing things.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is perhaps no coincidence that several of the names garnering attention are to be found in battleground states where their political skills and resilience have been put to the test. In Michigan, which has become a frontline state in the struggle between liberal versus Maga politics, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  handily won a double-digit re-election in her gubernatorial race against Tudor Dixon, an election denier.\par \par \uc2 Whitmer, 51, proved herself not only adept at fending off election subversion misinformation in a midwestern state, but she also withstood the pressures of the kidnap plot against her which led to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/26/gretchen-whitmer-michigan-governor-kidnap-plit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last month\u8217\'20\'19s convictions}}}\uc2  of three anti-government plotters. \u8220\'20\'1CAfter two terms as governor, Whitmer is going to be well placed for a move on to the national stage,\u8221\'20\'1D Trippi said.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/democrats-new-generation-candidates-midterms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New generation of candidates stakes claim to Democratic party\u8217\'20\'19s future}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Author and political analyst {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/michael-cohen"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michael Cohen}}}\uc2  has penned an opinion piece for the Observer, and finds America \u8220\'20\'1Calmost perfectly divided between Democrats and Republicans\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 Midterm elections in the United States are where the hopes and dreams of governing parties go to die. Since 1932, the party in power has lost on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/analyses/the-2022-midterm-elections-what-the-historical-data-suggest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 average 28 seats}}}\uc2  in the House of Representatives and four seats in the Senate. In 2018, two years after taking the White House and both Houses of Congress, Republicans lost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/statistics/data/seats-congress-gainedlost-the-presidents-party-mid-term-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40 House seats}}}\uc2  and control of the chamber. In 2010, Democrats lost 63 seats. In 1994, it was 54 and eight Senate seats. Every two years, after electing a new president, voters, generally speaking, go to the polls with buyer\u8217\'20\'19s remorse.\par \par \uc2 But not this year. In a truly stunning outcome, Democrats reversed the historical trend lines and, at least for the time being, protected American democracy from the worst excesses of the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -led Republican party.\par \par \uc2 While all the votes still need to be tabulated, it appears that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  will keep control of the Senate and have an outside chance of maintaining their narrow majority in the House of Representatives. At the beginning of the year such a scenario was virtually unimaginable. Democrats were facing not only historical headwinds but also rising inflation, a teetering economy and an unpopular incumbent president. Traditionally, these are the kinds of political dynamics that portend a Republican-wave victory in November.\par \par \uc2 Protestors at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights march in Washington DC in October. Photograph: Anadolu Agency/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 But then in June the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , removed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/abortion-rulings-history-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 50-year constitutional guarantee}}}\uc2  protecting reproductive health rights and virtually overnight turned American women into second-class citizens. Over the summer, congressional Democrats achieved a host of notable legislative successes and President Biden announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/24/joe-biden-us-student-loan-debt-cancellation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 billions in student loan forgiveness}}}\uc2 , fulfilling a promise he\u8217\'20\'19d made during the 2020 presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 By the autumn, the political winds shifted in the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 direction \u8211\'20\'13 and no issue loomed larger than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In August, a referendum in ruby-red Kansas, which would have made it easier for Republicans in the state legislature to outlaw the procedure, lost by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/02/us/politics/abortion-kansas-vote-result-no.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 whopping 18 points}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democratic campaign advisers took their cues from Kansas and made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the centrepiece of the autumn campaign. And in the states where Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 victories could have led to potentially greater \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, such as Michigan and Pennsylvania, Democrats won decisive victories. In suburban districts, the new linchpin of the Democratic coalition, white female college graduates, outraged by the supreme court decision, propelled House Democratic candidates to victory in toss-up races.\par \par \uc2 Republicans compounded the problem by nominating a host of Trump-endorsed first-time Senate and gubernatorial candidates. The closer a Republican was to Trump, the worse they did on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/13/democrats-victory-miraculous-but-us-still-split-down-middle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 triumph may be miraculous but US is still split down the middle | Michael Cohen}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Raskin: Trump could 'destroy' Republican party\par \par \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , the Democratic Maryland congressman who serves on the January 6 committee, and led the House prosecution at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s second impeachment trial, says the former president might \u8220\'20\'1Cdestroy\u8221\'20\'1D the Republican party.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 . Photograph: Jacquelyn Martin/AP\par \par \uc2 Raskin was speaking on \uc2 CBS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Face the Nation, five days after numerous Trump-endorsed candidates flopped in midterm election races. Emboldened by their failures, some senior Republicans are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/12/donald-trump-us-midterms-republicans-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 openly calling out Trump for the first time}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said he cautioned the Republican party during the impeachment last year to jettison Trump, but instead the Senate voted to acquit him:\par \par \uc2 When I was over in the Senate with the impeachment team, I told the Republicans there that this was our opportunity to deal with the problem of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who had committed high crimes and misdemeanors against the people of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And they needed to act on behalf of the country and the Constitution. But if they didn\u8217\'20\'19t, he would become their problem. And at this point, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the problem of the Republican Party and he may destroy their party.\par \par \uc2 enltrRaskin notes on \uc2 CBS\uc2  that Republicans had an opportunity to convict Trump after his second impeachment and prevent him from running for office, but they didn't, and now "he may destroy their party" {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ddaxECesrA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ddaxECesrA}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Aaron Rupar (@atrupar) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/atrupar/status/1591837838655385601?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The work of the January 6 committee investigating Trump\u8217\'20\'19s coup attempt will continue, Raskin says, though the panel is mindful that Republicans might win control of the House of Representatives and close the inquiry down:\par \par \uc2 In a democracy, the people have the right to the truth. And what we withstood was a systematic assault on democratic institutions in an attempt to overthrow a presidential election. So we have set forth the truth in a series of hearings.\par \par \uc2 And we\u8217\'20\'19re going to set forth the truth in our final report, along with a set of legislative recommendations about what we need to do to fortify American democracy, against coups, insurrections, electoral sabotage and political violence with domestic violent extremist groups involved.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re going to put all of that out there.\par \par \uc2 The January 6 final report is expected to be released before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/laughland-oliver"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oliver Laughland}}}\uc2  on the ongoing fallout from the midterm election results:\par \par \uc2 As the balance of power in the US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/house-of-representatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 House of Representatives}}}\uc2  remained unresolved on Sunday, Democrats are celebrating the projection that they won control of the Senate, marking a significant victory for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as Republicans backed by his presidential predecessor \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  underperformed in key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 While senior {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  remained guarded Sunday about the chances of keeping control of both chambers of Congress, House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  hailed the party\u8217\'20\'19s performance in the midterms following months of projections indicating heavy losses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWho would have thought two months ago that this red wave would turn into a little tiny trickle, if that at all,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi told CNN.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re still alive [for control of the House] but again the races are close. We don\u8217\'20\'19t pray for victory\u8230\'20\'26 but you pray that God\u8217\'20\'19s will will be done.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  receives applause from supporters on Sunday during a news conference celebrating her Senate victory in Nevada. Photograph: Ellen Schmidt/AP\par \par \uc2 As of Sunday morning Republicans remained seven seats shy of the 218 needed to win control of the House, with Democrats requiring 14, an indication that a majority on either side will be slim. As internal discussions between House Republicans intensify over potential leadership roles, with minority leader Kevin McCarthy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/11/11/politics/kevin-mccarthy-speakership-race-house-freedom-caucus/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 facing opposition}}}\uc2  from the far right freedom caucus, Pelosi remained circumspect about her own future, saying she would not make any announcements on her plans until after the House\u8217\'20\'19s control is decided.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy decision will then be rooted in what the wishes of my family [are], and the wishes of my caucus,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said, with reference to her husband Paul Pelosi \u8217\'20\'19s ongoing recovery following an allegedly politically motivated violent burglary and attack at their family home in San Francisco last month. She added: \u8220\'20\'1CThere are all kinds of ways to exert influence. The speaker has awesome power, but I will always have influence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Democrats were projected to maintain their control of the Senate on Saturday evening when a tight race in Nevada was called for the incumbent Catherine Cortez Mastro who defeated \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , a Trump-backed, former state attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/us-midterms-democrats-retain-senate-power-house-undecided"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats celebrate retaining control of Senate as Republicans take stock}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Biden 'not surprised' Democrats held Senate\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he always felt his party would keep control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The president is tweeting on his way to Indonesia, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/joe-biden-seek-us-china-red-lines-talks-xi-jinping-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he\u8217\'20\'19ll meet Chinese leader }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/joe-biden-seek-us-china-red-lines-talks-xi-jinping-talks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Xi Jinping}}}\uc2  on Monday ahead of the two-day G20 summit in Bali. He has been following closely election developments back home.\par \par \uc2 enltrI\u8217\'20\'19m an optimist but I\u8217\'20\'19m not surprised Senate Democrats held the majority.\par \par \uc2 Working together, we\u8217\'20\'19ve delivered historic progress for working families.\par \par \uc2 Americans chose that progress.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1591855917724844038?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2022}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2022/11/13/remarks-by-president-biden-in-press-gaggle-9/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Speaking to reporters}}}\uc2  in Cambodia late on Saturday during the Asean summit, Biden congratulated Democratic Senate leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  but appeared to acknowledge how a Republican-controlled House might affect his agenda going forward.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe feel good about where we are,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I know I\u8217\'20\'19m a cockeyed optimist \u8211\'20\'13 I understand that \u8211\'20\'13 from the beginning, but I\u8217\'20\'19m not surprised by the turnout.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s an interesting, and historic, statistic from the midterm elections, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://statesproject.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 States Project}}}\uc2 , an advocacy group promoting democracy at state level.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in almost 90 years, covering dozens of election cycles, the party in the White House retained every state legislative chamber it was defending, and this year gained two more.\par \par \uc2 enltrHISTORY. MADE. For the first time since 1934, the party who holds the WH didn\u8217\'20\'19t lose a *single* state leg chamber. AND we gained 2 new trifectas. I believe that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/StatesProjectUS?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @StatesProjectUS}}}\uc2  historic investment made the difference. Here\u8217\'20\'19s why.?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EKoeEVr0ZS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/EKoeEVr0ZS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Daniel Squadron (@DanielSquadron) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DanielSquadron/status/1591167640365854720?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 11, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Accusations fly in Republican 'civil war'\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a day of celebrations and recriminations so far in US politics after the Democrats retained control of the Senate in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/democrats-win-senate-control-midterm-elections-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stunning midterm election rebuke}}}\uc2  for previously confident Republicans.\par \par \uc2 A civil war appears to be under way inside the Republican party, with several senior party officials taking to the Sunday political talk shows to point fingers of blame.\par \par \uc2 In one camp, \u8220\'20\'1Clegacy\u8221\'20\'1D Republicans such as \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the retiring governor of Maryland, say responsibility for the failure rests with former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and his handpicked slew of extremist candidates who flopped at the polls.\par \par \uc2 Hogan, among those {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/12/donald-trump-us-midterms-republicans-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calling for a change of leadership}}}\uc2 , told CNN\u8217\'20\'19s State of the Union:\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s cost us the last three elections, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to see it happen a fourth time.\par \par \uc2 enltrThis is the third election in a row that Trump has cost us. Three strikes and you\u8217\'20\'19re out. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/F3LIkZYCsX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/F3LIkZYCsX}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  (@LarryHogan) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LarryHogan/status/1591820175829237763?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 In the other faction, Florida senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , head of the Republican Senate leadership committee, is among the Trump loyalists attempting to scapegoat Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Scott told Fox News\u8217\'20\'19 Sunday Morning Futures he wanted next week\u8217\'20\'19s party leadership elections postponed, claiming McConnell had strangled election strategy:\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said\u8230\'20\'26 we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to have a plan. We\u8217\'20\'19re just going to talk about how bad the Democrats are. Why would you do that?\par \par \uc2 Democrats, meanwhile, are jubilant. Massachusetts senator Elizabeth Warren told NBC\u8217\'20\'19s Meet the Press:\par \par \uc2 This victory belongs to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It belongs to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and the Democrats who got out there and fought for working people. The things we did were important and popular.\par \par \uc2 Things are less clear in the House of Representatives, where a number of close races are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/13/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 yet to be called}}}\uc2 , and Republicans are closing in on a narrow majority.\par \par \uc2 And in Arizona, we\u8217\'20\'19re awaiting a winner in the tight and heated governor\u8217\'20\'19s race between Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  and extremist Republican Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll have more news, commentary and reaction coming up through the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Analysts say {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/catherine-cortez-masto-wins-nevada-senate-race"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 victory by Democrat Catherine Cortez Masto}}}\uc2  in Nevada, which secured her party\u8217\'20\'19s control of the Senate for two more years, will be of massive importance to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s plans for filling judicial vacancies.\par \par \uc2 Retaining the majority in the chamber gives the president the opportunity to keep getting his picks confirmed, something for which the incumbent senator was a key ally even before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . Photograph: David Swanson/Reuters\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCortez Masto has been an excellent senator, who has represented Nevada very well. One example is her efforts to keep the federal district court vacancies in Nevada filled,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.richmond.edu/faculty/ctobias/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Carl Tobias}}}\uc2  , Williams professor of law at the \uc2 University of Richmond\uc2  and former lecturer in law at the \uc2 University of Nevada, Las Vegas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLast year, she and Senator [Jacky] Rosen recommended two well qualified, mainstream candidates whom Biden nominated and the Senate smoothly confirmed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 retention of the Senate majority will enable Biden and that majority to continue nominating and confirming highly qualified judges who are diverse in terms of ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, ideology and experience, like Justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/09/ketanji-brown-jackson-us-supreme-court-first-week"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson}}}\uc2  and Nevada district judges Cristina Silva and Anne Traum.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese nominees and appointees will mean that Biden and the Democrats have honored their promises to counter former President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s confirmation of 231 judges, especially on the Supreme Court and the appellate courts, who are extremely conservative.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor example, Biden and the Democrats have already appointed 25 appellate judges and are on track to confirm at least five, and perhaps as many as 10 more, judges for those courts this year. Biden and the Democratic majority can build on this success for two more years. \u8220\'20\'1C\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, the Republican election denier trailing Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  in the contest to become governor of Arizona, has been on Fox News complaining again about the pace of the count.\par \par \uc2 Although Arizona law dictates the process, and speed, by which the ballots are counted, Lake is also unhappy that Hobbs, as secretary of state, has involvement in the election, even though her opponent\u8217\'20\'19s role is at arm\u8217\'20\'19s length by certifying the count when it\u8217\'20\'19s complete.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI consider someone\u8217\'20\'19s vote their voice. I think of it as a sacred vote, and it\u8217\'20\'19s being trampled the way we run our elections in Arizona,\u8221\'20\'1D Lake said.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake\u8217\'20\'19s supporters at the Maricopa county elections office in Phoenix, Arizona. Photograph: Jim Urquhart/Reuters\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t be the laughingstock of elections anymore. Here in Arizona, and when I\u8217\'20\'19m governor, I will not allow it. I just won\u8217\'20\'19t.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a familiar gripe from Lake, who has pledged to be the media\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/kari-lake-arizona-governor-candidate-media-worst-fricking-nightmare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worst fricking nightmare}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D if she wins, and has refused to say if she would accept the result of the election if she lost.\par \par \uc2 Several dozen of her supporters, some in military-style fatigues, lent a menacing air to the count by gathering outside the Maricopa county elections in Phoenix on Saturday and hurling abuse at sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s deputies.\par \par \uc2 enltrVOTERS: All legal votes will be counted. Your vote will count equally whether it is reported first, last, or somewhere in between. Thank you for participating.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Maricopa County (@maricopacounty) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/maricopacounty/status/1591527776921976832?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Lake, the Arizona Republican party, and Republican national committee (\uc2 RNC\uc2 ), have all lobbed out unfounded allegations of misconduct and incompetence by election officials, as the count enters its sixth day.\par \par \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , chair of Maricopa\u8217\'20\'19s board of supervisors, hit back, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/11/12/politics/vote-counting-maricopa-county-elections-allegation-misconduct/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 telling CNN}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CThe suggestion by the \uc2 RNC\uc2  that there is something untoward going on here in Maricopa county, is absolutely false and offensive to these good elections workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The county is also rejecting online grumblings:\par \par \uc2 enltrSOCIAL MEDIA BOTS: Your disapproval is duly noted but your upvotes and retweets will not be part of this year\u8217\'20\'19s totals. This is not meant as an affront to your robot overlords, it\u8217\'20\'19s just not allowed for in Arizona law.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Maricopa County (@maricopacounty) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/maricopacounty/status/1591527781371887618?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Lake said she did not expect the race to be called until at least Monday. She conceded: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m willing to wait until every vote is counted. I think every candidate should wait until every vote is counted.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , the Florida senator who heads the Republican senatorial committee, has been pouring fuel on the post-midterms fire that threatens \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s future as Senate minority leader.\par \par \uc2 Despite helping to mastermind the election campaign strategy that fell flat when Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/democrats-win-senate-control-midterm-elections-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 retained control of the chamber}}}\uc2 , Scott, and other \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loyalists, say it\u8217\'20\'19s all McConnell\u8217\'20\'19s fault.\par \par \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . Photograph: Rebecca Blackwell/AP\par \par \uc2 On Fox News\u8217\'20\'19 Sunday Morning Futures, Scott repeated his call for next week\u8217\'20\'19s Republican leadership elections at least until after the Georgia Senate run-off on 6 December:\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said\u8230\'20\'26 we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to have a plan. We\u8217\'20\'19re just going to talk about how bad the Democrats are. Why would you do that?\par \par \uc2 What is our plan? What are we running on? What do we stand for? What are we hell bent to get done? The leadership of the Republican Senate says \u8216\'20\'18no, you cannot have a plan\u8217\'20\'19. We\u8217\'20\'19re just gonna run it on how bad the Democrats are, and actually they cave in to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Scott, and Senate colleagues \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  (Florida) and Ted Cruz (Texas) are among those with knives out for McConnell, aided and abetted by former members of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s inner circle who are keen to shift the blame for the Republican flop away from the former president.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s former senior policy advisor, continued the theme, also on Sunday Morning Futures:\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19re going to lose these close races because the Republican brand, set by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  on down, is not exciting, is not persuasive, is not convincing to voters.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 While Republicans, or some of them at least, are blaming \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the party\u8217\'20\'19s midterms misfire, leading Democrats have no doubt with whom the credit should lie: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Warren. Photograph: Michael Dwyer/AP\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts senator Elizabeth Warren, herself a former candidate for the party\u8217\'20\'19s presidential nomination, was almost giddy in her analysis of the elections in an appearance on NBC\u8217\'20\'19s Meet the Press this morning:\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with his preening and his selection of truly awful candidates, didn\u8217\'20\'19t do his party any favors.\par \par \uc2 But this victory belongs to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It belongs to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and the Democrats who got out there and fought for working people. The things we did were important and popular.\par \par \uc2 Remember, right after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was sworn in, all of the economists and the pundits in his ear who were saying, \u8220\'20\'1Cgo slow, go small.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn\u8217\'20\'19t listen to them. And in fact, he went big. He went big on vaccinations. He went big on testing, but he also went big on helping people who were still unemployed, on setting America\u8217\'20\'19s working families up so they could manage the choppy waters in the economy following the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 We were able to address the values and the economic security of people across this country. And it sure paid off. It paid off at historic levels.\par \par \uc2 Also on Meet the Press, White House senior advisor Anita Dunn reflected on Biden\u8217\'20\'19s pre-election strategy of bashing extremist \u8220\'20\'1CMaga Republicans\u8221\'20\'1D named for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Make America Great Again movement:\par \par \uc2 A lot of people thought it wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t work. Former President Trump kind of adopted it himself. But it was a very effective strategy for raising for the American people the hazards of going down that path with democracy denial, threats of political violence to achieve political ends, an extremist program that involves denying women the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , economic policies that continue to be trickled down, as opposed to bottom up and middle out.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party has to come up with what they\u8217\'20\'19re actually for. It\u8217\'20\'19s very clear what President Biden and the Democratic Party are for.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/sam-levine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Levine}}}\uc2  has news of a consequential victory for Democrats in Nevada:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/06/nevada-secretary-of-state-contest-cisco-aguilar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cisco Aguilar}}}\uc2 , a Democrat, was elected Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s top election official, beating Jim Marchant, a Republican who is linked to the QAnon sought to spread misinformation about the results of the 2020 race.\par \par \uc2 His victory is a significant win against efforts to sow doubt in US elections, a growing force in the Republican party.\par \par \uc2 The Nevada secretary of state race was one of the most competitive in the country and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/us-election-deniers-trump-big-lie-republican-midterm-candidates-running"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 closely watched}}}\uc2  because of Marchant\u8217\'20\'19s extreme views. It was also one of several contests in which Republican candidates who questioned the election results were running to be the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/us-election-deniers-trump-big-lie-republican-midterm-candidates-running"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 top election official}}}\uc2  in their state.\par \par \uc2 enltrThank you, Nevada!! It is the honor of my life to serve as your next Secretary of State. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/XiQtpCTlRu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/XiQtpCTlRu}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Cisco Aguilar (@CiscoForNevada) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CiscoForNevada/status/1591233367025934337?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 12, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Marchant, a former state lawmaker, said during the campaign that if he and other like-minded secretary of states were elected, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/10/republican-jim-marchant-nevada-trump-pledge-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 re-elected}}}\uc2  in 2024. He has also said that Nevada elections are run by a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/10/23/politics/fact-check-jim-marchant-nevada-soros-pelosi-schumer-schiff"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cabal}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, and that Nevadans haven\u8217\'20\'19t elected a president in over a decade.\par \par \uc2 He also has pushed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/how-rural-nevada-became-the-next-battleground-for-the-big-lie"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nevada counties}}}\uc2  to adopt risky and costly hand counts of ballots and leads the America First Secretary of State coalition, a group of secretary of state candidates running for key election positions who pledged to overturn the 2020 race.\par \par \uc2 Aguilar had never run for elected office, but cast himself as a defender of Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s democracy. His campaign emphasized the extremist threat Marchant posed. He far outraised Marchant and was much more present on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/cisco-aguilar-nevada-secretary-of-state-winner"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrat Cisco Aguilar defeats election denier in Nevada secretary of state race}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Hogan: Trump the '800lb gorilla'\par \par \uc2 Republicans have been bashing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the Sunday talk shows, with Maryland governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  calling him the \u8220\'20\'1C800lb gorilla\u8221\'20\'1D as the former president prepares to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/trump-inner-circle-rift-2024-presidential-campaign-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  announce a new White House run}}}\uc2  this week.\par \par \uc2 The party\u8217\'20\'19s less than stellar midterms performance, which included a slew of defeats for extremist candidates endorsed by Trump, have prompted growing chorus from senior officials that it\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/12/donald-trump-us-midterms-republicans-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ctime to move on\u8221\'20\'1D from him.}}}\par \par \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 . Photograph: Cj Gunther/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 Leading the call Sunday was Hogan, for so long one of very few Republicans daring to speak out against the twice-impeached former president.\par \par \uc2 Hogan, who is termed out of office in January, told CNN\u8217\'20\'19s State of the Union it would be \u8220\'20\'1Ca mistake\u8221\'20\'1D for Trump to run again, noting that the White House, Senate and House were all lost under his watch:\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s still the 800 pound gorilla. It\u8217\'20\'19s still a battle that\u8217\'20\'19s going to continue for the next few years. We\u8217\'20\'19re two years out from the next election. And the dust is settling from this one. I think it\u8217\'20\'19d be a mistake. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s cost us the last three elections and I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to see it happen a fourth time.\par \par \uc2 Over on NBC\u8217\'20\'19s Meet the Press, Louisiana senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  also laid Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 poor showing at Trump\u8217\'20\'19s door, alluding to his fixation with his 2020 defeat by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and candidates who bought into the lie that the election was stolen from him:\par \par \uc2 Those that were most closely aligned with the past, those are the ones that underperformed.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re not a cult. We\u8217\'20\'19re not like, \u8216\'20\'18OK, there\u8217\'20\'19s one person who leads our party\u8217\'20\'19. If we have a sitting president, she or he will be the leader of our party, but we should be a party of ideas and principles. And that\u8217\'20\'19s what should lead us.\par \par \uc2 What we\u8217\'20\'19ve been lacking, perhaps, is that fulsome discussion,\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/12/donald-trump-us-midterms-republicans-ron-desantis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s time to move on\u8217\'20\'19: have the US midterms finally loosened Trump\u8217\'20\'19s grip on the Republican party?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Pelosi: Democrats 'still alive' in House race\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  says Democrats are \u8220\'20\'1Cstill alive\u8221\'20\'1D in the race for control of the House of Representatives, but acknowledges the pathway to victory is narrow.\par \par \uc2 The speaker\u8217\'20\'19s party needs 218 seats in Congress to retain control of the chamber, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/13/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently has 203}}}\uc2 . Republicans, despite losing several seats they were expected to win handily, have 211 and are closing in on the majority.\par \par \uc2 On CNN\u8217\'20\'19s State of the Union just now, Pelosi was asked specifically about the loss of four House seats in usually reliably blue New York, and whether they would determine control of the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi said:\par \par \uc2 You cannot make sweeping overviews the day after the election, just every district at a time. Our message, people over politics, lower costs, bigger paychecks, safer communities, served us well in the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 I want to salute President Biden for his campaign and President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , all of it raising the urgency of the election, and the awareness that people must vote and that they shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t listen to those who say this is a foregone conclusion because of history, but it\u8217\'20\'19s about the future and get out there and vote.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re still alive. But again, the races are close.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi, 82, would not be drawn about her own future if Republicans take the House, despite hinting last week that the attack on her husband would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/07/nancy-pelosi-police-husband-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 influence her decision about whether to retire}}}\uc2 . House leadership elections are on 30 November.\par \par \uc2 My members are asking me to consider [running], but that\u8217\'20\'19s just through the eyes of the members.\par \par \uc2 We are so completely focused on our political time\u8230\'20\'26 and not worrying about my future, but for the future for the American people\par \par \uc2 But she said she was hurt by response to the attack, which included {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/kari-lake-mocks-paul-pelosi-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 offensive mockery}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just the attack, there was a Republican reaction to it which was disgraceful. An attack is horrible. Imagine how it feels to was the one who was the target, and my husband paying the price, and the traumatic effect on our family.\par \par \uc2 But that trauma is intensified by the ridiculous, disrespectful attitude that the Republicans had. There is no nobody disassociating themselves from the horrible response that they gave to it.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest midterms winners for the Democrats was Michigan governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , who was reelected by double digits. She\u8217\'20\'19s just been on CNN\u8217\'20\'19s State of the Union, speaking out against political violence she says extremists have been stoking.\par \par \uc2 Whitmer herself was the victim of a kidnap plot that resulted in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/26/gretchen-whitmer-michigan-governor-kidnap-plit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conviction of several rightwing extremists}}}\uc2 , and called out a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/paul-pelosi-attack-hospital-released"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hammer attack}}}\uc2  by another on the husband of House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 My opponent [election denier Tudor Dixon ] was a conspiracy theorist, and she has regularly stoked politically violent rhetoric [and] undermined institutions. Whether it is aimed at me, or it is aimed at a Republican congressman like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ktla.com/news/nexstar-media-wire/woman-arrested-after-racist-rant-assault-in-texas-restaurant-lot-caught-on-video/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ron Upton}}}\uc2  or Peter Meijer here in Michigan, it\u8217\'20\'19s unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 My heart goes out to the Pelosi family. I think that this is a moment where good people need to call this out and say we will not tolerate this in this country.\par \par \uc2 Whitmer says the key to her victory was focusing on basics, while her challengers were concentrating on divisiveness:\par \par \uc2 We stay focused on the fundamentals, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s fixing the damn roads or making sure our kids are getting back on track after an incredible disruption in their learning, or just simply solving problems and being honest with the people.\par \par \uc2 Governors can\u8217\'20\'19t fix global inflation. But what we can do is take actions to keep more money in people\u8217\'20\'19s pockets, protect our right to make our own decisions about our bodies.\par \par \uc2 And all of this was squarely front and center for a lot of Michigan voters, and I suspect that\u8217\'20\'19s probably true for voters across the country.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Among the happiest Democrats at the party\u8217\'20\'19s strong midterms performance is \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the Senate majority leader who gets to keep his job for another two years.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . Photograph: Sarah Yenesel/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 Speaking after \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/catherine-cortez-masto-wins-nevada-senate-race"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 victory in Nevada}}}\uc2  kept the chamber in Democrat hands, Schumer told reporters the results were a \u8220\'20\'1Cvindication\u8221\'20\'1D of the party\u8217\'20\'19s agenda, and a rejection of extremist candidates and \u8220\'20\'1Cdivisive\u8221\'20\'1D rhetoric put forward by the Republican party:\par \par \uc2 The election is a great win for the American people.\par \par \uc2 Three things helped secure the Senate majority. One, our terrific candidates. Two, our agenda and accomplishments. And three, the American people rejected the anti-democratic, extremist Maga (Make America Great Again) Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The American people soundly rejected the anti-democratic, authoritarian, nasty and divisive direction the Maga Republicans wanted to take our country, from the days of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/sep/11/trump-big-lie-republicans-democracy-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the big lie}}}\uc2 , which was pushed by so many, to the threats of violence and even violence itself against poll workers, election officials and electoral processes.\par \par \uc2 And of course, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-capitol-breach"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 violence on January 6}}}\uc2 , all of that bothered the American people.\par \par \uc2 And another thing that bothered them just as much, too many of the Republican leaders went along with that, didn\u8217\'20\'19t rebut that violence, and some of them even aided and abetted the words of negativity.\par \par \uc2 Where was the condemnation from the Republican leaders so often missing from so many of them?\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Americans have woken to the remarkable news that Democrats will retain control of the Senate for the next two years, secured by \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s projected {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/catherine-cortez-masto-wins-nevada-senate-race"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 victory}}}\uc2  over Republican \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  in Nevada that was declared on Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , who will remain Senate majority leader, hailed an achievement that appeared unthinkable amid talk of a red tsunami before last Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Only the Senate race in Georgia, which heads to a 6 December runoff, remains to be settled. But the outcome cannot affect control of the chamber as Democrats now have 50 seats, plus vice president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s tie breaking vote.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Things are less clear in the House of Representatives, where a number of close races are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/13/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 yet to be called}}}\uc2 , and Republicans are closing in on a narrow majority.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 And in Arizona, we\u8217\'20\'19re awaiting a winner in the tight and heated governor\u8217\'20\'19s race between Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  and extremist Republican Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll have plenty more news, commentary and reaction coming up in today\u8217\'20\'19s live blog, including from senior officials in both parties.\par \par \uc2 While we wait for the day to unfold, here\u8217\'20\'19s a catch up from \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Dani Anguiano in Las Vegas about Cortez Masto\u8217\'20\'19s majority clinching victory:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/13/catherine-cortez-masto-wins-nevada-senate-race"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto wins Nevada Senate race to hold Democrat seat}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221113eibd000ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
