\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How the fall of Roe shattered Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 midterm dreams\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino \par \uc2 1,549 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2022\par 15:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 37\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women and young people, furious over losing federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, helped Democrats defy expectations\par \par \uc2 This summer, after a tectonic decision by the supreme court to overturn Roe v Wade eliminated the nearly 50-year-old constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  predicted American women would revolt. Republicans, however, saw a \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D brewing, fueled by widespread economic discontent.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, after Democrats defied historical expectations in the first major election of the post-Roe era, Biden effectively declared: \u8220\'20\'1CI told ya so.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen in America made their voices heard, man,\u8221\'20\'1D the president told a crowd of supporters at the Howard Theater in Washington on Thursday. \u8220\'20\'1CY\u8217\'20\'19all showed up and beat the hell out of them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The 2022 midterm elections were expected to usher in staggering losses for Democrats. The party in power typically fares poorly, and with Biden\u8217\'20\'19s approval ratings mired in the low 40s, Republicans were expected to make significant gains.\par \par \uc2 That is not how the results unfolded. As the vote count continues in several key races, Republicans appear on track to win a far narrower House majority than they hoped, while Democrats may retain control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 As a fuller portrait of the results emerges, advocates, Democrats and even some Republicans say one thing is clear: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proved a defining issue. Fury over the loss of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections galvanized women and young people and delivered a string of unexpected victories for Democrats and new protections for reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou cannot have half of the population have their body autonomy put under threat and not expect it to be mobilizing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Heidi Sieck, the CEO and co-founder of #VoteProChoice. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that\u8217\'20\'19s what we saw across the board with young people showing up, women showing up, newly registered women being more than two- thirds of the newly registered voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Exit polls conducted for news networks by Edison Research showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the top issue for many Americans, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/abc-news-2022-exit-poll-analysis-night-trenches/story?id=92970521"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 especially people}}}\uc2  under the age of 30. And about 60% of voters said they were dissatisfied or angry with the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe, according to exit polls conducted by AP Votecast.\par \par \uc2 In every state where an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related measure was on the ballot, voters chose either to enshrine protections or reject new limits. And it was a decisive issue in battleground states like Michigan and Pennsylvania, where the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access was at risk.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, voters decisively approved a ballot initiative establishing a state constitutional right to reproductive freedom, effectively stopping a 1931 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from ever taking effect.\par \par \uc2 They also lifted Democrats to power at all levels of government, re-electing Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and Attorney General Dana Nessel and wresting control of the state legislature from Republicans for the first time in decades. Whitmer defeated Tudor Dixon, a Trump-backed Republican who said she opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly all cases, including when the pregnancy resulted from rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 You cannot have half of the population have their body autonomy put under threat and not expect it to be mobilizing\par \par \uc2 Heidi Sieck\par \par \uc2 Before the election, the Michigan congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , who was considered one of the most vulnerable Democrats, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/michigan-abortion-congressional-race-elissa-slotkin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Guardian}}}\uc2  that she believed her political fate was tied to the result of the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposal. On Tuesday, she held on comfortably.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, where exit polls showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the top issue on voters\u8217\'20\'19 minds, Josh Shapiro, the Democratic nominee for governor, won in a landslide. He defeated \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , a far-right Republican and one of his party\u8217\'20\'19s most strident opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  won Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s race for Senate against Republican Mehmet Oz, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/26/pennsylvania-senate-debate-mehmet-oz-john-fetterman"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 after seizing on a remark}}}\uc2  that Oz made during a debate in which he appeared to suggest that the decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should rest with women, doctors and \u8220\'20\'1Clocal political leaders\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The issue resonated in red states and blue ones. Like Michigan, California and Vermont approved ballot initiatives to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state constitutions. Meanwhile, in deep red Kentucky, voters rejected a ballot initiative that would have explicitly denied constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as they granted the Republican senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  another term. And in Montana, another reliably conservative state, an attempt to impose new restrictions on the procedure failed.\par \par \uc2 David Shor, a Democratic data analyst, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/audio/2022/nov/09/trump-quiet-as-democrats-pull-off-surprise-boost-politics-weekly-america-midterms-special"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Politics Weekly America that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been \u8220\'20\'1Cabsolutely instrumental\u8221\'20\'1D in Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 successes this cycle.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  really up until very recently was a pretty neutral issue for Democrats \u8211\'20\'13 that didn\u8217\'20\'19t mean it was bad, but it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily a vote-getter in most of the country,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. But following the the supreme court decision, he said, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  suddenly became \u8220\'20\'1Cthe single best issue for Democrats\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe extent to which voters cared about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also skyrocketed,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1COut of the 33 issue areas that we track, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  went from being the 30th out of 33 most important issues to being the 12th basically overnight.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the wake of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall, Democrats took every opportunity to highlight their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and contrast it with their opponents\u8217\'20\'19 views. They flooded the airwaves with nearly half a billion dollars on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ads.\par \par \uc2 Voters gave Democrats a wide advantage on the issue, as debates over the loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights raised new questions about miscarriage care and exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother. Republicans, on the defensive, sought to avoid the issue. In several instances, Republican candidates removed references to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from their campaign websites.\par \par \uc2 But months later, as the campaign season came to a close, Democrats were second-guessing the strategy. Many pre-election polls showed voters worried more about inflation and crime than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These surveys prompted a bout of angst and finger-pointing. Some Democrats argued that the party had focused too singularly on reproductive rights at the expense of offering a clear economic message.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  went from being the 30th out of 33 most important issues to being the 12th basically overnight\par \par \uc2 David Shor\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates say that logic is flawed \u8211\'20\'13 that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an economic issue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re saying inflation, if you\u8217\'20\'19re saying economy, you\u8217\'20\'19re saying reproductive freedom as well,\u8221\'20\'1D Sieck said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe absolutely miss that connection at our peril.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After Tuesday night, there was little doubt that the issue had been decisive in key races.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor everyone who said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a losing issue; for everyone who said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is controversial; for everyone who said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a fringe concern and not the meat-and-potatoes kitchen table problem Michiganders care about: you\u8217\'20\'19re wrong, and the \u8216\'20\'18yes\u8217\'20\'19 votes prove it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mini Timmaraju, the president of Naral Pro-Choice America.\par \par \uc2 Even some Republicans conceded the issue may have cost them electorally.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we lost because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an issue that was not on the ballot, if we lost because I\u8217\'20\'19m pro-life, because I believe every life has dignity, I\u8217\'20\'19m OK with that,\u8221\'20\'1D Matt Birk, the Republican nominee for lieutenant governor in Minnesota said in a concession speech.\par \par \uc2 The string of victories for supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights followed an August vote in Kansas, when voters overwhelmingly rejected an attempt to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from its state constitution. The result underscored the political potency of an issue Democrats had long tiptoed around.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Laura Kelly, the state\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor, held off a Republican challenge as did Democratic congresswoman \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 . With the veto pen, Kelly, like Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , who also won re-election in a closely watched race, can block any potential legislation that would impose new restrictions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin and North Carolina, Democrats kept Republicans from winning a supermajority in their state legislatures, meaning lawmakers will not have the power to override a Democratic governor\u8217\'20\'19s veto and pass new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a winning issue and will continue to be in elections to come\par \par \uc2 Mini Timmaraju\par \par \uc2 There were some bright spots for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates. Conservative judges were elected to the state supreme courts in Ohio and North Carolina, an encouraging sign for proponents bracing for a barrage of challenges to new laws restricting the procedure. Though Democrats may keep the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , they failed to win 52 seats, enough to overcome the resistance in their party to eliminating the filibuster and codifying Roe.\par \par \uc2 And key races have yet to be called, including in Arizona, where the Republican nominee for governor, Kari Lake, has backed a territorial-era \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban temporarily halted by a judge.\par \par \uc2 None of what unfolded this election cycle surprised longtime supporters of reproductive rights, but they hope the results will finally convince Democrats that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not only a winning political issue, but a foundational one that should be central to their campaign strategy and governing agendas at the state and federal level.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a winning issue and will continue to be in elections to come,\u8221\'20\'1D Timmaraju said on Friday. \u8220\'20\'1CThat means Democrats need to keep reproductive freedom at the top of their policy agenda and fight to not only codify Roe but expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and birth control.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221112eibc000bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
