\par\par \uc2 Television & radio\par \b \uc2 From brunch chat in Sex and the City to an excuse for a party in Girls: 50 years of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  on TV\b0\par\par\uc2 Hollie Richardson \par \uc2 1,823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par 14:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Half a century ago, a US sitcom called Maude broke explosive new ground by tackling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . How has the issue been taken on since? And will new shows have to be rewritten given the storyline is now illegal in parts of the US?\u8226\'20\'22 I starred in the first US TV show to address \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Why is my country now going backwards?\par \par \uc2 Abortions on television can be funny. They are sometimes heartbreaking or traumatic. They might help educate a woman watching at home who wants to make an informed decision about her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They can be all of these things and more \u8211\'20\'13 all that\u8217\'20\'19s important is that they are normalised. But if you\u8217\'20\'19ve ever watched a US TV show with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  storyline, there\u8217\'20\'19s a good chance that it would play out very differently if it were written today.\par \par \uc2 Take the latest series of Katori Hall\u8217\'20\'19s hit strip club drama, P-Valley. In an episode that aired on 24 July this year, Mercedes (Brandee Evans) drives her 14-year-old pregnant daughter Terricka (Azaria Carter) to the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Mississippi, talking through her options on the way. They walk past anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protestors outside the clinic. \u8220\'20\'1CSo, you just gonna suck it out?\u8221\'20\'1D Terricka asks nervously. \u8220\'20\'1CIf that\u8217\'20\'19s what you decide,\u8221\'20\'1D the nurse replies. After 24 hours, she goes ahead with the procedure. At the time of production this was a realistic, if precarious, reflection of life in a US state where it was legal to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 By the point of broadcast, the storyline had become an impossibility. The real-life \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, the one Terricka visits was based on \u8211\'20\'13 Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization \u8211\'20\'13 permanently closed its doors on 6 July 2022 after the landmark Roe v Wade case to legalise abortions across the US was overturned. The building has now been sold.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe always knew this season was going to be a time capsule of America during one of the most turbulent times in our history,\u8221\'20\'1D says Hall. \u8220\'20\'1CWe addressed the pandemic and police brutality in the Black community, but we never could have predicted that we were writing the Mississippi of yesteryear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As showrunners are forced to rethink how best to write this new reality, it\u8217\'20\'19s devastating to realise that this month marks 50 years since the first legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  storyline aired on mainstreamUS television in Norman Lear\u8217\'20\'19s popular 70s sitcom Maude. In a trailblazing 1972 double bill, Maude\u8217\'20\'19s Dilemma, the 47-year-old New Yorker (played by a brilliantly wry Bea Arthur) comes home and announces to her family that she\u8217\'20\'19s pregnant. She cries, jokes, ponders aloud and talks to her friend, daughter and partner about her options. \u8220\'20\'1CAt the age of 62, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to be the mother of an eagle scout,\u8221\'20\'1D she deadpans at one point.\par \par \uc2 It is Maude\u8217\'20\'19s grownup daughter, Carol (Adrienne Barbeau), who points out that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had already been legal in New York for two years at that time. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you were growing up it was illegal and dangerous and sinister,\u8221\'20\'1D she tells her mother. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a dirty word. It\u8217\'20\'19s not any more.\u8221\'20\'1D Eventually, Maude chooses to terminate the pregnancy and is clearly happy with her decision. It was a milestone moment for women on screen, as the episodes aired two months before the supreme court delivered its decision on the Roe v Wade case. Writing it was a bold move by \uc2 Lear\uc2 ; \uc2 CBS\uc2  received thousands of complaints and reported advertisers dropping out. But when asked about it in a recent interview, his reason for doing so was simple: \u8220\'20\'1CA woman; it\u8217\'20\'19s her body, and I say that as the father of five daughters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt showed us humour can be a great tool when discussing serious issues,\u8221\'20\'1D says Caren Spruch, Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s national director of arts and entertainment engagement, who collaborates with TV producers and writers to inform the content of their scripts on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Spruch says \u8220\'20\'1Ctears streamed down my face\u8221\'20\'1D while recently rewatching the episodes: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I heard Maude\u8217\'20\'19s daughter, Carol, say: \u8216\'20\'18We finally have the right to decide what we can do with our own bodies,\u8217\'20\'19 the impact of the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans hit me in a new way. That\u8217\'20\'19s the power of entertainment, and why we do this work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Maude\u8217\'20\'19s Dilemma really was pivotal in portrayals of women on TV. The following year, in US soap All My Children, Erica (Susan Lucci) became the first character to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it was finally legalised nationwide. Erica\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  did prove controversial, with the backlash proving there was still a long way to go \u8211\'20\'13 and then something ludicrous happened: the storyline was retroactively tweaked, with it emerging that Erica\u8217\'20\'19s doctor transplanted her foetus into his own infertile wife. Audiences stomached this turn of events more readily than a woman choosing to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The real gamechanger came in 1998 with the arrival of Sex and the City, specifically the episode Coulda, Woulda, Shoulda. When Miranda (Cynthia Nixon) announces she\u8217\'20\'19s pregnant over brunch, Carrie (Sarah Jessica Parker) and Samantha (Kim Cattrall) casually and confidently share that they had abortions they don\u8217\'20\'19t regret. Although \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal at that time, the show explores the social stigma still attached to it; Carrie lies to her boyfriend when he asks if she\u8217\'20\'19s ever had one, while Miranda\u8217\'20\'19s doctor repeatedly declares: \u8220\'20\'1CNo judgment!\u8221\'20\'1D after refusing to do the procedure. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t have a baby \u8230\'20\'26 I could barely find the time to schedule this appointment,\u8221\'20\'1D says Miranda at the clinic, where she later decides to keep the baby \u8211\'20\'13 a vital twist, in which a woman has the information and freedom to make a measured decision and change her mind.\par \par \uc2 Things got better from there. In a 2012 episode of Lena Dunham\u8217\'20\'19s Girls called Vagina Panic, Shoshanna (Zosia Mamet) throws Jessa (Jemima Kirke) an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  party \u8211\'20\'13 which Jessa doesn\u8217\'20\'19t turn up to because she miscarries while having sex with a stranger in a bar toilet. A few years later, in \uc2 Netflix\uc2  female wrestling show Glow, Ruth (Alison Brie) says: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not the right time, not the right baby,\u8221\'20\'1D before we see a doctor telling her she\u8217\'20\'19ll feel a pinch when the injection at her cervix numbs her uterus. And in Shonda Rhimes\u8217\'20\'19s political thriller, Scandal, Olivia Pope (Kerry Washington) unceremoniously gets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the soundtrack of the Christmas carol Silent Night.\par \par \uc2 For Hall, who was a child when Maude\u8217\'20\'19s Dilemma aired, it was Olivia\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was most significant to her own work: \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a rare occurrence of seeing a Black woman at the centre of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  story and I appreciated the lack of melodrama surrounding her decision. It was grounded, precise \u8230\'20\'26 it felt so real.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Such nuanced storytelling is crucial. In 2019, Jacobs Institute of Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31331660/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published a report}}}\uc2  that looked into the representation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in television between 2008 and 2018. It found 96 televised plotlines, with 40% depicting some aspect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure, 59% depicting a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure (of which about half were legal and half illegal) and 18% portrayed medication abortions. Of course, there\u8217\'20\'19s no set bar for how many storylines should centre on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The important thing is how they\u8217\'20\'19re shown. Why, for example, are there so few medication abortions \u8211\'20\'13 when the reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  practice, pre-this year\u8217\'20\'19s Roe v Wade overturn, was that more than half of US abortions are by medication?\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now illegal in at least 13 US states, Hall says: she will \u8220\'20\'1Cmost definitely continue addressing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in my writing, as the lives of women is the heart of my work\u8221\'20\'1D. She adds that, in the future, \u8220\'20\'1CI hope there is a flood of thoughtful and complicated articulations of the issue. I also think stories showing what not having access to this part of healthcare looks like is a necessary chapter, especially in places like the deep south.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTell the stories of women who miscarry but can\u8217\'20\'19t get a dead foetus extracted from their wombs, or address the labyrinthine legal process to actually get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to rape \u8230\'20\'26 these are just some of the stories we all need to see. This will only happen if we hold space for more women writers and producers in our industry.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This isn\u8217\'20\'19t just a US issue. The UK\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  act was passed in 1967 (excluding Northern Ireland), five years before Roe v Wade. When \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still illegal in 1965, Nell Dunn\u8217\'20\'19s BBC play Up the Junction, part of The Wednesday Play series, told the story of an unmarried woman who endures a horrific miscarriage after getting a backstreet procedure. On the one hand, it showed the brutal reality women faced, but what woman wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be terrified by this? They saw that they had no option. Another episode of The Wednesday Play in 1968, after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legalised, depicted a pregnant religious woman facing another fear: her Catholic church, which still banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After that, miscarriage was sometimes used as a broadcaster-pleasing cop-out arc. In Brookside in 1986, fortysomething Sheila Grant (Sue Johnston) becomes pregnant after being raped \u8211\'20\'13 but she miscarries before deciding whether to continue the pregnancy. Though this might not be the most positive portrayal, at least soaps could always be relied upon to explore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a more nuanced way.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Holloway (Susan Tully) had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on EastEnders in 1988, telling her partner Lofty: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sorry, I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t do anything else.\u8221\'20\'1D Delving deeper into the complexities for a more modern audience two decades later, Laurel Thomas (Charlotte Bellamy) and Jai Sharma (Chris Bisson) in Emmerdale decided to terminate a pregnancy after learning the baby would be born with Down\u8217\'20\'19s syndrome. Addressing a viewer petition against the storyline signed by 32,000 people, producer Laura Shaw explained it was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot about right or wrong\u8221\'20\'1D but about \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople making really, really difficult decisions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s an urgent need to continue showing these tough talking points: the newly appointed minister for women, Maria Caulfield, is a former member of the all-party parliamentary \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D group and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/oct/30/tory-mp-maria-caulfield-minister-for-women-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted against legalising \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  in Northern Ireland in 2019. Last month, she opposed the use of buffer zones around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 Spruch puts it very simply: \u8220\'20\'1CMaude and her family showed us that [legal] \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe, normal and common, and why everyone must be able to make this decision for themselves. It\u8217\'20\'19s been 50 years since that aired, but its message is still salient: people should have the right to make decisions about their own bodies, lives and futures. Period.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221111eibb001up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
