\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US midterm elections 2022: Trump backlash grows as top Virginia Republican says \u8216\'20\'18I could not support him\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levin (now), Maanvi Singh, Chris Stein and Martin Belam (earlier) \par \uc2 12,282 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par 10:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This live blog is now closed. You can find all our US midterms coverage hereNothing final in House or Senate as count continuesUS midterm elections results 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 live\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 This live blog is now closed. You can find all our coverage {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-midterm-elections-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 Counting is set to continue in all the races that are yet to declare. We\u8217\'20\'19re going to pause our live blog now, but will return if there is anything important to update you on.\par \par \uc2 If you want to know why the vote count is taking so long in some states, you can read our explainer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/when-midterm-results-timings-arizona-nevada-georgia-final-why-taking-so-long"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And if you want to continue to track the results live, you can do so {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/10/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 Today so far\par \par \uc2 Ballot counting continues, and it may still be a while until we find out which party will control the House and Senate. As Republicans wrestle with questions about why the elections failed to bring about the \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D they expected, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has continued in his party\u8217\'20\'19s performance.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a rundown of what has happened so far today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A top White House official{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d13108f08786f8dfabfd6#block-636d13108f08786f8dfabfd6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 insisted}}}\uc2  the Democrats still had a chance at winning the House.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A newly elected Republican congressman{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d29c78f08416f7753d23e#block-636d29c78f08416f7753d23e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  the party should \u8220\'20\'1Cmove forward\u8221\'20\'1D from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The former president is facing pressure to delay announcing his presidential bid after many of his endorsed candidates faltered in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The chair of the Texas GOP said the party {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d254e8f08786f8dfac0d7#block-636d254e8f08786f8dfac0d7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 needs to give voters}}}\uc2  a positive message if it wants to improve on what is looking to be a lackluster performance in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Montana became the latest state where voters said no to further \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s razor-thin Senate and governor races may not be known until Saturday, by when most ballots from the Democratic-leaning Clark county will be counted.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 An official in Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Maricopa county asked Trump-backed gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake to tone down her rhetoric about the elections.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Inflation declined in October, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636cfe8b8f0818507f5637aa#block-636cfe8b8f0818507f5637aa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new government data showed}}}\uc2 . potentially signaling the beginning of the end of the political perilous wave of price increases\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Democrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-636db5de8f088f91b4c64d9c#block-636db5de8f088f91b4c64d9c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 winner}}}\uc2  of the Oregon governor\u8217\'20\'19s race, defeating a Republican candidate in a race that was unusually close in the typically blue western state.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 Chris Stein and Maanvi Singh\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 Democrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  elected governor of Oregon\par \par \uc2 Democrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  is the winner of the Oregon governor\u8217\'20\'19s race, defeating a Republican candidate in an unusually close election in the typically blue western state.\par \par \uc2 enltrDemocrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  was elected governor of Oregon, topping a three-woman field in what was seen as the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s best chance in years to capture the office in the liberal state. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Bf11DqvGKB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Bf11DqvGKB}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MyksvnI10o"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/MyksvnI10o}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 AP Politics (@AP_Politics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AP_Politics/status/1590893483497488385?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 11, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Kotek was the longest serving speaker of the Oregon House, and the GOP candidate, Christine Drazan, was the former leader of the Republicans in the state House. Kotek had 47.1% of the vote and Drazan had 43.5%.\par \par \uc2 Kotek is making history with her win, joining \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  of Massachusetts as the first lesbians elected as governors in the US. Kotek said today:\par \par \uc2 It is an absolute honor. I can tell you that being who I am is important to Oregonians across the state. Lots of young people have come up to me and said thank you for running and thank you for being who you are.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Hi all \u8211\'20\'13 Sam Levin in Los Angeles here, taking over our live coverage for the rest of the evening.\par \par \uc2 Here in LA, voters are closely following the very tight race for mayor of the second largest city in the US, with many votes still to be counted. As of the latest count this evening, \uc2 Rick Caruso\uc2 , a billionaire real estate developer had 50.25% of the vote and \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , the Democratic congresswoman, was trailing slightly behind at 49.75%.\par \par \uc2 More than 545,000 votes have been counted so far, and on Wednesday, LA county officials {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2022-11-10/karen-bass-rick-caruso-la-mayor-election-vote-update"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  that there were still more than 1m votes to be counted. It could take a week for a winner to be called, according to the LA Times, which noted that in the primary, Bass was initially behind in votes, but as more were counted, she ended up surpassing Caruso.\par \par \uc2 Both nominally Democrats, Bass and Caruso come from starkly different backgrounds, and their down-to-the-wire contest comes at a particularly fraught time for Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 The region\u8217\'20\'19s homelessness crisis has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/04/hypothermia-deaths-of-unhoused-in-los-angeles-rise-sharply"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 become a humanitarian catastrophe}}}\uc2 , with LA county recording 69,000 unhoused people in this year\u8217\'20\'19s annual estimate, considered an undercount, including more than 48,000 living outside. City government is also in crisis. Recordings leaked last month captured {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/10/los-angeles-city-council-president-resigns-racist-comments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three councilmembers}}}\uc2 , including the council president, making bigoted and racist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2022-10-13/la-city-council-nury-martinez-racist-who-leaked-audio"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remarks}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 More here on the race:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/la-mayor-race-election-karen-bass-rick-caruso"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Angeles mayor hopefuls Karen Bass and Rick Caruso locked in tight race}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Texas has blocked Biden\u8217\'20\'19s student loan forgiveness program.\par \par \uc2 The Trump-appointed judge declared the policy unlawful in a ruling issued Thursday evening. The ruling comes after a stay on the program was granted in a separate lawsuit. There have been a number of legal challenges against the program from conservative groups and officials.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has more {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2022/11/10/student-loan-forgiveness-texas-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 details}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 In the Texas case, the plaintiffs argued, in part, that the Biden administration made arbitrary decisions about who would qualify for debt forgiveness and how much of their balance would be canceled. Biden\u8217\'20\'19s loan relief plan would cancel up to $10,000 in federal student debt for borrowers who earn up to $125,000 annually or up to $250,000 annually for married couples. Borrowers who received {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2022/08/26/what-is-pell-grant/?itid=lk_inline_manual_11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pell Grants}}}\uc2  are eligible for an additional $10,000 in forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 Biden has continued to promote the program, which could benefit tens of millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 Zoe Grueskin reports:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York}}}\uc2  state voters passed a ballot measure that would fund up to $4.2bn for environmental improvement projects \u8211\'20\'13 including increasing flood resiliency, reducing greenhouse gas emissions, electrifying school buses and creating more green and open spaces.\par \par \uc2 The proposal also aims to reach communities most affected by the climate crisis. If approved, it will allow the state to sell bonds in order to raise funds to finance several projects.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 60% of voters at the state level cast their ballot in support of the measure \u8211\'20\'13 in New York City, that number went up to 81%. The support is not entirely surprising: since the early 20th century, 11 environmental bond acts have made it to the ballot in New York. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rockinst.org/blog/new-yorks-environmental-bond-acts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 All but one, in 1990, passed with a substantial margin}}}\uc2  of the vote.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/new-york-passes-environmental-bonds-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York passes $4.2bn environmental bond act on midterm ballot}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has sent supporters an email trashing Florida governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who is broadly viewed as a potential presidential candidate after his midterm victory.\par \par \uc2 The former president, who has teased that he will soon announce his candidacy for 2024, referred to the governor as \u8220\'20\'1CRon DeSanctimonious\u8221\'20\'1D and accused him of lacking \u8220\'20\'1Cloyalty and class\u8221\'20\'1D for entertaining media questions about whether he\u8217\'20\'19d run against Trump for president.\par \par \uc2 Trump is facing pressure to delay announcing his presidential bid after many of his endorsed candidates faltered in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/trump-advised-delay-announcement-2024-run-georgia-run-off"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump urged to delay announcement on 2024 run until after Georgia runoff }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A top official in Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Maricopa county, the state\u8217\'20\'19s largest, has asked Kari Lake to tone down her rhetoric about the elections.\par \par \uc2 Lake, who is the Trump-endorsed Republican candidate for governor, has denied the 2020 election results and often spread false claims and misinformation about elections. Lake and her Democratic opponent, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , are locked in a tight race.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow, I feel 100% confident we are going to win this, I hate that they\u8217\'20\'19re slow-rolling and dragging their feet and delaying the inevitable. They don\u8217\'20\'19t want to put out the truth, which is that we won,\u8221\'20\'1D Lake said on the conservative {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TPostMillennial/status/1590758610283376640"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Charlie Kirk show}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to win this, and there\u8217\'20\'19s not a darn thing they can do about it. But they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to pour cold water on this movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Maricopa county, officials were not pleased.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone needs to calm down, tone the rhetoric down,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , chair of the Maricopa Board of Supervisors. \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s stop the name calling.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr"Quite frankly, it is offensive for Kari Lake to say these people behind me are slow rolling this."\par \par \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , chairman of the Maricopa County Board of Supervisors, is not very happy about some of the things being said about the elections department. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/OoUpd4oyLf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/OoUpd4oyLf}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 KTAR News 92.3 (@KTAR923) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KTAR923/status/1590851651157766144?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, where many voters vote early, officials have to verify and approve ballots in a process that can take days or weeks. It is normal in close races to have to wait a while for results.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Even though it remains unclear which party will take control of the House with several key races still undecided, House Republican leader Kevin McCarthy has established transition teams for the 118th congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe House Republican majority is getting right to work,\u8221\'20\'1D McCarthy said, despite the fact that it may be a while until we know whether Republicans have a majority. \u8220\'20\'1CThese transition teams will ensure we hit the ground running on issues that Democrats have ignored or made worse for the American people under one-party rule, all while shutting out our fellow citizens from the People\u8217\'20\'19s House.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McCarthy, a California Republican, has long sought the role of House speaker, but Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 underperformance in the midterms means that if he does ascend to the role, he might be helming a very small Republican majority. Several pro-Trump, Freedom Caucus Republicans have also expressed disappointment with McCarthy, and there\u8217\'20\'19s a chance they could mount a challenge against him.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy has named Republican whip Steve Scalise, and congressmen James Comer, Jim Jordan and Bryan Steil as leaders in his transition team \u8211\'20\'13 which could be a strategic move to keep potential rivals close.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here's why votes take so long to count in Arizona\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/edpilkington"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ed Pilkington}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/sam-levine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Levine}}}\uc2  report:\par \par \uc2 Several of the most consequential races are happening in the border state of Arizona. A \uc2 US Senate\uc2  contest between the Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly and Republican challenger Blake Masters could determine which party controls the Senate.\par \par \uc2 There are also consequential state races, including for governor and secretary of state, in which prominent election deniers endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have a shot at winning. So far only 70% of the Arizona vote has been counted.\par \par \uc2 To understand why that is, you have to zoom in to Maricopa county, which covers the state capital, Phoenix. It contains 60% of all votes in Arizona and is the second largest voting jurisdiction in the nation.\par \par \uc2 The number of people who vote early has increased dramatically since the pandemic. This year Maricopa county also saw a surge in the number of early ballots that were dropped off on election day \u8211\'20\'13 they are known as \u8220\'20\'1Clate earlies\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 rising to 290,000, the largest number in the state\u8217\'20\'19s history and 100,000 more than in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Each early ballot has to be verified to check that the voter\u8217\'20\'19s signature matches the signature in the voter rolls, and after that is done it is sent to a bipartisan panel for approval and processing. That all takes time, as we are witnessing.\par \par \uc2 Many people have drawn a comparison of Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s vote count with that of Florida, which called its results within hours of polls closing on Tuesday. That state\u8217\'20\'19s system allows election officials to begin counting mail-in ballots {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncsl.org/research/elections-and-campaigns/vopp-table-16-when-absentee-mail-ballot-processing-and-counting-can-begin.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as soon as they are received}}}\uc2  ; mail-in ballots have to be requested and must be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dos.myflorida.com/elections/for-voters/voting/vote-by-mail/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received by an election supervisor}}}\uc2  no later than 7pm on election day.\par \par \uc2 But the main reason why \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  won his re-election race so quickly on Tuesday was because it was a blowout, with the incumbent Republican governor garnering 59% of the vote while his challenger, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , received only 40%.\par \par \uc2 Had the candidates we are watching in Arizona or elsewhere had such a convincing lead, we would probably not still be waiting for their races to be called. Nonetheless, there are questions that Arizona is going to have to face in future elections.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Richer, who is the recorder of Maricopa county, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/stephen_richer/status/1590404229457661952?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1590404231668051968%7Ctwgr%5Eda01c3109daaf41eac96f07ce6b9acb61f7420d1%7Ctwcon%5Es2_&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.fox10phoenix.com%2Fnews%2F2022-election-why-is-it-taking-so-long-for-arizona-to-count-ballots"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said that after the dust settles}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwe will likely want to have a policy conversation about which we value more: convenience of dropping off early ballots on election day or higher percentage of returns with 24 hours of election night\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.08pm GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/oliver-milman"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oliver Milman}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/nina-lakhani"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nina Lakhani}}}\uc2  in Sharm el-Sheikh report:\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has accused Republicans of treating the climate crisis like \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s all a hoax\u8221\'20\'1D while at the Cop27 climate talks in Egypt, where the US delegation is attempting to remain upbeat about continued progress on dealing with global heating despite uncertainty over the midterm election results.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi, the speaker of the House of Representatives, made a surprise appearance at the climate summit in Sharm el-Sheikh on Thursday. The trip may be one of Pelosi\u8217\'20\'19s last as speaker, with most forecasts predicting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  will eke out a narrow majority in the House.\par \par \uc2 There has been \u8220\'20\'1Cshall we say, a disagreement on the subject\u8221\'20\'1D of the climate crisis between the parties, Pelosi said at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/cop27"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cop27}}}\uc2 , adding that Republicans have said \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18Why are we having this discussion? There is no climate crisis. It\u8217\'20\'19s all a hoax.\u8217\'20\'19 We have to get over that. This is urgent, long overdue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo we cannot just have any political disagreement or the power of the fossil fuel industry cramping our style as we go forward with this, but to show a path that gets us to where we need to be,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi\u8217\'20\'19s appearance at Cop27 comes at a critical point for the future of democracy in the US and the future of the planet. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was able to pass the country\u8217\'20\'19s most significant piece of climate legislation this year because {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  have the majority in both the House and the Senate. With that set to change, the mounting anger at the US for obstructing meaningful global climate action, despite being the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest polluter and richest country, may only get worse.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/nancy-pelosi-cop27-republicans-climate-crisis-hoax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pelosi accuses Republicans of treating climate crisis like \u8216\'20\'18it\u8217\'20\'19s all a hoax\u8217\'20\'19 at Cop27}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Key House races are still undecided\par \par \uc2 Republicans are inching towards a House majority, but several key races, especially in the west, remain undecided.\par \par \uc2 Many of these close races are in California, where elections often take days or weeks to tabulate. In Orange county, the Democratic star \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  is very narrowly leading Republican \uc2 Scott Baugh\uc2  in her newly formed swing district. Meanwhile, the Republican incumbent Michelle Steel, who ran an intensely negative campaign falsely painting her Taiwanese American opponent as having ties to Chinese communism, is leading in her race. And the Democrat Adam Gray is barely trailing the Republican \uc2 John Duarte\uc2  in the Central Valley.\par \par \uc2 in Colorado, the far-right firebrand \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  remains in an unexpectedly tight virtual tie with her opponent, the Democrat Adam Frisch.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, the Democratic representative Susie Lee is leading in a tight race against the Republican lawyer April Becker, who challenged the 2020 presidential election results. And the incumbent Democrat \uc2 Dina Titus\uc2  is pulling ahead of her Republican challenger in a redrawn district.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Voter turnout this year was the second highest of any midterm since 1940, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/interactive/2022/voter-turnout-2022-by-state/?itid=lk_inline_manual_23"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to}}}\uc2  the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , which analyzed AP and US Elections Project data.\par \par \uc2 About 112.5m people \u8211\'20\'13 or about 47% of eligible voters \u8211\'20\'13 cast ballots in the midterms. In 2018, about 50% of eligible voters cast ballots, according to the Post.\par \par \uc2 And {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://now.tufts.edu/2022/11/09/millions-youth-cast-ballots-decide-key-2022-races"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to }}}\uc2  researchers at \uc2 Tufts University\uc2 , about 27% of eligible voters 18-29 turned out:\par \par \uc2 This 2022 youth turnout is likely the second-highest youth turnout rate for a midterm election in the past 30 years, behind only the historic 31% turnout in 2018. Votes cast by young people made up 12% of all votes in this election, nearly matching the 13% youth share of the vote from the 2014 and 2018 midterms, according to National Election Pool surveys.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just spoke in Washington to thank Democratic volunteers for their work in securing the party a better-than-expected night in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 He noted that several Republicans who embraced baseless fraud claims about his own election win in 2020 ended up conceding their races without much drama:\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden says none of the 2020 election deniers contested the results of the 2022 midterm elections when they lost:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTuesday was a good day \u8230\'20\'26 for democracy, and it was a strong night for Democrats.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/NZlZwNsyIL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/NZlZwNsyIL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1590807646734811136?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 He also linked the surprising support many Democrats received to the party\u8217\'20\'19s pledge to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access:\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden touts the overwhelming support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights during the 2022 midterms:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWomen in America made their voices heard, man \u8230\'20\'26 Y\u8217\'20\'19all showed up and beat the hell out of them.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Kc2qZUAwu1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Kc2qZUAwu1}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1590808487906275328?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 He closed by touting his own legislative accomplishments, including moves intended to lower the country\u8217\'20\'19s fiscal deficit:\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t wanna hear from Republicans calling Democrats big spenders. We\u8217\'20\'19re the ones bringing down the deficit. They\u8217\'20\'19re the ones that blew it up over four years.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/5N7j0ExfT0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/5N7j0ExfT0}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1590809109783199745?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/maanvi-singh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maanvi Singh}}}\uc2  is now taking over the blog, and will cover the rest of today\u8217\'20\'19s elections and politics news.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Senate\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t banking on his party continuing to hold the chamber for another two years.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg Government reports that Schumer intends to prioritize confirming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s judges and other nominees before the year ends and the new Congress begins:\par \par \uc2 enltr??Scoop: Senate leader is preparing to pull the defense authorization bill off the floor and instead focus on federal nominees and judges and majority next year is still in limbo. Instead annual defense bill will materialize as an informal conference bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/business?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @business}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BGOV?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @BGOV}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NDAA?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NDAA}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Roxana Tiron (@rtiron) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/rtiron/status/1590795606548951040?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Democrats still have a path to keeping the Senate majority, particularly if Mark Kelly in Arizona and \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  in Nevada win their races. Counting is ongoing in both.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Even as some Republicans blame him for their party\u8217\'20\'19s struggles in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election, Morning Consult has new data out that confirms he remains the most-liked man in the GOP, though perhaps not as popular as he once was:\par \par \uc2 frltr2024 National Republican Primary:\par \par \uc2 Trump 48% (+22)\par \par \uc2 DeSantis 26%\par \par \uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MorningConsult?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @MorningConsult}}}\uc2 , 1,691 RV, 11/2-7 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/AdYWBaSMK0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/AdYWBaSMK0}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/PZ1AJdcudQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/PZ1AJdcudQ}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Political Polls (@Politics_Polls) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Politics_Polls/status/1590800527217946624?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s most recent popularity peak came in August, after his actions had received scrutiny from the January 6 committee and his resort searched by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as part of its investigation into potentially unlawful retention of government secrets.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Nevada ballot counting could continue till Saturday\par \par \uc2 It may not be until Saturday that the outcomes of Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s razor-thin Senate and governor races are known, The Nevada Independent reports.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s based on comments made at a press conference by Joe Gloria, registrar of Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s most-populous county Clark, where Las Vegas is located. The outcome of the two races is expected to hinge on votes from its residents, who tend to lean Democratic:\par \par \uc2 enltrGloria on today's numbers:\par \par \uc2 He notes that Clark reported results from about 14k mail ballots last night.\par \par \uc2 He says there are still more than 50k ballots that must be counted in Clark.\par \par \uc2 "Majority of mail should be counted in Clark County by Saturday."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sean Golonka (@s_golonka) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/s_golonka/status/1590794080015233025?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrYesterday, Gloria said Clark received 12.7k in mail on Wednesday, and in the afternoon, the county announced it received 56.9 from Election Day drop boxes.\par \par \uc2 The 14k reported yesterday was apparently Monday drop off and Tues mail. But Gloria now says there are 50k uncounted.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sean Golonka (@s_golonka) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/s_golonka/status/1590795913622343680?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, NBC News reports a Trump adviser says the former president still plans to announce another run for the White House on Tuesday:\par \par \uc2 enltrA senior Trump adviser just confirmed Tues announcement & said \u8220\'20\'1CThe media, the corporate elites, and political establishment has all moved in unison against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at their own peril. It\u8217\'20\'19s like they want to recreate 2015-2016. Let them. We are doing it again. Buckle up\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Marc Caputo (@MarcACaputo) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MarcACaputo/status/1590783778280337408?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrInvites for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s announcement should go out today.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Marc Caputo (@MarcACaputo) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MarcACaputo/status/1590783944542519296?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 'I could not support him', top Virginia Republican says of Trump\par \par \uc2 Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s Republican lieutenant-governor Winsome Sears said she could not support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he again ran for the White House, telling \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2  in an interview that the former president has become a \u8220\'20\'1Cliability\u8221\'20\'1D for the GOP:\par \par \uc2 enltr\u8220\'20\'1CA true leader understands when they have become a liability. A true leader understands that it\u8217\'20\'19s time to step off the stage, and the voters have given us that very clear message... I could not support him.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14VA Lt. Gov. Winsome Sears (R) comes out against Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 candidacy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/0g1plfHJmu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/0g1plfHJmu}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1590782275968720897?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The comments are significant considering \uc2 Sears\uc2  did something last year that Trump hasn\u8217\'20\'19t done in more than six: win an election. Voters in the Democratic-leaning state elected Sears as the running mate of Glenn Youngkin in his gubernatorial campaign, and she made history as the first woman and first person of color to serve as Virginia\u8217\'20\'19s lieutenant governor.\par \par \uc2 Her comments also underscore the tension among Republicans over Trump\u8217\'20\'19s influence on the party, particularly since many candidates he backed did not fare well in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Montana voters reject 'born alive' law\par \par \uc2 Montana has become the latest state where voters said no to further \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions by rejecting a law that was meant to stop the killing outside the womb of babies who survive a failed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which is already illegal.\par \par \uc2 The so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cborn alive\u8221\'20\'1D law would have allowed medical providers to face criminal charges if they don\u8217\'20\'19t take \u8220\'20\'1Call medically appropriate and reasonable actions to preserve the life\u8221\'20\'1D of infants, according to the AP.\par \par \uc2 The defeat puts Montana among the ranks of Republican-leaning states where voters have rejected attempts to further tighten down on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access following the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe v Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on the failed law from the AP:\par \par \uc2 Health care professionals and other opponents argued the proposal could rob parents of precious time with infants born with incurable medical issues if doctors are forced to attempt treatment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s win sends a clear message to state leadership: Montanans demand our right to make private health care decisions for ourselves and our families with the help of our trusted medical teams \u8212\'20\'14 and without interference from politicians,\u8221\'20\'1D said a statement from Hillary-Anne Crosby, a spokesperson for an organization called Compassion for Montana Families that opposed the measure.\par \par \uc2 The outcome comes after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-abortion-8779f3ee57d4d20d54861a5ed6ba72ff"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 series of wins}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters in states around the country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was directly on the ballot during the midterm elections. Voters enshrined \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections into state constitutions in Michigan, California and Vermont. They also voted down an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  constitutional amendment in conservative Kentucky, just as voters did in Kansas in August.\par \par \uc2 Supporters said the proposed Montana law was meant to prevent the killing of infants outside the womb in rare occurrence of a failed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something that is already is illegal. Penalties for violating the proposed law would have included up to $50,000 in fines and up to 20 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 At least half of U.S. states have similar post-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  born-alive laws in place, according to \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , a Washington, D.C.-based organization that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , aid in dying and infant stem cell research.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis initiative would have criminalized doctors, nurses and other health care workers for providing compassionate care for infants, and, in doing so, overridden the decision-making of Montana parents,\u8221\'20\'1D said a statement from Lauren Wilson of the Montana Chapter of the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.06pm GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/adam-gabbatt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Adam Gabbatt}}}\uc2  has taken a look at the latest round in the long-running American political parlor game, \u8216\'20\'18Has \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  Finally Dumped Trump?\u8217\'20\'19:\par \par \uc2 On election day, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  was clear about how his efforts to support Republican candidates should be seen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I think if they win, I should get all the credit,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mattwilstein/status/1590105710440112128"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  NewsNation. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they lose, I should not be blamed at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately for Trump, he did not get what he hoped for. Instead the former president has seen conservative news outlets, the \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  -owned ones in particular, turn on him, in some cases with gleeful abandon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrumpty Dumpty\u8221\'20\'1D blared {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/nypost/status/1590674429524295680"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the front page}}}\uc2  of Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s New York Post, the tabloid Murdoch has owned since 1976. Editors went so far as to mock up Trump as Humpty Dumpty, his enlarged orange head stuffed into a white shirt and a signature red tie.\par \par \uc2 Next to the picture of Trump as an egg perching precariously on a brick wall, the text goaded: \u8220\'20\'1CDon (who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t build a wall) had a great fall \u8211\'20\'13 can all the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s men put the party back together again?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Post cover offered the most visceral insight into Murdoch\u8217\'20\'19s thinking, and its contempt was far from an outlier in the mogul\u8217\'20\'19s news empire.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump Is the Republican Party\u8217\'20\'19s Biggest Loser\u8221\'20\'1D was the verdict of the Wall Street Journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/donald-trump-is-the-gops-biggest-loser-midterm-elections-senate-house-congress-republicans-11668034869"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 editorial board}}}\uc2 . A subheading added: \u8220\'20\'1CHe has now flopped in 2018, 2020, 2021 and 2022.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The piece was just as scathing as the headline, running through nine races this November the paper said Trump had effectively tanked through his continued election denial, his various wars with more moderate Republican candidates and his general unpopularity nationwide.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince his unlikely victory in 2016 against the widely disliked \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , Mr Trump has a perfect record of electoral defeat,\u8221\'20\'1D the editorial said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe GOP was pounded in the 2018 midterms owing to his low approval rating. Mr Trump himself lost in 2020. He then sabotaged Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s 2021 runoffs by blaming party leaders for not somehow overturning his defeat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It added: \u8220\'20\'1CNow Mr Trump has botched the 2022 elections, and it could hand Democrats the Senate for two more years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/rupert-murdoch-trump-desantis-ny-post"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Has \u8216\'20\'18Trumpty Dumpty\u8217\'20\'19 taken a great fall from Rupert Murdoch\u8217\'20\'19s grace?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  is not happy about how Democrats performed in her state, New York, in the midterms \u8211\'20\'13 a series of House losses helping (probably) hand the chamber to Republicans, though \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , the governor, did fend off an unexpectedly strong challenge from her Trumpist opponent, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 . Photograph: Reuters\par \par \uc2 The New York City congresswoman popularly known as AOC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theintercept.com/2022/11/09/alexandria-ocasio-cortez-new-york-state-democratic-party-midterms/?utm_campaign=theintercept&utm_source=twitter&utm_medium=social"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Intercept}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CNew York, I think, is the glaring aberration in what we see in this map \u8230\'20\'26 what happened in New York really bucks a lot of the trends in what we saw nationwide.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26 I think, in New York, the way that those campaigns were run were different than the way a lot of winning campaigns across the country were run. And I think the role of the state party had very strong national implications. If Democrats do not hang on to the House, I think that responsibility falls squarely in New York state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Identifying key election themes in New York, Ocasio-Cortez said: \u8220\'20\'1CI think policing was a big one, I think the choice among certain Democrats to \u8230\'20\'26 amplify Republican narratives on crime and policing, running ads on it \u8230\'20\'26 validating these narratives actually ended up hurting them much more than a different approach. I think that what we saw in other races was that they were able to really effectively center either their narratives and the narratives that they wanted to run with, whether it was \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, whether it was democracy, whether it was \u8230\'20\'26 other key and top priorities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think Democrats in New York, they did a couple of things. They ran ads around that were explicitly very anti-defund [the police], which only served to reinvoke the frame and only served to really reinforce what Republicans were saying. If we\u8217\'20\'19re going to talk about public safety, you don\u8217\'20\'19t talk about it in the frame of invoking defund or anti-defund, you really talk about it in the frame of what we\u8217\'20\'19ve done on gun violence, what we\u8217\'20\'19ve done to pass the first gun reform bill in 30 years. Our alternatives are actually effective, electorally, without having to lean into Republican narratives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26 And I think another prime mistake is that in New York state, [ex-governor Andrew] Cuomo may be gone, but \u8230\'20\'26 much of his infrastructure and much of the political machinery that he put in place is still there. And this is a machinery that is disorganised, it is sycophantic. It relies on lobbyists and big money. And it really undercuts the ability for there to be affirming grassroots and state-level organising across the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd so \u8230\'20\'26 you\u8217\'20\'19re leaving a void for Republicans to walk into \u8230\'20\'26 it\u8217\'20\'19s a testament to the corruption that has been allowed to continue in the New York state Democratic party.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , the senator from Missouri who may or may not run for the Republican presidential nomination in 2024 but definitely did {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/24/josh-hawley-republican-senator-ran-running-capitol-attack-kansas-city-star"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 run from}}}\uc2  Capitol rioters on 6 January 2021, even after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/15/josh-hawley-january-6-fist-photo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raising a fist to the mob}}}\uc2 , thinks Republicans did not do as well as they might\u8217\'20\'19ve done on Tuesday night because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t run on his populist, not to say Trumpist, principles.\par \par \uc2 He tweets, in a message at least partially counter to the emerging consensus that Republicans suffered (if probably winning the House and maybe winning the Senate can be called suffering) because voters wanted to rebuke their Trumpist drift:\par \par \uc2 enltrWashington Republicanism lost big Tuesday night. When your \u8220\'20\'1Cagenda\u8221\'20\'1D is cave to Big Pharma on insulin, cave to Schumer on gun control & Green New Deal (\u8220\'20\'1Cinfrastructure\u8221\'20\'1D), and tease changes to Social Security and Medicare, you lose\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  (@HawleyMO) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HawleyMO/status/1590734455832219652?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 A refresher on how Hawley ran, as shown by the House January 6 committee, is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/24/josh-hawley-republican-senator-ran-running-capitol-attack-kansas-city-star"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Video of \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  running, meanwhile, is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/video/2022/jul/22/january-6-committee-watches-josh-hawley-flee-from-capitol-riots-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 My colleague David Smith has been speaking to anti-Trump conservatives about where the former president stands now, after a disappointing midterm election for his endorsed candidates and the Republican party, and with conservative media including Murdoch-owned papers showing signs of throwing in their lots with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the Florida governor who had a contrastingly strong night on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still expected to announce his third run for the Republican presidential nomination next week, though a chorus of party voices seems to wish he wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 Tara Setmayer, a senior adviser to the anti-Trump group the Lincoln Project, tells David Trump \u8220\'20\'1Cis wounded and that\u8217\'20\'19s evidenced by the rightwing media ecosystem putting out collective rebukes in the wake of a disappointing midterm result because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was at the centre once again.\par \par \uc2 He cost them a much larger victory in the midterms. He is the albatross around the Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s electoral neck and will continue to be as long as he is alive and breathing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Setmayer, a former Republican communications director on Capitol Hill, is not convinced Trump is a spent force yet: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not holding my breath that this posture will remain. We saw this before after the election in 2020 and it lasted as long as the ratings started to crash.\par \par \uc2 When they started losing ratings to \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  and other media outlets, they went right back to the fawning coverage of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Are they willing to remain steadfast this time around because they think now that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is the heir apparent? We\u8217\'20\'19ll see how long that lasts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Joe Walsh, a former Tea Party congressman from Illinois who ran in the 2020 Republican primary as an anti-Trump candidate, also thinks the former president isn\u8217\'20\'19t done with his party, or America, yet.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen this movie before. He led a fucking insurrection and the party still bowed to him. So will the dam finally break with this one? No, I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it will. I still think it\u8217\'20\'19s his party.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis whole DeSantis thing is overrated. Trump knows that. He knows who DeSantis is. I still expect him to come out this month and announce he\u8217\'20\'19s running and I don\u8217\'20\'19t expect many Republicans to have the balls to say, \u8216\'20\'18Donald, you suck\u8217\'20\'19. I just don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19ll happen.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/trump-advised-delay-announcement-2024-run-georgia-run-off"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump advised to delay announcement on 2024 run until after Georgia run-off}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The day so far\par \par \uc2 Ballot counting continues across the United States, with the outcomes of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d071a8f08a5a2b4f08875#block-636d071a8f08a5a2b4f08875"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two key Senate races}}}\uc2  and several House races up in the air. Meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636cfe8b8f0818507f5637aa#block-636cfe8b8f0818507f5637aa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new government data showed}}}\uc2  inflation declined in October, potentially marking the beginning of the end of the political perilous wave of price increases that has gripped the country for more than a year.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a rundown of what has happened so far today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A top White House official{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d13108f08786f8dfabfd6#block-636d13108f08786f8dfabfd6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 insisted}}}\uc2  the Democrats still had a chance at winning the House.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A newly elected Republican congressman{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d29c78f08416f7753d23e#block-636d29c78f08416f7753d23e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  the party should \u8220\'20\'1Cmove forward\u8221\'20\'1D from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The chair of the Texas GOP said the party {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d254e8f08786f8dfac0d7#block-636d254e8f08786f8dfac0d7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 needs to give voters}}}\uc2  a positive message if it wants to improve on what is looking to be a lackluster performance in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 How did \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Democratic victor in Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s Senate race, pull it off? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/10/midterm-elections-2022-results-senate-house-us-democrats-republicans-live-updates-latest-news?page=with:block-636d0b828f0818507f563843#block-636d0b828f0818507f563843"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Guardian traveled to the state to find out}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Candidates who deny \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2020 election win were rife on ballots nationwide, and the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has been tracking how they fared in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Have a look:\par \par \uc2 enltrHow the 291 election deniers have fared so far (30 races uncalled as of Thursday at 11 a.m. ET) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/vda4lBIoFL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/vda4lBIoFL}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/YoKePByT11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/YoKePByT11}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Adri\u225\'00\'E1n Blanco (@AdrianBlancoR) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AdrianBlancoR/status/1590737719826665472?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  plans to address the press after his race for re-election against Republic \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  went to a run off.\par \par \uc2 The two men will face off again on 6 December in a race that, depending on the outcome of Senate contests in Nevada and Arizona, could decide control of the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Warnock announced he will speak at 1pm in Atlanta. Meanwhile, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2022-election/warnock-campaign-lays-strategy-georgia-senate-runoff-calling-walker-co-rcna56592"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NBC News reports}}}\uc2  that Warnock\u8217\'20\'19s campaign manager is signaling optimism to supporters about the senator\u8217\'20\'19s prospects of winning a six-year term.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CReverend Warnock will win the runoff by continuing the strategic investments in paid communication and field organizing, continuing to hold the diverse coalition that has driven Reverend Warnock\u8217\'20\'19s success, and emphasizing that this race is about who is able to represent our state,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Quentin Fulks\uc2  wrote in a memo today.\par \par \uc2 He characterized former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star Walker as \u8220\'20\'1Ccompletely unqualified for a job that requires knowledge of the issues and an interest in listening and learning.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Whatever the results of the midterms, Democrats are assured about eight more weeks of controlling both the House and Senate, and have a packed schedule of lawmaking ahead of them.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with National Public Radio, Democratic senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  offered a preview of what the party\u8217\'20\'19s priorities will be in the closing weeks of the year:\par \par \uc2 enltrCould Democrats avert a debt ceiling crisis in 2023 by extending the debt limit this year, in the lame duck session? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CoryBooker?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @CoryBooker}}}\uc2  says on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MorningEdition?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @MorningEdition}}}\uc2  he would "hope" to do this, but there is a crowded agenda and party leaders haven't said if they will try it. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NPR?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NPR}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Steve Inskeep (@NPRinskeep) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NPRinskeep/status/1590702690094702595?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrIn the lame duck, which might be the Democrats' last chance to legislate before R's take a chamber, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CoryBooker?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @CoryBooker}}}\uc2  says "a lot of things" are "urgent for Democrats": writing "marriage equality" into law, making a child tax credit "permanent," and "confirming a lot of judges."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Steve Inskeep (@NPRinskeep) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NPRinskeep/status/1590703687386927105?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Republicans found unexpected enthusiasm in New York, where they won several tight House races including in New York City\u8217\'20\'19s exurbs, where Mike Lawler triumphed over sitting Democrat \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But in an interview with CNN this morning, Lawler signaled he was willing to break from the party\u8217\'20\'19s embrace of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 enltrMike Lawler, the New York Republican who unseated the Democratic campaign chair, tells us he wants to see the GOP move on from Trump. \u8220\'20\'1CI would like to see the party move forward. I think any time you are focused on the future, you can't so much go to the past,\u8221\'20\'1D Lawler says. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/8b3nxlB6Ll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/8b3nxlB6Ll}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kaitlan Collins (@kaitlancollins) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kaitlancollins/status/1590702848190607361?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A number of Republicans are doing some soul searching after the \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D they hoped for did not arrive.\par \par \uc2 The party still could win the House and Senate, but it\u8217\'20\'19s clear Americans weren\u8217\'20\'19t as enthusiastic about Republicans as they expected.\par \par \uc2 Matt Rinaldi\uc2  is chair of the Republican party in Texas, where the GOP suffered no major setbacks on Tuesday and is generally the dominant political force in the state. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he shared his view of what went wrong for GOP candidates elsewhere:\par \par \uc2 enltrElection takeaways:\par \par \uc2 1. Florida had a wave election. Texas had a very good night. Results elsewhere were mixed. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his optimistic brand of bold and unapologetic conservatism is a winning brand and the GOP should take note. (1/8)\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Matt Rinaldi\uc2  (@MattRinaldiTX) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MattRinaldiTX/status/1590392436886745093?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr4. Obviously, the results from outside Florida & Texas were largely disappointing. We have an outside shot of a one vote senate majority & will likely have a narrow margin in the House. Kari Lake will likely pull it out. (5/8)\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Matt Rinaldi\uc2  (@MattRinaldiTX) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MattRinaldiTX/status/1590392444608450562?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr5. But the lesson is, the GOP can\u8217\'20\'19t rest on Dem incompetence & 70% of voters believing America is on the wrong track to win elections. It needs a positive message and governing agenda. It needs to stand for something. (6/8)\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Matt Rinaldi\uc2  (@MattRinaldiTX) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MattRinaldiTX/status/1590392447028559875?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrRon DeSantis created a FL wave because his optimistic, bold & unapologetic conservatism won hearts & minds. He led, rather than followed polls. He stood for something. He took risks. He rejected moderation. He inspired people. This is the path forward for the GOP. (7/8)\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Matt Rinaldi\uc2  (@MattRinaldiTX) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MattRinaldiTX/status/1590392452552458240?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 While voters may have displayed surprising enthusiasm for the Democrats on Tuesday, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s party isn\u8217\'20\'19t out of the woods yet, \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/davidsmith"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 David Smith}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 It was a result that, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said on Wednesday, gave everyone a \u8220\'20\'1Cwhew! sigh of relief\u8221\'20\'1D that Make America Great Again (Maga) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  are not taking over the government again.\par \par \uc2 Biden won and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/democrats-house-senate-performance-polls-predictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 midterm elections}}}\uc2  to decide control of Congress. But just as in 2020, a collective exhalation is not enough to spell the end of political dysfunction in America. Things are about to get messy.\par \par \uc2 For all their deflation, Republicans appear on course to capture a majority in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/house-of-representatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 House of Representatives}}}\uc2 , albeit by a far smaller margin than history has suggested or crystal ball gazers had forecast.\par \par \uc2 That means the end of Democrat \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reign as House speaker, at least for now. Republican leader Kevin McCarthy has announced his intention to take the speaker\u8217\'20\'19s gavel. It might be better described as a poisoned chalice.\par \par \uc2 Should McCarthy prevail, his achingly slim majority will afford little room for maneuver when it comes to legislating. McCarthy will have to do deals either with Democrats or far-right Trump loyalists. In a House where every member fancies him or herself as president, the speaker could find himself perpetually bending to the will of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/08/campaign-millions-maga-extremist-marjorie-taylor-greene-marcus-flowers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/biden-legislative-agenda-republicans-control-house-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats breathe a sigh of relief but their troubles are far from over}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Democrats have picked up another House seat.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press confirms Gabriel Vasquez has ousted incumbent Republican \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2  in New Mexico\u8217\'20\'19s second district. Herrell took the seat from Democrat \uc2 Xochitl Torres\uc2  Small in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Politicians across the country broke barriers in the midterms, with the first member of generation Z elected to Congress and the first openly gay woman winning a governorship.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more about the historic firsts that resulted from Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s vote:\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Some of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 best prospects for keeping the House are indeed in the west. Consider Colorado, where rightwing firebrand \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  is locked in an unexpectedly close race for reelection, the Associated Press reports:\par \par \uc2 The controversial far-right Republican \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  was locked in a tight race with fewer than 100 votes separating her and her opponent on Wednesday night, in her bid for reelection to a US House seat in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/colorado"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado}}}\uc2  against the Democrat Adam Frisch, a businessman and former city councilman from the posh, mostly liberal ski town of Aspen.\par \par \uc2 Boebert\u8217\'20\'19s contest was being watched nationally as Republicans try to flip control of the US House. The Trump loyalist established herself as a partisan flashpoint in Washington in her first term and had been favored to win reelection after redistricting made the conservative and mostly rural district more Republican.\par \par \uc2 The margin in the race puts it in the recount zone of about 800 votes or less, or 0.5% of the leader\u8217\'20\'19s vote total. Both Boebert and Frisch had 50% of the vote as of Wednesday night with about 97% of votes counted.\par \par \uc2 Frisch contends Boebert sacrificed her constituents\u8217\'20\'19 interests for frequent \u8220\'20\'1Cangertainment\u8221\'20\'1D in accusing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , of seeking to destroy the soul of the nation. He vowed to join the bipartisan \u8220\'20\'1CProblem Solvers Caucus\u8221\'20\'1D, a sharp turn from Boebert\u8217\'20\'19s repudiation of across-the-aisle consensus-building.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/colorado-republican-lauren-boebert-tight-race-against-democrat-adam-frisch-house"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado Republican Lauren Boebert locked in tight race against Democrat Adam Frisch}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House deputy chief of staff \uc2 Jen O\u8217\'20\'19Malley Dillon\uc2  said this morning that the Biden administration believes it\u8217\'20\'19s possible Democrats could keep their majority in the House:\par \par \uc2 enltrWH's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JOD46?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @JOD46}}}\uc2  says on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Morning_Joe?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Morning_Joe}}}\uc2  the House is still in play for Democrats: "When we look at the votes coming in in the West, there's definitely still a path for Democrats to hold the House."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jordan Fabian (@Jordanfabian) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Jordanfabian/status/1590716379778277376?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Doing so would be hugely unexpected. No president has managed to hold on to their majorty in the House in their first midterms since \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  in 2002.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just put out a statement cheering the latest inflation data, saying it \u8220\'20\'1Cshows that we are making progress on bringing inflation down, without giving up all of the progress we have made on economic growth and job creation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy economic plan is showing results, and the American people can see that we are facing global economic challenges from a position of strength,\u8221\'20\'1D the president said in a nod to his administration\u8217\'20\'19s argument that global events like Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine have played a major role in the price spike.\par \par \uc2 He closed his statement by referring to the possibility that his Democrats will soon lose their majorities in one or both houses of Congress, and GOP leaders may take aim at some of the legislation Biden secured passage of during his first two years in office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will work with anyone \u8211\'20\'13 Democrat or Republican \u8211\'20\'13 on ideas to provide more breathing room to middle-class and working families. And I will oppose any effort to undo my agenda or to make inflation worse,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Democrats scored a win in the race for Senate control when \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  beat a Republican to take control of Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s open Senate seat in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/laughland-oliver"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oliver Laughland}}}\uc2  traveled to the Keystone State to find out how Fetterman pulled it off:\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19re going to be hearing a lot on this blog today about Arizona and Nevada. Why?\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not out of love for gambling, cactuses and canyons. Rather, the southwestern states are home to the two Senate races that have not yet been called, and will determine who controls the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Both seats up for grabs are occupied by Democrats, and if Mark Kelly in Arizona and \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  in Nevada both win reelection, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s allies will control the chamber for another two years. If neither wins, the GOP will have the majority. And if only one triumphs, then the 6 December runoff election for Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s Senate seat will determine which party wins a majority in the chamber.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Democrats may have dodged the inflation bullet in the midterms, but it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean the trend is over, or that voters won\u8217\'20\'19t punish \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s party at a later date. Here\u8217\'20\'19s The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/lauren-aratani"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lauren Aratani}}}\uc2  on what the latest data means for the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy:\par \par \uc2 Prices of goods and services in the US were up 7.7% in October compared with the same time last year, a sign that inflation is slowly starting to cool after reaching decades-high records over the last few months.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reported on Thursday that in October, the consumer price index showed a 7.7% rise in prices over the last 12 months, a 0.5 percentage-point decrease from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/oct/13/us-inflation-rate-september-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September}}}\uc2 , which saw a rate of 8.2%. The October inflation rate is the lowest since January, when rates rose to 7.5%.\par \par \uc2 Along with the slight cooling in the overall inflation rate, the core inflation rate, which excludes the volatile energy and food sectors, also showed a small tempering off, reaching 6.3% \u8211\'20\'13 a 0.3% increase compared to the core inflation rate last month.\par \par \uc2 While October\u8217\'20\'19s inflation rate is slightly lower than analysts expected, it is still much higher than the 2% target rate that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been trying to reach.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/nov/10/us-inflation-rate-october-consumer-prices-index"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US inflation rate cools slightly as consumer prices rise 7.7% in October}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US inflation showed signs of cooling in October\par \par \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s white-hot inflation was expected to be one of the most potent trends influencing voters in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s elections, but government data released just minutes ago indicates it may have started cooling.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reports}}}\uc2  consumer prices rose 7.7% in October compared to the same month last year, down from the 8.2% rate seen in September. On a monthly basis, prices rose 0.4%, the same rate as in September.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has climbed to levels not seen for four decades over the course of the past year, souring voters on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s presidency and making Democrats nationwide fear they\u8217\'20\'19d be booted from office in the 8 November midterms. While all the votes haven\u8217\'20\'19t been counted yet, Biden allies appear to have done better than expected on Tuesday, a sign that their arguments about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 threat to democracy may have overcome voters\u8217\'20\'19 economic anxieties. However consumers are still paying much more for household staples than they were a year ago. Food prices are up 10.9% compared to October 2021, and gasoline 17.5%, for example.\par \par \uc2 The bigger question is if this latest report is a sign that the bout of inflation that accompanied the economy\u8217\'20\'19s recovery from the Covid-19 pandemic in 2020 is finally easing for good. There are an array of opinions out there about this, but here are the views of \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  economics professor \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 enltrCore CPI rose by 0.3% in October, which is 0.2 points below expectations, and down from 0.6% last month. It's now running at 6.3% over the year down from 6.6%.\par \par \uc2 This is a very encouraging inflation report. There's a good chance that inflation has peaked, and is now turning down.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1590699155458240514?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrHeadline inflation also yields good news: Up only 0.4% in October, a rise that's 0.2% below consensus.\par \par \uc2 Inflation over the past year has been 7.7%, down from a rate of 8.2%. last month and a peak of 8.9% in June.\par \par \uc2 Again, it looks like inflation has peaked and is now falling.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1590699929927704576?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrWithin core inflation, we're seeing roughly similar rates of inflation in the goods sector (5.1% over the year) as the service sector (6.7%).\par \par \uc2 But over more recent months goods inflation has pretty much stopped: -0.4% in October, 0% in September, and 0.5% and 0.2% in prior months\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1590700624269971456?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrSo focus will shift to the service sector, where inflation is running at 6.7%.\par \par \uc2 Some good news: The biggest cost in the service sector is labor, and labor costs aren't providing much inflationary pressure. Service sector inflation should moderate over time.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1590700952725909504?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Good morning from Washington. This is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/chris-stein"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chris Stein}}}\uc2 , taking over from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/martin-belam"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Martin Belam}}}\uc2  in London, and directing your attention away from the nation\u8217\'20\'19s capital momentarily to a place much further to the west: Nevada.\par \par \uc2 There, it\u8217\'20\'19s starting to look like Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  may be regaining her lead in the race for the state\u8217\'20\'19s crucial Senate seat as more mail-in ballots are counted, according to Cook Political Report, which also sees Democrat Mark Kelly retaining his Senate seat in Arizona:\par \par \uc2 enltrAfter last night's NV mail ballot trend, excellent chance now that Dems will have 50 Senate seats/control in hand heading into the GA runoff.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Dave Wasserman (@Redistrict) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Redistrict/status/1590693791576358912?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Last night, The Nevada Independent reached much the same conclusion:\par \par \uc2 enltrHere's what we know on the Nevada Senate race, if we assume the rurals are all but spent, will only add a couple thousand at most to Laxalt's lead:\par \par \uc2 If CCM continues to win urban mail at current clip, taking 65%, she will easily overtake Laxalt with 110,000 mail (at least) left.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jon Ralston (@RalstonReports) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RalstonReports/status/1590595387005337600?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrIf her margin decreases from 65-30 to 60-30, for instance, she would still in decisively.\par \par \uc2 If it is 60-35, same.\par \par \uc2 If it's 55-30, same.\par \par \uc2 If it's 55-35, same.\par \par \uc2 She wins in all those models.\par \par \uc2 Buckle up.\par \par \uc2 Good night to all. See you for more math tomorrow!\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jon Ralston (@RalstonReports) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RalstonReports/status/1590596892966023171?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Over at Politico Alex Isenstadt has written about the big decision that Republican hopefuls have to take on if they want to run for the White House in 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 are they prepared for a tussle with former US president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  first? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/11/10/donald-trump-potnetial-2024-presidential-rivals-00066128"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Isenstadt writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 For all the interest many Republicans have in running for president, would-be candidates \u8212\'20\'14 and their donors, staffers and family members \u8212\'20\'14 are going to have to decide whether they have the stomach to take on a former president known for having a vindictive streak.\par \par \uc2 Potential 2024 hopefuls have spent the last two years raising money, giving big speeches and traveling to early states under the thinly veiled cover of helping the party prepare for the midterms or fueling their reelection campaigns. That excuse doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work anymore, and further steps toward a presidential race risk direct conflict with Trump.\par \par \uc2 During the 2016 campaign, Trump mercilessly savaged those who tried to stop him. While some eventually made their way back into Trump\u8217\'20\'19s good graces, others never did.\par \par \uc2 Should Trump indeed decide to run again in 2024, the Republican political class will face a similar decision. While the prospect of defeating Trump may be what some in the party want, they will also have to gauge their comfort level with possible retaliation should he make it back into the White House.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Emma Brockes writes her Guardian column today on the topic of the midterms, saying what a relief it is she has been been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/10/election-day-americans-donald-trump-candidates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 denied her favourite election day hobby}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 hating fellow Americans:\par \par \uc2 There were the races that were so starkly depressing that no amount of fancy footwork could neutralise them. Chief among these was the Pennsylvania Senate race between Dr Oz, the rightwing TV host who said in a recent debate that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a matter between \u8220\'20\'1Cwomen, doctors and local political leaders\u8221\'20\'1D, and the Democratic candidate, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/video/2022/nov/09/we-bet-on-the-people-of-pennsylvania-john-fetterman-beats-mehmet-oz-for-key-senate-seat-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Fetterman}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The importance of this race was underscored when both Biden and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  turned up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-11-05/obama-biden-join-forces-in-bid-to-rescue-vulnerable-fetterman"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stump for Fetterman}}}\uc2  on Saturday, undoing all the detachment I\u8217\'20\'19d managed to achieve about the midterms. Watching Obama do his thing in front of a stadium of people in Pittsburgh was intensely moving. It was also a hard reminder of how far we had fallen since 2008. Accustomed as most Americans are these days to seeing the apparent lunatic in any race win, Obama\u8217\'20\'19s appearance seemed to guarantee Oz would ascend to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman won with 50.4% of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/08/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the vote}}}\uc2 . By midday, while it was still unclear whether Congress would remain in the hands of the Democrats, it was apparent there would be no red wave. There was no big revival in support for Trump-backed candidates. And there were some hugely cheering results from the centre of the country, where for example in Kentucky voters defeated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/09/vermont-abortion-vote-first-us-state-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  constitutional amendment. For the first time in ages, it was possible to think warmly of people one was used to dismissing.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/10/election-day-americans-donald-trump-candidates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Emma Brockes \u8211\'20\'13 What a relief I\u8217\'20\'19ve been denied my favourite election day hobby, hating fellow Americans}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/10/election-day-americans-donald-trump-candidates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What a relief I\u8217\'20\'19ve been denied my favourite election day hobby \u8211\'20\'13 hating fellow Americans | Emma Brockes}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sheera Frenkel and Steven Lee Myers report for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that researchers who study election disinformation said most efforts to stoke doubt about the results of the midterms had failed to spread widely. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/09/technology/midterms-election-misinformation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 They write}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The major social media platforms all struggled to combat misinformation and disinformation online as the results were tabulated, but researchers who study the problem said efforts to stoke doubt about the outcome of the American democratic process had \u8212\'20\'14 at least so far \u8212\'20\'14 failed to take root. Some saw it as a hopeful sign of the political system\u8217\'20\'19s resilience, though few declared victory in the fight against misinformation.\par \par \uc2 According to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis, more than half of 370 candidates who in some way had cast doubt on President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s victory had won their races as of midday on Wednesday. They included 170 members of the House, where Republicans appeared to be closing in on reclaiming a majority. Although the party fell short of the \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D that many had anticipated, its successes may have tempered some of the conspiracy theories that emerged early Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 With prominent Republican candidates still on the cusp of winning, calling attention to fraud could undercut those victories, including majority control of the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 However, the article still identifies some corners of the web where anger over results and the electoral process continues to seethe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a lot of anger and noise on the mainstream platforms like \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , but the most aggressive statements on the day of the midterms, including calls to violence, are found on the alt platforms including Gab, Parler and Telegram,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alex Stamos, the director of the Stanford Internet Observatory, which tracked election disinformation online as part of the Elections Integrity Partnership. Users in some cases called for storming polling stations or using violence, though no significant attacks unfolded on election day.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Adam Gabbatt has been in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania for the Guardian, and today has profiled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/john-fetterman-pennsylvania-senator-elect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Fetterman\u8217\'20\'19s rise from small-town mayor to Pennsylvania senator}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Arguably it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who launched the political career of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Pennsylvania Democrat who clinched the state\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seat in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 In the days after the 2020 presidential vote, the former US president infamously claimed there had been widespread election fraud, including in Pennsylvania, where Fetterman was lieutenant governor. Fetterman disagreed.\par \par \uc2 When Trump later said he would sue Pennsylvania over alleged, but nonexistent, voter fraud, Fetterman\u8217\'20\'19s response was succinct. \u8220\'20\'1CThe president can sue a ham sandwich,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  speaks during his 2022 midterm elections night party in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. Photograph: Quinn Glabicki/Reuters\par \par \uc2 Fetterman\u8217\'20\'19s image as an earthy, working-class hero enabled him to position himself as the opposite to Mehmet Oz Oz\u8217\'20\'19s elitist, rich man vibe, and to appeal to a broad range of Pennsylvanians, although the truth isn\u8217\'20\'19t quite that simple.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat has an MBA from the \uc2 University of Connecticut\uc2  and a master\u8217\'20\'19s from Harvard, and has acknowledged growing up in a \u8220\'20\'1Ccushy\u8221\'20\'1D environment made possible by his father\u8217\'20\'19s successful insurance business. Fetterman has said he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/republicans-target-john-fetterman-blue-collar-image-20220803.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received financial assistance}}}\uc2  from his parents for almost all of his 13 years as Braddock\u8217\'20\'19s mayor \u8211\'20\'13 the job only paid $150 a month \u8211\'20\'13 until he became Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s lieutenant governor in 2019.\par \par \uc2 There seems little disingenuous about Fetterman, however, who chose not to move into the lieutenant governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion when elected, instead remaining in Braddock, where he lives, as he has often told crowds at rallies, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L2Dexro_9Wk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposite a steel mill}}}\uc2 . His passion for the town is visible on his right forearm, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://john-fetterman.medium.com/the-truth-about-my-tattoos-cc4b2940ceb6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nine tattoos}}}\uc2  mark the dates that people were killed \u8220\'20\'1Cthrough violence\u8221\'20\'1D in Braddock while he was mayor.\par \par \uc2 Having spent the bulk of his political career in charge of a town of 2,000 people, Fetterman will now represent Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s population of nearly 13 million. As he spoke to the crowd in the early hours of Wednesday, it seemed like a challenge he was ready for.\par \par \uc2 Read more from Adam Gabbatt here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/john-fetterman-pennsylvania-senator-elect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Fetterman\u8217\'20\'19s rise from small-town mayor to Pennsylvania senator}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/john-fetterman-pennsylvania-senator-elect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Fetterman\u8217\'20\'19s rise from small-town mayor to Pennsylvania senator}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 CNN\u8217\'20\'19s Fredreka Schouten reports that allegations of intimidation or harassment of Arizona voters continued, with a total of 21 complaints \u8211\'20\'13 including one threatening government officials \u8212\'20\'14 being forward to federal and state law enforcement, according to information released yesterday. She writes:\par \par \uc2 One of the 21 complaints involved a threat against government officials. The 22 October email, which said it was a warning to \u8220\'20\'1CCorrupt and Treasonous Government Officials\u8221\'20\'1D, mentioned the violence of the French Revolution and promised to use property tax records to find workers\u8217\'20\'19 homes. The secretary of state\u8217\'20\'19s office has referred it to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . All of the complaints forwarded to federal and state law enforcement so far involve reports of alleged intimidation before election day.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Alexi McCammond\uc2  and Emma Hurt write for Axios this morning that Democrats and Republicans are readying to pump hundreds of millions of dollars into Georgia over the next few weeks if, as seems very possible, control of the Senate comes down, once more, to a Georgia runoff. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/11/10/georgia-runoff-walker-warnock-senate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 They write}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Depending on the outcome of other races that still haven\u8217\'20\'19t been called, Georgia may end up being Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 last chance to deny Democrats a Senate majority. Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  performed significantly worse than Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , who easily won re-election Tuesday while Walker failed to crack 50% of the vote.\par \par \uc2 Republicans turning out to vote for Kemp may have helped pull Walker\u8217\'20\'19s scandal-plagued campaign close to 50% \u8212\'20\'14 meaning he could have a harder time rallying support on his own in the runoff.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Back to Arizona for a second, and Meg Kinnard at the AP has this explainer of just why the counting is going so slowly, even by their standards. She writes that part of it is because of all of the ballots that got dropped off on election day in Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s biggest county.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Maricopa County, Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s most populous and home to Phoenix, estimated Wednesday there were about 400,000 votes left to count, with about 275,000 of those being ballots that came in on Election Day itself votes known in some places as \u8220\'20\'1Clate earlies,\u8221\'20\'1D the counting of which has been known to hold up tabulation.\par \par \uc2 There are also about 17,000 outstanding ballots, about 7% of those cast in person on election day that were set aside due to a Tuesday printing problem at about a quarter of the county\u8217\'20\'19s vote tabulation centers. A judge denied a request from Republicans to keep the polls open, saying he didn\u8217\'20\'19t see evidence that people were not allowed to vote, and officials said those votes would be tallied throughout the week.\par \par \uc2 Election officials said they also received about 8,000 provisional ballots on election day, which included those cast by people who did not have ID, or those whose records showed they had already voted by mail.\par \par \uc2 Outside of Maricopa County, about 200,000 ballots remained to be counted, the bulk of them in Pima County, which includes Tucson.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 Adam Taylor has written the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Today\u8217\'20\'19s WorldView newsletter today, with a focus on Florida\u8217\'20\'19s governor, asking is the world ready for President DeSantis? Taylor writes:\par \par \uc2 A disappointing night for most Republicans turned into a very good night for one Floridian. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  not only won a second term in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections but also did so by a sizeable margin.\par \par \uc2 The results cemented many expectations that DeSantis would run for president in 2024. And to some Democrats, the double-digit wins seen by not only DeSantis but Florida Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  on Tuesday have firmly ended the chapter where the state could be seen as a swing state.\par \par \uc2 Taylor picks out a couple of implications for a future DeSantis run for the White House, noting:\par \par \uc2 DeSantis is not Trump. He may not always act like it, but DeSantis\u8217\'20\'19s r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 is more of a run-of-the-mill Republican civil servant than the bombastic-businessman-turned-political-arsonist Trump. In some ways, DeSantis\u8217\'20\'19s background makes him look closer to former secretary of state \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , whose more interventionist leanings were sometimes at odds with Trump.\par \par \uc2 Taylor also picks up that DeSantis\u8217\'20\'19 Florida roots would potentially signal a change in priorities for US foreign policy:\par \par \uc2 Unlike Trump, born wealthy in New York City and only belatedly becoming a resident, DeSantis is a real Florida man. And to some extent, he lives up to the reputation, notably paying extra attention to foreign issues close to many Floridians: Including Cuba, Venezuela, Colombia and Haiti.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis is happy to use brash rhetoric and even cruel stunts to make his point. He has flown Venezeulean migrants from Texas to Martha\u8217\'20\'19s Vineyard in a bid to own liberals and battled with \uc2 Disney\uc2  over gay rights \u8212\'20\'14 breaking with Republican orthodoxy to complain about corporate power. He has said France would fold if Russia invaded and sided with Elon Musk over Ukrainian leaders after the U.S. billionaire suggested Kyiv needed to negotiate a peace deal with Russia.\par \par \uc2 And while DeSantis appears to have accepted the reality of climate change\u8217\'20\'19s likely impact on Florida, he has favored throwing money at climate adaptation rather than working to actually mitigate the problem.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here is how my colleague Nicola Slawson is summing things up for today\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/series/guardian-us-briefing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 First Thing}}}\uc2  newsletter, which should be hitting in-boxes shortly:\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hailed \u8220\'20\'1Ca good day\u8221\'20\'1D for democracy after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/democrats-house-senate-performance-polls-predictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats defied history and outperformed expectations}}}\uc2  in the US midterm elections, leaving control of Congress on a knife-edge.\par \par \uc2 With ballots still being counted, Democrats were hopeful about holding the Senate, though the outcome of the tight races in Arizona and Nevada were still uncertain yesterday evening and another key race in Georgia was heading to a runoff. Democrats need to win two of those seats to maintain Senate control.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, meanwhile, felt they were on course to win the House \u8211\'20\'13 but by a much narrower margin than widely predicted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe had an election yesterday \u8211\'20\'13 it was a good day, I think, for democracy,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden told reporters at the White House. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd I think it was a good day for America.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The president added: \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the press and the pundits are predicting a giant red wave, it didn\u8217\'20\'19t happen \u8230\'20\'26 Democrats had a strong night.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Who will have control of the Senate? Control of the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/election-us-senate-race-control-republicans-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 still up in the air}}}\uc2  this morning as several hotly contested seats remained uncalled and the fierce race between Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic incumbent senator, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , and his Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , moved to a runoff.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Who was the midterms biggest loser? He wasn\u8217\'20\'19t on any ballot but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is widely seen as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/the-winner-of-the-midterms-is-not-yet-clear-but-the-loser-is-donald-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the night\u8217\'20\'19s biggest loser}}}\uc2  as so many of his endorsed candidates failed and he was being {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/trump-desantis-election-2024-florida"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blamed by members of his own party}}}\uc2  for the failure of the Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 anticipated \u8220\'20\'1Cred wave\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Where can I see thefull live results of the congressional midterms? You can view them {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/10/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  seat by seat.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/10/first-thing-biden-hails-midterms-as-a-good-day-for-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden hails midterms as a \u8216\'20\'18good day for democracy\u8217\'20\'19 | First Thing}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 Arizona Democrats are maintaining small but dwindling leads over their Republican rivals in the races for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and governor, the AP reports. The races remained too early to call two days after the election, with some 600,000 ballots left to count, about a quarter of the total cast.\par \par \uc2 Protracted vote counts have for years been a staple of elections in Arizona, where the overwhelming majority of votes are cast by mail and many people wait until the last minute to return them. But as Arizona has morphed from a Republican stronghold to a competitive battleground, the delays have increasingly become a source of national anxiety.\par \par \uc2 After opening big leads early on election night, when only mail ballots returned early were reported, Democrats have seen their leads dwindled as more Republican ballots have been counted. On Thursday morning, Democrats led in the races of Senate, governor and secretary of state, while the race for attorney general was essentially tied. It could take several days before it\u8217\'20\'19s clear who won some of the closer contests.\par \par \uc2 Democratic candidate for governor of Arizona, Arizona secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . Photograph: Reuters\par \par \uc2 Republican Kari Lake was about half a point behind Democratic secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  in the race for governor, a contest that has centered heavily on Lake\u8217\'20\'19s baseless claims of fraud in the 2020 election. The Republican candidate for attorney general also trailed narrowly.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had more comfortable 5-point margins in the races for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and secretary of state, but with so many ballots outstanding, the races remain too early to call.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 David Smith, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Washington DC bureau chief, offers this analysis of the results today, arguing that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/biden-legislative-agenda-republicans-control-house-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats are breathing a sigh of relief \u8211\'20\'13 but their troubles are far from over}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 For all their deflation, Republicans appear on course to capture a majority in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/house-of-representatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 House of Representatives}}}\uc2 , albeit by a far smaller margin than history has suggested or crystal ball gazers had forecast.\par \par \uc2 That means the end of Democrat \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reign as House speaker, at least for now. Republican leader Kevin McCarthy has announced his intention to take the speaker\u8217\'20\'19s gavel. It might be better described as a poisoned chalice.\par \par \uc2 Should McCarthy prevail, his achingly slim majority will afford little room for maneuver when it comes to legislating. McCarthy will have to do deals either with Democrats or far-right Trump loyalists. In a House where every member fancies him or herself as president, the speaker could find himself perpetually bending to the will of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/08/campaign-millions-maga-extremist-marjorie-taylor-greene-marcus-flowers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is hardly a prescription for national unity. Whatever happens in the Senate, which may be decided again in a Georgia runoff, America is returning to an era of divided government and two years of grinding trench warfare.\par \par \uc2 That spells trouble for Biden\u8217\'20\'19s legislative agenda, echoing the plight of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/barack-obama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Barack Obama}}}\uc2 , who did big things in his first two years as president but found slim pickings over the following six.\par \par \uc2 Biden, who campaigned as an apostle of bipartisanship and did enjoy some wins \u8211\'20\'13 on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/nov/08/bidens-infrastructure-success-is-a-historic-and-sorely-needed-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infrastructure}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/25/joe-biden-signs-gun-control-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gun safety}}}\uc2 , military veterans\u8217\'20\'19 benefits \u8211\'20\'13 will now find Republicans more combative as everything comes to be seen through the prism of the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 Read more of David Smith\u8217\'20\'19s analysis here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/biden-legislative-agenda-republicans-control-house-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats are breathing a sigh of relief. But their troubles are far from over}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/09/biden-legislative-agenda-republicans-control-house-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats are breathing a sigh of relief. But their troubles are far from over}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 US midterms 2022: what we know so far\par \par \uc2 Welcome to our continued coverage of the 2022 US midterm elections, where Senate control is still considered a toss-up as key races remain uncalled, and in the battle to control the House the Republicans have secured 217 seats compared to 184 that have gone for the Democrats. With 391 of 435 races called, the Republicans have gained six seats, which may well just be enough to put them on course for the slimmest of majorities. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what we know so far:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The fierce race between Georgia \u8217\'20\'19s Democratic incumbent senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  moved to a runoff. Warnock is narrowly leading Walker, but neither candidate will be able to clear the 50% threshold needed to win outright after the polls closed on Tuesday and avoid a 6 December runoff.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Nevada as of midday on Wednesday, with about 77% of the votes counted, Democratic incumbent \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  was trailing her Republican rival \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , 47.2% to 49.9%. Both candidates have urged patience as residents wait to hear the outcome of the race and several other close elections, which could take days.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Arizona \u8217\'20\'19s Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly was ahead of his Republican challenger, Blake Masters, 51.4% to 46.4%, with 45% of the vote counted.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Democrats need to win two of those Senate seats to effectively maintain control of the Senate with a 50-50 split on Senators and the vice president having the casting vote.\par \par \uc2 You can find all of the latest results on our interactive: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/10/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterm election results 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 live}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/nov/10/midterm-election-results-live-2022-map-us-midterms-latest-winners-seats-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterm elections 2022: live results}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221110eiba001xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
