\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Did Democrats just have the best midterms by a president\u8217\'20\'19s party in years?\b0\par\par\uc2 Osita Nwanevu \par \uc2 1,241 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2022\par 19:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s approval rating stands at about 41%, yet the Democrats performed far better than many had expected\par \par \uc2 Leave the rest of the numbers aside for a moment and consider these figures. The party of the sitting president has lost seats in the \uc2 US House of Representatives\uc2  in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/22899204/midterm-elections-president-biden-thermostatic-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all but two}}}\uc2  midterms since 1946. The average loss is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gazette.com/government/midterm-elections-in-historical-perspective-cronin-and-loevy/article_dbcfb8a8-5568-11ed-830d-37d11589ad86.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 27 seats}}}\uc2 . According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/economic-discontent-fuels-gop-hopes-midterms-draw-close/story?id=92679694&cid=social_twitter_abcn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one analysis}}}\uc2 , presidents with approval ratings above 50% have seen their parties lose an average of 14 seats since 1946. Presidents with approval ratings below 50% have seen their parties lose an average of 37 seats.\par \par \uc2 Polls suggest \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s current approval rating stands at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/biden-approval-rating/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 41%}}}\uc2 . Republicans needed to take five seats to win the chamber. It remains likely that they\u8217\'20\'19ll get them in the hours and days ahead. But the fact that they haven\u8217\'20\'19t already ?\u8211\'20\'13 and that Democrats have a non-trivial chance of actually keeping the chamber ?\u8211\'20\'13 is highly significant. The Democrats may have had the best midterm a president\u8217\'20\'19s party has experienced in 20 years ?\u8211\'20\'13 since 9/11 brought Republicans to slight, trend-bucking gains in the House and Senate in 2002.\par \par \uc2 Of course, much of the relief and exuberance Democratic leaders and candidates are feeling this morning will dissipate if and when the Republican majority, whatever its final size, is secured. While it\u8217\'20\'19s preferable to lose the House by a little rather than a lot, losing it by any margin guarantees that toxicity and inanity will grip Washington again for a minimum of two years ?\u8211\'20\'13 irrespective of the outcome in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Biden cannot expect cooperation from Republicans on major issues in divided government \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s unclear, in fact, whether Congress will even manage to raise the debt ceiling again if Democrats don\u8217\'20\'19t figure out a way to settle the issue, and avert a crisis that\u8217\'20\'19s been gestating for over a decade now, before January.\par \par \uc2 It is clear, though, that House Republicans would use their majority to harass the administration and Democrats with investigations, hearings, cant and conspiracy theories in the run-up to 2024. The main character of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s Congress thus far has been Joe Manchin; there\u8217\'20\'19s a decent chance that he\u8217\'20\'19ll be turning the spotlight over to \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Political science and political history suggest there was little Democrats might have done to fully avoid that possibility. The party\u8217\'20\'19s House majority was incredibly narrow and presidents have consistently lost seats under a wide variety of conditions. The rate of inflation, the subject of most of the last year\u8217\'20\'19s punditry and predictions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/analyses/the-2022-midterm-elections-what-the-historical-data-suggest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is poorly correlated}}}\uc2  with midterm outcomes, as is employment growth.\par \par \uc2 Income growth is a more reliable indicator, but as best as those who\u8217\'20\'19ve studied midterms can tell, the factors that reliably hobble the party holding the White House may be more elemental. Voters that just won the presidency are more likely to blow off the midterms, voters that just lost it are more likely to angrily show up, and some voters ?\u8211\'20\'13 in keeping with messaging and norms impressed upon them by the press, political elites and grade school civics teachers ?\u8211\'20\'13 vote against the party in power just to bring more partisan balance to Washington.\par \par \uc2 Despite all that, Democrats managed to significantly outperform expectations. Republicans and centrist commentators widely predicted voters would sharply recoil at the scale of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s policy agenda and the inflation it contributed to. They didn\u8217\'20\'19t. Over the last few weeks, many of the same voices insisted that the Democratic closing argument on democracy wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t matter or resonate. Perhaps it did. And over the last few months, we were told repeatedly that anxieties about rising crime would boost Republicans across the country ?\u8211\'20\'13 even in New York, where it was supposed that Republican \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  had a real shot at winning the governor\u8217\'20\'19s race.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 shocking overperformance amounts to a shocking underperformance on the Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 part\par \par \uc2 That didn\u8217\'20\'19t happen \u8211\'20\'13 suggesting that the electorate was more troubled by other anxieties that hurt the right, including worries about reproductive freedom. In the aftermath of the Dobbs decision, voters in deeply conservative Kansas surprised many by rejecting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Last night, voters in Kentucky ?\u8211\'20\'13 a state \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won by double digits ?\u8211\'20\'13 rejected another anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure while California, Michigan and Vermont voted to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 As loudly as many pundits argued that Democrats would be crushed on Tuesday absent deeper moderation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other issues, it should be plain now that the burden of moderation, as far as much of the electorate is concerned, falls primarily upon the Republican party: the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 shocking overperformance amounts to a shocking underperformance on its part.\par \par \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 substantive radicalism on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and elsewhere aside, it should also be plain that they\u8217\'20\'19ve been burdened by their outre personalities, including the man at Mar-a-Lago and candidates who spent much of the last several months regurgitating his nonsense about the 2020 campaign to weary general election voters. Some of them are still at it. The Arizona gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake, for instance, narrowly behind in her race at time of writing, alluded to conspiracy theories about voter fraud in her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/atrupar/status/1590212165570551808?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwterm%5E1590212165570551808%7Ctwgr%5Eabc31e1fcf2cbf091fa4b86bf5c368a7b25becfe%7Ctwcon%5Es1_&ref_url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.rollingstone.com%2Fpolitics%2Fpolitics-news%2Fgop-gubernatorial-candidates-implementing-the-2020-election-denial-playbook-1234627435%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election night speech}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, according to a number of reports, some Republican insiders and elites are privately wising up to realities already obvious to those who\u8217\'20\'19ve studied the current political landscape closely. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a political amateur who narrowly won an electoral college victory in an unusual election six years ago. He\u8217\'20\'19s a deeply polarizing and unpopular figure; most of the electorate reviles his political style and his policy ideas, to the extent that he has them.\par \par \uc2 The structural advantages that the federal system affords the most conservative parts of the country have prevented the Republican party from fully bearing the costs of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s rise and presidency \u8211\'20\'13 and they may well bring him to victory again in the next election. But Trump has been costly, and we can expect a cadre of Republican power-brokers and money men to pursue alternative candidates with more urgency now.\par \par \uc2 That ought to trigger a shift in messaging from Democrats. Throughout this election and the last, Biden and other party figures and candidates labored to give voters the impression Trumpism is a passing fad on the right; the dream of a redeemable Republican party is still alive in the rhetoric of Democratic leaders, if not genuinely in their hearts. But it\u8217\'20\'19s substantively untrue and strategically unwise to maintain that the right\u8217\'20\'19s threats to equality and the democratic process are contained fully in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s person and the figures who\u8217\'20\'19ve tethered themselves closely to him.\par \par \uc2 Over the last quarter century in particular, our politics have been coarsened and destabilized not by a narrow faction of Super-Ultra-Extra-Mega-Magnum-Maga Republicans, but by the Republican party and the wider conservative movement as a whole. It\u8217\'20\'19s long past time for Democrats to make that case to the public plainly and unapologetically.\par \par \uc2 If they don\u8217\'20\'19t ?\u8211\'20\'13 and the Republicans do, in fact, take part or all of Congress ?\u8211\'20\'13 gridlock in Washington, invented scandals and boredom with Biden may encourage a pivotal share of voters to give Republican governance another chance in 2024.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Osita Nwanevu is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221110eiba0035z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
