\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18No Republican blowout\u8217\'20\'19: our panel reacts to the initial US midterm results\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan, Cas Mudde, Robert Reich, Bhaskar Sunkara, LaTosha Brown and Ben Davis \par \uc2 1,897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par 16:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While much remains unclear about Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s elections, we know that Democrats did much better than expectedUS midterm elections 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 latest live news updates\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan: \u8216\'20\'18It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t meant to be this close\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 It was not supposed to be this close. Midterms are always hard for the party in power. In the past, when Democrats have faced a midterm election when they controlled both the White House and Congress, the Republicans had a blowout.\par \par \uc2 In 1994, during \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s first term, Republicans gained huge margins in the house. In 2010, it was even bigger. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has proved to be a president with little of his own constituency and few legislative achievements to show for his first two years of unified government, thanks in no small part to how narrow Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 majorities were in Congress in 2020. Meanwhile, inflation is at roughly 8%. It was supposed to be a blowout night for the Democrats, the kind of humiliation that sent the Biden administration a firm rebuke. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not that there were no disappointments. There were some painful losses for Democrats: the odious \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  acolyte JD Vance has won a Senate seat in Ohio; candidates that perennially capture the imagination and hope of national democrats, like \uc2 Beto O\u8217\'20\'19Rourke\uc2  and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , lost.\par \par \uc2 But Republican margins are narrow, and even when the party had the wind at their back. Trump-backed, election-denying candidates did poorly; so did those who most vocally oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The Republican party is in disarray, unable to quit Trump, but unable to thrive while anchored to him. If they do end up winning a majority, they will do so weakened and vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Cas Mudde: \u8216\'20\'18No red tsunami\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Much remains unclear at the time of writing this, but we know the following: first and foremost, there is no red tsunami. The Republicans are doing better than in 2020, but far less well than was expected just a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 Second, while the Dobbs \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling did not bring the blue wave that Democratic operatives had promised, the pro-choice counter-mobilization has definitely mitigated Republican wins.\par \par \uc2 Third, while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  comes out of the midterms relatively unscathed, this cannot be said of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Several of his hand-picked and personally endorsed outsiders might have achieved shocking primary victories, and some might even still win their elections. But still: the vast majority clearly underperformed in comparison to more traditional Republican candidates in the same states.\par \par \uc2 The much-watched state of Georgia provided perhaps the most embarrassing result for Trump: \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , the candidate he campaigned hardest against, was comfortably re-elected governor, while \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , his hand-picked Senate candidate, polled almost 5% behind Kemp and is probably facing a highly uncertain runoff against \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fourth, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s main rival within the Republican party, Florida governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , not only convincingly won re-election, but polled almost 2% ahead of Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and gifted his party three new, gerrymandered, House seats.\par \par \uc2 All of this means that, even if the Republican party does seize control of the House and/or Senate, it is facing a very uncertain period in the run-up to the 2024 presidential elections. It is now overly clear to everyone that Trump is both a necessity in the primaries and a liability in the elections. Everyone but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that is.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Cas Mudde is a Guardian US columnist and the Stanley Wade Shelton UGAF professor in the school of public and international affairs at the University of Georgia\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 : \u8216\'20\'18Democrats didn\u8217\'20\'19t do too badly\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Let me focus on four ways today\u8217\'20\'19s election was unique:\par \par \uc2 1. Compared to previous midterm elections when the party that occupied the White House took a major drubbing (Clinton lost 54 House seats; Obama, 63; Trump, 40), Democrats didn\u8217\'20\'19t do too badly \u8211\'20\'13 even though, when the dust settles, they are likely to lose control of the House.\par \par \uc2 2. Compared to the amount of money spent on previous elections, this one was staggering. Total spending on federal and state races {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/Vx3TNPrJlWmCE12JpbsAWg~~/AAAAAQA~/RgRlR3_-mRj8GtQW1ITcnJlaWNoQGJlcmtlbGV5LmVkdVgEAAAAAA~~"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could exceed $16.7bn}}}\uc2 , according to estimates by Open Secrets.\par \par \uc2 American billionaires will have spent an estimated $1bn, mostly on Republican candidates and causes. (\uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  alone sunk $30m into the Senate campaigns of JD Vance in Ohio and Blake Masters in Arizona.) That\u8217\'20\'19s 44% higher than billionaires\u8217\'20\'19 total spending during the 2018 midterm cycle, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/11/03/american-billionaires-spent-a-record-880-million-on-the-us-midterm-elections-.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  published Thursday by the group Americans for Tax Fairness.\par \par \uc2 What will the super-rich get back on their investments? Republicans won\u8217\'20\'19t have the votes to override Biden\u8217\'20\'19s vetoes, so they\u8217\'20\'19ll likely try to weaponize raising the debt ceiling (as they did in 2011) to force Democrats to agree to more tax cuts and regulatory rollbacks for their wealthy patrons.\par \par \uc2 3. Compared to other elections in which Russia has denied seeking to affect the outcome, in this one, Russia, in the form of a Russian oligarch close to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , openly boasted of such interference.\par \par \uc2 4. Finally, compared with what\u8217\'20\'19s been at stake in previous elections, the stakes in this one are especially high for the future.\par \par \uc2 Last June, half of Americans lost the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , courtesy of the Trump supreme court, and Republicans in Congress have threatened to ban abortions nationally. Meanwhile, more than half of Republican candidates in today\u8217\'20\'19s election sided with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in denying that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s decided today in races for Congress as well as for state offices will affect the trajectory of both issues \u8211\'20\'13 the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and of democracy \u8211\'20\'13 including Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presumed effort to become America\u8217\'20\'19s first dictator.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , a former US secretary of labor, is professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/saving-capitalism-for-the-many-not-the-few/9780345806222"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-common-good-9780525436379/9780525436379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His new book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-system-who-rigged-it-how-we-fix-it/9780525659044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It,}}}\uc2  is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Bhaskar Sunkara: \u8216\'20\'18Tonight is a wake-up call\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Tonight should be a wake-up call for Democrats. Yes, the worst has been avoided for the party, but their dominant midterm strategy simply didn\u8217\'20\'19t work.\par \par \uc2 Of course, circumstances conspired against them \u8211\'20\'13 midterms are always difficult for incumbent parties. Add to that an unfavorable set of seats up for grabs, inflation and general concerns about the cost of living, a crime spike since 2019, and it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to imagine how Democrats could have maintained the House of Representatives this cycle.\par \par \uc2 But there were opportunities that could have been exploited in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  that were thwarted by the rhetoric and priorities of the party. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/commentisfree/2022/oct/10/democrats-midterms-victory-economy-bernie-sanders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bernie Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 October op-ed}}}\uc2  right here in the Guardian reads like prophecy: \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t win elections unless you have the support of the working class of this country.\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a crucial issue galvanizing millions of people, many of them workers, to vote. Yet Sanders was right to say that it was \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitical malpractice for Democrats to ignore the state of the economy and allow Republican lies and distortions to go unanswered\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Consider the strong performance of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in Pennsylvania, who at the time of writing looks poised to win and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in Ohio, who outperformed Biden\u8217\'20\'19s 2020 mark despite coming up short against JD Vance. They ran campaigns with clear economic focused messages and focused on everyday concerns. There\u8217\'20\'19s no reason more candidates like them couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have been put forward.\par \par \uc2 Biden has a relatively strong policy record as president so far, but his and the Democratic leadership\u8217\'20\'19s inability to win on the economy and present themselves as the party of working people hurt them tonight. No matter how low expectations were, a loss is a loss: millionaire-funded NGOs can\u8217\'20\'19t again be allowed to dominate the rhetoric and priorities of the party.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Bhaskar Sunkara is the president of the Nation, the founding editor of Jacobin, and the author of The Socialist Manifesto: the Case for Radical Politics in an Era of Extreme Inequalities\par \par \uc2 LaTosha Brown: \u8216\'20\'18Trump can still win in 2024\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 One thing is clear: we are not in a post-Trump world. We are in a Trumpian era. It is not far-fetched that Trump could rise to power again. In fact, we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen many candidates who share his values capture seats in this election.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic party needs to do far more to reach out to Black voters. In Georgia, where \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  lost to \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , we didn\u8217\'20\'19t see an investment on the ground as we saw in previous cycles.\par \par \uc2 This was the largest election since a slew of voter-suppression bills were signed into law, and we are dealing, in part, with the legacy of that. The political landscape has shifted, and we need a multi-racial, multi-generational pro-democracy movement to respond to that.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 LaTosha Brown is the co-founder of Black Voters Matter\par \par \uc2 Ben Davis: \u8216\'20\'18This should have been a Republican blowout. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 This has been a weird and contradictory electoral cycle, but one thing is clear: this is the best midterm for any administration since the 2002 election when the country was gripped by the war fever of 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Democrats will probably lose seats: perhaps no one \u8220\'20\'1Cwon\u8221\'20\'1D this election. Some states look like they have shifted to the right (Florida appears to be lost to Democrats forever) while some seem extremely strong for Democrats. This year has been confused, because the government is confused.\par \par \uc2 While the Democrats control the actual elected federal government, the primary transformative policy change that has happened came from the hard right, overturning Roe v Wade. It certainly hasn\u8217\'20\'19t felt like the Democrats have power over the last two years. The big takeaway so far is there is no red wave and there is no systemic bias in polling toward Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The first term of a Democratic presidency with Democratic control of the Senate and House, high inflation, and most of the country disappointed in the direction of the country should be a Republican blowout. As of the time of writing, it looks likely the Republican party takes back the House and there\u8217\'20\'19s a real chance they take back the US senate, depending on the results of some razor-thin races.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to call this a win for the Republicans or a loss for the Democrats and the Biden administration. If this were a first midterm wipeout like in 2010 or 2018, the Republicans could claim victory. Instead, they have underachieved nearly everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Two things have happened: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  activated turnout that won\u8217\'20\'19t go away, and the Dobbs decision further polarized the electorate along culture war lines. Once people get in the habit of voting they rarely stop, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  activated so many people on both sides that dreary midterms are a thing of the past.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ben Davis works in political data in Washington. He worked on the data team for the Bernie Sanders 2020 campaign\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221109eib9000uw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
