\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US voters head to the polls to cast ballots \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Gloria Oladipo, Lauren Aratani and Martin Belam \par \uc2 17,647 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par 09:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Millions across the country vote while Florida state department tells DoJ that federal election monitors won\u8217\'20\'19t be permittedMidterms election-night live blog \u8211\'20\'13 follow the latestUS midterm elections results 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 live\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from me today!\par \par \uc2 Thank you for reading on several midterm election races happening across the country. Here\u8217\'20\'19s a summary of everything that happened:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm election races could mean losing control of Congress. Several closely watched Senate races could determine the outcome of which party will control Congress for the rest of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s first term in office.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , the US climate envoy, has vowed that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s administration will press ahead on climate action regardless of the outcome of today\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections, which are expected to go badly for Democrats. Kerry delivered that promise while speaking at the Cop27 climate talks in Egypt.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  caught up with a number of voters across several key states, including Michigan, Georgia, and Pennsylvania. Many spoke about a number of issues that were motivating them to cast a ballot today, including the state of democracy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The first set of exit polling from CNN spells dismal results for Democrats, as a majority of the US electorate is angry and dissatisfied with the state of the country.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 GOP lawsuits challenging voter rules have caused hold ups in some districts, including Philadelphia, as election judges are now required to go through an extra checking process to monitor for double voting, a process that could take three days. Another lawsuit caused mail-in ballots in Philadelphia that have inaccurate or missing dates to be rejected, causing thousands to show up and recast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Florida state department sent a letter to the \uc2 US Department of Justice\uc2  saying that federal election monitors won\u8217\'20\'19t be permitted inside the state\u8217\'20\'19s polling places. The letter comes as several key races are happening in Florida, including the election for Florida governor.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cast a ballot at a polling place in Palm Beach, Florida. Trump gave some brief comments to media waiting outside the polling place, and confirmed that he voted for incumbent governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You can follow {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/08/midterm-elections-2022-polls-results-senate-house-us-midterm-voting-republicans-democrats-live-updates-latest-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  this blog}}}\uc2 , which will update as polling places across the country begin to close.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/08/midterm-elections-2022-polls-results-senate-house-us-midterm-voting-republicans-democrats-live-updates-latest-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Midterm elections 2022: first US polls to close as Republicans eye gains in Senate and House \u8211\'20\'13 live}}}\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re in line to vote, stay there!\par \par \uc2 Have a great night and happy election day, everyone!\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Climate action will press ahead even if Dems lost Congress, says US climate envoy\par \par \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , the US climate envoy, has vowed that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s administration will press ahead on climate action regardless of the outcome of today\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections, which are expected to go badly for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Kerry, speaking at the Cop27 climate talks in Egypt, said that he hoped there would be a Congress would act on the climate crisis but \u8220\'20\'1Ceven if we don\u8217\'20\'19t, folks, president Biden is more determined than ever to continue what we are doing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost of what we are doing cannot be changed by anyone else coming along,\u8221\'20\'1D Kerry added, noting that cities and states across America banded together to commit to the Paris climate agreement when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  removed the US from the pact when president.\par \par \uc2 US officials in Egypt hope that the inflation reduction act bill passed in August, which contains more than $370bn in climate spending and called \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the single most important pieces of legislation over the past 50 years or so\u8221\'20\'1D by Kerry in his speech, will drive deep cuts in emissions regardless of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But Kerry conceded it was possible that Republicans could \u8220\'20\'1Claunch an assault\u8221\'20\'1D on Biden\u8217\'20\'19s climate agenda if they win at least one house of Congress in the elections. The GOP has decried what it calls Biden\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cradical green agenda\u8221\'20\'1D and could stall or hamper the rollout of measures aimed at boosting renewable energy deployment. Biden arrives at Cop27 on Friday, potentially in a downcast mood following the outcome of the elections.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Aben\u233\'00\'E9 Clayton, reporting on the California gubernatorial race from Los Angeles\par \par \uc2 It may have been one of the least anxiety-inducing midterm races in the nation. California governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is expected to cruise to re-election and easily beat Republican state senator Brian Dahle to serve his second term at the helm of the US\u8217\'20\'19 most populous state.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Newsom survived a recall attempt driven by critics of his handling of the pandemic. This year, with re-election in deep blue California all but secure, he took his message to the national stage, spending millions of dollars on ads addressing issues like gun control and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in states including Mississippi and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Newsom\u8217\'20\'19s national effort has sparked speculation among political analysts and officials he\u8217\'20\'19s considering a run for president -- a rumor Newsom has consistently denied.\par \par \uc2 But as my colleague Maanvi Singh {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/02/gavin-newsom-presidential-run-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  a few weeks ago: \u8220\'20\'1CDenial is the tradition of pretty much every politician who has flirted with chief executive ambitions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 More reporting on voting in Michigan, from the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Sam Levine\par \par \uc2 Taylor Morgan, 26, cast her vote Tuesday just a few blocks from the main campus of the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , in Ann Arbor. A driving force getting her to the polls is Proposal 3, an effort to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the Michigan constitution.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CReproductive rights and reproductive freedom are super important to me as a Black woman in Michigan,\u8221\'20\'1D she said just before she cast her ballot. \u8220\'20\'1CI just want to make sure that those rights are maintained. I feel like if that\u8217\'20\'19s not something that you believe in or support, you have that right. But you don\u8217\'20\'19t have the right to tell the people what they can do with their bodies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrTaylor Morgan, a 26-year old medical student at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , said she was motivated to come out and vote today in support of Proposal 3. \u8220\'20\'1CReproductive rights and reproductive freedom are super important to me as a Black woman in Michigan,\u8221\'20\'1D she said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Z6TdYUvvKU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Z6TdYUvvKU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sam Levine (@srl) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/srl/status/1590082903232614400?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Morgan said she\u8217\'20\'19s concerned about efforts to overturn the election, but hopes that the margin will be so wide, any efforts will quickly fall apart.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy hope is that a lot of people turn out so everything is indisputable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Morgan, a medical student.\par \par \uc2 Abby Anderson, a senior at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  studying performance art management, said she also was motivated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m very pro-choice. It\u8217\'20\'19s a cause that I care a lot about. Particular since I\u8217\'20\'19m not from Michigan, and I could have chosen to vote in the state that I\u8217\'20\'19m from, but I felt that my vote had more of an impact here because it\u8217\'20\'19s more of a swing,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s some concern about potentially low turnout from young voters, but both Morgan and Anderson said they thought young people would vote in high numbers. Some of her professor had even said it\u8217\'20\'19s OK if students couldn\u8217\'20\'19t make it to class on Tuesday because they needed to vote, Anderson said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m young and a lot of my friends, they\u8217\'20\'19re very adamant about coming out to vote,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems like a lot of people are lining up to go to the on campus polling places. I feel like young people are starting to realize what they stand for and what they don\u8217\'20\'19t stand for. So you\u8217\'20\'19re gonna see a lot of young people coming out in droves to make their voices heard. At least that\u8217\'20\'19s what it looks like to me,\u8221\'20\'1D Morgan said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 CNN's first exit polls show a majority of Americans are angry with state of country\par \par \uc2 CNN has published its first set of exit polls.\par \par \uc2 The poll readings spell fairly dismal news for Democrats ahead of midterm election results, with polls showing that the majority of the American electorate are angry with the state of the country under Biden\u8217\'20\'19s presidency.\par \par \uc2 enltrBreaking: CNN US Midterm Election exit poll makes grim reading for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 73% of voters are dissatisfied or angry about the way things are going\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 46% say Biden\u8217\'20\'19s policies are hurting the country\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 54% disapprove of Biden as President\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 32% used vote to oppose Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Wz0lYUds0P"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Wz0lYUds0P}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jamie Johnson (@JamieoJohnson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JamieoJohnson/status/1590105073568616448?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 While all eyes are on New York\u8217\'20\'19s gubernatorial race, the outcome of New York\u8217\'20\'19s 21st Congressional District contest might speak to the staying power of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRWF4z7rK7mlp73bNwixKVUg/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.theguardian.com%2Fus-news%2F2022%2Fjul%2F11%2Felise-stefanik-republican-rise-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MAGA-style politics}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14or whether voters have grown weary of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s inflammatory politics. Republican incumbent \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  is running against Democrat Matt Castelli, a former CIA officer who served on the national security council.\par \par \uc2 Stefanik became the No. 3 House Republican after \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  was kicked out of the role of conference chair amid the Wyoming congresswoman\u8217\'20\'19s ongoing criticism of Trump. While Stefanik was a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRWF4z7YmAKZ6B7-kg0n9ikz/https%3A%2F%2Fheritageaction.com%2Fscorecard%2Fmembers%2FS001196%2F114"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moderate}}}\uc2  when she entered the House in 2015, the congress member has increasingly moved right.\par \par \uc2 Some consider Stefanik to be a potential 2024 vice-presidential candidate. Not everyone in Stefanik\u8217\'20\'19s district is enthused by her political ascent\u8212\'20\'14including her statements relating to baseless claims that there were sweeping \u8220\'20\'1Cirregularities\u8221\'20\'1D in the previous national election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRWF4z_sbGnBKJ4Q-QztOQ29/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cnn.com%2F2021%2F05%2F06%2Fpolitics%2Ffact-check-stefanik-big-lie-election-trump%2Findex.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 per CNN.}}}\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Mann\uc2 , a Castelli campaign volunteer and chair of the Brunswick, New York Democratic Committee, decried Stefanik\u8217\'20\'19s statements about the 2020 election. \u8220\'20\'1CShe does not support democracy. She {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRWF40Iy8uXQNrqUygBr9p9o/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.northcountrypublicradio.org%2Fnews%2Fstory%2F42990%2F20210107%2Fstefanik-objects-to-certifying-the-vote-in-pennsylvania-not-arizona"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted against}}}\uc2  supporting the results of the election even though she knew\u8212\'20\'14 she had information\u8212\'20\'14there was no fraud. I find that terrifying.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mann, who cast her ballot early, said that many in the district are worried about affordable healthcare and prescription drugs; Stefanik, she said, \u8220\'20\'1Cvoted against their interests every single time.\u8221\'20\'1D Like many other voters, Mann voiced frustration over polarization, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m tired of the parisian politics.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As for the governor\u8217\'20\'19s race, Mann said: \u8220\'20\'1CHochul is not my ideal candidate, I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to lie. She is at least somebody who will support the democratic process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe will make sure that women\u8217\'20\'19s rights are guaranteed for us\u8212\'20\'14not having the government take it from us,\u8221\'20\'1D Mann said. Stefanik was among the some 80 Republicans supporting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRWF40A__x-sob8zNA0ThU--/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.wwnytv.com%2F2022%2F09%2F15%2Fstefanik-backs-federal-ban-most-abortions-after-15-weeks%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national legislation}}}\uc2  that would prohibit most abortions after 15 weeks. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s another thing I really dislike about Stefanik: She wants to take away states\u8217\'20\'19 rights by sponsoring this nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In New York, the Democratic governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , and Republican challenger \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  are making their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRV2IhdwcNvig92xKwFfab-e/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.theguardian.com%2Fus-news%2F2022%2Fnov%2F06%2Frepublicans-democrats-midterm-elections-closing-arguments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last-minute pitches}}}\uc2  to voters in the gubernatorial race. While the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRV2IhdCQro14sOP6A_OB6c_/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cookpolitical.com%2Fratings%2Fgovernor-race-ratings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cook Political Report}}}\uc2  predicts that the governor\u8217\'20\'19s race will \u8220\'20\'1Clikely\u8221\'20\'1D be for Democrats, her lead over Zeldin has slimmed dramatically\u8212\'20\'14from a double-digit margin to just four points over the course of several weeks, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRV2IhdYmFiEnbdWrQcoE57Z/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cnbc.com%2F2022%2F11%2F07%2F2022-midterms-ny-gov-kathy-hochuls-top-donors-sound-alarm-over-gop-candidate-lee-zeldin-surge.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  according to CNBC}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis city is back and I want to lead this state into the next four years and possibly beyond with a sense of optimism we have not had here in a long time,\u8221\'20\'1D Hochul said during a campaign stop on Manhattan\u8217\'20\'19s Upper East Side Tuesday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRV2IhlJSWbbSJUuYAG3RMlI/https%3A%2F%2Fnypost.com%2F2022%2F11%2F08%2Fny-election-day-live-updates-hochul-zeldin-governor-race-others%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the New York Post}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThat will start first thing tomorrow morning.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zeldin, who like most Republicans across the US has made crime a key tenet of his platform, tried to hammer Hochul on public safety during a campaign stop at the Nassau County, Long Island Republican headquarters. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you go and ask a lot of Democrats who are inside of New York City who are fed up, they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be told that the crime in the subway is just a perception. They should just look away, there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing to see here,\u8221\'20\'1D the Post quoted Zeldin as saying.\par \par \uc2 Democrats outside of New York have taken notice of Zeldin\u8217\'20\'19s rise in the polls and appear to be considering him a serious threat. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  appeared alongside Hochul on Sunday to encourage voters to cast their ballots in what has become an unexpectedly tight race. \u8220\'20\'1CNew York, you have two days to make sure the rest of New York knows the good that they have in \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://streaklinks.com/BRV2IhdFxWgK6CKk7gnjT2iP/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.politico.com%2Fnews%2F2022%2F11%2F06%2Fbiden-stumps-for-hochul-as-election-nears-00065348"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden said, according to Politico}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CTwo days until the most important election in our lifetime.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Philadelphia voters have encountered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/live/election-2022-results-pennsylvania-senate-governor-candidates-voting-updates-news-20221108.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some issues}}}\uc2  while trying to cast their votes, both mail in ballots and in-person votes on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of mail-in ballots were recast in the Brotherly Love city following a ruling on a GOP lawsuit that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/11/07/pennsylvania-voters-scramble-cast-new-ballots-after-gop-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invalidated mail-in ballots}}}\uc2  that had missing or inaccurate dates.\par \par \uc2 The decision, made last Tuesday, launched a mass volunteer effort in the city on Monday night to alert those impacted about their ballots.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, election officials were also mandated to check for double voting, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/11/08/under-pressure-gop-lawsuit-philadelphia-takes-step-that-will-slow-vote-count/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thanks to a separate GOP lawsuit}}}\uc2 . The checking process could take several days and slow down the vote count process.\par \par \uc2 From the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The process, known as \u8220\'20\'1Cpoll book reconciliation,\u8221\'20\'1D is a way to prevent double voting that Philadelphia implemented in 2020 amid a dramatic expansion of mail ballots in the state. It requires that election workers interrupt ballot counting to scan poll books so that the lists of voters who returned mail ballots can be compared to those who voted in-person. The process generally takes three days, court records show.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some helpful context for Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections and what it could mean for Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 control of Congress, from the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/chrismcgreal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chris McGreal }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/joan-greve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joan E Greve}}}\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration was braced for a bad night on Tuesday as the US midterm election results threatened to rob the Democrats of control of Congress, just as former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appears ready {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/big-announcement-donald-trump-holds-back-decision-on-2024-presidential-run"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to announce}}}\uc2  another run for the White House.\par \par \uc2 But the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  were holding out hope that they might just retain control of the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  if a handful of closely fought races fell their way.\par \par \uc2 The final results, which will determine control of Congress for the remainder of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s first term as president and further constrain his legislative agenda, could take days or even weeks in some closely fought Senate races. Delayed results are likely to fuel legal challenges and conspiracy theories about vote-rigging, particularly if the remaining seats determine control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The ground was already being laid in Pennsylvania, where a close \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race is being fought between Mehmet Oz, a Trump-backed Republican, and Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who has been battling to assure voters he is fit for office after a stroke. Earlier on election day on Tuesday, the agency overseeing the voting in Philadelphia said it will delay counting thousands of paper ballots because of a Republican lawsuit that said the process was open to duplicate voting.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of Republican candidates for the Senate, the House of Representatives and other major offices have refused to confirm that they will accept the result if they lose amid a swirl of false claims of fraud, stemming from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s assertion that the 2020 presidential election was stolen from him and kept alive by the Republican party leadership.\par \par \uc2 Read the full article {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/2022-midterms-democrats-tightly-fought-races-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/2022-midterms-democrats-tightly-fought-races-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterms: Democrats pin Senate hopes on tightly fought races}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 More reporting on Californians voting on Proposition 30, from the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Maanvi Singh\par \par \uc2 Californians are voting today on a ballot measure that would tax the state\u8217\'20\'19s richest residents in an effort to get more electric vehicles on the road.\par \par \uc2 The measure, Proposition 30, would hike taxes by 1.75% on those earning $2m or more annually to raise between $3bn and $5bn annually to subsidize households, businesses and schools; buy zero-emission cars, trucks and buses; fund infrastructure to charge electric vehicles; and bolster wildfire prevention efforts.\par \par \uc2 Proponents of the measure, including the coalition of environmental and labor groups that developed it, say the tax would provide urgently needed funds to hasten the transition to zero-emission vehicles, and reduce the disproportionate burden of pollution on low-income, minority communities across the state.\par \par \uc2 Detractors, including the California governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , claim the proposal is a corporate carve-out for \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , the ride-hailing company that has backed the measure and helped fund its campaign.\par \par \uc2 A screengrab from a video featuring \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  urging voters to reject Proposition 30. Photograph: AP\par \par \uc2 Read more about Proposition 30 and the fraught battle over it here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/14/california-proposition-30-gavin-newsom-electric-vehicles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A California measure would tax the rich to fund electric vehicles. Why is the governor against it?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The latest on Los Angeles\u8217\'20\'19 mayoral race, from the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19sLois Beckett\par \par \uc2 By the time Los Angeles residents headed to polls on Tuesday, mayoral hopeful and billionaire real estate developer \uc2 Rick Caruso\uc2  had poured more than $100m of his own fortune into his campaign to become the city\u8217\'20\'19s next leader.\par \par \uc2 Caruso, who\u8217\'20\'19s battling Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  in a closely contested race, has backed his own campaign with $101m as of late last week, campaign ethics filing show, outspending his opponent by more than 10 to one.\par \par \uc2 The developer, who is running a pro-police, tough-on-crime campaign, came in second to Bass, a former community organizer and leader of the Congressional Black Caucus, in the city\u8217\'20\'19s June primary.\par \par \uc2 But Bass\u8217\'20\'19 strong lead over Caruso in recent weeks evaporated, according to a recent poll of likely voters, with her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/politics/story/2022-11-04/final-mayoral-poll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 45% to 41% lead over Caruso}}}\uc2  within the poll\u8217\'20\'19s margin of error.\par \par \uc2 Caruso, who has an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/profile/rick-caruso/?sh=1846ac1428fd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated net worth of $5.3bn}}}\uc2 , is nearing a mayoral campaign record set by billionaire \uc2 Mike Bloomberg\uc2 , who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/local/bloomberg-spent-109m-to-barely-win-a-third-term/1915254/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent $109m of his own money to win}}}\uc2  his third term as mayor of New York City in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Total political spending of more than $120m on a mayoral race is a striking sum, especially for a contest in which the key issue is LA\u8217\'20\'19s homelessness. There are at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/08/los-angeles-homelessness-unhoused-people-number"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 41,000 unhoused people}}}\uc2  in Los Angeles county, many of them unsheltered, and living, in tents, cars, RVs and makeshift structures.\par \par \uc2 Bass has repeatedly attacked Caruso\u8217\'20\'19s campaign spending, saying that if she had $90m or $100m to spend, she would spend it on affordable housing.\par \par \uc2 enltrRick Caruso has spent $90 million lying about himself and lying about me.\par \par \uc2 I was just asked if I had $90 million, what I would do with it.\par \par \uc2 The answer is simple: I would build housing for thousands of people who sleep on our streets every night. Right away. Without hesitation.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  (@KarenBassLA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KarenBassLA/status/1587236680041234432?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 1, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Concerns over the spread of misinformation and disinformation on social media platforms are a recurring theme of each election but with the recent mass layoffs at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  following Elon Musk\u8217\'20\'19s acquisition of the platform civil liberties groups are particularly on high alert.\par \par \uc2 The company laid off a reported 50% of the workforce or an estimated 3,700 workers last week just days before the midterms. \uc2 Twitter\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s head of safety and integrity, \uc2 Yoel Roth\uc2  said that layoffs affected 15% of the company\u8217\'20\'19s trust and safety team which is charged with moderating content including combating misinformation. That has the leaders of civil liberties groups such as Color of Change, \uc2 Free Press\uc2  and the National \uc2 Association for the Advancement of Colored People\uc2 , who met with Musk to discuss how \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will deal with hate speech, convinced that the company will not be sufficiently staffed to handle attempts to mislead voters today.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRetaining and enforcing election-integrity measures requires an investment in the human expert staff, factcheckers, and moderators, who are being shown the door today,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/nov/04/twitters-mass-layoffs-days-before-us-midterms-could-be-a-misinformation-disaster"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  Jessica J Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez, co-CEO of \uc2 Free Press\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While it\u8217\'20\'19s still early in the day, months-old viral videos and tweets falsely claiming Republicans were being barred from the polls have already been recirculated today, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/elections/2022/11/08/election-day-disinformation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Washington Post. }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania is at the heart of the battle for control of Congress and key governorships across the country. To get a sense of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 political fortunes in this consequential battleground, I spoke to \uc2 Ed Rendell\uc2 , a former Democratic governor of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Rendell is bullish on the governor\u8217\'20\'19s race, where Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  has maintained a consistent lead over his Republican opponent, the election-denying conservative \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 . But he has jitters about the Senate race between \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Democrat, and Mehmet Oz, the Republican, which is rated a toss up.\par \par \uc2 Key to the race, he says, is Black voter turnout in cities like Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. If Democrats fail to mobilize this crucial constituency, it will be hard, if not impossible, for them to eke out a win in a close contest.\par \par \uc2 Rendell is hopeful Obama\u8217\'20\'19s visit to the state on Saturday will have a \u8220\'20\'1Cdramatic impact\u8221\'20\'1D on turnout, especially among Black voters, by reminding voters of the stakes and making the affirmative case for electing Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And he does not believe, as some in his party do, that Biden\u8217\'20\'19s campaign appearance in Philadelphia on Saturday, alongside Obama, Fetterman and Shapiro, will hurt the Democratic ticket,\par \par \uc2 In fact, he said it\u8217\'20\'19s possible that the enduring affection for Biden in places like Scranton, where the president was born, may help boost support for Democrats in that industrial corner of the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you don\u8217\'20\'19t like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and you want to send him a message... you vote against him, but not because he appears with Fetterman,\u8221\'20\'1D Rendell said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat doesn\u8217\'20\'19t change anybody\u8217\'20\'19s mind.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/07/biden-obama-democrats-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden and Obama make last-ditch effort as Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 mood darkens}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Aben\u233\'00\'E9 Clayton, reporting on voting conditions from Los Angeles\par \par \uc2 A winter storm has brought days of rain, snow and flood warnings to southern and northern California, and while it may have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/07/us/california-fire-season.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ended California\u8217\'20\'19s fire season}}}\uc2  it also has led midterm election hopefuls to implore voters to defy the conditions to cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 There are more questions than clear answers around the impact that weather has on voter turnout and ultimately the results of an election, but candidates aren\u8217\'20\'19t leaving anything up to chance or people\u8217\'20\'19s instincts to stay dry, warm and off of wet roads.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince we don\u8217\'20\'19t like rain, I have to make sure that people vote. We can\u8217\'20\'19t lose this election because of the rain. That would be crazy,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , the progressive candidate for mayor of Los Angeles, said during a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kfiam640.iheart.com/featured/la-local-news/content/2022-11-08-election-day-rain-in-la-area-could-dampen-voter-turnout-experts-say/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pre-election day Instagram live}}}\uc2  interview with the actor Rosario Dawson.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the state, Brooke Jenkins, San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s interim district attorney who is running to retain her seat, is calling on people to come out to the polls, \u8220\'20\'1Crain or shine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrHappy Election Day!\par \par \uc2 Rain or shine, we are working across San Francisco today to get out the vote!\par \par \uc2 Polls close at 8PM. Please go vote and take a friend or family member (or two or three) to the polls with you!\par \par \uc2 You can find your polling place here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/UeOxfVuedn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/UeOxfVuedn}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ExE3C2FHyF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ExE3C2FHyF}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Brooke Jenkins ??? (@BrookeJenkinsSF) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BrookeJenkinsSF/status/1590002272356139008?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Matt Gunderson, a Southern California state senate candidate whose campaign promises include repealing laws that downgrades crimes from felonies to misdemeanors.\par \par \uc2 enltrRain or shine it\u8217\'20\'19s Election Day!\par \par \uc2 This morning my family got up bright and early to make it to the polls before an afternoon of final campaigning.\par \par \uc2 Doing your civic duty is so much fun when you get to do it with the ones you love.\par \par \uc2 Happy Election Day! {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/gowithgunderson?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #gowithgunderson}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/WdRBgBmcjL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/WdRBgBmcjL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Matt Gunderson (@GundersonForCA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/GundersonForCA/status/1590028701785526273?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who lost Arizona in 2020, has weighed in on the tabulation machine issues in Maricopa county.\par \par \uc2 Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/inespohl/status/1590060725648510976?s=20&t=OEgmCl9kDO5g_tuLeMuVQA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted on Truth Social}}}\uc2 , saying the machine problems were mostly affecting conservative or Republican areas. It\u8217\'20\'19s not clear exactly which sites have been experiencing the tabulation problems, but voters around the county have reported them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCan this possibly be true when a vast majority of Republicans waited for today to Vote? Here we go again? The people will not stand for it!!!\u8221\'20\'1D Trump wrote.\par \par \uc2 enltrTrump is already starting his messaging that the election is fraud via Truth Social. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4m8MGavEO5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/4m8MGavEO5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ines Pohl (@inespohl) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/inespohl/status/1590060725648510976?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Tuesday, election officials in Maricopa County reported that about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/video/maricopa-county-reports-issues-vote-tabulator-machines-92899735"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 20% of polling places }}}\uc2  in the county were experiencing problems.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Erum Salam reports on the voting situation in Texas\par \par \uc2 Things are heating up in Texas, one of the most difficult states to cast a vote. Reports have emerged of voters being turned away from eight polling sites in Bell county, an area north-east of Austin, after check-in machines malfunctioned because of an issue relating to the time change.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/BREAKING?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #BREAKING}}}\uc2  Voters being turned away at 8 polling sites in Bell County. Elections office says the equipment isn\u8217\'20\'19t working - because of the time change.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Joey Horta (@JoeyHorta) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JoeyHorta/status/1589977558254497792?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Bell county election officials requested an additional hour for voting from the Texas secretary of state due to the issues.\par \par \uc2 At a time when doubt is being unnecessarily cast on the integrity of the American electoral process, Bell county does not inspire confidence in those already skeptical, but county officials told the Guardian that the issue with the machines only affected voters\u8217\'20\'19 ability to check in, not their ability to vote.\par \par \uc2 Bell county\u8217\'20\'19s public information officer, James Stafford, said: \u8220\'20\'1CFor some reason, computers at those eight locations did not automatically update to the new time. As a result, the central computer, recognizing a discrepancy, would not allow those devices to come online, and we were unable to open those sites to voters.\par \par \uc2 The critical issue that we want to communicate is that we have not had any issues related to ballots or tabulation machines. The issue was limited to those check-in devices. I also would say that, seeing the work and the passion of both our elections and technology services staff as they worked diligently to get the issue resolved as quickly as possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Confidence in the electoral process is integral to the preservation of democracy and boosting that confidence has been the primary objective of some. The \uc2 US Department of Justice{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-monitor-polls-24-states-compliance-federal-voting-rights-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced yesterday}}}\uc2  it will send federal monitors to polling sites across the country, including three counties in Texas, to ensure smooth sailing on election day.\par \par \uc2 During early voting, the Beaumont chapter of the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  alleged Black voters were being harassed and intimidated by election workers in Jefferson county. Jessica Daye, a local voter, alleged she witnessed other Black voters being shadowed by election workers who demanded they say their addresses out loud, despite already being checked in to vote.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Daye, the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  and \uc2 The Lawyers\u8217\'20\'19 Committee for Civil Rights Under Law\uc2  filed a lawsuit and a federal judge issued an order prohibiting the discriminatory behavior.\par \par \uc2 On the morning of election day, the Election Protection coalition \u8211\'20\'13 the nation\u8217\'20\'19s oldest and largest non-partisan voter protection coalition \u8211\'20\'13 held a virtual press conference \u8220\'20\'1Coutlining resources and guidance available to voters in need of information or facing intimidation at the polls\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On the call were leaders from the Committee for Civil Rights Under Law and the Texas Civil Rights Project, and top of the agenda was addressing any voting incidents at polling sites or ballot drop boxes in key states across the country.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers\u8217\'20\'19 Committee For Civil Rights Under Law called the incident in Beaumont \u8220\'20\'1Ca gross instance of invasion of privacy and voter intimidation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In the call, the Texas Civil Rights Project said it received more reports of machine malfunctions, voter intimidation, polling sites opening late, poll workers dressed in partisan attire in a few places, and issues with mail ballots that were either not received or rejected.\par \par \uc2 Voting in Texas ends at 7pm central.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 As millions across the country voted on Tuesday, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tweeted an encouragement for people to participate in elections.\par \par \uc2 enltrAt the core of our democracy is a basic principle: the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 With it, anything is possible.\par \par \uc2 Vote today.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1590064108665245707?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Behind the scenes, Biden also spoke with a number of key leaders in the Democratic party.\par \par \uc2 From the White house press office:\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, the President spoke individually by phone with \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2  Chair Roy Cooper, DCCC Chair Sean Patrick Maloney, DSCC Chair Gary Peters, and DNC Senior Advisor \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 . He also spoke jointly to DNC Chair Jaime Harrison and House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Carlisa Johnson reports on Joy to the Polls, an arts-based voting initiative in Georgia\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://circle.tufts.edu/latest-research/youth-can-have-decisive-role-2022-midterms-georgia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 young voters make up 17% }}}\uc2  of the state\u8217\'20\'19s voting population. However, young voter turnout remained lower than in previous elections at the end of early voting. Now, the final get out the vote efforts are happening to capture young voters.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the state, voting rights organizers are taking to the streets to help voters get to the polls, cast their votes and have their voices heard. Today, Joy to the Polls, an arts-based initiative, is making eight stops throughout the metro area to encourage voters to head to the polls and celebrate the joy of voting.\par \par \uc2 enltr\u8220\'20\'1CWe all know that the system is broken and things need to be changed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kaelyn Kastle one of the hosts for the event. \u8220\'20\'1CYoung people have to understand that their vote does matter because we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen how close elections can be.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/FfctRbMfQ7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/FfctRbMfQ7}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Carlisa Johnson (@CarlisaNJohnson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CarlisaNJohnson/status/1590038395451211776?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 At its third stop of the day, Joy to the Polls featured a performance by recording artist Tate \u8220\'20\'1CBaby Tate\u8221\'20\'1D Farris in East Atlanta. Farris, a Georgia native, said she came out to remind young people to vote. \u8220\'20\'1CYoung people have power. This is the opportunity to take that power that [they] have and use it by being active in the election process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This initiative, which originated in 2020, creates party-like atmospheres at polling places throughout the state, hoping to ease the stress of the voting process. Kaelyn Kastle, co-host of the East Atlanta stop, believes events like Joy to the Polls are critical in places like Georgia, where elections are won by thin margins. \u8220\'20\'1CWe all know that the system is broken, and things need to be changed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kastle. \u8220\'20\'1CYoung people have to understand that their vote does matter because we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen how close elections in Georgia can be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrIn East Atlanta, recording artist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/imbabytate?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @imbabytate}}}\uc2  is taking the mobile stage as part of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JoyToThePolls?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @JoyToThePolls}}}\uc2  a GOTV effort using the power of music to celebrate the importance of voting. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/r7xwlPC9K0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/r7xwlPC9K0}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Carlisa Johnson (@CarlisaNJohnson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CarlisaNJohnson/status/1590033995143983104?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Andrew Lawrence reports from Decatur, Georgia\par \par \uc2 Long voter queues were hard to come by, a sign that can be taken one of two ways: either most residents are already part of the giant early turnout, or simply got turned around. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause the polling places are different from the early voting locations, that\u8217\'20\'19s caused some confusion,\u8221\'20\'1D says Karen \u8220\'20\'1CMix\u8221\'20\'1D Mixon, vice-chair of the DeKalb County Democratic Committee.\par \par \uc2 On a cloudless 75F day, Mixon stood outside an early voting location in the South Dekalb Mall to redirect voters toward their assigned polling place. With SB 202, the aggressive voter suppression law President Biden dubbed \u8220\'20\'1CJim Crow 2.0\u8221\'20\'1D, finally baring its teeth on election day, Mixon notes mobilizer groups have to be extra careful about extending help this election cycle. \u8220\'20\'1CThe law is you have to be 150ft from the building where people are voting, or 25 feet from voters who are standing in line to wait to vote,\u8221\'20\'1D Mixon says. \u8220\'20\'1CWe ordered tape measures that are 150ft long and spray chalk, and we mark that off so that we are following the law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stickers are seen on a table inside a polling location in Columbus, Georgia. Photograph: Cheney Orr/Reuters\par \par \uc2 With mobilizing groups forbidden from approaching voters to make sure they\u8217\'20\'19re at the right place (much less offer them water), volunteers can basically only wave and smile and yell and hope lost voters approach them. Mixon made herself easy enough to find outside the mall with a sign urging passers by to remind friends to vote.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Andrew Lawrence reports from Atlanta\par \par \uc2 Mike South cast his ballot at Grace International Church with the economy top of mind. \u8220\'20\'1CMy retirement has shrunk by 50%,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. To say he worked hard for it barely tells the story.\par \par \uc2 After launching a decade long career at \uc2 Nasa\uc2  as computers expert following the Challenger explosion, South, 64, pivoted to directing and acting in adult movies before cementing his legend as an industry gossip columnist. In 2013, CNBC pronounced him one of porn\u8217\'20\'19s 10 most powerful people.\par \par \uc2 Porn drove his political engagement. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a time when free speech would\u8217\'20\'19ve been an issue, especially in Atlanta,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CMost of the people in my business are hardcore Democrats, but I kinda stand out because I\u8217\'20\'19m more libertarian \u8211\'20\'13 which I would expect them to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 South says he voted for libertarian candidates whenever possible, including in the senate race between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . (\u8220\'20\'1CThe truth is I don\u8217\'20\'19t like either one of them,\u8221\'20\'1D he quipped, as his support dog Lola heeled.) Third-party voters could have a significant impact on that race, which is trending closer toward another senate runoff. A new Warnock ad tips voters to the possibility of having to return to the polls again over the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 After being barraged with campaign ads for the past seven years, in the country, South winces at the thought of yet another trip to the polls. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m over it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Election officials in Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s largest county removed two workers from a polling site on the morning of Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm races after their colleagues shared social media posts of the pair at the US Capitol attack on 6 January 2021.\par \par \uc2 Fulton county elections officials told media outlets that they fired the workers \u8211\'20\'13 a mother and her son \u8211\'20\'13 about 15 minutes before the polls opened Tuesday morning. They had been assigned to a polling site at a library in the community of Johns Creek.\par \par \uc2 The mother and son fell under scrutiny after the woman made a comment that caught the attention of a colleague while they were at an event for poll workers, Fulton county \u8217\'20\'19s interim elections director, Nadine Williams, told the local news station {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsbtv.com/news/local/fulton-county-fires-2-poll-workers-after-concerns-over-social-media-posts/KRX6B5XXKFG4FAEFEOGZ4X5PHE/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSB-TV}}}\uc2 . Colleagues of the woman also found social media posts by her which were reported to the county, WSB-TV added.\par \par \uc2 Williams would not elaborate on the nature of the posts. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/11/08/election-live-results-updates-2022/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Washington Post}}}\uc2  reported that it was provided with copies of the social media screeds in question, and they showed the woman\u8217\'20\'19s family forming part of the mob of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters who staged the Capitol attack.\par \par \uc2 According to the newspaper, one of the posts read: \u8220\'20\'1CI stood up for what\u8217\'20\'19s right today in Washington DC. This election was a sham. [Trump vice-president] \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is a traitor. I was tear gassed FOUR times. I have pepper spray in my throat. I stormed the Capitol building. And my children have had the best learning experience of their lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump supporters attacked the Capitol in a failed attempt to prevent the congressional certification of the former Republican president\u8217\'20\'19s defeat to his Democratic rival \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the 2020 election. One of the mob\u8217\'20\'19s stated aims was to hang Pence after it falsely accused him of failing to avail himself of the ability to single-handedly prevent Biden\u8217\'20\'19s certification.\par \par \uc2 Officials have linked nine deaths to the insurrection, including suicides by law enforcement officers traumatized after successfully defending the building from the pro-Trump mob. Hundreds of participants have been charged criminally in connection with the attack, and many have either pleaded guilty or otherwise have been convicted over their roles.\par \par \uc2 During the 2020 presidential race, Fulton county experienced long lines at the polls, administrative mistakes and death threats against election workers. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported that the turmoil during the election two years earlier prompted Fulton county to prepare for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/08/tuesday-briefing-first-edition-what-you-need-to-know-ahead-of-us-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterms}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which many regard as a referendum on American democracy \u8211\'20\'13 by assigning police to more than half of its 300 or so polling places, with other officers patrolling between sites.\par \par \uc2 Georgia is holding some of Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s most-closely watched elections, including the race between incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  that could determine which political party controls the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A rematch from the 2018 electoral showdown between incumbent Republican governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and Democratic challenger \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is also being watched nationwide on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 When Jeff Zapor showed up at his polling place in South Lyon, in the Detroit suburbs, on Tuesday, the most pressing race on his mind was the contest for secretary of state, the elected official who oversees voting and elections in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 That in itself is extraordinary. Long overlooked downballot races, there has been an enormous amount of attention on secretary of state races since the 2020 election, when their role in overseeing vote counting came into focus as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to overturn the election. Michigan is one of several states where the Republican nominee for secretary of state questioned and tried to overturned the results of the 2020 race.\par \par \uc2 Standing outside his polling place, an elementary school, Michigan, Zapor said he voted for \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2 , a Democrat, who is running for a second term. She leads her opponent Kristina Karamo, a community college professor, in the polls.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think election deniers on the ballot is a very dangerous thing,\u8221\'20\'1D Zapor, a 46-year-old mental health counselor said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you\u8217\'20\'19re running on a platform of complete abject falsehoods, to me, that shows a complete lack of character. And you\u8217\'20\'19re running for the exact wrong reason.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zapor added that he was concerned that there could be a repeat of efforts to overturn the election, like there were in 2020.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s a certainty. I\u8217\'20\'19m very concerned. Both in Michigan and in the nation, in 2024, I guess even in this election, will continue to be divisive and to see violence would not surprise me. I really hope I\u8217\'20\'19m wrong, but that\u8217\'20\'19s what I think,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 South Lyon is a competitive area in Oakland county, a battleground in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Another voter, who would only give his middle name, Alex, said he was also deeply concerned about election denialism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m concerned in general that the truth in general has eluded us and many will continue to leverage what happened in 2020 and for false information in general,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Another voter, who would only give his first name, Tom, said he voted for Michigan governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, because she was the \u8220\'20\'1Clesser of two evils.\u8221\'20\'1D He said he also voted for Benson, who sees the state\u8217\'20\'19s motor vehicle offices in addition to elections, because he recently had a quick appointment renewing his driver\u8217\'20\'19s license.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe did what she said he was going to do,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, students are coming out for Slotkin in Lansing, whether they like her or not.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t like much about Slotkin, but everyone else was worse,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kathryne Ford, 26, a biochemistry PHD candidate at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  outside of the Don Johnson Fieldhouse this morning. \uc2 Ford\uc2  called Slotkin a \u8220\'20\'1Ccop\u8221\'20\'1D, with reference to her career as a CIA analyst, but lists \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as her top concern this election cycle. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a human right. The government should not be able to control what happens to your own body,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Camille Mikolas, 25, wore braids and a big smile as she went to cast her vote for Slotkin. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to have a baby!\u8221\'20\'1D she laughed. Talking about her support for the referendum looking to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s state constitution, she added \u8220\'20\'1CConservatives are pretty good at saying my body my choice when it comes to vaccines or whatever, but not when it comes to this. And I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that\u8217\'20\'19s fair.\u8221\'20\'1D Mikolas said she\u8217\'20\'19ll be voting Democrat down the ticket today.\par \par \uc2 Camille Mikolas. Photograph: Poppy Noor/The Guardian\par \par \uc2 But the issue runs both ways for other voters. \u8220\'20\'1CI like Elissa,\u8221\'20\'1D said Belinda Fitzpatrick, 59, a swing voter. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I voted against her. My biggest issue is the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  thing. People are just using it like contraception,\u8221\'20\'1D said Fitzpatrick, who said she will also be voting against the proposal to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Fitzpatrick will vote Republican down the ticket, as Michiganders also choose their governor and attorney general today. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s time for Republicans to have the power back,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump briefly stopped to speak with reporters outside the polling place, confirming that he did vote for incumbent Florida governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Republican.\par \par \uc2 enltrFormer President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  confirmed that he voted for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 reelection after he and Melania exit a polling station in Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYes I did.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ci05YZ9bLS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ci05YZ9bLS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Gabby Orr (@GabbyOrr_) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/GabbyOrr_/status/1590023598793961472?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump casts vote in Florida\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has cast his vote during today\u8217\'20\'19s midterm election, with photos captured of him and Melania Trump outside a Florida polling place.\par \par \uc2 Donald and Melania Trump walk together after voting in Palm Beach, Florida. Photograph: Joe Raedle/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Over in Kentucky, Ona Marshall, who is a co-owner of one of the last two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics left in Kentucky, said the line was overflowing at the polling location where she went to vote today in Louisville, around 11am.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot even in a presidential year have we seen that number of people, and this is mid-morning,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Of course, there\u8217\'20\'19s no way of knowing whether those voters were out in favor of or against Amendment 2, the ballot proposal looking to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kentucky. But Marshall said she is hopeful.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhatever happens, for our country and democracy, it\u8217\'20\'19s extremely important that we have a higher turnout at the polls for every election, so to see it in a midterm election is definitely hopeful,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Maggie Acosta, an organizer door-knocking for Democratic candidates with the hospitality \uc2 union Unite Here\uc2 , noted she has heard from many fellow Arizonans who are outraged over the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s recent decision to overturn Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of going forward, I\u8217\'20\'19m being pushed back,\u8221\'20\'1D Acosta said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo it\u8217\'20\'19s time that we stand up and fight for what we deserve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The results of today\u8217\'20\'19s election could have sweeping implications for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Arizona, which has a pre-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban still on the books. If Democratic gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  cannot win today, Republican lawmakers may move to enforce that law, which has become the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/abortion-rights-advocates-midterm-elections-impact-access-arizona/story?id=92298559"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subject of court battles}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  touched on those stakes yesterday, when she participated in a virtual reception with \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  organizers. Acosta had the chance to pose a question to Harris, and she asked the vice-president for her message to young women who are devastated by the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKnow your power,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris replied. \u8220\'20\'1CSo many of our movements that have been about progress have been led by young leaders, and we need them now. We need them to lead.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Carlisa Johnson reports for the Guardian in Georgia:\par \par \uc2 Georgia voters will decide between Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and the incumbent Republican governor, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . This race has received immense national attention as it set the stage for the rematch of Abrams and Kemp this election season. However, the gubernatorial race isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only race receiving national attention. Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s Senate race could determine which party holds power next year in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s capital. Current Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is facing off against controversial Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Coleman Williams, a voter in Avondale Estates, Georgia, said he feels the weight of this election as a Georgia voter. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve watched the debates, and there\u8217\'20\'19s just so much at stake for everyone,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m feeling nervous but hopeful because Georgia knows that we have to get out there, and we clearly have.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrColeman Williams, a Dekalb County resident, says he\u8217\'20\'19s voting because he feels the weight of this election.\u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve watched the debates and there\u8217\'20\'19s just so much at stake for everyone. I\u8217\'20\'19m feeling nervous but hopeful because Georgia knows that we have to get out there and we have.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/cTPP6gyoFq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/cTPP6gyoFq}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Carlisa Johnson (@CarlisaNJohnson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CarlisaNJohnson/status/1590003315123060737?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Maggie Acosta has been knocking on the doors of her Arizona neighbors for months, trying to convince them to support Democrats in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Acosta, a member of the hospitality \uc2 union Unite Here\uc2 , has participated in campaign work since 2017, and she said the stakes feel particularly high this time around.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is very important \u8211\'20\'13 not only for me, but for my future, my kids, my grandchild,\u8221\'20\'1D Acosta said.\par \par \uc2 This election cycle, \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s organizers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/29/unite-here-voter-drive-democrats-us-midterms-arizona-nevada-pennsylvania"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have been on the ground}}}\uc2  in four battleground states \u8211\'20\'13 Arizona, Georgia, Nevada and Pennsylvania. Collectively, the union\u8217\'20\'19s members have knocked on 2.7 million doors, including 750,000 in Acosta\u8217\'20\'19s home state of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work builds on their momentum from 2020, when the union knocked on 3 million doors after developing a contactless canvassing plan to keep organizers safe from coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 Acosta, a cancer survivor, had her concerns about door-knocking in 2020, but she chose to do so in part because of her outrage over Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 handling of the pandemic. Now she has returned to her post, in the hopes of electing Senator Mark Kelly and gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , both of whom are locked in closely contested races.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo house is going to be left unknocked,\u8221\'20\'1D Acosta said. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like being out there and talking to people face to face is the only way that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to make a difference.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Typically, a party\u8217\'20\'19s soul searching begins after the polls close and the results are in. But not this year.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, anticipating heavy losses after a seesaw election season, were already pointing fingers.\par \par \uc2 It began weeks ago. In an op-ed for the Guardian, Vermont senator Bernie Sanders, the party\u8217\'20\'19s progressive standard-bearer, blamed Democrats for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/commentisfree/2022/oct/10/democrats-midterms-victory-economy-bernie-sanders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failing to deliver a strong economic message}}}\uc2  that addresses the pocketbook concerns of most Americans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn my view,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote, \u8220\'20\'1Cit would be political malpractice for Democrats to ignore the state of the economy and allow Republican lies and distortions to go unanswered.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, many top Democrats say their party mishandled the issue of crime and public safety.\par \par \uc2 Stanley Greenberg, a veteran Democratic pollster, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prospect.org/politics/how-democrats-mishandled-crime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued}}}\uc2  in the American Prospect that the party had badly mishandled the issue of crime and public safety, writing that the 2022 midterms would be remembered \u8220\'20\'1Cas a toxic campaign, but an effective one in labeling Democrats as \u8216\'20\'18pro-crime.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But others say Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 challengers are much deeper. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thirdway.org/memo/overcoming-the-democratic-party-brand"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 policy memo}}}\uc2  released on the eve of the election, the centrist Democratic think-tank Third Way concluded that the party\u8217\'20\'19s brand had become toxic to swing voters. \u8220\'20\'1CDespite a roster of GOP candidates who are extreme by any standard, voters see Democrats as just as extreme, as well as far less concerned about the issues that most worry them,\u8221\'20\'1D it stated.\par \par \uc2 Progressives wholeheartedly disagree. A pre-election memo from the \uc2 Working Families Party\uc2  national director, Maurice Mitchell, credits progressives for pushing Biden to deliver on key parts of his economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats\u8217\'20\'19 passing big and popular legislation and Republican extremism have kept many races much closer than the normal midterm patterns would dictate,\u8221\'20\'1D Mitchell wrote. But he warned: \u8220\'20\'1CIf Democrats face significant losses, those failures of governance and the Democrats who blocked a pro-working-class agenda will bear much of the blame.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s just after 12pm here in New York, which means that voting is well under way in most places across the country. Here\u8217\'20\'19s a look at what\u8217\'20\'19s happening at polling locations across the US.\par \par \uc2 Tucker, Virginia. Photograph: Ben Gray/AP Yukon, Oklahoma. Photograph: Chris Landsberger/AP Las Vegas, Nevada. Photograph: Mario Tama/Getty Images Detroit, Michigan. Photograph: Rebecca Cook/Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In Columbus, Ohio, Jeffrey Weisman voted for the bestselling author and Republican candidate for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , JD Vance. But not with great enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI vote Republican pretty much all the way and that is my main reason why,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Vance has a slim lead over his Democratic rival, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , in a state that has come to give large majorities to Republican candidates. The tight race in part reflects the strength of Ryan\u8217\'20\'19s campaign for a seat that may decide whether Democrats retain control of the Senate. But it\u8217\'20\'19s also a reflection of voter doubts about Vance\u8217\'20\'19s sincerity after he dramatically shifted from slagging off Trump as a \u8220\'20\'1Cfraud\u8221\'20\'1D and a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral disaster\u8221\'20\'1D to becoming a fervent supporter in order to win his endorsement in the Republican primaries.\par \par \uc2 Weisman, who owns a retail jewellery store, said it didn\u8217\'20\'19t matter that Vance was endorsed by former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI like the Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 stuff when it comes more to the economy. I\u8217\'20\'19m a business owner and I feel that things are not going in the right direction with the Democrats in charge. I\u8217\'20\'19m hoping that maybe the Republicans in charge will get things going a little better economy wise,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Weisman twice voted for Trump in the presidential elections. But with the former president apparently on the brink of announcing another run for the White House, Weisman would prefer he stayed out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a tough one. I like his politics. His mouth scares a lot of people. So I personally do not think he can win because of the mouth, the controversialness of him. And so I think that would be a tough road for him,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 enltrIn Columbus, Ohio, jewellery store owner Jeffrey Weisman voted Republican in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/midterms?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #midterms}}}\uc2 . But he's not looking forward to Trump attempting a comeback: "It's a tough one. I like his politics. His mouth scares a lot of people. So I personally do not think he can win." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/hAi0Gdl1rK"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/hAi0Gdl1rK}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris McGreal (@ChrisMcGreal) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ChrisMcGreal/status/1590010891604733953?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dani Anguiano is reporting for the Guardian in Las Vegas:\par \par \uc2 The fate of the Senate could lie in the hands of voters in Nevada where incumbent Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is locked in a close race with \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican former state attorney general who aided in efforts to overturn \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the state.\par \par \uc2 The contest between \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , the first Latina senator and successor to the late Democratic leader Harry Reid, and Laxalt has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/polls/senate/2022/nevada/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 neck and neck.}}}\uc2  Laxalt has received the endorsement of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , while \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/13/republican-adam-laxalt-opposed-14-family-members-nevada-senate-cortez-masto"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14 members of Laxalt\u8217\'20\'19s family}}}\uc2  have backed Cortez Masto. The race, said to be among the closest of this year\u8217\'20\'19s contests, is crucial for Democrats to maintain control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in Henderson, Nevada, on 6 November. Photograph: Steve Marcus/AP\par \par \uc2 Laxalt has sought to appeal to voters by focusing on the economy, which more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/poll-economy-dominates-priority-list-for-nevada-voters-biden-remains-unpopular"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in four}}}\uc2  voters have said is the most important issue facing the state, and tying \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thenevadaindependent.com/article/poll-economy-dominates-priority-list-for-nevada-voters-biden-remains-unpopular"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 low approval rates}}}\uc2  in Nevada. Democrats rallying for the senator have critisized Laxalt for his opposition to reproductive freedom and argued he is in the pocket of big oil as the state sees record high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo you see, gas prices going up, grocery prices going up \u8211\'20\'13 that takes a bite out of your paycheck. It\u8217\'20\'19s no joke. It hurts. The question, though, you should be asking is \u8211\'20\'13 who\u8217\'20\'19s going to actually try to do something about it?\u8221\'20\'1D Obama said at an event for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CShe hasn\u8217\'20\'19t forgotten where she came from. Which is why she\u8217\'20\'19s gone after big oil for high gas prices, why she takes on pharmaceutical companies to lower prescription drug costs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  has the backing of the local Culinary Workers Union, which is running a campaign to knock on one million doors by election day. Canvassers with the union say the voters they talk to are concerned about growing housing costs and inflation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone is working side hustles to be able to live a normal life,\u8221\'20\'1D said Arlett Tovar, a canvasser with the union. \u8220\'20\'1CVegas used to be such a great affordable place to live and it\u8217\'20\'19s not anymore.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sam Levine is reporting for the Guardian in Detroit, Michigan:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m here at Greater Grace temple, a polling site in north-west Detroit. About 400-500 people have voted here so far, the polling site manager told me. Many voters have already cast their ballots by mail, the manager said.\par \par \uc2 There were some glitches with the e-poll books used to check in voters this morning, but those have been fixed. Among other issues, the system was showing that some voters had already cast a mail-in ballot. She also said there haven\u8217\'20\'19t been any incidents involving intimidation or poll watchers, which has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/08/02/michigan-poll-911-gop-00049332"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a big concern in Michigan}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Xhosoli Nmumhad, 35, said she\u8217\'20\'19s only voted twice before, once in 2008 and then again in 2012 for Barack Obama. But she said she kept seeing political commercials on television and was motivated to vote on Tuesday to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/07/michigan-proposal-2-voting-access-overturning-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support a constitutional amendment}}}\uc2  on the ballot that would dramatically expand voting rights in Michigan. \u8220\'20\'1CI believe everyone should be able to vote,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 A voter casts their ballot in Detroit on 8 November. Photograph: Brandon Bell/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 Ruth Draines, 72, another voter here, said she always voted in elections. This cycle, she said she was motivated by another proposal on the ballot that would amend the state constitution {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/31/michigan-abortion-proposal-3-campaign"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to protect}}}\uc2  access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t like the fact that they want to take away a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right. Because some women get raped and they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be reminded of that,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. So basically that\u8217\'20\'19s one of the big motivator.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Draines said she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t particularly concerned this cycle about efforts to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think that\u8217\'20\'19s all just hype,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She added that she thinks Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat on the ballot for re-election today, has been doing a \u8220\'20\'1Cpretty good job\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just hopes she keeps up the good work,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Residents of a New Orleans suburb who were casting ballots for the midterm elections Tuesday were navigating more than their fair share of hurdles to exercise their right to vote.\par \par \uc2 A bomb threat to a school in Kenner, Louisiana, which was a polling site for a couple of precincts in the city of about 65,000 people, on Tuesday morning forced vote workers to relocate the site, John Tobler \u8211\'20\'13 a spokesperson for the state\u8217\'20\'19s top elections official \u8211\'20\'13 told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 The threat to the Kenner Discovery school displaced the polling site set up there to the campus of Audubon elementary about a mile and a half away, Tobler said.\par \par \uc2 enltr?? Kenner Discovery School received a bomb threat earlier this morning. Kenner Police are on scene investigating and trying to determine if this will be related to a bomb threat the school received on Thursday, November 3, 2022. ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/A1q9ZxzbAS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/A1q9ZxzbAS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kenner PD (@KennerPolice) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KennerPolice/status/1590013448292773888?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Kenner\u8217\'20\'19s police chief, Keith Conley, confirmed to the Guardian that officers were investigating a bomb threat to Kenner Discovery, which was closed for class to allow to the school to serve as a polling site. But officials said there was no indication the threat, which affected more than 2,500 voters across two precincts, was politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s top elections official, the secretary of state R \uc2 Kyle Ardoin\uc2 , said the early findings of an investigation into the threat suggested the motive was unrelated to politics.\par \par \uc2 Conley added that it was the second similar threat to Kenner Discovery in the last five days. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wdsu.com/article/kenner-discovery-bomb-threat-evacuation/41857790"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Local reports }}}\uc2  said the earlier threat on 3 November \u8211\'20\'13 which came in when there were no election-related activities at the school \u8211\'20\'13 demanded payment in the form of the cryptocurrency Bitcoin.\par \par \uc2 Though it has a significant population of Black and Latino residents, Kenner\u8217\'20\'19s white majority tends to overwhelmingly side with Republican political candidates. Much of the city \u8211\'20\'13 including the Kenner Discovery polling site \u8211\'20\'13 sits in the congressional district of the Republican House whip, Steve Scalise, who is projected to cruise to re-election in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterms.\par \par \uc2 Officials nationwide have been concerned about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/06/how-close-is-the-us-to-civil-war-barbara-f-walter-stephen-march-christopher-parker"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 political violence}}}\uc2  in general in recent years after cases such as the US Capitol insurrection on 6 January 2021 and the attack that left House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s husband, Paul Pelosi, critically injured on 28 October.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s threat at Kenner Discovery came after Ardoin\u8217\'20\'19s office announced that a mobile app, an online portal and a telephone hotline containing polling place and ballot information for Louisiana voters were all \u8220\'20\'1Cexperiencing technical difficulties\u8221\'20\'1D within hours of the polls opening Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Ardoin, a Republican, later said the technical issues had been resolved.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Carlisa Johnson reports for the Guardian in Georgia:\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, election day has started without issue. Voters are experiencing limited wait times across the state as they vote in person. Many attribute this to the record-breaking early voting the state saw last month. Still, Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s polling locations are seeing a steady stream of concerned voters making their voices heard in this critical election.\par \par \uc2 Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s secretary of state {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/gabrielsterling/status/1589970084453584898?s=42&t=lBom_BgMC1e1_I9AFl3_zA%20https://twitter.com/gabrielsterling/status/1589970084453584898?s=42&t=lBom_BgMC1e1_I9AFl3_zA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  reports}}}\uc2  that Georgians are accessing their voter information in record numbers. This morning, voters accessed their \u8220\'20\'1CMy Voter Page\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 which confirms a voter\u8217\'20\'19s registration and polling location \u8211\'20\'13 on the Secretary of State website at a rate of 19,215 users a minute.\par \par \uc2 People wait in line to cast their ballots at Central Baptist church in Columbus, Georgia. Photograph: Cheney Orr/Reuters\par \par \uc2 SB202, Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s new election law, eliminated access to provisional ballots for voters who go to the wrong polling location on election day. Voting advocates throughout the state are working diligently to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/gcpagenda/status/1590007818392080385?s=46&t=lBom_BgMC1e1_I9AFl3_zA%20https://twitter.com/gcpagenda/status/1590007818392080385?s=46&t=lBom_BgMC1e1_I9AFl3_zA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  remind voters}}}\uc2  of this change and the importance of confirming their polling locations.\par \par \uc2 Mariah Headman is a Dekalb county resident and a Georgia teacher. While she has voted absentee in the past, voting in person on election day was more convenient for her this year. She says she is voting with her students in mind. \u8220\'20\'1CFor me, voting means the difference between well-funded public schools or underfunded ones,\u8221\'20\'1D said Headman. \u8220\'20\'1CThis election is huge, and Georgia has to pull through.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrMariah Headman is one of Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s dedicated teachers. She says she is voting in-person this morning for her students. \u8220\'20\'1CThis election is huge and Georgia is pulling through. For me, voting means the difference between well funded public schools or underfunded ones,\u8221\'20\'1D said Headman. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1qZ6gPoPCK"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/1qZ6gPoPCK}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Carlisa Johnson (@CarlisaNJohnson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CarlisaNJohnson/status/1590002042159759362?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Outside a voting station at the largest Greek Orthodox church in Ohio, Ashley Sica said her vote for the Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in the election for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  was decided by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruling striking down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI voted based off of my values, and maybe not necessarily what I would do, but just thinking globally of choices that other people should be able to have. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that government officials should be in charge what people do with their own bodies,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Sica, a nurse, said that the removal of the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  motivated a lot of women to vote in the midterms who might not usually turn out.\par \par \uc2 Sica said she was also frightened by the Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 blocking of tighter gun control laws even after a series of school massacres.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy children\u8217\'20\'19s daycare is just a mile from here. There was an issue with someone shooting a gun around their daycare. So that\u8217\'20\'19s another thing that kind of brought me out to vote for stricter laws in regards to guns. Having kids that are of school age now really brings that kind of thing into focus, thinking about their safety and the safety of others,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 enltrIn Columbus, Ohio - Ashley Sica, a nurse, said her vote for the Democrat in the state's \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race was decided by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruling striking down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional right: "Government officials shouldn't be in charge of what people do with their bodies". {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/a3EumS3Y29"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/a3EumS3Y29}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris McGreal (@ChrisMcGreal) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ChrisMcGreal/status/1590005382843629570?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  election in Pennsylvania is expected to be one of the closest in the country, as \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the state\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic lieutenant governor, takes on Mehmet Oz, a Republican celebrity doctor.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman held {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/polls/senate/2022/pennsylvania/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a commanding lead}}}\uc2  in the polls for months, but in the days ahead of the vote Mehmet Oz has closed the gap, and the race appears to be a toss-up. Fetterman had a stroke in May, and continues to have issues with speaking and processing the spoken word \u8211\'20\'13 something that was laid bare in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/25/us/politics/fetterman-oz-debate-senate-pa.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a difficult debate performance}}}\uc2  two weeks ago. Fetterman and his team have insisted he is fit and able to work, but his health and recovery has been cruelly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/23/dr-oz-aide-john-fetterman-stroke-pennsylvania"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mocked}}}\uc2  by Oz\u8217\'20\'19s campaign.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI liked Fetterman, except for the man had a stroke,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steve Schwartz, who had just cast his vote for Oz in Beaver county, 30 miles north-west of Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t even know if he can drive to work yet. You don\u8217\'20\'19t wanna hire him and then he\u8217\'20\'19s going to be on disability for a little bit. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think his health allows him to do what he\u8217\'20\'19s doing right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Signs for Oz and Fetterman are seen side by side in Pennsylvania. Photograph: Tracie van Auken/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 Schwartz, who also voted for the Republican candidates for governor and the US House, said he would have \u8220\'20\'1Cseriously considered\u8221\'20\'1D Fetterman, were it not for the stroke.\par \par \uc2 Beaver county, named after the Beaver River, which is named after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.timesonline.com/story/news/2015/02/25/native-american-heritage-behind-names/18515341007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 either}}}\uc2  the Lenape chief King Beaver or the animal the beaver, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/2020-election/results/pennsylvania/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted for Trump}}}\uc2  in 2020 and 2016, but Trump\u8217\'20\'19s margin of victory was smaller here than in other counties.\par \par \uc2 Mike Moore, a 41-year-old loan closer, said he had voted for Fetterman for Senate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI like the way he is, I\u8217\'20\'19ve met him a couple of times, he seems like a real genuine guy. I kind of don\u8217\'20\'19t like Dr. Oz, because he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t live in Pennsylvania \u8211\'20\'13 and that\u8217\'20\'19s kind of like: \u8216\'20\'18How can he represent me?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Moore said, referencing Oz\u8217\'20\'19s long-term residency in New Jersey. Oz lived in a mansion in New Jersey for decades, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/crudits-snooki-john-fetterman-voters-remember-dr-ozs/story?id=87765213"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has said}}}\uc2  he moved to Pennsylvania in late 2020.\par \par \uc2 The most important issue for Moore, he said, was \u8220\'20\'1Cbipartisanship\u8221\'20\'1D, something that seems unlikely to spring from this midterm cycle.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis country is so polarized now, it\u8217\'20\'19s a shame. You know, we got to work together. We got to be Americans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 This midterm election is already the most expensive in history, and depending on how today goes, it could get even more pricey.\par \par \uc2 Spending on this election season has exceeded $16.7bn across federal and local candidates \u8211\'20\'13 over $2bn more than the 2018 midterms. Federal candidates have spent over $8.9bn while state candidates, party committees and ballot measure committees have spent $7.8bn, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opensecrets.org/news/2022/11/total-cost-of-2022-state-and-federal-elections-projected-to-exceed-16-7-billion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 OpenSecrets}}}\uc2 , which tracks campaign spending.\par \par \uc2 The arrival of election day doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean the spending will stop, especially if runoff elections have to take place. The race for a Georgia Senate seat between incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a race that has been riddled with controversy \u8211\'20\'13 could lead to a runoff in December if neither candidate gets over 50% of the vote. Both parties have already spent over $241m on the race \u8211\'20\'13 what the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/a-quarter-billion-dollars-for-the-walker-warnock-georgia-senate-race-buys-a-toss-up-11667748797"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal}}}\uc2  calculated is $30.83 for each of the state\u8217\'20\'19s 7.8 million registered voters.\par \par \uc2 enltrHappy Election Day from Georgia, where the big question is whether voters get one more day of these types of ads or one more month of them. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RuJRcqgq8g"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RuJRcqgq8g}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sahil Kapur (@sahilkapur) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sahilkapur/status/1589994642019581952?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, one of the most important races for Republican leaders is to take full control of the state supreme court. Control because candidates for the bench run not on their judicial qualifications but on party tickets with an implicit commitment to make decisions in line with their political values.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have technically controlled the court since 1986, holding four of the seven seats. But one of the judges, the outgoing chief justice, Maureen O\u8217\'20\'19Connor, is widely regarded by the party leadership as having gone rogue by siding with Democrats in some politically sensitive cases including over gerrymandering. And now the highly contentious issues of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be on the agenda following the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decision overturning Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 The three conservative candidates, already on the court and seeking reelection, in recent days joined a Republican bus campaign tour alongside the party\u8217\'20\'19s contenders for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and state governor. No separation of powers there.\par \par \uc2 At a stop in Columbus, one of the judges, Pat Fischer, told a rally that it was important to keep the court out of the hands of \u8220\'20\'1Cactivist judges that are Democrats\u8221\'20\'1D. Not activist judges as such, just ones who are Democrats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s critical that the three of us win this race and we go back to having a conservative Ohio supreme court,\u8221\'20\'1D he told the rally.\par \par \uc2 The new state supreme court is expected to decide whether to let stand the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond six weeks of pregnancy, brought in after Roe v Wade was overturned. A lower court blocked the measure last month. Although the three Republican contenders for the court all say they will apply the law and not political judgements, each has already made clear their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another of the Republican judges, Sharon Kennedy, is running to become chief justice. She has indicated that she would not enforce previous court rulings to stop Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s Republican legislature from gerrymandering elections.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Florida: federal election monitors won't be permitted in polling places\par \par \uc2 The Florida state department sent a letter to the \uc2 US Department of Justice\uc2  saying that federal election monitors won\u8217\'20\'19t be permitted inside the state\u8217\'20\'19s polling places.\par \par \uc2 enltrFlorida Department of State to DOJ:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cfederal election \u8216\'20\'18monitors\u8217\'20\'19 are not permitted inside polling places as it would be counterproductive and could potentially undermine confidence in the election.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/I3TdaozUxG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/I3TdaozUxG}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jason Delgado (@byJasonDelgado) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byJasonDelgado/status/1589861017622048771?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Florida\u8217\'20\'19s state department said that the monitors \u8220\'20\'1Cwould be counterproductive and could potentially undermine confidence in the election\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The justice department regularly sends monitors to states on Election Day in an attempt to curb voter intimidation at polling places. Yesterday, the department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-monitor-polls-24-states-compliance-federal-voting-rights-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  it would send out workers to 64 jurisdictions across 24 states. In 2020, the department sent out workers to 44 jurisdictions. The states include key swing districts in Arizona, Michigan and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMonitors will include personnel from the Civil Rights Division and from US attorneys\u8217\'20\'19 offices,\u8221\'20\'1D the department said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Florida\u8217\'20\'19s state department said the DOJ did not \u8220\'20\'1Cdetail the need for federal monitors\u8221\'20\'1D in the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNone of the counties are currently subject to any election-related federal consent decrees. None of the counties have been accused of violating the rights of language or racial minorities or of the elderly or disabled,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 While Louisiana isn\u8217\'20\'19t expected to see many surprises during today\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections, voters there were among those enduring technical difficulties to cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of state, R \uc2 Kyle Ardoin\uc2 , Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s top election official, said a mobile app, an online portal and a telephone hotline containing polling place and ballot information were all \u8220\'20\'1Cexperiencing technical difficulties\u8221\'20\'1D within hours of the polls opening Tuesday. Ardoin, a Republican, by midday said the technical problems had been resolved.\par \par \uc2 Before the issues were taken care of, Ardoin had directed residents to call the registrar of voters office in their parish \u8211\'20\'13 which is the term Louisiana uses for county \u8211\'20\'13 or to email his office at elections@sos.la.gov to ask any necessary questions.\par \par \uc2 Ardoin\u8217\'20\'19s announcement came after officials in Virginia reported technical difficulties with equipment that verifies\u8217\'20\'19 voter information and poll staff in Texas said they were having problems with check-in machines.\par \par \uc2 The Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s whip in the \uc2 US House of Representatives\uc2 , Steve Scalise, is among those up for re-election in Louisiana on Tuesday. So is Republican US Senator \uc2 John N Kennedy\uc2 . Both are expected to cruise to re-election.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Outside the Eastern high school athletic club in Lansing, Michigan on a small patch of neatly trimmed grass, \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a congresswoman running in the most expensive house race in the country, spoke to reporters. She cast her vote alongside a steady stream of voters around 9am.\par \par \uc2 Slotkin seemed disturbed but prepared for attempts already being made to undermine election results in Michigan \u8211\'20\'13 on Monday, a judge dismissed an attempt by Republicans to toss out votes in Detroit as lacking a \u8220\'20\'1Cshred of evidence\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is what happens when a leadership climate is set in our country, trying to undercut democracy when one side loses. It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear what my opponent will do if he loses. The good news is, we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen this movie before \u8211\'20\'13 in 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 we were prepared,\u8221\'20\'1D said Slotkin.\par \par \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  speaks in Lansing on 1 November. Photograph: Rebecca Cook/Reuters\par \par \uc2 She said it was undeniable that inflation has been on everybody\u8217\'20\'19s mind in Michigan, but added that ballot initiative to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Michigan is a \u8220\'20\'1Ccountervailing wind\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was at the Michigan State rally last night with campus organizers, and Roe v Wade is really motivating students,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A few polling places have reported technical issues this morning. The Virginia elections department confirmed that there were issues with polling books, which verifies voters\u8217\'20\'19 information. The department said the issue has been resolved, though one poll worker told Kelly Avellino of local news station NBC12 that they were still have issues with their poll books.\par \par \uc2 enltrA rep from the VA Dept. of Elections did confirm that there have been issues with polling books this morning (computers which verify voters when they come in), not the actual voting machines that accept the ballots.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kelly Avellino NBC12 (@KellyAvellino) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KellyAvellino/status/1589970726735740930?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 In Temple, Texas, some elections staff noticed technical issues with their check-in machines.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt this time, it is possible that some sites will experience delays this morning, but election officials are confident that the problem has been isolated and should be resolved quickly,\u8221\'20\'1D James Stafford, a local official, said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tdtnews.com/news/central_texas_news/article_1b2779dc-5f69-11ed-8c9a-ab5373d5d9dd.html?utm_campaign=blox&utm_medium=social"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 news release}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elections departments are under heavy pressure to ensure smooth sailing at the polls today as some conservatives have essentially said they will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/02/trump-playbook-us-midterms-republicans-election-denial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fight election results}}}\uc2  if they lose, taking a page from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2020 playbook. Reports of voter and election workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/04/voter-intimidation-midterm-elections-arizona"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 intimidation}}}\uc2  have also been seen recently, like people video-taping ballot drop-off boxes and tabulation offices in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://circle.tufts.edu/latest-research/more-8-million-youth-are-newly-eligible-voters-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  8.3 million young Americans are eligible to vote for the first time this election. The question remains how many of them \u8211\'20\'13 and other young voters \u8211\'20\'13 will turn out to the polls.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to younger voters, the stakes are higher for Democrats than Republicans: 57% of young Americans between the ages of 18 and 29 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/democrats-should-be-worried-about-the-youth-vote/2022/11/05/91864eac-5d0a-11ed-bc40-b5a130f95ee7_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 favor}}}\uc2  a Democrat-controlled Congress over Republican control. But younger voters also tend to vote less than older voters (though more {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://circle.tufts.edu/latest-research/half-youth-voted-2020-11-point-increase-2016"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  in 2020 than ever before).\par \par \uc2 Early data has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/10/28/young-voters-dem-early-00063929"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shown}}}\uc2  that young voters have been turning out in smaller fractions compared to the 2020 election. But young voters are less likely to vote early or by mail, so it\u8217\'20\'19s too early to tell exactly what their turnout will be today.\par \par \uc2 Still, amid the uncertainty, some young voters have already visited the polls today. One mom in Georgia, Christy Houchins, an immigrant from Laos who became a citizen, brought her daughter to the polls today, according to Greg Bluestein of the Atlanta Journal Constitution.\par \par \uc2 enltrYour {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ajc?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ajc}}}\uc2  reporters are all over Georgia talking to voters. Christy Houchins, an immigrant from Laos who is now a citizen, brought her 18-year-old daughter to vote. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we want change, we have to use our voice,\u8221\'20\'1D she told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/taylor_rcroft?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @taylor_rcroft}}}\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/gapol?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #gapol}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/gasen?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #gasen}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/6ieK1utfcn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/6ieK1utfcn}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Greg Bluestein (@bluestein) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/bluestein/status/1589967571905675264?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 8, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 This is Lauren Aratani taking over for Martin Belam.\par \par \uc2 More polls have opened up around the country, and many voters have already casted their ballots this morning. Here are some pictures that have been taken at polling places around the country as election day gets rolling.\par \par \uc2 Sandy Springs, Georgia. Photograph: Alex Wong/Getty Images Freedom, Ohio. Photograph: David Maxwell/EPA Phoenix, Arizona. Photograph: Brian Snyder/Reuters Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. Photograph: Mike Segar/Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In the past, midterm elections have been kind of predictable. The president is slightly unpopular, his party loses some seats and most voters disappear for two years until they turn up in greater numbers for the main show.\par \par \uc2 This year might not buck any of those long-term trends. The Democratic president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has watched his popularity {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/06/briefing/joe-biden-approval-rating-covid.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slump}}}\uc2  from 53% to 42% over the past year. Polls suggest that Republicans will win the House of Representatives and might even win the Senate too (though if they do, it\u8217\'20\'19s likely to be by a narrower margin). And even though turnout is looking set to be historically {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/23/us-midterm-elections-early-voting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high}}}\uc2  by midterm standards, it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely to beat the numbers in 2020. So, it\u8217\'20\'19s all pretty much electoral business as usual, right?\par \par \uc2 Not quite. Here are some of the factors that could shape the votes that come in over the next few days \u8211\'20\'13 and, as a result, shape the country over the next few years.\par \par \uc2 Redistricting\par \par \uc2 Under Trump, gerrymandering and other anti-democratic measures scaled up. There have been concerns about the way that kind of partisan redistricting might play out in this year\u8217\'20\'19s elections \u8211\'20\'13 where the electoral district is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/24/gerrymandering-wisconsin-purple-state-rightwing-measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 purple}}}\uc2 , this is likely to work in Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 favor including parts of Texas and Florida.\par \par \uc2 But, as the \uc2 New York Times{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/30/upshot/midterms-gerrymandering-republicans.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pointed out}}}\uc2  in September, this might not wind up being such a huge advantage for Republicans. While it\u8217\'20\'19s true that the map does work in their favor, the edge isn\u8217\'20\'19t insurmountable for Democrats (and it certainly isn\u8217\'20\'19t irreversible either).\par \par \uc2 Restrictions\par \par \uc2 In the space of just six months, 22 new laws were passed in 2021 that restricted people\u8217\'20\'19s access to vote in 14 states. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/voting-laws-roundup-may-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Research}}}\uc2  from the Brennan centre has indicated that voter suppression is at its worst level in over a decade. Whether those laws make it hard to count mail-in ballots or make it difficult to vote in person, their overall effect tends to disenfranchise those who are already disempowered by other systems in the country \u8211\'20\'13 whether it\u8217\'20\'19s people with disabilities or people of color who are more likely to be affected by voter ID laws. It\u8217\'20\'19s also worth pointing out that laws like these are uncommon in most other countries that call themselves democracies.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/dec/27/democracy-under-attack-trump-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 led}}}\uc2  these efforts in the hopes that voter access laws will work in their favor.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the economy\par \par \uc2 Sure, there are the same ideological arguments about immigration and the economy that show up in any election but this year feels different. There is a general atmosphere of distrust fueled by attacks on election officials and lies about \u8220\'20\'1Cstolen\u8221\'20\'1D ballots that has led some to feel that they are voting about democracy itself. And, since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s 1973 Roe v Wade decision was overturned in June, this year\u8217\'20\'19s vote has for many felt like a vote about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Even the economy has taken on a greater urgency than in recent midterms with rising consumer prices squeezing many US household budgets.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, polling is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/commentisfree/2020/nov/04/the-pollsters-were-wrong-again-heres-what-we-know-so-far"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 still}}}\uc2  deeply problematic so all predictions about the House and the Senate should be treated with caution until the votes are counted. A Wall Street Journal survey that found white suburban women are switching parties was based on just 297 respondents (which, shockingly, is actually a pretty good response rate for such a specific slice of the country). That kind of research is not only imprecise but it has the potential to be undemocratic when headlines tell voters what the future looks like before they have even had their say.\par \par \uc2 Read more of Mona Chalabi\u8217\'20\'19s analysis here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/us-midterms-2022-voter-trends-factors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterms 2022: what are the factors shaping voters\u8217\'20\'19 choices?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Officials in Philadelphia have said that counting votes will take longer than expected after a last minute u-turn to reinstate what is known as poll book reconciliation. The process, described as \u8220\'20\'1Ctime consuming and labor-intensive\u8221\'20\'1D by the Philadelphia Inquirer, is used to flag mail ballots submitted by voters who also voted in person.\par \par \uc2 City officials had agreed last week not to use it, but following a flurry of Republican lawsuits at an emergency meeting this morning just as polls opened, they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/philadelphia-election-vote-count-slowed-poll-book-reconciliation-20221108.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reversed the decision}}}\uc2 . It could have an impact on how quickly the Pennsylvania senate race can be called.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Jason Lange is in Washington for Reuters and he has a warning \u8211\'20\'13 be ready for a long night and maybe days of waiting before it is clear whether Republicans or President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Democrats will control Congress.\par \par \uc2 He reports that with dozens of races expected to be close and key states like Pennsylvania already warning it could take days to count every ballot, experts say there\u8217\'20\'19s a good chance America goes to bed on election night without knowing who won.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen it comes to knowing the results, we should move away from talking about election day and think instead about election week,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nathan Gonzales, who publishes the nonpartisan newsletter Inside Elections.\par \par \uc2 Because Democrats vote by mail more often than Republicans, states that let officials get an early jump on counting mail ballots could report big Democratic leads early on that evaporate as vote counters work through piles of Republican-leaning ballots that were cast on election day.\par \par \uc2 In these \u8220\'20\'1Cblue mirage\u8221\'20\'1D states - which include Florida and North Carolina - election officials are allowed to remove mail ballots from their envelopes before election day and load them in vote counting machines, allowing for speedy counting.\par \par \uc2 States including Pennsylvania and Wisconsin don\u8217\'20\'19t allow officials to open the envelopes until election day, leading to a possible \u8220\'20\'1Cred mirage\u8221\'20\'1D in which Republican-leaning election day ballots are reported earlier, with many Democratic-leaning mail ballots counted later.\par \par \uc2 Experts like Joe Lenski, co-founder of Edison Research, which will be tracking hundreds of races on Tuesday and supplying Reuters and other media organizations with results, will keep an eye on the mix of different types of ballots each state is counting throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBlue mirage, red mirage, whatever. You just have to look at what types of votes are getting reported to know where you are in that state,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lenski.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A number of candidates will make history if they prevail in their races today. In particular, the departure of 46 members from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/house-of-representatives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 House of Representatives}}}\uc2  has created an opening for a new class of young and diverse candidates to seek federal office.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell Frost, Democratic congressional candidate for Florida\u8217\'20\'19s district 10. Photograph: Lynne Sladky/AP\par \par \uc2 Two House candidates, Democrat Maxwell Frost of Florida and Republican Karoline Leavitt of New Hampshire, would become the first Gen Z members of Congress if they win their elections. Leavitt would also set a record as the youngest woman ever elected to Congress if she can defeat Democrat \uc2 Chris Pappas\uc2  in their hotly contested race, which is considered a toss-up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/ratings/house-race-ratings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by the Cook Political Report}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire Republican 1st Congressional District candidate Karoline Leavitt. Photograph: Charles Krupa/AP\par \par \uc2 In Vermont, Democrat Becca Balint is favored to win her House race, which would make her the first woman and the first openly LGBTQ+ politician to represent the state in Congress. If Balint wins, all 50 US states will have sent at least one woman to Congress, as Vermont became the sole outlier on that metric in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Some House races will even make history regardless of which party\u8217\'20\'19s candidate prevails. In New York\u8217\'20\'19s third congressional district, either Democrat Robert Zimmerman or Republican George Devolder-Santos will become the first openly gay person to represent Long Island in the House.\par \par \uc2 As Republicans look to take back the House, their playbook has relied upon nominating a diverse slate of candidates in battleground districts that will probably determine control of the lower chamber. The strategy builds upon the party\u8217\'20\'19s momentum from 2020, when Republicans flipped 14 House districts where they nominated a woman or a person of color.\par \par \uc2 Overall, Republicans have nominated 67 candidates of color in House races, according to the National Republican Congressional Committee. Those candidates could allow the party to dramatically expand its ranks of members of color, given that just 19 non-white Republicans serve in the House now. With Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/2022-election-forecast/house/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heavily favored}}}\uc2  to take back the House, many of those candidates of color could join the new session of Congress in January.\par \par \uc2 Read more of Joan E Greve\u8217\'20\'19s report here: Midterm elections \u8211\'20\'13 the candidates who will make history if they win\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/midterm-elections-more-diverse-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Midterm elections: the candidates who will make history if they win}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the earliest images we\u8217\'20\'19ve been sent over the newswires of people voting in-person as polling gets underway in today\u8217\'20\'19s crucial {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-midterm-elections-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 midterm elections}}}\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 Voters walk near a polling location at Rodef Shalom Congregation in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. Photograph: Quinn Glabicki/Reuters Voters wait in line to cast their ballots in the midterm elections in Rydal, Pennsylvania. Photograph: Matt Rourke/AP A local resident waits in line to cast her ballot during the midterm elections at Calvary Baptist Church in Austell, Georgia. Photograph: Carlos Barr\u237\'00\'EDa/Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Paul J Webber and Acacia Coronado have been in Austin, Texas for Associated Press, and report on a trio of US House races in South Texas which carry high stakes. For decades Republicans rarely bothered to compete along the US-Mexico border, but have made the heavily Hispanic region a priority in their pursuit of retaking control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 The unusually competitive atmosphere in South Texas, including for Democratic US Rep Henry Cuellar, underlines the shifting political winds in an important stronghold for Democrats \u8212\'20\'14 and the ramifications beyond this election.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, victories by any of three Latina candidates running for House seats in the region \u8212\'20\'14 Rep Mayra Flores, Monica De La Cruz and Cassy Garcia \u8212\'20\'14 would deepen inroads the Republican party is making nationally with Hispanic voters, and demoralise Democrats in a place that has long been their turf.\par \par \uc2 US Representative Mayra Flores (R) takes a photo with a supporter at a campaign event in Mcallen, Texas in October. Photograph: Allison Dinner/AFP/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 In a sign of Republican optimism, Gov Greg Abbott was hosting his Election Night party in the border city of McAllen, reflecting Republican eagerness to show conservatives are expanding their territory.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans unleashed an aggressive play for South Texas after counties up and down the border swung toward former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020, stunning Democrats who viewed the region safe and creating a new battleground overnight. Millions of dollars have since poured into the region, and all three Republican House candidates out-raised their Democratic opponents this summer.\par \par \uc2 Underscoring the intensity of the races heading into Tuesday, former president \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  swung through South Texas on Monday to campaign for the Democratic party. Big-name Republicans have also swooped into the region, including House minority leader Kevin McCarthy over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The most widely seen competitive race was in the 15th Congressional District, where De La Cruz and Democrat Michelle Vallejo were competing to represent one of the two new US House districts that Texas was awarded last year following the release of new census figures.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and Democratic candidate for Texas' 15th Congressional District Michelle Vallejo in Edinburg, Texas on Monday. Photograph: Delcia Lopez/AP\par \par \uc2 Neither candidate was running to the middle with their policy platforms. De La Cruz has defended \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, promised tougher border security measures and drew praise from Trump during a recent rally in Texas. Vallejo has courted liberal voters in a progressive campaign that has called for a $15 an hour minimum wage and challenged conventional wisdom that Democrats along the border are more moderate.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Andrew Gawthorpe is a historian of the United States at \uc2 Leiden University\uc2  and host of the podcast America Explained, and he writes for the Guardian today to argue that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/08/future-of-american-democracy-stake-midterm-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the future of American democracy is at stake in the midterm elections}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Never before in American history has there been an organized movement which was only one vote away from having the motivation and opportunity to make that election America\u8217\'20\'19s last. Never that is, until now. Today\u8217\'20\'19s anti-democratic movement is propelled not by genuine controversy or scandal, but rather by their commitment to ending competitive elections in the United States. There is no other way to interpret their belief that only one side, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2 , can legitimately be considered to win, and the plans that they hold to make this belief a reality.\par \par \uc2 The problems can be expected to start this November, when Republican candidates who lose will question the validity of the results and try to stir unrest. State officials who do win will begin to act on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/2022/9/2/23331716/election-denier-2024-mastriano-lake-finchem"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their plans}}}\uc2  to sabotage future polls by centralizing power in their own offices, de-registering millions of voters, and moving to error-prone hand-counting systems. Then, if voter suppression doesn\u8217\'20\'19t prevent a Democratic win in 2024, they\u8217\'20\'19ll just suppress the evidence instead and announce that they are sending Republican electors to the electoral college. Meanwhile, the majority of Republican House candidates in 2022 are election-deniers, and a Republican-controlled Congress might attempt to sabotage the certification of the presidential vote on 6 January 2025.\par \par \uc2 Each of these potential points of failure threatens the integrity of the 2024 presidential election. The breadth and depth of the anti-democratic movement also means that they are likely to pose other problems which are difficult to anticipate. Whatever means they find of sabotaging the vote, it would be foolish to rely on the conservative-dominated supreme court to stop them, particularly if the country has been plunged into civil unrest and violence.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/08/future-of-american-democracy-stake-midterm-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andrew Gawthorpe \u8211\'20\'13 The future of American democracy is at stake in the midterm elections}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/08/future-of-american-democracy-stake-midterm-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The future of American democracy is at stake in the midterm elections | Andrew Gawthorpe}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 If you would like something to listen to about the US midterm elections, then Today in Focus is for you. Today\u8217\'20\'19s edition is called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2022/nov/08/us-midterms-is-it-still-the-economy-stupid-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterms: is it still the economy, stupid?}}}\uc2  and it features the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Washington correspondent Lauren Gambino talking to Michael Safi.\par \par \uc2 She tells him that the Democratic party has learned the hard way to keep its election campaigns laser-focused on the economy. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the economy, stupid,\u8221\'20\'1D a slogan used in \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 1992 campaign, has resonated down the ages. But Democrats have spent much of the past few months campaigning on women\u8217\'20\'19s rights after the seismic supreme court decision removing the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  used his final major speech of the campaign to warn voters of the threat to democracy itself.\par \par \uc2 Democrats go into these elections lagging in the polls, she says, and it\u8217\'20\'19s left some hardened campaigners, such as Bernie Sanders, wondering if the party has neglected its most famous mantra.\par \par \uc2 You can listen to it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2022/nov/08/us-midterms-is-it-still-the-economy-stupid-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2022/nov/08/us-midterms-is-it-still-the-economy-stupid-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterms: is it still the economy, stupid? - podcast}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had a late night last night, after appearing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/07/joe-biden-us-midterm-elections-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  at a rally in Maryland}}}\uc2 , where he said \u8220\'20\'1COur lifetimes are going to be shaped by what happens the next year to three years. It\u8217\'20\'19s going to shape what the next couple decades look like.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Joan E Greve was in Bowie, Maryland for the Guardian, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/07/joe-biden-us-midterm-elections-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported that Biden repeated his promise}}}\uc2  to shore up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights if Democrats expand their congressional majorities. The president and his wife, Dr Jill Biden, arrived back at the White House at around 1.20am.\par \par \uc2 US President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  arrives back at the White House in the early hours after participating in a rally in Maryland on the eve of midterm elections. Photograph: Will Oliver/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Philadelphia Inquirer this morning has been touting a series of quotes from voters in the area which highlight the different priorities of Republican and Democratic party voters. Here are four quotes that they picked for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inquirer.com/newsletters/morning/sign-up/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their newsletter}}}\uc2  today:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like a critical time. We\u8217\'20\'19ve got to pick a path, and the path that we\u8217\'20\'19re on is not right,\u8221\'20\'1D Nate DeFazio, a small business owner worried about inflation, said outside of a Mehmet Oz rally.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we do not maintain a majority in the Senate, Roe falling will be just the beginning,\u8221\'20\'1D Ronna Dewey, 54, said at a \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  rally.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really important to me to be able to graduate high school and enter adulthood in a state of having all my rights intact and not feeling like, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, I wish I\u8217\'20\'19d graduated 10 years ago,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Jordan Bailkin, a Democratic high school senior who is voting for the first time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPersonal freedom and the freedom to dissent \u8211\'20\'13 I feel that both of those things are under attack,\u8221\'20\'1D Jason Dekker, a conservative from York County said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.25am GMT\par \par \uc2 Overnight Stephen Collinson at CNN offered this analysis of how \u8211\'20\'13 and why \u8211\'20\'13 he expects things to pan out in favor of the Republicans in the next few days. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2022/11/07/politics/democrats-nightmare-scenario-election-eve-analysis/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 He writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s too early for postmortems. Forty million Americans have already voted. And the uncertainty baked into modern polling means no one can be sure a red wave is coming. Democrats could still cling onto the Senate even if the House falls.\par \par \uc2 But the way each side is talking on election eve, and the swathe of blue territory \u8211\'20\'13 from New York to Washington state \u8211\'20\'13 that Democrats are defending offer a clear picture of Republican momentum.\par \par \uc2 A nation split down the middle politically, which is united only by a sense of dissatisfaction with its trajectory, is getting into a habit of repeatedly using elections to punish the party with the most power.\par \par \uc2 That means Democrats are most exposed this time.\par \par \uc2 If the president\u8217\'20\'19s party takes a drubbing, there will be much Democratic finger-pointing over Biden\u8217\'20\'19s messaging strategy on inflation \u8211\'20\'13 a pernicious force that has punched holes in millions of family budgets.\par \par \uc2 Just as in last year\u8217\'20\'19s losing off-year gubernatorial race in Virginia, Democrats are closing the campaign warning about democracy and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s influence while Republicans believe they are addressing the issue voters care about most.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 When will we know who won US midterm races \u8212\'20\'14 and what to expect on election day\par \par \uc2 We probably won\u8217\'20\'19t know the winners on election night\par \par \uc2 In many races, we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to know who won on election night. After the polls close, candidate vote totals are likely to shift as local officials continue to count ballots.\par \par \uc2 Once the polls close, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://electionlab.mit.edu/research/election-return-reporting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election workers tabulate}}}\uc2  the votes in each precinct and transmit them to the county\u8217\'20\'19s central election office. Each county reports their results to the state.\par \par \uc2 Vote totals are likely to shift throughout the evening as well as in the days that follow election day as votes continue to be counted. That shift isn\u8217\'20\'19t unusual and can be explained by two dynamics, said Charles Stewart III, a political science professor at MIT who specializes in election administration.\par \par \uc2 First, he said, the places that report their votes first tend to be smaller, Republican jurisdictions. Second, many places report their in-person election day vote first and their mail-in absentee vote later. Those votes tend to skew towards {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Because these totals can change, there should be deep skepticism of attempts to claim victory before votes are counted.\par \par \uc2 Projections about which candidates will win are separate from official results\par \par \uc2 As officials report election results, news organizations, including the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ap.org/about/our-role-in-elections/how-we-call-races"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Associated Press}}}\uc2  and major television networks, scrutinize data to try to make projections about which candidate will win. This process is independent of the official election efforts to count votes.\par \par \uc2 In some races, experts are able to quickly make a projection about who is going to win a race. If a candidate from one party has consistently won a race, for example, and the voting patterns on election night appear to immediately be in line with previous elections, news organizations may feel confident in making a projection with only a fraction of the vote counted.\par \par \uc2 But in competitive races where there\u8217\'20\'19s a slim margin between the candidates, and a lot of the vote hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been reported yet, experts are much more cautious and will not make a prediction.\par \par \uc2 As the vote count continues, forecasters compare the margin separating the candidate with where in the state there are still votes outstanding. Once they feel confident that there\u8217\'20\'19s no path to victory for one candidate, they will declare a winner.\par \par \uc2 Some will claim that they see election errors (and most will turn out to be false)\par \par \uc2 Every election day, there are voters who claim to see something amiss at the polls or during the vote counting process. In 2020, many of those claims were loudly amplified by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and continue to live on today, even though they\u8217\'20\'19ve been debunked over and over again.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, we\u8217\'20\'19re likely to hear similar claims. Each one of those should be taken with a \u8220\'20\'1Cgiant grain of salt\u8221\'20\'1D, David Becker, the founder and executive director of the Center for Election Innovation and Research, said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would look at any claim from anyone saying that, regardless of party of election, highly skeptically.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese things are largely driven by losing candidates or candidates that expect to lose,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. The claims, he said, are \u8220\'20\'1Calmost always proven to be false. Or easily explainable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2020, for example, there were claims from observers about ballots being wheeled into a central counting facility in Detroit late during election night. Those ballots had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/fact-checking-9961890106"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already been verified}}}\uc2  by the local election office and there was nothing wrong with their late arrival time. The claim was nonetheless touted by those who sought to sow doubt about the election results.\par \par \uc2 You can read more from Sam Levine\u8217\'20\'19s explainer here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/midterm-election-day-race-won-when-timing-deadlines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 When will we know who won US midterm races \u8212\'20\'14 and what to expect on election day}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/midterm-election-day-race-won-when-timing-deadlines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 When will we know who won US midterm races \u8212\'20\'14 and what to expect on election day}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 If you missed it last night, here is the clip of former US president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , speaking at a rally in Ohio, saying he will be making a \u8220\'20\'1Cbig announcement\u8221\'20\'1D on 15 November, hinting that he will mount a 2024 presidential run.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m going to be making a very big announcement on Tuesday, 15 November at Mar-a-Lago in Palm Beach, Florida,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump told supporters at the rally for Republican Senate candidate JD Vance. Trump declined to elaborate, saying he did not want to \u8220\'20\'1Cdetract from tomorrow\u8217\'20\'19s very important, even critical election\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.00am GMT\par \par \uc2 Polls open for midterms voting\par \par \uc2 Polls have opened in some parts of the US for the 2022 midterm elections, so called because they fall halfway through a president\u8217\'20\'19s \u8211\'20\'13 in this case, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8211\'20\'13 presidency and so are often seen as a referendum on the incumbent.\par \par \uc2 Many voters have already cast their ballots, with more than 41 million people taking part in early voting.\par \par \uc2 At stake are all 435 seats in the House of Representatives, 35 seats in the 100-member Senate, 36 state governorships, along with numerous other races for local officials and mayors.\par \par \uc2 Republicans need to gain five seats to win a majority in the House and only one to take the Senate. Typically, an incumbent president\u8217\'20\'19s party expects to lose House seats in the midterms, but this year\u8217\'20\'19s races are being closely watched for any gains for election-denying candidates: Republicans who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/us-election-deniers-trump-big-lie-republican-midterm-candidates-running"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 still believe the lie that Donald Trump won the 2020 presidential election}}}\uc2 , some of whom actively worked to overturn the result that put Biden in the White House.\par \par \uc2 In 36 states, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/what-are-midterm-elections-when-does-voting-start-explained"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other issues are on the ballot}}}\uc2 , including laws on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in California, Kentucky, Michigan, Montana and Vermont.\par \par \uc2 We will have all the news and results on our live blog, so stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 Nimo Omer spoke to David Smith, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Washington DC bureau chief, about why these midterms matter so much and what the results could mean for America:\par \par \uc2 There are a number of contests that everyone is keeping a very close eye on. Perhaps the biggest is Georgia : \u8220\'20\'1CThe rule used to be whichever way Florida goes, so goes the nation,\u8221\'20\'1D says David, but \u8220\'20\'1CGeorgia has, in many ways, replaced Florida as the pivotal state in the nation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s senate race is extremely important. Raphael Warnock \u8217\'20\'19s win in 2021 was key to the Democrats securing control of the senate. Now Warnock faces off against Herschel Walker, a former football player who \u8220\'20\'1Chas no discernible political experience or qualifications\u8221\'20\'1D, David says. Walker has been embroiled in controversy for a year as stories of his affairs, extramarital children and allegations of domestic violence came to light. Most recently, a former girlfriend asserted that he paid for for her to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite Walker running on a hardline anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  platform.\par \par \uc2 And Georgia is also where Democratic favourite (and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/19/stacey-abrams-president-earth-star-trek-georgia-governor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Star Trek\u8217\'20\'19s president of a United Earth}}}\uc2  ), Stacey Abrams, will again try to wrestle the governership from Brian Kemp. A victory for Abrams would ensure voting and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are bolstered in the state.\par \par \uc2 Other races to watch out for are Ohio, where author of Hillbilly Elegy, Trump critic turned sycophant JD Vance is running: \u8220\'20\'1CIf Democrats win in a state that has really been trending Republican in recent years, there\u8217\'20\'19ll be a lot of blame on Vance and perhaps {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  for backing him,\u8221\'20\'1D David says.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, home of Joe Biden, is another crucial state with TV personality Dr Mehmet Oz running against the 6\u8217\'20\'198\u8221\'20\'1D tattooed lieutenant governor, John Fetterman, in the senate race. Oz secured a Trump endorsement, as did Doug Mastriano, who is running for governor of the same state. Mastriano was part of the effort to overturn the 2020 elections and appeared outside the US Capitol during January 6 riots. He could be a key part of a Trump presidential run in 2024.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels as if there are two separate campaigns and conversations happening, that are operating on different planets,\u8221\'20\'1D David says. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the past, at least, there was a shared set of issues, and both parties would be looking to be the best on inflation or healthcare.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans have focused on inflation, specifically petrol prices, and the cost of living crisis. They have also made characteristic campaign points about crime and other culture war topics such as immigration. Conversely, Democrats have been focused on reproductive rights following the supreme court decision to overturn Roe v Wade, as well as the threats to democracy, voting rights and the climate crisis. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of opinion polls are suggesting that Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 issues are likely to win the day, because so often, people vote according to their pocketbook and the economy,\u8221\'20\'1D says David.\par \par \uc2 Read more of Nimo Omer\u8217\'20\'19s conversation with David Smith in today\u8217\'20\'19s First Edition: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/08/tuesday-briefing-first-edition-what-you-need-to-know-ahead-of-us-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tuesday briefing \u8211\'20\'13 What you need to know ahead of US midterms}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/08/tuesday-briefing-first-edition-what-you-need-to-know-ahead-of-us-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tuesday briefing: What you need to know ahead of US midterms}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 Hello and welcome to our coverage of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-midterm-elections-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterm elections}}}\uc2 . As my colleague Nimo Omer wrote in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/08/tuesday-briefing-first-edition-what-you-need-to-know-ahead-of-us-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today\u8217\'20\'19s First Edition newsletter}}}\uc2 , ballots will be cast for senators, representatives and local officials in one of the most important contests in recent years. She added that it has become tiresome to describe every American election as uniquely significant, but there is a lot at stake with these midterms as the chasm between Democrats and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republicans}}}\uc2  grows ever wider, and the supreme court decision to no longer protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights hangs in the air.\par \par \uc2 The result of the election also has global implications, as concerns are expressed that a Republican win might dampen US enthusiasm for pouring financial and military aid into Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s war for survival against the latest Russia invasion of its territory. And the outcome will set the stage for the presidential election battle to come during the next two years \u8211\'20\'13 a race which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/08/big-announcement-donald-trump-holds-back-decision-on-2024-presidential-run"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 looks likely now to feature a certain Donald J Trump}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will have non-stop rolling live coverage of the election over the next couple of days, so I hope you will join us.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221108eib8001mh\par }\page {\par