\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Tuesday briefing: What you need to know ahead of US midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Nimo Omer \par \uc2 2,427 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par 06:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In today\u8217\'20\'19s newsletter: As Americans vote for senators, representatives and local officials, our Washington DC bureau chief explains why this contest is so important and how the balance of power might shiftSign up here for our daily newsletter, First Edition\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Today the midterm elections are being held across America. Ballots will be cast for senators, representatives and local officials in one of the most important contests in recent years. It has become tiresome to describe every American election as uniquely significant, but there is a lot at stake with these midterms as the chasm between Democrats and Republicans grows ever wider, and the supreme court decision to no longer protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights hangs in the air.\par \par \uc2 Despite a slim majority in Congress, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats have spent the past two years pushing through new laws on gun control, the climate crisis, child poverty and infrastructure \u8211\'20\'13 much more than many thought possible. But any change in the balance of power will bring that momentum to a grinding halt.\par \par \uc2 And for many Democrats this is not just an election about policy, it is a fight for democracy itself. Two hundred candidates are running, some of them in key seats, who believe that the last election was stolen from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Hearings on the January 6 insurrection have been shocking \u8211\'20\'13 and only two weeks ago the husband of the US House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/28/paul-pelosi-nancy-pelosi-attack-san-francisco-home"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attacked in their house}}}\uc2 . If Republicans were to enjoy a resounding success, it is far from clear they would accept any future Democratic victory in a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to David Smith, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Washington DC bureau chief, about why these midterms matter so much and what the results could mean for America.\par \par \uc2 Five big stories\par \par \uc2 Climate |\par \par \uc2 Low-income countries will need {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/nov/08/developing-countries-climate-crisis-funding-2030-report-nicholas-stern"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approximately $2tn (\u163\'00\'A31.75tn)}}}\uc2  in climate funding by 2030 to help cut their emissions and cope with the effects of the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 Russia |\par \par \uc2 Putin ally and influential Russian businessman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/07/putin-ally-yevgeny-prigozhin-admits-interfering-in-us-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin admitted to interfering in the US elections}}}\uc2  and has said that interfering will continue in the coming midterms.\par \par \uc2 Politics |\par \par \uc2 A senior civil servant said that \uc2 Gavin Williamson\uc2  subjected them to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/nov/07/senior-civil-servant-claims-gavin-williamson-told-them-slit-your-throat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign of bullying}}}\uc2  when he was defence secretary, allegedly telling them to \u8220\'20\'1Cslit your throat\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cjump out of the window\u8221\'20\'1D on two separate occasions.\par \par \uc2 Weather |\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/nov/07/met-office-predicts-severe-flooding-across-england-in-february"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Met Office}}}\uc2  predicts severe flooding across England in February despite the country remaining in drought. The floods will be a result of La Ni\u241\'00\'F1a, a weather phenomenon influenced by cooler temperatures in the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 Courts |\par \par \uc2 Hollie Dance \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/nov/07/archie-battersbee-mother-asks-coroner-to-examine-role-of-tiktok-in-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the mother of Archie Battersbee}}}\uc2 , a 12-year-old boy who sustained a catastrophic brain injury in April and died in August \u8211\'20\'13 wants a coroner to examine the role of exposure to TikTok videos may have played in his death. Dance believes her son was hurt by taking part in an online challenge known as the \u8220\'20\'1Cblackout challenge\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In depth: \u8216\'20\'18History suggests a good night for Republicans\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Midterm elections are usually high-stakes affairs, often viewed as a referendum on the sitting president. But this year\u8217\'20\'19s are particularly consequential. All 435 seats in the House of Representatives are up for grabs, about one-third of the Senate, and 36 state governorships, among other local positions that have a say on how votes will be counted at future elections. As things stand, the Democrats have control in Washington \u8211\'20\'13 from the presidency to Congress to the Senate (the Senate is currently divided 50-50 but Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the tie-breaking vote). But it\u8217\'20\'19s famously hard for a sitting president to maintain an advantage, even more so during a cost of living crisis.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s conventional wisdom that Republicans will probably win the House at least, says David: \u8220\'20\'1CHistory suggests Republicans will have a good night because, on the vast majority of occasions, the party that holds the White House loses seats. And polling in recent days seems to underline that.\u8221\'20\'1D A win in the house would give Republicans the power to cut spending for aid to Ukraine and welfare spending. Republicans have also said they plan to disband the January 6 committee and start a slew of investigations into their Democratic opponents. There have even been calls to impeach \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , although senior Republicans have been downplaying the likelihood of that happening. A fully Republican Congress could also push for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban \u8211\'20\'13 although any changes to such legislation would be vetoed by the president.\par \par \uc2 If the GOP wins the senate as well, they will be able to obstruct Biden\u8217\'20\'19s political agenda, as well as blocking many of his cabinet secretaries and judicial appointments.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The key races\par \par \uc2 There are a number of contests that everyone is keeping a very close eye on. Perhaps the biggest is Georgia: \u8220\'20\'1CThe rule used to be whichever way Florida goes, so goes the nation,\u8221\'20\'1D says David, but \u8220\'20\'1CGeorgia has, in many ways, replaced Florida as the pivotal state in the nation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s senate race is extremely important. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s win in 2021 was key to the Democrats securing control of the senate. Now Warnock faces off against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , a former football player who \u8220\'20\'1Chas no discernible political experience or qualifications\u8221\'20\'1D, David says. Walker has been embroiled in controversy for a year as stories of his affairs, extramarital children and allegations of domestic violence came to light. Most recently, a former girlfriend asserted that he paid for for her to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite Walker running on a hardline anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  platform.\par \par \uc2 And Georgia is also where Democratic favourite (and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/19/stacey-abrams-president-earth-star-trek-georgia-governor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Star Trek\u8217\'20\'19s president of a United Earth}}}\uc2  ), \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , will again try to wrestle the governership from \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . A victory for Abrams would ensure voting and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are bolstered in the state.\par \par \uc2 Other races to watch out for are Ohio, where author of Hillbilly Elegy, Trump critic turned sycophant JD Vance is running: \u8220\'20\'1CIf Democrats win in a state that has really been trending Republican in recent years, there\u8217\'20\'19ll be a lot of blame on Vance and perhaps \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for backing him,\u8221\'20\'1D David says.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, home of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , is another crucial state with TV personality Dr Mehmet Oz running against the 6\u8217\'20\'198\u8221\'20\'1D tattooed lieutenant governor, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , in the senate race. Oz secured a Trump endorsement, as did \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who is running for governor of the same state. Mastriano was part of the effort to overturn the 2020 elections and appeared outside the US Capitol during January 6 riots. He could be a key part of a Trump presidential run in 2024.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 A divided nation\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels as if there are two separate campaigns and conversations happening, that are operating on different planets,\u8221\'20\'1D David says. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the past, at least, there was a shared set of issues, and both parties would be looking to be the best on inflation or healthcare.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans have focused on inflation, specifically petrol prices, and the cost of living crisis. They have also made characteristic campaign points about crime and other culture war topics such as immigration. Conversely, Democrats have been focused on reproductive rights following the supreme court decision to overturn Roe v Wade, as well as the threats to democracy, voting rights and the climate crisis. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of opinion polls are suggesting that Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 issues are likely to win the day, because so often, people vote according to their pocketbook and the economy,\u8221\'20\'1D says David.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 What it means for the rest of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s first term\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidential approval rating hovers around 40%. A poll conducted by Reuters and \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  found that 69% of Americans believe the country is on the wrong track, while just 18% said it was on the right track. While the Democrats have managed to recover from a summer slump in the polls, a big Republican victory could further entrench his political weakness, freeze up his administration for its final two years and lead to calls for Biden to step aside for another Democrat in the presidential race in 2024.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The T word\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has still had time to have a weirdly active role in these midterm elections, having endorsed more than 200 candidates on all levels of the political system. His senate endorsements in particular will be a litmus test for the Republican party. \u8220\'20\'1CIn a normal world, if all of his candidates lost and they got wiped out, there could be a sense that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really does not have the political midas touch that many believed he had,\u8221\'20\'1D David says. But this is not a normal world, and it\u8217\'20\'19s likely that regardless of what happens, Trump will claim the victory as his own: \u8220\'20\'1CIf they lose, he\u8217\'20\'19ll say they failed to follow his advice, maybe they did not embrace the \u8216\'20\'18big lie\u8217\'20\'19 enough. Or he could just say the vote was rigged and it\u8217\'20\'19s all another scam.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, if Trump candidates do win, he will be the first to claim it was all down to him and that he has been vindicated. It has been reported that Trump plans to launch his next presidential campaign around the week of 14 November on the back of any momentum from the midterms.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 When will we know for the results?\par \par \uc2 Even though voters will be casting their ballots today, it might be days, perhaps even weeks, before there is a clear picture of results. Republicans might seem to have a huge early lead, but that will be because \u8211\'20\'13 for the second election in a row \u8211\'20\'13 their votes will be counted and reported first in several battleground states. It\u8217\'20\'19s a deliberate change made by Republican officials in some states, making it easier to cast doubt on results when the final tally differs markedly from early announcements. This is coupled with the fact that Democrats traditionally use mail-in ballots far more than Republican voters, and those ballots can take longer to tally and tend to be reported in the days after the election.\par \par \uc2 There will be some idea of how the election went tomorrow morning. In the meantime, read more of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-midterm-elections-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections coverage}}}\uc2  here.\par \par \uc2 What else we\u8217\'20\'19ve been reading\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 There are so many parts of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/07/cherish-moment-motherhood-baby-new-parents"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rhiannon Lucy Cosslett\u8217\'20\'19s latest column}}}\uc2  that will chime for parents \u8211\'20\'13 but for me it was the way she captures the constant change of early parenthood that really struck home, every shift gifting \u8220\'20\'1Ca whole new phase, while mourning that which came before\u8221\'20\'1D. Toby Moses, head of newsletters\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2022/nov/07/the-ultimate-enemy-is-women-the-secret-world-of-incels"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Benjamin Zand spent a year}}}\uc2  inside theincel community in the UK and abroad, uncovering a world marred by desperation, loneliness and violent misogyny. Nimo\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/2022/nov/07/conservative-party-conference-music-friendly-fires-dream-fatboy-slim-dandy-warhols"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elle Hunt\u8217\'20\'19s lovely feature}}}\uc2 , talking to the bands who had their music coopted by politicians, is perhaps best encapsulated by this quote from Friendly Fires\u8217\'20\'19 response to \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  using their song as entrance music: \u8220\'20\'1CIf we\u8217\'20\'19d have intended them to use it, we\u8217\'20\'19d have named the track Blue Bunch of Corrupt Wankers.\u8221\'20\'1D Toby\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/nov/07/migration-railways-bad-data-british-politics-inaccurate-incomplete"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Georgina Sturge unpacks}}}\par \par \uc2 how \u8220\'20\'1Cbad data\u8221\'20\'1D infiltrated British politics and what the implications are on policy when a government relies on erroneous or partial information. \u8220\'20\'1CNumbers hold enormous power,\u8221\'20\'1D Sturge writes, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut in the end, we must remember that we govern them \u8211\'20\'13 not the other way round.\u8221\'20\'1D Nimo\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ham in a can is back, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/food/2022/nov/07/spam-is-back-chefs-on-their-favourite-recipes-from-katsu-curry-to-spam-fries-with-cheesy-kimchi-sauce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stuart Heritage\u8217\'20\'19s tour}}}\uc2  through the best Spam recipes from the great and good of the culinary world offers one particularly dangerous idea: Spam french fries, anyone? Toby\par \par \uc2 Sport\par \par \uc2 World Cup 2022 |\par \par \uc2 Six out of 10 people in the UK think {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/nov/07/six-in-10-in-uk-oppose-qatar-hosting-world-cup-over-anti-gay-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  that the World Cup should not be held in Qatar}}}\uc2  because of its criminalisation of homosexuality. The same poll found that only 43% of people think that England and Wales should take part in the World Cup.\par \par \uc2 Football |\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/nov/07/champions-league-last-16-draw-tie-by-tie-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Liverpool}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/nov/07/manchester-united-to-face-barcelona-in-europa-league-knockout-play-off"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Manchester United}}}\uc2  face tough European challenges against Real Madrid and Barcelona in the next round of Champions and Europa Leagues.\par \par \uc2 Football\par \par \uc2 | Rio Ferdinand is typically thoughtful on the subjects of racism and homophobia in this {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/nov/07/rio-ferdinand-interview-racism-england-world-cup-documentary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revealing interview}}}\uc2  with Donald McRae.\par \par \uc2 The front pages\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  leads this morning with \u8220\'20\'1CPoor nations \u8216\'20\'18paying twice\u8217\'20\'19 for climate breakdown\u8221\'20\'1D. The i has \u8220\'20\'1CRed alert for Earth: gravest warning yet on climate change\u8221\'20\'1D while the Metro covers Cop27 as well with \u8220\'20\'1CSunak turns on the Sharm\u8221\'20\'1D, geddit? The Daily Mail asks \u8220\'20\'1CJust what planet are they on?\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 it says incredulously that campaigners want the UK to pay $1tn in climate reparations to poorer nations. Other papers show \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  embracing \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  at Cop27 but it is not their lead story. \u8220\'20\'1CWelfare and pensions set to rise with inflation\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s the Times while the Daily Telegraph has \u8220\'20\'1CGas deal set to ease energy crisis\u8221\'20\'1D and the Daily Express goes with \u8220\'20\'1CRishi: I will get \u8216\'20\'18grip\u8217\'20\'19 on migrant crisis\u8221\'20\'1D. The Mirror\u8217\'20\'19s splash is inspiring but also a bit challenging: \u8220\'20\'1CBrave mum\u8217\'20\'19s TV dissection to educate millions\u8221\'20\'1D (about cancer \u8211\'20\'13 the \u8220\'20\'1Cextraordinary broadcast\u8221\'20\'1D will take place on Channel 4). It is still on the trail of Lord Lucan as well \u8211\'20\'13 a puff box says \u8220\'20\'1CLucan brother: he DID escape and become a Buddhist\u8221\'20\'1D. The top story in the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  today is \u8220\'20\'1CChancellor lines up stealth raid on inheritance tax to shore up finances\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Today in Focus\par \par \uc2 US midterms: is it still the economy, stupid?\par \par \uc2 The Democrats have learned hard lessons over the years about what happens when election campaigns neglect the economy, so {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2022/nov/08/us-midterms-is-it-still-the-economy-stupid-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has the party been strong enough in its messaging for today\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections?}}}\uc2  Lauren Gambino reports\par \par \uc2 Cartoon of the day | Steve Bell\par \par \uc2 The Upside\par \par \uc2 A bit of good news to remind you that the world\u8217\'20\'19s not all bad\par \par \uc2 During monsoon season in south-western Bangladesh, when there is little dry land on which to grow food, farmers keep their businesses afloat \u8211\'20\'13 quite literally \u8211\'20\'13 by growing vegetables on rafts made from invasive water hyacinths. These floating gardens help ensure food security in low-lying regions, where the climate crisis has resulted in waterlogging and flooding. Photographer Mohammad Ponir Hossain, who won a Pulitzer for his images of Rohingya refugees, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/gallery/2022/nov/07/the-floating-gardens-of-bangladesh-in-pictures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has captured the practice and the people behind it}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/feb/12/the-upside-sign-up-for-our-weekly-email"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up here}}}\uc2  for a weekly roundup of The Upside, sent to you every Sunday\par \par \uc2 Bored at work?\par \par \uc2 And finally, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s crosswords to keep you entertained throughout the day \u8211\'20\'13 with plenty more on the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Puzzles app for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apps.apple.com/app/id1487780661?mt=8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 iOS}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=uk.co.guardian.puzzles&hl=en_GB&gl=US"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Android}}}\uc2 . Until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/crosswords/quick/16382"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Quick crossword}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/crosswords/cryptic/28909"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cryptic crossword}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221108eib8000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
