\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A new political hellscape\u8217\'20\'19: sweeping gains for Republicans could stifle Biden\u8217\'20\'19s presidency\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par 20:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Momentum appears to be with Republicans capitalising on economic frustration as experts say party peaking at right time\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is fighting a rearguard action to stave off defeat in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections as Republicans look poised to make sweeping gains in the \uc2 US Congress\uc2 , setting up two years of political trench warfare.\par \par \uc2 The president, along with former president \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , has been crisscrossing America in a last-ditch bid to persuade voters that a Democratic victory is critical not only to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/29/us/politics/bidens-agenda-midterms-congress.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s legislative agenda}}}\uc2  but the preservation of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 But momentum appears to be with Republicans capitalising on frustration over inflation and fears of crime and illegal immigration. Election forecasters and polls say it is highly likely that the party of ex-president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will win a majority in the House of Representatives and also have a shot of taking control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans are peaking at the right time ,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BrendanBuck?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brendan Buck}}}\uc2 , a former aide to Republican House speakers \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats did a good job defying political gravity for a long time but it\u8217\'20\'19s finally catching up to them. It feels like a healthy Republican majority in the House and, if I were a betting man, I would guess that Republicans pick up the one Senate seat that they need. \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Midterms are held every four years but in 2022 they are far from routine and have seen a huge increase in early voting turnout. Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election represents the first nationwide test of democracy since Trump\u8217\'20\'19s followers staged a deadly insurrection at the US Capitol on January 6 last year.\par \par \uc2 At stake are all 435 seats in the House, 35 seats in the 100-member Senate, 36 state governorships, three US territory governorships and numerous city mayorships and local offices. Some 129 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/2022_ballot_measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot measures}}}\uc2  in 36 states include laws on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in California, Kentucky, Michigan, Montana and Vermont.\par \par \uc2 A surprise Democratic victory in the House and Senate would give Biden a mandate to pursue a sweeping legislative agenda on issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, police reform and voting rights during his two remaining years in the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 But Republican control of either chamber would be enough to derail such ambitions and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/newsletters/axios-sneak-peek-5302531f-696a-4026-bedd-926aa9a6f578.html?chunk=2&utm_term=emshare#story2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raise questions}}}\uc2  over the US\u8217\'20\'19s open-ended support for Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s war against Russia. Biden might face congressional investigations into everything from the withdrawal from Afghanistan to his son Hunter\u8217\'20\'19s foreign business dealings.\par \par \uc2 Michael Steele, a former chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://membership.theguardian.com/event/politics-weekly-america-live-the-midterm-elections-425325599317"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CAt the end of the day it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter: one chamber, two chambers, if Republicans have control, the next 18 to 24 months in this country are going to be a new political hellscape unlike anything we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen ever.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As a long campaign enters the home straight, both major parties are pouring millions of dollars into TV adverts, blitzing social media, knocking on thousands of doors and staging rallies with their biggest stars. Biden\u8217\'20\'19s final swing implies a defensive posture in states that Democrats already hold \u8211\'20\'13 California, Illinois and New Mexico \u8211\'20\'13 along with battleground Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Midterms often serve as a referendum on the president of the day. Biden\u8217\'20\'19s public approval rating has remained below 50% for more than a year, coming in at 40% in a recent Reuters/ Ipsos poll. That same survey showed that 69% of Americans believe the country is on the wrong track and just 18% said it was on the right track.\par \par \uc2 History is also against Democrats. The party in power typically loses House seats halfway through a president\u8217\'20\'19s four-year term. In 2006, \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  said his Republicans took a \u8220\'20\'1Cthumping\u8221\'20\'1D in the midterms. In 2010, \uc2 Obama\uc2  called his party\u8217\'20\'19s loss of 63 House seats a \u8220\'20\'1Cshellacking\u8221\'20\'1D. In 2018, two years into Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidency, the Republican party surrendered 41 House seats. In all three cases, control of the House flipped.\par \par \uc2 This year Republicans need to gain only five seats to assume the majority. As if anticipating a Republican takeover, 31 House Democrats announced they were retiring or seeking other office, the most for the party since 1992.\par \par \uc2 Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 prospects have been further enhanced through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2021/nov/12/gerrymander-redistricting-map-republicans-democrats-visual"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gerrymandering}}}\uc2 , the practice by which one party manipulates congressional district lines to entrench its own power during the once-a-decade redistricting process.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile Republicans need to gain one seat to take control of the Senate, currently divided 50-50 with Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  casting the tie-breaking vote. Candidates such as TV doctor Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania and former American football star {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/oct/27/herschel-walker-worst-candidate-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Herschel Walker in Georgia}}}\uc2  have proved more formidable than expected. Campaigns for Democratic-held seats in Arizona and Nevada are also closely contested.\par \par \uc2 Polls have been wrong before, however, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/newsletters/axios-sneak-peek-5302531f-696a-4026-bedd-926aa9a6f578.html?chunk=0&utm_term=emshare#story0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 there could yet be surprises}}}\uc2 . Among the uncertainties this time is the fallout from the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in June overturning the constitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections of Roe v Wade, which resulted in a surge of protest votes in a Kansas referendum and sparked a rise in voter registrations among women nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have spent nearly $320m on TV adverts focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/3cMUbquzrgOSwTtYnPh0Kg~~/AAAAAQA~~"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the New York Times reported,}}}\uc2  which is 10 times as much as they have spent on commercials about inflation, which has driven up the cost of food and petrol. But polls show that the economy remains a higher concern for voters, suggesting that anger over the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision will not be enough to save Democrats.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/experts/elaine-kamarck/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elaine Kamarck}}}\uc2 , a former official in the Bill Clinton White House, told a press briefing at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  thinktank in Washington: \u8220\'20\'1CIt has been a race between inflation and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for some weeks now. At the end of the summer, we thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was going to really move votes in this race.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow, the conventional wisdom seems to have shifted away from that and towards inflation and questions about the economy. The fact of the matter is, however, it is a completely tight race. In state after state after state, the polls are within the margin of error and we are going to have to look at turnout because turnout is going to determine this race .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Turnout of women is key, Kamarck added, but if Republicans do prevail, the postmortems will begin. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Democrats will have a lot of soul searching to do about their position, how they went into this race. Did they overestimate the power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Did they underestimate the economic message? \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some commentators believe that the answer is yes. Buck, the former Republican aide who is now a partner at strategic communications firm Seven Letter, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt should not have been a surprise to anyone that this election is about economic issues \u8211\'20\'13 inflation, gas prices \u8211\'20\'13 and they largely ceded the playing field to Republicans on this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Biden has pinballed between messages on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to democracy to Republican plans to cut social security and healthcare programs, Buck noted. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just basic political communications 101 that you need to stick to some messages and hammer them over and over again and they\u8217\'20\'19ve been all over the map and so it\u8217\'20\'19s not surprising that whatever they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to get across to voters isn\u8217\'20\'19t breaking through.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There is frustration on the left that Democrats did not better communicate achievements such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/child-tax-credit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the child tax credit}}}\uc2 , which during the coronavirus pandemic provided relief to working families on a historic scale.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have also argued that a climate and healthcare package passed by Congress in August will help reduce inflation by making prescription drugs more affordable. The White House also moved to forgive some student-loan debt, potentially boosting turnout among younger voters.\par \par \uc2 But 8.5% inflation and anxiety about a possible recession have been central to Republican arguments in the election\u8217\'20\'19s final weeks. They have also heavily invested in at times blatantly racist ads stoking voters\u8217\'20\'19 fears about a rise in violent crime, tying Democrats to so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cdefund the police\u8221\'20\'1D efforts.\par \par \uc2 Critics say {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/03/republicans-law-order-midterm-election-investigations-scandals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this is hypocritical}}}\uc2  from a party that still seeks to play down the violent coup attempt at the US Capitol on January 6. In a primetime address from nearby Union Station last week, Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/02/joe-biden-speech-political-violence-preserve-democracy-midterm-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned that democracy itself}}}\uc2  is on the ballot and issued a dark warning about the threat of voter intimidation and political violence.\par \par \uc2 Highlighting estimates of more than 300 election deniers running for every level of office in America, and their unwillingness to accept the results of elections that they are running in, Biden said: \u8220\'20\'1CThat is a path to chaos in America. It\u8217\'20\'19s unprecedented, it\u8217\'20\'19s unlawful, and it\u8217\'20\'19s un-American. As I\u8217\'20\'19ve said before, you can\u8217\'20\'19t love your country only when you win.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Indeed, just as in 2020, there are fears that a disputed election assailed by disinformation and conspiracy theories could tear America apart. The recent hammer attack on the House speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s husband, Paul, at their San Francisco home may foreshadow worse to come.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/experts/john-hudak/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Hudak}}}\uc2 , a senior fellow in governance studies at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s only a matter of time until there is a high-level political assassination in the United States given the tinder box that we have right now and that\u8217\'20\'19s a really unfortunate situation. We live in a country now that is not used to political assassinations and that is a luxury that is not afforded to citizens of other countries. But there is a real changing dynamic and undercurrent there .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many election deniers will get into office, Hudak predicted. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat happens when they begin coordinating with each other? That coordinated assault by elected officials on American democracy to me is scarier than simply their arrival into office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTheir ability to communicate and coordinate through official platforms, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s associations of secretaries of state et cetera, or through quieter platforms, individual communication, presents extreme risks to what the vote count will look like and how elections are administered in the future .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But there is little sign that fears over democracy are cutting through with Republican voters. The midterms campaign has often felt like a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/16/us-midterms-republicans-democrats-major-issues"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 split screen}}}\uc2  with Democrats and Republicans are largely talking past each other.\par \par \uc2 John Zogby, an author and pollster, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.state.gov/briefings-foreign-press-centers/2022-midterm-elections-battleground-states-and-key-races"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told a state department Foreign Press Centers briefing}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CIn every election in the past, there is a common set of issues that everybody agrees on, and one party says, this is how we will attack these issues, and the other party says, no, this is how we will attack these issues. The difference today: two different parties, two different sets of issues, two different realities, two different sets of facts to support those realities. It is like two planets revolving around the sun and on separate orbits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whatever the outcome, speculation over the 2024 presidential race is likely to begin even before the last vote is cast in 2022. If Democrats suffer heavy losses, Biden might face calls, especially from the left, to announce that he is not running again. He turns 80 on 20 November and is already the oldest president in American history.\par \par \uc2 Trump, himself 76, appears {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/11/04/trump-presidential-run-2024-announcement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 likely to announce his candidacy}}}\uc2  sooner rather than later, even if his anointed candidates have a bad night. At a rally on Thursday night supporting Republican candidates in Iowa, he declared: \u8220\'20\'1CGet ready that\u8217\'20\'19s all I\u8217\'20\'19m telling you \u8211\'20\'13 very soon. Get ready.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221107eib7000s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
