\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 These companies claim to support  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights. They are backing anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  Republicans\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Kirchgaessner in Washington and Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par 19:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 An analysis of major companies\u8217\'20\'19 donations, including \uc2 Meta\uc2  and Amazon, reveals donations to candidates calling for banning the procedure\par \par \uc2 The pharmaceutical company \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  was one of the most vocal opponents of a sweeping anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that passed in its home state of Indiana, last August, saying that the measure would make it hard to attract talent and would force it to look outside the state for growth.\par \par \uc2 But in the weeks and months that followed, Lilly continued to financially support Republican candidates and politicians who support bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country, including many who celebrated the reversal of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 It was not alone.\par \par \uc2 A Guardian analysis of other major US companies\u8217\'20\'19 political donations shows that those who suggested they would help female employees skirt statewide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, by offering to pay for out-of-state medical costs for those seeking abortions in states where the option was illegal, continued to financially back candidates who have called for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. They include \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the company that owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Comcast\uc2 , \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 AT&T\uc2  and Amazon.\par \par \uc2 The analysis suggests that while some of America\u8217\'20\'19s largest employers want to be seen as supporting reproductive health for their female workers and their families, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue has not affected their financial support for Republican candidates who have promised to further erode those workers\u8217\'20\'19 reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Lilly made financial contributions to Texas state senators anti-choice Republicans Charles Schwertner and Charles Perry, and Texas state speaker Dade Phelan, who has said he does not see any need to change Texas\u8217\'20\'19s current law, which forces women who have been raped to carry their pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 Since Roe was overturned, Lilly has also given financial donations to US senators \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , Oklahoma\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 , and Mike Crapo, among others who supported overturning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The company did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Amazon, the second-largest private employer in the US, said it would cover out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel for employees on its healthcare plan, but not contractors who make up most of its workforce.\par \par \uc2 But even as it vowed to help some of its female workers get access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, it continued to support Republican candidates like \uc2 Bruce Westerman\uc2  of Arkansas, who wrote in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://westerman.house.gov/media-center/in-the-news/fights-not-over"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 op-ed}}}\uc2  for the Arkansas Democrat Gazette that the fight against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Creally just beginning\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will always stand for the rights of the unborn until \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not only illegal in all 50 states, but unconscionable,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\u8217\'20\'19s political action committee also gave donations to David Valadao, a California Republican who co-sponsored a \u8220\'20\'1Clife at conception\u8221\'20\'1D act, which states that it would guarantee a right to life at the \u8220\'20\'1Cmoment of fertilization\u8221\'20\'1D, and Tony Gonzales, who has an A+ rating from anti-choice group Susan B Anthony List. Amazon did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , the US telecommunications company, has said it would cover the cost of travel for medical procedures within 100 miles of an employee\u8217\'20\'19s home address because it values the health of its employees to make sure they can access \u8220\'20\'1Ca full range of health care benefits when they need them\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But the company has also supported dozens of Republican candidates since the 24 June decision to overrule Roe, including Texas\u8217\'20\'19s Jodey Arrington, who has called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Ca moral stain on the fabric of America\u8221\'20\'1D and supports a federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has also donated to Greg Steube, a Florida Republican who has said that, with Roe overturned \u8220\'20\'1Cno misguided judicial decision can block states from applying murder and assault statutes to protect the unborn from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. In Georgia, it supported Republican \uc2 Andrew Clyde\uc2 , who has said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be \u8220\'20\'1Cabolished entirely\u8221\'20\'1D except if the mother\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk, and Barry Loudermilk, who has tweeted the work of the pro-life community was \u8220\'20\'1Cjust beginning\u8221\'20\'1D after the Dobbs decision that overturned a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Maryland, \uc2 AT&T\uc2  supported Republican congressman Andy Harris, who said Dobbs had not created a crisis in healthcare, and Jack Bergman of Michigan who supports a federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 AT&T\uc2  spokesperson said the company\u8217\'20\'19s political action committee has \u8220\'20\'1Cnever based contribution decisions on a legislator\u8217\'20\'19s position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson added: \u8220\'20\'1COur employee Pacs contribute to candidates in both parties and focus on policies and regulations that are important to investing in broadband networks and hiring, developing and retaining a skilled workforce with competitive wages and benefits. It is inaccurate to assert that contributions to elected officials equate to supporting all of their policy positions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the aftermath of Dobbs, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Meta\uc2  said it would reimburse travel expenses \u8220\'20\'1Cto the extent permitted by law\u8221\'20\'1D for those who need to access out-of-state healthcare and reproductive services. But it also supported \u8211\'20\'13 among others \u8211\'20\'13 candidates like \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  of Nebraska and Bob Latta of Ohio who co-sponsored a bill to ban abortions federally. A \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesperson did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Citibank has said post-Dobbs that it would provide travel benefits to employees who need \u8220\'20\'1Caccess to adequate resources\u8221\'20\'1D but continued to support Republican candidates who support a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like John Hoeven of North Dakota. It also donated to \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2  of Kansas, who has said life begins at conception and \u8220\'20\'1Csupports legislation protecting life at its earliest stages and in all conditions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Kara Findlay, head of corporate communications at \uc2 Citi\uc2 , declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Comcast\uc2 , the parent company of NBC Universal, has said it would support thousands of dollars of medically necessary travel expenses after Roe was overturned, but continued to make political donations to Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, like Benjamin Cline of Virginia, who once proposed legislation that would mark the anniversary of the Roe v Wade decision as the \u8220\'20\'1CDay of Tears\u8221\'20\'1D, which would commemorate \u8220\'20\'1C59 million lives lost\u8221\'20\'1D due to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services being protected.\par \par \uc2 The company did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Are you an employee at one of these companies and have a tip? Please contact: Stephanie.Kirchgaessner@theguardian.com\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221106eib6000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
