\par\par \uc2 Art and design\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18This is a state of emergency\u8217\'20\'19: the US billboards using art to urge  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par 13:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In states across the US with restricted access to abortions, artists have designed billboards to insist on voters to prioritise women\u8217\'20\'19s health in time for the midterm elections\par \par \uc2 For the next few weeks, passersby in 12 US cities, many in states with curtailed or tenuous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, will see clear, strikingly positive messages responding to the reproductive health crisis in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is Healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D says one billboard in Louisville, Kentucky, where the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/kentucky-abortion-vote-will-test-support-post-roe-state-bans-2022-10-18/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on the ballot this week}}}\uc2 , around two pink stethoscope cords in the shape of a uterus. In New Orleans, a sign blares \u8220\'20\'1CTHANK GOD FOR \b \highlight22\uc2 ABORTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D in stark black and white, an angel flying off the top right corner. In Atlanta, site of two of the most closely watched midterm races this year, a background of lush green leaves underscores blooming text composed of tropical flowers: \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 ABORTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  IS LIFE.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The billboards, 14 in total, form the exhibition {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.saveartspace.org/abortionrights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vote for \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights}}}\uc2 , curated by the Swedish American conceptual artist Michele Pred and the Brooklyn-based non-profit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.saveartspace.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SaveArtSpace}}}\uc2 . The installation from 10 different artists, including Pred, encourages viewers to channel their fury, apathy, despair or confusion over the erosion of reproductive health access in the US at the ballot box this month.\par \par \uc2 Pred conceived of the project in May, when a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/supreme-court-abortion-draft-opinion-00029473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked draft opinion}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/02/roe-v-wade-abortion-supreme-court-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all but confirmed}}}\uc2  that the supreme court would overturn a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  guaranteed for 50 years by Roe v Wade, as it did in late June. The conservative majority\u8217\'20\'19s 6-3 vote in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/19-1392_6j37.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs vs Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization}}}\uc2  relegated reproductive choice to state governments, some of which immediately outlawed any abortions.\par \par \uc2 Since June, trigger bans or hastily passed restrictions have outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 18 states; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usnews.com/news/elections/articles/2022-11-03/states-with-abortion-on-the-ballot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 five more states}}}\uc2  have put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ballot for the midterm elections this week.\par \par \uc2 Roadside advertisements are often a tool of conservative messaging \u8211\'20\'13 arguably the most famous billboard in my home state of Ohio, where fundamentalist Christian signs dot the interstates and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access hangs by a court injunction, simply says: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-sereligion-roadsigns/u-s-highways-lead-to-heaven-and-hell-for-drivers-idUSN2641838120070129"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HELL IS REAL}}}\uc2 . Pred recognized the common billboard as \u8220\'20\'1Can incredible form of public art that spread to a very large audience\u8221\'20\'1D, she told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd, I think, a very important audience: people who don\u8217\'20\'19t go to museums or galleries, which is the majority of the population.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whether on a freeway or in a neighborhood, billboards are \u8220\'20\'1Creally one of the most democratic forms of artwork, because everyone gets exposed to them,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. The goal of the project, which took submissions from over 400 artists, was to get the message physically in states where conservative voting blocs or minority rule governments threaten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, including Arizona, Texas, Louisiana, Wisconsin, Georgia, Nevada, Tennessee and Kentucky. Participating artists include Bud Snow, Holly Ballard Martz, Laney Baby, Lena Wolf and Hope Meng, Shireen Liane, Viva Ruiz, Wildcat Ebony Brown and Yvette Molina. Pred, whose project curation dovetails with her recent show {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nancyhoffmangallery.com/exhibition-michele-pred-sepetember-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Equality of Rights}}}\uc2  in New York, also has an installation \u8211\'20\'13 a picture of a purse emblazoned with the year 1973, when Roe was decided \u8211\'20\'13 hanging until 13 November above Manhattan\u8217\'20\'19s West Side Highway, at West 46th Street and 12th Avenue.\par \par \uc2 Though Pred has installed art explicitly concerned with reproductive justice in New York or near her home base of Oakland, California, she was wary of \u8220\'20\'1Cpreaching to the choir\u8221\'20\'1D, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI made a commitment to do more work in these other states, where it\u8217\'20\'19s most important. Where it\u8217\'20\'19s essential, actually \u8211\'20\'13 maybe it could change minds or inspire people or provoke people to think in a different way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As such, Pred intentionally chose artists whose designs were not \u8220\'20\'1Cviolent or angry or bloody\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cif it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a bloody coat hanger, that\u8217\'20\'19s not going to change anyone\u8217\'20\'19s minds in those states,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, referring to iconography based in fear of returning to a pre-Roe era of dangerous backroom or self-administered abortions. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s not inspiring anyone, that\u8217\'20\'19s not changing any minds.\u8221\'20\'1D Such imagery, however emotionally accurate, mirror \u8220\'20\'1Cthe negative images that they are presenting to us\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 misleading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  images of bloody fetuses or hellish condemnation, found along many a highway in the US.\par \par \uc2 The designs in Vote For \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights present a positive reframing rather than a pro-choice campaign attack ad \u8211\'20\'13 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as healthcare, as freedom, as life. Thank God for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Viva Ru\u237\'00\'EDz\u8217\'20\'19s New Orleans billboard, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thankgodforabortion.com/about"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shares the tagline}}}\uc2  of her activism work. \u8220\'20\'1CTalk about flipping a script, right?\u8221\'20\'1D said Pred. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the way to maybe inspire some people to think differently. I wanted to put billboards out that are hopefully thought-provoking in a positive way to people who might be voting against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Not all billboard operators, a combination of national chains and local businesses, were receptive to the messages. London-based artist Shireen Liane hoped her design, \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is healthcare\u8221\'20\'1D in green text over an American flag, would be mounted around Phoenix, Arizona, where she had previously accessed two abortions. But the design was only approved by a company in Las Vegas. \u8220\'20\'1CThe billboard companies were usually conservative, and we went into this knowing that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pred, acknowledging the lack of control in determining location for each installation. \u8220\'20\'1CThe goal was to get them up as much as possible, all over the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Whether catching a motorist\u8217\'20\'19s eye or reminding someone committed to reproductive rights to get to the polls for the midterms, Pred said she was hopeful the nationwide project will keep up crucial momentum against entrenched anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers, and the likelihood that things will get worse before they get better. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m extremely worried there\u8217\'20\'19s still not enough women who are going to go vote, not enough people who are going to go vote,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a state of emergency for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221107eib7000xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
