\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Frontline  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  workers in the US west are fatigued. Midterm results may make things worse\b0\par\par\uc2 Claire Wang in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,195 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par 15:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While Colorado remains a precarious sanctuary in the midwest, the west coast states have rallied to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 States across the western US have seen a surge in women traveling for days and spending thousands of dollars to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the five months since the supreme court overtuned Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Providers and advocates are coping with the influx through a patchwork of groups providing financial and practical assistance to patients from the south, midwest and south-west, where more than a dozen states have banned or restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 With the midterm elections just days away, those on the frontlines of this network describe daily fatigue and concern for the future. They say their ability to fortify a \u8220\'20\'1Csanctuary\u8221\'20\'1D of care in the west is at stake, as states across the country decide whether to safeguard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or vote for conservative lawmakers who could further restrict access, sending even more desperate patients their way.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado, \u8216\'20\'18a safe haven and a swing state\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The fight has become especially urgent in Colorado, which, along with New Mexico, is encircled by Republican-controlled states that have implemented near total bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In 2021, roughly 1,500 patients traveled to the state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, according to the Colorado health department. Since the supreme court ruling, that number has risen dramatically; in the first two weeks of October alone, Planned Parenthood reported seeing 900 out-of-state patients, more than half of whom were from Texas.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Linhorst, an independent obstetrician-gynecologist based in the Denver-metro area, has seen a tenfold increase in patients seeking abortions and sterilizations over the past four months. For the vast majority of women traveling from Texas and Oklahoma, the combined cost of the trek and the procedure is prohibitively high.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the wave of new patients is pushing the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s capacity and leaving staff burned out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re definitely seeing more people facing more wait times,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, noting that providers often have to skip lunch. \u8220\'20\'1CWe try to fit everyone in but there\u8217\'20\'19s only so many hours in the day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jack Teter, the regional director of government affairs at Planned Parenthood of the Rocky Mountains, described Colorado as a special access point for people living in the center of the country.\par \par \uc2 But its position as a sanctuary is precarious. While the state\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic leadership {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/07/colorado-governor-executive-order-protect-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  has taken steps}}}\uc2  to protect care access, Republicans here have a history of trying to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime \u8211\'20\'13 Democrats defeated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.denverpost.com/2022/02/25/colorado-democrats-stop-3-gop-abortion-bills/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two}}}\uc2  such proposals just this year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re both a safe haven and a swing state,\u8221\'20\'1D Teter said.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the midterms, Colorado \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates are campaigning for Democratic candidates so the party can retain control of the state legislature and expand on this year\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/hb22-1279"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reproductive Health Equity Act}}}\uc2 , which enshrined the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the constitution, and expand other protections such as requiring private insurers to cover abortions and reversing a ban on state funding for abortions.\par \par \uc2 We try to fit everyone in but there\u8217\'20\'19s only so many hours in the day\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Linhorst\par \par \uc2 Keeping control of the state legislature next month, Teter said, is the difference between losing or preserving the lone \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  refuge in the American heartland. Many women in the midwest and south, Teter said, are already making extraordinary sacrifices to get treated in Colorado \u8211\'20\'13 their choices would be even more grim should they be forced to travel to coastal states .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen patients who have driven 1,000 miles one way,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen women come with kids sleeping in the backseat because they can\u8217\'20\'19t find day care or take a day off work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A western coast \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18alliance\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Some western states have banded together in their efforts. After the supreme court ruling in June, the Democratic governors of California, Oregon and Washington vowed to make the west coast a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/us-states-abortion-rights-california-oregon-washington"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 refuge}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, freeing up {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/sacramento/news/california-launching-new-20m-fund-aimed-at-helping-out-of-state-women-travel-for-abortions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 millions}}}\uc2  in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.heraldandnews.com/news/local_news/oregon-touts-progressive-bonafides-with-15m-abortion-fund-opposition-to-dont-say-gay/article_291535d3-37dc-5ed3-aa3d-fc746c35a4c6.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 funding}}}\uc2  to help subsidize travel costs for out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 That pledge is being tested in Oregon, where the number of abortions performed rose by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opb.org/article/2022/10/31/oregon-abortions-increase-18-percent-after-roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 18%}}}\uc2  over the summer, according to recent data from the Society of Family Planning. Montana Pindell, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient navigator for Planned Parenthood Columbia-Willamette, which covers eastern and northern Oregon, said health centers have seen an explosive rise in patients from Idaho and Texas, as well as Arizona and Florida.\par \par \uc2 Some face extreme financial hardship: a Planned Parenthood clinic in Bend, Oregon, recently helped reimburse a woman who spent $1,200 on a cab ride from Idaho. Planned Parenthood is also opening new clinics across the state, including one in Ontario, a city on the Idaho border.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople have always faced hurdles even before Roe fell because of the stigma and politics around this type of care,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just really heartbreaking.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For most cases, Pindell said, Planned Parenthood would partially cover the cost of the procedure, while partnering with mutual aid groups to procure funds for travel, accommodation and other logistical needs. Many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics also work closely with local charities that have been inundated with requests for assistance.\par \par \uc2 The cost to organizations can be exorbitant. The Denver-based Cobalt \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund, which works with Linhorst\u8217\'20\'19s clinic, spent $6,000 in 2021 covering travel and boarding and roughly $200,000 covering procedures, spokesperson Laura Chapin said. Since Roe fell, spending on travel has soared: the fund has already used $160,000 on practical expenses \u8211\'20\'13 $74,000 on hotels and $40,000 on flights \u8211\'20\'13 nearly all of which came in the aftermath of the Roe decision. That\u8217\'20\'19s in addition to $250,000 spent on more than 1,000 procedures, two-thirds of which are for patients from Texas.\par \par \uc2 California, meanwhile, has in many ways emerged as ground zero of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  resistance. Over the past few months, Planned Parenthood leaders across the country have been meeting in the state\u8217\'20\'19s capital to map out a national strategy, which Hicks said includes legislation shoring up data and legal protections for patients and providers.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen women come with kids sleeping in the backseat because they can\u8217\'20\'19t find daycare or take a day off work\par \par \uc2 Jack Teter\par \par \uc2 In September, California lawmakers passed 15 bills aimed at bolstering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, including making the procedure cheaper for women with private insurance, creating scholarships for students studying to perform abortions and shielding providers from out-of-state prosecution. Voters will get a chance on Tuesday to amend the state constitution to guarantee the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe got over $200m in investment to ensure people can get care no matter what their zip code is,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jodi Hicks, the president and CEO of Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California, one of the groups that helped shape the state\u8217\'20\'19s new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 But despite the progress, advocates say they are watching Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s vote with nervous anticipation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis election is a choice between keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal and making it a crime,\u8221\'20\'1D said Teter.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221104eib400003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
