\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  insists Democrats can hold or expand Senate majority \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein in Washington \par \uc2 6,117 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2022\par 20:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Majority leader acknowledges midterms will be \u8216\'20\'18tight\u8217\'20\'19Sign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 We are five days away from the 8 November midterm elections, and last night \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363bcd68f08f79d02f03a10#block-6363bcd68f08f79d02f03a10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gave a primetime speech}}}\uc2  in which he sought to remind Americans that many Republican candidates hold views that could threaten the country\u8217\'20\'19s democracy. Meanwhile, the top Senate Democrat \uc2 Chuck Schumer{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-63640c1d8f0842be36fefe0d#block-63640c1d8f0842be36fefe0d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck an optimistic note}}}\uc2  about his party\u8217\'20\'19s chances of keeping hold of the chamber. We may soon find out if he\u8217\'20\'19s right.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a look at what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Republicans rolled their eyes at Biden\u8217\'20\'19s speech, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363c6f08f0842be36fef9a4#block-6363c6f08f0842be36fef9a4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senate\u8217\'20\'19s GOP leader calling}}}\uc2  it a distraction from crime and inflation. He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363ca0a8f08f17200b979cd#block-6363ca0a8f08f17200b979cd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was echoed by}}}\uc2  the party\u8217\'20\'19s candidate for governor in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A noted domestic violence researcher{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363c2a68f0842be36fef967#block-6363c2a68f0842be36fef967"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed with Biden\u8217\'20\'19s warnings about democracy}}}\uc2 , saying that research indicates only a minority of Americans support violence in politics \u8211\'20\'13 though that still may be as many as 13 mn people.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A top aide to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363e7408f08f79d02f03c83#block-6363e7408f08f79d02f03c83"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she has advised the former president}}}\uc2  to announce his 2024 run for office after the midterms. Some Democrats hoped Trump\u8217\'20\'19s return to the presidential campaign trail before the election would rally their voters.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act was a major legislative accomplishment for Biden, but many people aren\u8217\'20\'19t even aware it passed, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6364153d8f08f79d02f03f48#block-6364153d8f08f79d02f03f48"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a progressive polling firm found}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Across the country, vest-wearing canvassers are knocking on doors in neighborhoods and asking people about their voting history and who they live with, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/republican-door-knockers-intimidate-voters-while-hunting-voter-fraud-say-2022-11-03/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reuters reports}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The canvassers aren\u8217\'20\'19t affiliated with any government, but rather with groups aligned with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that are trying to use information gathered from their visits to prove voter fraud, according to Reuters\u8217\'20\'19 investigation. Officials worry the groups are impersonating government employees and intimidating voters. In Michigan, Reuters reports that one organization already has plans to use alleged irregularities they found to challenge voters in the swing state\u8217\'20\'19s elections on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more from the report:\par \par \uc2 The activists often seem more interested in undermining confidence in U.S. democracy than trying to improve it, said Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Maricopa County Recorder Stephen Richer, a Republican. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re hoping that we fail. They\u8217\'20\'19re hoping that mistakes occur and they\u8217\'20\'19re even trying to do things to disrupt the system,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Shasta County\uc2 , a rugged, mountainous region of more than 180,000 people where pro-Trump Republicans dominate the local government, clerk Cathy Darling Allen said she noticed problems in the middle of September when three residents complained about canvassers on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When Allen contacted the voters, they all asked whether the county had sent the canvassers. Allen replied that the visitors had nothing to do with her office.\par \par \uc2 A week later, a fourth resident called police when canvassers showed up at his door and demanded voting information that made him suspicious, according to a report by the Redding Police Department.\par \par \uc2 In a public statement issued Sept. 26, Allen warned that canvassers\u8217\'20\'19 actions amounted to intimidation and violations of election laws. \u8220\'20\'1CI was very concerned that it would have a chilling effect on people\u8217\'20\'19s willingness to be registered to vote, and that\u8217\'20\'19s not OK,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Reuters identified at least 23 state-wide or local efforts where canvassers may have crossed the line into intimidation, according to election officials and voting rights lawyers. Some carried weapons, wore badges, asked people who they\u8217\'20\'19d voted for or demanded personal information, election officials said.\par \par \uc2 The Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights\uc2 , a coalition of more than 200 civil rights groups, said it has received more such reports than in previous elections. \u8220\'20\'1CThese tactics are very concerning,\u8221\'20\'1D said YT Bell, an election adviser for the coalition.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s deciding your vote in next week\u8217\'20\'19s midterm election? \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  would like to hear from voters across the United States about the issues that are swaying their choices for House representative, Senator or governor when they head to the polls Tuesday. Details of how to reach us are at the link below:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/us-voters-what-issues-are-deciding-your-vote-next-week-midterms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US voters: what issues are deciding your vote next week?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act is one of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s biggest legislative achievements, and was passed only after months of stop-and-start negotiations that at times looked like they would lead to nothing.\par \par \uc2 But for all the drama that preceded its August signing, progressive think tank Data for Progress finds comparatively few Americans are aware of its passage:\par \par \uc2 enltrDespite the historic achievement in passing the Inflation Reduction Act, a new poll from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DataProgress?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @DataProgress}}}\uc2  finds likely voters are relatively unaware of its provisions \u8212\'20\'14 or its status.\par \par \uc2 Just 39% of voters know the Inflation Reduction Act is signed law. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BYDHXVZt9x"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/BYDHXVZt9x}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sean McElwee (@SeanMcElwee) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SeanMcElwee/status/1588207586402336770?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Look closely at the numbers and many voters express ignorance about what it would do. The most known aspect of the law is that it allows Medicare to negotiate lower prescription drug prices with pharmaceutical companies, which 44% of those surveyed are aware of. But only about a third of those surveyed know it pays for the hiring of more agents at the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 , raised the minimum tax on large corporations, or offers credits for clean energy production.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the economy top of mind for voters as they prepare to cast their ballots in the midterm elections, it is clear that Democratic messaging on the key economic provisions of the Inflation Reduction Act is failing to reach voters,\u8221\'20\'1D Data for Progress concludes. \u8220\'20\'1CAs Democrats work to keep their majority in Congress, it\u8217\'20\'19s crucial that voters are aware of what Democrats have accomplished in the past two years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 As Andrew Lawrence writes, if a Republican wins the race for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/oregon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oregon}}}\uc2  governor, it will be largely thanks to one man: a co-founder of the sportswear giant \uc2 Nike\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Phil Knight\uc2  is the 84-year-old co-founder and chair emeritus of \uc2 Nike\uc2 , the house that Michael Jordan and Tiger Woods built.\par \par \uc2 In the race to govern Oregon, a bastion of west coast liberalism, Knight has thrown full support behind the Republican Christine Drazan, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , tough-on-crime former lobbyist pushing \u8220\'20\'1Celection integrity\u8221\'20\'1D. In a rare {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/15/us/politics/kotek-drazan-oregon-governor.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interview with the New York Times}}}\uc2 , Knight made his motive clear: Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s next governor can be anyone but the Democratic nominee, \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Knight\u8217\'20\'19s lavish support of the right would seem to betray \uc2 Nike\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s pursuit of social equality and environmental protection. After all, this is the \u8220\'20\'1CJust Do It\u8221\'20\'1D brand that champions Serena Williams, that kneels with Colin Kaepernick, that featured Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s first trans female soccer player in a recent ad.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, the company has pledged millions to organizations dedicated to leveling the playing field in all spheres of life. But it has also come under fire for crafting a progressive PR image as cover while manufacturing products in Asian sweatshops with forced labor practices \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Full report:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/nov/03/nike-phil-knight-christine-drazan-tina-kotek"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why is Nike founder Phil Knight so desperate to prevent a Democratic win in Oregon?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Top Senate Democrat insists party still has shot at keeping majority\par \par \uc2 The Senate\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  believes the party can keep or even expand its majority in Congress\u8217\'20\'19s upper chamber in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections, despite polls showing its candidates losing their leads in crucial races.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI believe Democrats will hold the Senate and maybe even pick up seats,\u8221\'20\'1D Schumer said in an interview {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-congress-government-and-politics-50b9ae25b0709c74c3111d50a8fdc8f5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with the Associated Press}}}\uc2  published today, while acknowledging that the race is \u8220\'20\'1Ctight.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the summer, Democrats appeared to have a clear path to preserving their majority in the Senate as legislative victories and the shock over the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights rallied their supporters. But polls have indicated that enthusiasm ebbed as the 8 November election grows closer, and earlier this week, a survey from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/31/us/politics/democrats-republicans-senate-election-polls.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found Democrats have only slight advantages}}}\uc2  in several crucial races.\par \par \uc2 Schumer told the AP he \u8220\'20\'1Cdoesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to give the illusion that these are all slam dunks,\u8221\'20\'1D but said voters \u8220\'20\'1Care seeing how extreme these Republican candidates are and they don\u8217\'20\'19t like it. And second, they\u8217\'20\'19re seeing the Democrats are talking to them on issues they care about, and that we\u8217\'20\'19ve accomplished a great deal on things.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 As the midterm elections loom in the US and Republican hopes of retaking Congress rise, it appears it is now a matter of when, not if, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will announce his third White House run. Martin Pengelly reports\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has trailed another White House campaign ever since his 2020 defeat by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , a contest Trump refused to concede, pursuing the lie about electoral fraud which fueled the deadly attack on Congress and his second impeachment.\par \par \uc2 In Texas last month, Trump said: \u8220\'20\'1CIn order to make our country successful, safe and glorious again, I will probably have to do it again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Now, a flurry of reports say Trump will move swiftly after the midterms, seeking to capitalise on likely Republican wins fueled by focusing on economic anxieties and law and order.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m like 95% he\u8217\'20\'19s going to run,\u8221\'20\'1D Reince Priebus, the former Republican chairman who became Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first White House chief of staff, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-donald-trump-41b12f18f536017920d2f74803980899"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Associated Press}}}\uc2  this week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe real question is are other big challengers going to run? If President Trump runs, he will be very difficult for any Republican to defeat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/donald-trump-white-house-2024-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s third run for the White House appears a matter of when not if}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 More from Hillary Clinton \u8217\'20\'19s interview with CNN earlier, in which she discussed Republican midterms messaging that seems set for success next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . Photograph: Franck Robichon/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 The former first lady, senator, secretary of state and Democratic presidential nominee focused on \u8220\'20\'1Cthis emphasis on crime that we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in every ad that I run across from the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI find it ironic and frankly disturbing that when Paul Pelosi is attacked by an intruder in his own home with a hammer, the Republicans go silent about that crime.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re not concerned about voter safety, they just want to keep voters scared because they feel that if voters are scared, if they\u8217\'20\'19re responding to negative messages, they\u8217\'20\'19ll have a better chance and that\u8217\'20\'19s really regrettable. Unfortunately, sometimes it works, and we can\u8217\'20\'19t let people just hear that and believe it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pelosi, the 82-year-old husband of the Democratic House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , was attacked in San Francisco last week. Clinton referred to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/kari-lake-mocks-paul-pelosi-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comments about the attack by Republicans}}}\uc2  including Kari Lake, the Trump-aligned candidate for governor in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a horrifying incident,\u8221\'20\'1D Clinton said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut sadly a real indicator of where we are in our country right now that you would have people on the Republican ticket, like the woman running in Arizona, laughing about an attack on anyone, let alone an 82-year-old man whose wife happens to be the second-in-line to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am rarely shocked anymore, but the reaction I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen from a number of Republicans, both in person and online making fun of that attack, somehow trying to turn it into a joke, the same party that wants us to be worried about crime. The hypocrisy is incredibly obvious.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Clinton also discussed threats to democracy around the world \u8211\'20\'13 and linked them to what she said was the Republican threat at home.\par \par \uc2 She said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a time of great ferment, and it is a time when the United States should be standing strongly on behalf of our values of democracy and freedom, of opportunity and equality, instead of being engaged in this culture war driven by the political opportunism of people on the Republican side of the ledger.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8230\'20\'26 The best thing we can do to lead the world in this struggle between democracy and autocracy is to get our own house in order and I hope that we\u8217\'20\'19ll do that starting Tuesday.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.00pm GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/hillary-clinton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hillary Clinton}}}\uc2  has been talking about the economy \u8211\'20\'13 which is top of many people\u8217\'20\'19s minds as the midterm elections roar towards us and voting is underway.\par \par \uc2 She acknowledged in talking to CNN earlier today that the economy was of course something that needed to be talked about this election cycle. Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 prospects are blighted by record inflation and a cost of living crunch and Clinton wants them to talk up their record and put current economic challenges into the wider context.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat I wish we could convey more effectively, if you look at what has been accomplished in the first two years of the Biden presidency, with Congress working hand in hand, there has been an enormous amount of commitment of new building, new infrastructure, new investments in manufacturing, new ways to lower healthcare costs,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The former first lady and secretary of state added: \u8220\'20\'1CIn fact the work that\u8217\'20\'19s been done by the Democrats in helping the economy and helping people deal with what is global inflation, not just American inflation, is truly impressive, and we\u8217\'20\'19ve got to get that message across more effectively.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HillaryClinton?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @HillaryClinton}}}\uc2  : "The work that is being done by the Democrats in helping the economy and helping people deal with what is global inflation, not just American inflation, is truly impressive, and we've got to get that message across more effectively." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/rZQx1ItZ6I"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/rZQx1ItZ6I}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Hill (@thehill) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/thehill/status/1588222222811922437?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Clinton is one of the headliners at a Get Out The Vote event in New York City to bolster New York state\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kathy-hochul"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kathy Hochul}}}\uc2  , who is not home and dry against her Republican challenger \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 State attorney general Letitia James will be there as well as other grandees and the top headliner will be US vice president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US government\uc2  representatives visit jailed basketball star Brittney Griner in Russia\par \par \uc2 The White House has announced that US representatives today visited US basketball player {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/brittney-griner"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brittney Griner}}}\uc2  in Russia, where she has been imprisoned since the early days of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s war on Ukraine, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 The two-time Olympic gold medallist was arrested on 17 February at a Moscow airport with vape cartridges containing cannabis oil, which is banned in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/russia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russia}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She was sentenced on 4 August to nine years in a penal colony. Last month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/25/us-basketball-star-brittney-griners-appeal-rejected-by-russian-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 her appeal}}}\uc2  against that harsh sentence failed and there are fears Griner could be moved to one of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s far-flung prison colonies within weeks.\par \par \uc2 US basketball player Brittney Griner appears on a screen via video link from the detention centre during a court hearing to consider an appeal against her prison sentence, in Krasnogorsk, Moscow Region, Russia on October 25. Photograph: Evgenia Novozhenina/Reuters\par \par \uc2 Although at that time, Griner\u8217\'20\'19s legal team said she was not \u8220\'20\'1Cexpecting any miracles\u8221\'20\'1D from the appeals process, the decision nonetheless would be a blow to the sports star, who pleaded guilty to the drug charges in July and has thrown herself several times on the mercy of the Russian court only to be given an unusually harsh sentence, even for Russia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are told she is doing as well as can be expected under the circumstances,\u8221\'20\'1D White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  told reporters today aboard Air Force One as she accompanied US president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on an election campaign trip to New Mexico, followed by California.\par \par \uc2 FILE - Phoenix Mercury\u8217\'20\'19s Brittney Griner, left, watching practice with teammate Diana Taurasi in 2018 in Arizona. Photograph: Matt York/AP\par \par \uc2 Earlier in October, Brittney Griner\u8217\'20\'19s wife, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/cherelle-griner-brittney-griner/?ftag=CNM-00-10aab7e&linkId=184506777"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cherelle Griner told CBS Mornings}}}\uc2  that Brittney, who was on her way to play in Russia during the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  offseason when she was arrested, is afraid of being abandoned by the United States.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe\u8217\'20\'19s very afraid about being left and forgotten in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/russia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russia}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Cherelle Griner said.\par \par \uc2 She said Brittney told her in a phone call that she felt \u8220\'20\'1Clike my life just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2022/jul/14/brittney-griner-trial-wnba-lgbtq-trailblazer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brittney Griner\u8217\'20\'19s story always transcended sport. She\u8217\'20\'19s a real American trailblazer}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Democrats in deep trouble on crime messaging, veteran pollster argues\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s clear that the spike in voter support Democrats experienced over the summer has worn off in the final weeks before the midterms, raising the possibility of a disastrous Tuesday for the party as it tries to defend its slim hold on both chambers of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Longtime Democratic strategist \uc2 Stanley B. Greenberg\uc2  has published an explanation of one reason why Democrats failed to keep their momentum: their own voters lost faith in their ability to tackle crime.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prospect.org/politics/how-democrats-mishandled-crime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Writing in The American Prospect}}}\uc2 , Greenberg argues that Republicans effectively used increasing fears of violence nationwide to tar Democrats as soft on the issue, and the strategy was so potent even some racial groups that traditionally vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s allies saw the GOP as better able to tackle the problem.\par \par \uc2 Greenberg based his conclusions on a polling effort he oversaw:\par \par \uc2 New York City has seen citywide shooting incidents increase by 13 percent compared to July 2021, and the number of murders increased for the month by 34 percent compared to this time last year. Philadelphia and Chicago experienced prominent shoot-outs on the subway, and in Philadelphia overall shootings have increased by 3 percent and violent crimes are up 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 As a result, crime was a top-tier issue in the midterm election, and that included Blacks, who ranked it almost as high as the cost of living in poll after poll. For Hispanics and Asian Americans, crime came just below the cost of living as a priority. And Republicans continued to remind voters that Democrats continued to support \u8220\'20\'1Cdefunding the police,\u8221\'20\'1D even by linking candidates to organizations they took money from, like Planned Parenthood, which back in 2020 called for defunding.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats had so little credibility on crime that any message I tested this year against the Republicans ended up losing us votes, even messages that voters previously liked.\par \par \uc2 The only message that worked with voters was one in which Democrats promised to greatly expand police forces and publicly called out members seen as not doing enough to fight crime, Greenberg writes. He adds that it\u8217\'20\'19s a far cry from much of the party\u8217\'20\'19s messaging since the racial justice protests that began in the summer of 2020, after which many Democrats focused more on police abuses than on communities\u8217\'20\'19 fears of violence:\par \par \uc2 In a mid-October poll, I was able to test a crime message that got heard. It got heard because it dramatized more police, said Democrats heard our communities on violent crime, and also called out the small minority of Democrats who failed to address violent crime, and said, \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats in Congress are mainstream\u8221\'20\'1D and support our \u8220\'20\'1Cfirst responders.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To be honest, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to open up this debate during the campaign when Democrats could do little to address it. That is why I am writing this article now, being published right before the election.\par \par \uc2 Our effective crime message began with respect for police, but this time, the Democrat proposes to add 100,000 more police. That is a pretty dramatic offer that says, my crime plan begins with many more police. The message includes the same urgent reforms, but also adds, \u8220\'20\'1Cthose very communities want us to get behind law enforcement\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cfight violent crime as a top priority.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This crime message defeats by 11 points a Republican crime message that hits Democrats for defunding the police, being with Biden who is soft on crime, and presiding over Democratic cities with record homicide rates. Democrats are in so much trouble on crime, yet this message wins dramatically in the base and competes with working-class targets.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The day so far\par \par \uc2 We are five days away from the 8 November midterm elections, and \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363bcd68f08f79d02f03a10#block-6363bcd68f08f79d02f03a10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last night gave a primetime speech}}}\uc2  in which he sought to remind Americans that many Republicans on ballots this year hold views that could threaten the country\u8217\'20\'19s democracy. We\u8217\'20\'19ll soon find out if voters believed him.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a look at what has happened today so far:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Republicans rolled their eyes at Biden\u8217\'20\'19s speech, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363c6f08f0842be36fef9a4#block-6363c6f08f0842be36fef9a4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senate\u8217\'20\'19s GOP leader calling}}}\uc2  it a distraction from crime and inflation, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363ca0a8f08f17200b979cd#block-6363ca0a8f08f17200b979cd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was echoed by}}}\uc2  the party\u8217\'20\'19s candidate for governor in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A noted domestic violence researcher{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363c2a68f0842be36fef967#block-6363c2a68f0842be36fef967"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed with Biden\u8217\'20\'19s warnings about democracy}}}\uc2 , saying that research indicates only a minority of Americans support violence in politics \u8211\'20\'13 though that still may be as many as 13 mn people.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A top aide to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/nov/03/midterms-2022-biden-speech-trump-election-politics-latest?page=with:block-6363e7408f08f79d02f03c83#block-6363e7408f08f79d02f03c83"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she has advised the former president}}}\uc2  to announce his 2024 run for office after the midterms. Some Democrats hoped Trump\u8217\'20\'19s return to the presidential campaign trail before the vote would be positive for democratic turnout.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 One of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s top advisors Kellyanne Conway held forth with reporters today about what she advised the former president when it comes to announcing his next run for office, Semafor reports.\par \par \uc2 Trump is widely expected to run for president again in 2024, but the bigger question is when he will announce. Some Democrats hoped he would so before the midterms, so they can steer voters\u8217\'20\'19 attention back to the divisive former leader.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what Conway, one of his best known aides, told reporters:\par \par \uc2 enltrKellyanne Conway, at a roundtable with reporters, says she advised Trump not to announce before the midterms "if he does at all." She then said it'll happen soon and mentioned \uc2 Tiffany Trump\uc2 's wedding as his estate.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kadia Goba (@kadiagoba) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kadiagoba/status/1588163687164133378?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr"I personally think he should do what he wants to do and I understand that he wants to make right all the issues that he made right while he was president." -- Conway when asked if she personally thinks Trump should run in 2024.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kadia Goba (@kadiagoba) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kadiagoba/status/1588166992250503168?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrConway's projection on Trump's biggest threat. to the presidency: a spoiler. She did not name a specific potential Republican candidate.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kadia Goba (@kadiagoba) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kadiagoba/status/1588173124142796800?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  argued democracy is on the ballot on Tuesday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/amy-westervelt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amy Westervelt}}}\uc2  reports that outcome could also have a major impact on climate change:\par \par \uc2 Climate is on the ballot in a big way this November, despite the fact that it is not front and center in any of the campaigns. Even when it comes to voter turnout, the mood of climate voters has been a topic of conversation among political consultants for months.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSeveral months ago I was very concerned about the apathy we were seeing in young climate voters because of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 failure to even talk about the successes they have had,\u8221\'20\'1D Rania Batrice, political strategist and founder of Batrice & Associates, says. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I do feel like there\u8217\'20\'19s been a little bit of a renewed sense of urgency. In Georgia, for example, early voting just started and it\u8217\'20\'19s already breaking all kinds of records.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Batrice says the fallout from the supreme court decision in Dobbs, which overturned the Roe v Wade precedent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is a big part of that urgency, but that the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s increased action on climate this year plays a role too.\par \par \uc2 For the campaigns she\u8217\'20\'19s working on this midterm cycle \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Beto O\u8217\'20\'19Rourke\uc2  for governor of Texas, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  for Senate in Pennsylvania, Charles Booker for Senate in Kentucky and Mandela Barnes for Senate in Wisconsin \u8211\'20\'13 Batrice says her advice on climate is simple: \u8220\'20\'1CMeet people where they\u8217\'20\'19re at, and talk about climate in ways that relate to people\u8217\'20\'19s daily lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/climate-crisis-midterm-elections-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A renewed sense of urgency\u8217\'20\'19: climate on the ballot in US midterm elections}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/chrismcgreal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chris McGreal}}}\uc2  reports that a major pro-Israel group is facing criticism for backing Republicans who denied the 2020 election, but not a Democratic candidate who would make history if elected:\par \par \uc2 More than 240 Jewish American voters in Pittsburgh have signed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pdfhost.io/v/YwJnncYeH_Public_Letter_Supporting_Summer_Lee_Against_Harmful_AIPAC_Attacks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter}}}\uc2  denouncing the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest pro-Israel group for backing extremist Republican election candidates while spending millions of dollars to oppose a Democrat who would be Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s first Black female member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 The letter condemned the powerful American {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/israel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Israel}}}\uc2  Public Affairs Committee (\uc2 Aipac\uc2 ) for its attempts to defeat Summer Lee, a candidate for the district that includes Pittsburgh, after failing to block her during the Democratic primaries earlier this year because of her criticisms of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s oppression of the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 The signatories said they were \u8220\'20\'1Coutraged that at this critical moment in American history, \uc2 Aipac\uc2  has chosen to cast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  like Lee as extremists\u8221\'20\'1D while endorsing more than 100 Republican candidates who voted to overturn the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 The letter suggested that \uc2 Aipac\uc2  does not represent the views of the majority of American Jews and is working against their interests by also endorsing Republicans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/18/pro-israel-lobby-group-aipac-midterms-election-deniers-and-extremist-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who promote white supremacy}}}\uc2 , a particularly sensitive issue in a city where 11 worshippers at the Tree of Life synagogues were murdered in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pittnews.com/article/151986/news/the-tree-of-life-massacre-what-happened/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 antisemitic attack}}}\uc2  four years ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe also condemn \uc2 AIPAC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s endorsement of lawmakers who have promoted the antisemitic \u8216\'20\'18Great Replacement\u8217\'20\'19 conspiracy theory that helped inspire the murder of eleven members of the three synagogues housed at Tree of Life,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/pittsburgh-jews-denounce-aipac-republican-extremists-pro-israel-lobby"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pittsburgh Jews decry pro-Israel group\u8217\'20\'19s support for Republican extremists}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Georgia is home of one of the country\u8217\'20\'19s tightest Senate races, and yesterday, Republican candidate and former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  attempted to contrast himself with \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/martin-pengelly"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Martin Pengelly}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 Hitting back after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/barack-obama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Barack Obama}}}\uc2  questioned his fitness for a \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seat, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CPut my r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 against his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Obama, 61, was a civil rights attorney and community organiser in Chicago, an Illinois state politician, a US senator from 2005 to 2008, then 44th president of the United States from 2009 to 2017.\par \par \uc2 Walker, 60, won the Heisman Trophy, the top honor in college football. He had a stellar \uc2 NFL\uc2  career, mostly with the \uc2 Dallas Cowboys\uc2 , then went into business.\par \par \uc2 His entry into politics, endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and seeking to unseat the Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in Georgia, has been anything but smooth. Less than a week from election day, however, the two men are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/polls/senate/2022/georgia/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 locked in a close race}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/herschel-walker-barack-obama-midterms-georgia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Herschel Walker hits back at Barack Obama: \u8216\'20\'18Put my r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 against his\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 How big of a deal are Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s midterms? So big that spending on advertisements has exceeded the 2020 presidential elections. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/adam-gabbatt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Adam Gabbatt}}}\uc2  takes a look at the messages Americans are seeing on TV, the web and elsewhere:\par \par \uc2 As the US midterm elections loom, Republicans and Democrats have spent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/10/22/1130445425/midterm-elections-biden-dr-oz-tim-ryan-ads"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost $10bn}}}\uc2  (\u163\'00\'A38.6bn) so far on ads. It\u8217\'20\'19s a staggering figure, one that exceeds even the spending on the 2020 presidential election, and is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://adimpact.com/2022-political-spending-projections/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost triple}}}\uc2  the amount spent during the last midterms.\par \par \uc2 Both parties \u8211\'20\'13 and their dark money backers \u8211\'20\'13 have splashed exorbitant amounts on TV, digital and print advertising, but their focus has been very different.\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a key issue. The party has spent almost 20 times more than it did on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ads in the 2018 midterms, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/10/22/1130445425/midterm-elections-biden-dr-oz-tim-ryan-ads"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NPR reported}}}\uc2 . For Republicans, there have been different messages: that inflation, crime and taxes are out of control.\par \par \uc2 The result has been a whirling atmosphere for the average American, where to turn on the TV is frequently to see the two parties, and their candidates, talking straight past one another about different things.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/republicans-democrats-political-ads-us-midterms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ad wars: Republicans and Democrats spend big to push messages on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 , inflation and crime}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, Kari Lake is running for governor on a platform of refusing to accept \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s election, or even her own potential defeat. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/maanvi-singh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maanvi Singh}}}\uc2  reports on how Lake has embraced \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Maga ideology in her quest for the state\u8217\'20\'19s top office:\par \par \uc2 Local news anchor Kari Lake refused to announce that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had won {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/arizona"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona}}}\uc2  on election night two years ago. Now, she\u8217\'20\'19s the telegenic new face of Maga Republicanism, poised to possibly become the state\u8217\'20\'19s next governor.\par \par \uc2 With early voting underway, polls show Lake in a dead heat with her opponent \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic secretary of state. The contest will test the strength of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s enduring influence on the Republican party and its supporters. And the entire enterprise of free elections in Arizona hangs in the balance.\par \par \uc2 If Lake wins, her administration will oversee the 2024 elections in a key state that could help determine who wins the presidency. She could work with the likes of Mark Finchem, the far-right Oath Keeper who is running to become the state\u8217\'20\'19s top election official. Already, she has said she\u8217\'20\'19ll only accept the 2022 election results if \u8220\'20\'1Cfair, honest and transparent\u8221\'20\'1D by her standards, declining to say whether she\u8217\'20\'19d accept defeat.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/03/kari-lake-trump-arizona-maga-republicanism-midterms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A really dangerous candidate\u8217\'20\'19: Kari Lake, the new face of Maga Republicanism}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 When Biden warned about Republican extremists last night, just who did he mean? \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/sam-levine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Levine}}}\uc2  and Rachel Leingang report on the candidates who present a direct threat to democracy and are on the ballot in Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s vote:\par \par \uc2 There are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/11/us-midterms-congress-senate-house-races-to-watch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several races on the ballot}}}\uc2  this fall that will have profound consequences for American democracy. In several states, Republican candidates who doubt the 2020 election results, or in some cases actively worked to overturn them, are running for positions in which they would have tremendous influence over how votes are cast and counted. If these candidates win, there is deep concern they could use their offices to spread baseless information about election fraud and try to prevent the rightful winners of elections from being seated.\par \par \uc2 In total, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2022/10/06/elections-deniers-midterm-elections-2022/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 291 Republicans }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a majority of the party\u8217\'20\'19s nominees this cycle, have questioned the outcome of the 2020 presidential election, according to a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  tally.\par \par \uc2 Election deniers are running for offices up and down the ballot that could play a critical role in future elections.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re running to be governors, who play a role in enacting election rules. They\u8217\'20\'19re running to be secretaries of state, who oversee voting and ballot counting. They\u8217\'20\'19re running attorneys general, who are responsible for investigating allegations of fraud handling litigation in high-stakes election suits. They\u8217\'20\'19re running to be members of Congress, who vote to certify the presidential vote every four years. They\u8217\'20\'19re running to be state lawmakers, who can pass voting laws, launch investigations, and, according to some fringe legal theories, try and block the certification of presidential electors.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/01/us-election-deniers-trump-big-lie-republican-midterm-candidates-running"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US midterms 2022: the key candidates who threaten democracy}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican candidate for governor Tudor Dixon brought up Biden\u8217\'20\'19s speech as she campaigned today:\par \par \uc2 enltrDixon says Democrats are trying to \u8220\'20\'1Ccontrol people with fear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLook at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s speech yesterday! \u8216\'20\'18Do not vote for Republicans because they have better plans.\u8217\'20\'19 That\u8217\'20\'19s what I heard.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 David Weigel (@daveweigel) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/daveweigel/status/1588158077299265537?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2022}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/2022-election-forecast/governor/michigan/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Polls indicate}}}\uc2  Dixon is trailing incumbent Democrat \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in the state.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In his response to Biden\u8217\'20\'19s speech last night, Senate Republican leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  sought to refocus the attention to crime, the economy and immigration, issues the GOP has campaigned on nationwide:\par \par \uc2 enltrPresident Biden is desperate to change the subject from inflation, crime, and open borders. Now he's claiming that democracy only works if his party wins.\par \par \uc2 What nonsense. Americans aren't buying it.\par \par \uc2 Ask how the last two years have affected your family, and then get out and vote!\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Leader McConnell (@LeaderMcConnell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LeaderMcConnell/status/1587993151003889667?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Stepping away for a minute from its context in relation to the midterms, here\u8217\'20\'19s what \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  political violence expert Robert A. Pape had to say about the substance of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s speech last night:\par \par \uc2 The data shows that President Biden is right: The violent threat to our democracy comes from an extreme minority\u8212\'20\'14 not just a minority of all Americans but a minority of even pro-Trump Republicans. Our national survey from September shows that an estimated 13 million adults support the use of force for Trump. That means well over 80% of Americans, both Democrats and most Republicans, reject violence for political reasons. Bipartisan majority opposes political violence. Now it is crucial to turn that democratic majority into a bipartisan coalition against political violence.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the University of Chicago Project on Security and Threats\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cpost.uchicago.edu/publications/the_jan_6th_hearings_are_reducing_the_violent_threat_to_democracy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 latest study}}}\uc2  on political violence, which indicates only a small minority would support restoring \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to office through violent means. But as the data makes clear, that group may be five percent of those surveyed, but it\u8217\'20\'19s still representative of about 13 mn people.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  gave an address warning of dire consequences if voters elect Republican extremists to office next Tuesday. Today, he\u8217\'20\'19s heading west to campaign not in the tightest contests or those considered the best chances to oust sitting Republicans, but rather for incumbent Democrats trying to hang on to their seats.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the perfect illustration of the dynamic for Biden, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/biden-approval-rating/?ex_cid=rrpromo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than a year after his approval ratings tanked}}}\uc2  and stayed there. His presidential bully pulpit allowed him to make a speech where he warned, \u8220\'20\'1CMake no mistake, democracy is on the ballot for all of us.\u8221\'20\'1D But his unpopularity had forced him into a cautious approach to campaigning \u8211\'20\'13 almost a tacit admission that when it comes to the races that could define the next two years of his presidency, his ability to help is limited.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-biden-politics-new-mexico-california-9f075331780338a41559880656790482"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Associated Press today}}}\uc2  published a piece looking at what today\u8217\'20\'19s travel itinerary says about the dynamic. Here\u8217\'20\'19s how they put it:\par \par \uc2 His itinerary illustrates the limited political clout of a president who has been held at arm\u8217\'20\'19s length by most Democrats in tough races this cycle. It also suggests that the president, whose approval rating remains underwater, has concluded that he can be most effective using the waning days before polls close to shore up support for Democratic candidates in areas that he easily won in 2020.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 'We\u8217\'20\'19re facing a defining moment', Biden argued. Will voters agree?\par \par \uc2 Good morning, US politics blog readers. Last night, \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/02/joe-biden-speech-political-violence-preserve-democracy-midterm-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 made a primetime address}}}\uc2  to warn Americans about the threats to democracy posed by political violence and Republicans who deny the outcome of the 2020 election. It\u8217\'20\'19s a salient message, given that many GOP candidates nationwide have embraced baseless conspiracy theories about the election that brought Biden to power, but there\u8217\'20\'19s a problem: poll after poll has shown most Americans have a sour view of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s time in office, to the point that the president is avoiding many swing states in the final days before the 8 November midterms. Last night\u8217\'20\'19s speech was meant as a reminder to voters of what the stakes are in next week\u8217\'20\'19s elections. We\u8217\'20\'19ll see if they care.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else is happening today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Biden heads out west, first to deliver remarks on student debt relief at a community college in Albuquerque, New Mexico, then to rally with democratic candidates. After that, he\u8217\'20\'19s in San Diego, California, where he will appear at an event for democratic congressman \uc2 Mike Levin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will go to New York to rally Democrats.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Midterm countdown: we are five days away from Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221103eib3001v8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
