\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans and Democrats are spending billions on ads \u8211\'20\'13 with very different messages\b0\par\par\uc2 Adam Gabbatt \par \uc2 1,785 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2022\par 14:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While Democrats focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Republicans are talking about inflation, crime and taxes\par \par \uc2 As the US midterm elections loom, Republicans and Democrats have spent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/10/22/1130445425/midterm-elections-biden-dr-oz-tim-ryan-ads"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost $10bn}}}\uc2  (\u163\'00\'A38.6bn) so far on ads. It\u8217\'20\'19s a staggering figure, one that exceeds even the spending on the 2020 presidential election, and is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://adimpact.com/2022-political-spending-projections/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost triple}}}\uc2  the amount spent during the last midterms.\par \par \uc2 Both parties \u8211\'20\'13 and their dark money backers \u8211\'20\'13 have splashed exorbitant amounts on TV, digital and print advertising, but their focus has been very different.\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a key issue. The party has spent almost 20 times more than it did on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ads in the 2018 midterms, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/10/22/1130445425/midterm-elections-biden-dr-oz-tim-ryan-ads"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NPR reported}}}\uc2 . For Republicans, there have been different messages: that inflation, crime and taxes are out of control.\par \par \uc2 The result has been a whirling atmosphere for the average American, where to turn on the TV is frequently to see the two parties, and their candidates, talking straight past one another about different things.\par \par \uc2 A\par \par \uc2 bortion\par \par \uc2 After the conservative-dominated supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned}}}\uc2  the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, the issue of who should have control over women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies has been front and center in many midterm races.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have run more than 240 ads related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, seeking to draw attention to the extreme positions of many Republican candidates. These largely centered the personal stories of women who have had abortions. One of the most powerful ads has run in South Carolina, where \uc2 Joe Cunningham\uc2 , a Democrat, is bidding to defeat \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , the state\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wagx_taMFuM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the ad}}}\uc2 , a woman named Fran explains that she was raped by two men when she was 12 years old. She later found out she was pregnant. Fran was able to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to Roe v Wade, then recently decided, which legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRoe versus Wade gave me the opportunity to become an educator, a mother and grandmother,\u8221\'20\'1D Fran says in the ad.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI did what was best for an 88-pound 12-year-old with no other options. I am a survivor of rape: my body is not yours, and it is not the state\u8217\'20\'19s, it\u8217\'20\'19s mine \u8211\'20\'13 yet our governor, \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , wants to ban all abortions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, Democrats have repeatedly targeted Mehmet Oz, a celebrity doctor running as a Republican for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , over his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and an ad launched in mid-October features a Pennsylvania doctor describing how, pre-Roe v Wade, he was trained to treat the victims of \u8220\'20\'1Cback-alley abortions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToo often, women died. I thought those days were long behind us. But not so, with Mehmet Oz,\u8221\'20\'1D the doctor says.\par \par \uc2 One of the most harrowing ads of the entire election cycle comes from \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , a Democratic congressman for California.\par \par \uc2 It shows a family eating dinner at home when two police officers to arrest the mother for \u8220\'20\'1Cunlawful termination of a pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D. When her partner attempts to intervene, the officers draw their guns on him, prompting screaming from the couple\u8217\'20\'19s young children as the woman is handcuffed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CElections have consequences,\u8221\'20\'1D a voiceover says. \u8220\'20\'1CStop Republicans from criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  everywhere.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republicans, by contrast, have spent a fraction of the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 total on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ads. When candidates have addressed the issue, there has been a two-pronged approach: commercials have claimed, often spuriously, that their Democratic opponents are too extreme on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the Republican candidates themselves are moderate.\par \par \uc2 An ad that aired in Arizona in September, where Republican Blake Masters is hoping to win Mark Kelly\u8217\'20\'19s Senate seat, managed to combine both.\par \par \uc2 His ad, prosaically titled Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s Mark Kelly Supports Painful Late Abortions, is full of mistruths and dishonest statements \u8211\'20\'13 a theme that has run through a majority of TV ads nationwide during the election campaign \u8211\'20\'13 claiming wrongly that Kelly supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cright up to the due date\u8221\'20\'1D, and stating that Masters himself has sought \u8220\'20\'1Ccompromise\u8221\'20\'1D on the issue, despite the Republican {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/blake-masters-arizona-abortion_n_632c7455e4b0d12b54027d69"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 having backed proposals}}}\uc2  that would ban all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 C\par \par \uc2 ost of living\par \par \uc2 The big spending from Republicans has come on cost of living issues, including taxation and inflation, which reached a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jul/13/high-us-gas-food-prices-june-led-to-largest-annual-increase-inflation-40-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40-year high}}}\uc2  of 9.1% in June and was at 8.2% in September. Across the country, Republican ads have sought to blame \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for the rise, frequently citing the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/03/11/biden-1point9-trillion-covid-relief-package-thursday-afternoon.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $1.9tn coronavirus relief bill}}}\uc2  he signed in March 2021 as the cause.\par \par \uc2 In September, ads on inflation accounted for 32% of all pro-GOP advertising, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mediaproject.wesleyan.edu/issues-sept22/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Wesleyan Media Project}}}\uc2 , and in South Carolina, Republican Nancy Mace has tied the rise in price of everyday items not just to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , too, in a TV ad called Eggs.\par \par \uc2 The ad shows Mace, a Republican congresswoman, pouring a glass of milk and cooking some bacon. The price of both has increased, she notes, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CI have had it with crazy inflation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the ad continues, Mace offers her plan to bring down the cost of living. It is an unusual plan.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHere\u8217\'20\'19s what I\u8217\'20\'19m going to do to Biden\u8217\'20\'19s tax and spend agenda,\u8221\'20\'1D Mace says. She then cracks an egg and tips the contents into a frying pan.\par \par \uc2 Other ads have been less avant-garde. An ad running against \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic senator, also features a clip of some bacon, along with a gas pump, as a breathless voiceover claims Georgia has been \u8220\'20\'1Chit hard by sky-high inflation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Economists tend to agree that the American Rescue Plan did worsen inflation, Vox {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/2022/10/17/23401726/inflation-stimulus-american-rescue-plan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported recently}}}\uc2 , but there are varying estimates as to how much, and others note that the plan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/us-policy/2022/10/09/inflation-economy-biden-covid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 did improve the economy}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats, meanwhile, haven\u8217\'20\'19t done much in the way of pushing back. Wesleyan said inflation makes up only 8% of the party and its backers\u8217\'20\'19 ads, and some Democratic leaders themselves {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2022-election/democrats-struggle-message-inflation-final-midterm-push-rcna52676"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agree}}}\uc2  that their message has been unconvincing.\par \par \uc2 The party seems to be picking up the idea up, though, and has released ads on the economy in the last week, including one in New Hampshire which is undermined slightly by some rather unconvincing acting:\par \par \uc2 T\par \par \uc2 he Party\par \par \uc2 bosses\par \par \uc2 In a neat symmetry, both parties appear to be running from their leaders. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/interactive/2022/political-ads/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Washington Post}}}\uc2 , since early September Democrats have spent just $3m on ads centering \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and Republicans have spent a mere $807,000 on ads highlighting Trump.\par \par \uc2 Each party has, though, run plenty of ads focusing on their opposing party\u8217\'20\'19s bosses. Republicans have blasted out ad after ad criticizing Biden, mostly over spending during the pandemic. Mindful of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s low approval rating \u8211\'20\'13 which is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/biden-approval-rating/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently averaging}}}\uc2  about 42% \u8211\'20\'13 Republicans have sought to tie Democratic candidates across the country to the president.\par \par \uc2 That effort contributed to possibly the daftest ad of the entire campaign season: a singsong affair titled Hidin\u8217\'20\'19 Biden that ran against Democratic congresswoman \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2  in Kansas:\par \par \uc2 Complete with lyrics like: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , what\u8217\'20\'19s she hidin\u8217\'20\'19, \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , she\u8217\'20\'19s hidin\u8217\'20\'19 Biden\u8221\'20\'1D, the piano-driven tune highlights that Davids has frequently voted for Biden-backed policies.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a similar but less jaunty story in New Hampshire, where voters are warned that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , the incumbent US senator who is being challenged by Don Bolduc, a Republican and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bostonglobe.com/2022/09/17/opinion/this-republicans-pivot-should-fool-no-one/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an election denier}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cvotes with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over 96% of the time\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 Republicans are clearly banking that Biden is unpopular enough to turn voters away from candidates who have even the loosest connection to the president. The GOP, as FiveThirtyEight {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/videos/we-watched-100-republican-tv-ads-so-you-dont-have-to/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pointed out}}}\uc2 , has replicated this format in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rcmR6GghpwI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f_IeybXRCIo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michigan}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nED9dTfp4hk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nhrTE2ZUhw0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 C\par \par \uc2 rime\par \par \uc2 Crime is the only issue where Republicans and Democrats come close to equal spending, according to the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . Republicans, frequently pushing a dystopian vision of cities ridden by murder and violent crime, have spent $49m on ads discussing crime since early September, compared to $36m invested by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The number of murders in major cities has fallen so far in 2022, but remains above the numbers in 2019, and a survey {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://majorcitieschiefs.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by the Major Cities Chiefs Association}}}\uc2  found that violent crime had risen 4.2% through the first six months of this year compared with 2021.\par \par \uc2 That has given Republicans plenty of fodder to paint Democrats as soft on crime, in some cases, with racist overtones. \u8220\'20\'1CIn states as disparate as Wisconsin and New Mexico, ads have labeled a Black candidate as \u8216\'20\'18different\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18dangerous\u8217\'20\'19 and darkened a white man\u8217\'20\'19s hands as they portrayed him as a criminal,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 New York Times{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/25/us/politics/crime-ads-racism-republicans.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  of the trend.\par \par \uc2 Mandela Barnes is the subject of the Wisconsin ad, which ends with Barnes\u8217\'20\'19s face positioned next to \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  and \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 all women of color \u8211\'20\'13 while the words \u8220\'20\'1Cdifferent\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cdangerous\u8221\'20\'1D flash across the screen:\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, Oz has run several ads on crime, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VEMwQiZ2trw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claiming}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s kids aren\u8217\'20\'19t safe in our communities.\u8221\'20\'1D One spot, titled Crazy Dangerous Ideas, accuses Fetterman of \u8220\'20\'1Cemptying our prisons\u8221\'20\'1D, which, according to the ad, would lead to \u8220\'20\'1Cmore hardened criminals on our streets\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 Democrats, for their part, have run ads featuring themselves with police officers in an effort to rebut Republican claims that the left will defund police forces.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman has created ads highlighting the work he did to bring down violent crime in Braddock, where he spent 13 years as mayor, with one {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GenzEUEsp6I"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 featuring a local sheriff}}}\uc2 . Barnes, meanwhile, recruited a retired police sergeant for an ad in September:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI worked on the force for 30 years,\u8221\'20\'1D the retired officer, called Rick, says.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve seen plenty of politicians. But Mandela, he\u8217\'20\'19s the real deal. Mandela doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to defund the police. He\u8217\'20\'19s very supportive of law enforcement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 With less than a week to go until America votes, it remains to be seen which of the party\u8217\'20\'19s strategies will have the most impact.\par \par \uc2 In the weeks following the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s Roe v Wade decision, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights became {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2022/08/23/abortion-rises-in-importance-as-a-voting-issue-driven-by-democrats/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of the most important}}}\uc2  issues for voters \u8211\'20\'13 and with Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 huge investment in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ads, the party has been counting on it turning out the vote.\par \par \uc2 But as inflation and gas prices have risen, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/10/31/2022-midterms-voters-worried-about-the-economy.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polls}}}\uc2  show that the economy has emerged as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/404243/economy-top-election-issue-abortion-crime-next.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the key issue}}}\uc2  for voters.\par \par \uc2 Over the past couple of weeks some Democrats, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/10/23/sanders-worried-about-democratic-midterm-turnout-urges-focus-on-economy-00063043"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including Bernie Sanders}}}\uc2 , have urged their party to focus on plans for economic recovery, and late-running ads could reflect voters\u8217\'20\'19 concerns. As the election looms, Democratic supporters will be hoping it\u8217\'20\'19s not a case of too little too late.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221103eib30015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
