\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Michigan Democrat\u8217\'20\'19s lead shows  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  may be the issue that decides midterm races\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor in East Lansing \par \uc2 1,670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par 14:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 28\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As several key battleground states fight over whether to protect or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Democratic candidate \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  says it will motivate voters: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen anything like it\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  is a straight shooter. She doesn\u8217\'20\'19t miss a beat when asked a tough question. She speaks up often, and forcefully, against things she perceives as unjust \u8211\'20\'13 whether perpetrated by her opponents or her own Democratic party. But when asked what she\u8217\'20\'19ll think if the proposal to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s state constitution doesn\u8217\'20\'19t pass this November, she clams up.\par \par \uc2 Slotkin fidgets, stroking one thumb over the other, in a repetitive, soothing motion.\par \par \uc2 Is she discombobulated?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYes,\u8221\'20\'1D she answers, back to her usual, rapid-fire pace.\par \par \uc2 Why?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ll tell you this,\u8221\'20\'1D Slotkin begins. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it fails to pass, I won\u8217\'20\'19t be re-elected. Because it means I\u8217\'20\'19m fundamentally out of touch.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She pauses, cautiously, and adds: \u8220\'20\'1CBut I don\u8217\'20\'19t believe that to be the case. I think I\u8217\'20\'19m going to win.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a big statement. Slotkin is running in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cookpolitical.com/ratings/house-race-ratings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of}}}\uc2  the country\u8217\'20\'19s most tightly contested House seats, as a Democrat who won Trump voters back from the Republican party in both 2018 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 She is also running in a midterm election full of twists and turns \u8211\'20\'13 one that has seen Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 hopes to avoid the typically poor showing of the party in power begin to rise, only to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/newsletters/politico-nightly/2022/10/24/making-sense-of-the-gops-october-surge-00063246"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plummet again}}}\uc2 . But even with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/sep/28/a-global-recession-looks-increasingly-likely-but-heres-how-australia-could-escape-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mixed economy}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/oct/16/us-inflation-federal-reserve-interest-rate-hikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rising inflation}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2022/10/20/bidens-job-rating-is-similar-to-trumps-but-lower-than-that-of-other-recent-presidents/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unfavourable polling for the president}}}\uc2 , people are putting their money on Slotkin in huge numbers: the race for Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s seventh, a newly drawn district pitting Slotkin against state senator Tom Barrett, has become the most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/michigan/2022/10/21/democrat-elissa-slotkin-republican-tom-barrett-michigan-7th-district-congress/69518573007/?gnt-cfr=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expensive race}}}\uc2  in the country in terms of outside spending. Outside spending, generally, is a good barometer for how important a race is, with the largest amounts coming from the national parties \u8211\'20\'13 and in the case of the seventh, $27m has been poured into the race.\par \par \uc2 The race had been neck-and-neck the whole time, but in September something strange happened: Slotkin surged by 18 points. She has held at least a six-point lead over Barrett ever since.\par \par \uc2 The key reason? \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen anything like it,\u8221\'20\'1D says Slotkin. \u8220\'20\'1CEverywhere I go, Democrats, Independents and Republicans are talking about this issue. They\u8217\'20\'19re talking about how scared they are of a 1931 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban coming back in Michigan. They don\u8217\'20\'19t want it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Across the country, the Democrats need to hold on to just a handful of congressional seats this year to keep a majority in the House of Representatives. That has made Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s seventh, and other races like it, so important: whereas single issues, other than the economy aren\u8217\'20\'19t usually enough to turn an entire election, this year several key battleground states are fighting over whether to protect or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and that might just be the issue that decides the races.\par \par \uc2 As Slotkin puts it, if the Democrats can win districts in Michigan, the so-called swingiest of swing states, \u8220\'20\'1CWe still have a path to winning the House.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Still, Slotkin faces a tough challenger in Barrett, an army vet who fought in Iraq and may play to voters in a manufacturing district as the safe choice. He hails from Charlotte, a city in the newly drawn seventh, while the redistricting process \u8211\'20\'13 which was decided on by an independent panel to reduce political gerrymandering \u8211\'20\'13 has placed Slotkin in a separate district from the family farm where she used to live; she has now moved to Lansing, to live in the area where she\u8217\'20\'19s fighting the race.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s really familiar to a good number of the people in this district, which is also quite conservative,\u8221\'20\'1D says Jenna Bednar, a political scientist at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CTom Barrett doesn\u8217\'20\'19t present himself in any sense as a threatening force. And he is likely to enjoy a lot of support from the rural communities in the district.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But he too has a good challenger in Slotkin \u8211\'20\'13 a fiercely bipartisan politician who worked as a CIA analyst and served under both the Bush and the Obama administrations.\par \par \uc2 Recent political maneuvers suggest Barrett, who has previously called himself \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/dccc/status/1564695448652156928"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 100% pro-life}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D starting from conception, knows his positioning on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unpopular: this summer, he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sjdemas/status/1563918987045793794"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 changed his campaign website}}}\uc2  to soften his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance. Barrett has since stated that his stance hasn\u8217\'20\'19t changed \u8211\'20\'13 that he remains anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including in cases of rape and incest, and claimed his website was changed by his campaign team, probably to reflect \u8220\'20\'1Cmore salient\u8221\'20\'1D issues such as inflation, crime and the border.\par \par \uc2 Slotkin disputes that.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re reading polling,\u8221\'20\'1D she asserts. \u8220\'20\'1CThey realize that in this moderate district, an extreme position does not work \u8230\'20\'26 and they have bent over backwards to try and mix the position they really believe in with something that will get them elected.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Indeed, in Michigan, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one of the top issues that comes up on the doorstep, perhaps on par \u8211\'20\'13 depending where you are in the state \u8211\'20\'13 with inflation. Voters are particularly concerned about impacts on doctors and the health of pregnant people. They see women miscarrying in Texas and being turned away from the hospital until they \u8220\'20\'1Ccome back sicker, with a higher fever, bleeding harder\u8221\'20\'1D, according to Slotkin.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo many nurses are super freaked out,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd of course, the doctors are fearing litigation. It\u8217\'20\'19s too much, even for Republican pro-life women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Erika Farley, 45, is one of those Republican women. Despite working for the GOP in Michigan for 20 years, this year she says she will vote for Slotkin. \u8220\'20\'1CI was really disturbed by the overturning of Roe v Wade, and I know where Senator Barrett stands on that,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 In many ways, being from such a competitive district keeps Slotkin in tune with voters, she thinks. She gave a speech on the House floor in September about a bill that Republicans were trying to delay, that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://time.com/6214024/veterans-affairs-abortion-fight/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 would allow veterans access to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  care if raped}}}\uc2 . On that issue, she says, \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans were so out of touch with the average American. I was coming from a very competitive district, [whereas] all three of [those trying to delay the bill] were from very easy, ruby-red Republican districts. The only debate they\u8217\'20\'19ve ever had on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is who\u8217\'20\'19s more pro-life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jeff Timmer, a Republican strategist for more than 30 years before turning away from the party because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , agrees with her take on those Republicans. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t even recognize that their position is in such a distinct minority \u8211\'20\'13 that every time [Barrett] utters [his pro-life stance], it repels far more voters than it attracts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Slotkin believes there is a level of anger floating below the surface for women that she hasn\u8217\'20\'19t seen before. She sees it, she says, because people come to elected officials with their pain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople tell me some of the most intimate things \u8211\'20\'13 things I could barely tell my close friends. They just want me to hear it and say that I\u8217\'20\'19m going to do something about it,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 One group she says she is seeing come over to the Democrats because of their anger is women without a college degree. Those are the women, she points out, who, if denied \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, would have the hardest time taking time off work, who don\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily have the money to travel to another state for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, \u8220\'20\'1Cwho don\u8217\'20\'19t have the privilege of just escaping from their life, to drive five hours or more to Canada or Chicago for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But she accepts the Democrats have their own issues being trusted in the midterms. She mentions Michigan families who have had to cut back because of rising costs: she reckons that\u8217\'20\'19s every family she\u8217\'20\'19s spoken to.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s real frustration at the party in power. There\u8217\'20\'19s no doubt \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s the lead foot for my opponent. [He] is almost gleeful about the economy when gas prices start ticking up,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Slotkin doesn\u8217\'20\'19t hold back from criticizing her own party, either \u8211\'20\'13 especially on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Asked whether it was misleading for Biden to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/18/biden-roe-v-wade-codify-democrats-win-midterm-elections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promise to codify Roe}}}\uc2  if the Democrats win the election, Slotkin scoffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe does not have the authority to do that \u8211\'20\'13 it just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get anywhere in the Senate.\u8221\'20\'1D What\u8217\'20\'19s worse, she says, is that none of this would have been needed had the Democrats done a better job at codifying Roe in the first place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to own the fact that we had a real failure at the federal level to protect women\u8217\'20\'19s rights,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1COver the 50 years since Roe has been in place, no one ever backed it up in legislation. Holy moly \u8211\'20\'13 that, to me, feels like we missed a major opportunity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And she was frustrated to see the national party without a plan after Roe fell. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were totally taken by surprise,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 What bothers Slotkin most, and the reason she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t answer my first question about what happens if proposal 3 doesn\u8217\'20\'19t pass, is that for other states, she feels it\u8217\'20\'19s too late. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re in Alabama, I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what you do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why the fight for Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s seventh seems to her like a harbinger of America\u8217\'20\'19s future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe United States is going through something. We\u8217\'20\'19ve had these periods of instability in our past, where the average American wondered if the country was going to continue as they knew it \u8211\'20\'13 if their kids and grandkids were going to have the same opportunities they had. So to me, this election is a marker of: are we coming out of this period of extremes?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221101eib1000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
