\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 No one knows his name \u8211\'20\'13 but he could win a tight race because of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor in Sterling Heights \par \uc2 1,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par 06:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a swing district in a swing state, Michiganders will vote on whether to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s true that Carl Marlinga doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have name recognition. Despite running for a hugely competitive seat, in a district where he lives and has been a judge for 30 years, not a single one of his yard signs is visible on a day spent following campaigners in Sterling Heights, Michigan. And when you ask him why he isn\u8217\'20\'19t polling as well as his opponent, John James \u8211\'20\'13 the Trump-backed candidate who lost two recent campaigns for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even live in the district \u8211\'20\'13 Marlinga says it himself.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s name recognition, that\u8217\'20\'19s what my pollsters are telling me,\u8221\'20\'1D Marlinga replies at a packed campaign event on an inconsiderately cold October day.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJudges are fairly invisible, and that\u8217\'20\'19s OK: as judges, we don\u8217\'20\'19t seek to be individuals, we seek to represent the rule of law,\u8221\'20\'1D Marlinga says. The only problem is that now he\u8217\'20\'19s running for office in Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s 10th, a newly drawn congressional district that includes Macomb, a place that has been named \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://19thnews.org/2020/08/white-women-swing-county-state-voters-trump/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 swing county, swing state}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Here, voters flip, flop and often pick their candidate on the day. And for the last three elections, it was Trump specifically who won their hearts.\par \par \uc2 But out on the doorstep, it seems Marlinga might just have an opportunity to win people over \u8211\'20\'13 even without them knowing his name.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know how I\u8217\'20\'19m going to vote. But I know I don\u8217\'20\'19t support Tudor Dixon,\u8221\'20\'1D says Stephanie House, a 42-year-old loan officer talking through a screen door at her house in Sterling Heights when campaigners knock. She\u8217\'20\'19s referring to the staunchly anti-choice Republican candidate for governor, who has said she believes in no exceptions for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just like James, Marlinga\u8217\'20\'19s opponent. \u8220\'20\'1CI usually decide on the day, but it\u8217\'20\'19s the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue that\u8217\'20\'19s going to push it for me, straight down the ticket,\u8221\'20\'1D says House.\par \par \uc2 This November, Michiganders will have the chance to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution, which could benefit Democrat and pro-choice candidates voters down ballot, experts say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt should benefit Democrats because of the supreme court decision. Public opinion has moved leftward on the issue [of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ], and the pro-choice side is more motivated,\u8221\'20\'1D explains Matthew Grossmann, a political scientist at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is not just swinging voters toward Democrats, though. Another couple, Melody and Ed Bristow, will be voting for James because they want more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, even though they don\u8217\'20\'19t know who John James is yet. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll just vote for the Republican, whoever it is that\u8217\'20\'19s pro-life,\u8221\'20\'1D they say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI like John James. He\u8217\'20\'19s very charismatic. He\u8217\'20\'19s very smart. I\u8217\'20\'19ve voted for John in the past, and I\u8217\'20\'19ve endorsed [him],\u8221\'20\'1D says the mayor of Sterling Heights, Michael Taylor. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I\u8217\'20\'19m disappointed that he isn\u8217\'20\'19t using his voice to push back on the extremes in the Republican party, which I really think are an existential threat to our way of life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Taylor, 39, admits that James seems more popular than Marlinga, especially in Sterling Heights. Trump has been out campaigning with James, something likely to chime with voters here, and James is on TV \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat seems like every day\u8221\'20\'1D, according to Taylor. Meanwhile he says, he\u8217\'20\'19s not sure he\u8217\'20\'19s even seen an ad from Marlinga\u8217\'20\'19s campaign.\par \par \uc2 That makes sense: James\u8217\'20\'19s campaign has attracted huge funding and with James denying the results of his own Senate loss in 2020, he appeals to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18big lie\u8217\'20\'19, and those who still argue, baselessly, that Trump won in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But, crucially, Taylor thinks that for those people whose vote swings on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they won\u8217\'20\'19t see this as a popularity contest, just a simple test of right versus wrong.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople here feel very strongly about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, that it should be a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose,\u8221\'20\'1D says Taylor. He says inflation and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem to be the top issues in this district, and adds: \u8220\'20\'1CTo me, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far more important than inflation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is something that is going to be around forever \u8230\'20\'26 There\u8217\'20\'19s inflation all over the world, the president can\u8217\'20\'19t wave a magic wand and make it go away. Inflation goes up and down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNational forecasters are giving this race to the Republicans too quickly,\u8221\'20\'1D says Jeff Timmer, a member of the Lincoln Project, and a former GOP strategist for more than 30 years. Referencing a recent Detroit News poll that gave James an eight-point lead over Marlinga, he adds:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat same {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.detroitnews.com/restricted/?return=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.detroitnews.com%2Fstory%2Fnews%2Fpolitics%2Fmichigan%2F2022%2F10%2F10%2Fjames-leads-marlinga-eight-points-poll-us-house-race%2F69553010007%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poll}}}\uc2  put [Gretchen] Whitmer up like 23 points. If Whitmer is winning that district by double digits, John James is not going to be a congressman,\u8221\'20\'1D says Timmer.\par \par \uc2 Marlinga\u8217\'20\'19s traditional approach could also benefit him in this race. He is old-school. He doesn\u8217\'20\'19t use soundbites and he gives straight answers, even when disadvantageous to him or his campaign, because he is motivated by a deep sense of responsibility. \u8220\'20\'1CAren\u8217\'20\'19t you happy to be out of all that?\u8221\'20\'1D his wife had joked, while the two watched the news unfurl from the January 6 insurrection on TV. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sorry to tell you but my reaction is a little bit different,\u8221\'20\'1D he replied. \u8220\'20\'1CAristotle once said that if good people do not get involved in politics, their punishment is to be governed by bad people.\u8221\'20\'1D He decided to run.\par \par \uc2 In a state where Republicans have equivocated on their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messaging \u8211\'20\'13 unsure of how it will chime with voters \u8211\'20\'13 Marlinga could stand out because he is very easy to read.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVoters see this issue in terms of right and wrong. They see criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as wrong. A supermajority of voters think that way, even in Macomb. And there\u8217\'20\'19s an automatic association, that Republicans are anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Democrats are pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights,\u8221\'20\'1D says Timmer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue should benefit Democrats,\u8221\'20\'1D says Grossmann.\par \par \uc2 But doesn\u8217\'20\'19t that also mean people might try to have it both ways \u8211\'20\'13 protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  specifically in the referendum, and then voting for John James if they prefer him on the economy?\par \par \uc2 Taylor, the mayor of Sterling, thinks not.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think there\u8217\'20\'19s any person out there, that\u8217\'20\'19s gonna say my most important issue is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I\u8217\'20\'19m still going to vote for John James.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221102eib2000rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
