\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US abortions decrease by 10,000 since repeal of Roe v Wade in June\b0\par\par\uc2 Gloria Oladipo \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par 15:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Research by #WeCount shows a 6% decline, with fall of 22,000 in most restrictive states partially offset by 12,000 rise elsewhere\par \par \uc2 There have been at least 10,000 fewer abortions since the nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights established by Roe v Wade were repealed by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  in June.\par \par \uc2 New research from the national research project #WeCount shows that with federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections rolled back, there have been 10,570 fewer legal abortions \u8211\'20\'13 a 6% decline \u8211\'20\'13 than estimates in April {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-decision-reaction-republicans-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 before the June ruling. }}}\par \par \uc2 #WeCount is a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporting project with the Society of Family Planning, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception science group. The research project has been tracking changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access since the overturning of Roe v Wade, collecting data from medical offices, hospitals, telemedicine providers, and clinics.\par \par \uc2 The data does not include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&rct=j&q=&esrc=s&source=web&cd=&cad=rja&uact=8&ved=2ahUKEwjtv6eu0Yr7AhVBlIkEHZhnDVgQFnoECBAQAQ&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.theguardian.com%2Fworld%2F2022%2Fjun%2F25%2Fabortion-ban-options-travel-pills-financial-legal-help&usg=AOvVaw3FZ2w7Omq4IgEGkSIKzmx1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-managed abortions}}}\uc2 , which could lower the overall decrease in terminations, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/30/upshot/legal-abortions-fall-roe.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  according to the New York Times}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The recent statistics are a net calculation, meaning the total loss accounts for a decreased number of abortions in some states as well as an increase in abortions in other areas, for people who traveled out of state to terminate their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 In the states that saw declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  numbers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/us/abortion-laws-roe-v-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including 13 states that have banned most abortions}}}\uc2 , terminated pregnancies decreased by about 22,000.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, other states that have protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw about 12,000 more abortions performed.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/SFPWeCountReport_AprtoAug2022_ReleaseOct2022-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 published data}}}\uc2  shows that there has not been a total absorption of patients from other states who can no longer access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, meaning there were thousands of people who \u8220\'20\'1Cfelt they had no options\u8221\'20\'1D, Ushma Upadhyay \u8211\'20\'13 #WeCount co-chairperson and a public health social scientist as well as professor at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 said to FiveThirtyEight.\par \par \uc2 The data suggest that people in restrictive states traveled far to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a burden that is exacerbated for people with fewer financial resources.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome of these states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned \u8211\'20\'13 Alabama, Louisiana, Mississippi, for example \u8211\'20\'13 are some of the poorest states in our country, and people would have to cross multiple state lines to get to another state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kari White, a \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  researcher who is on the #WeCount research steering committee, to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 States have also been exploring possible ways to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/us-abortions-travel-wave-of-restrictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 target criminalizing interstate travel for an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , creating an additional barrier for those who are living in restrictive states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven for the people who make it to another state, this is a hardship,\u8221\'20\'1D White added.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221031eiav002jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
