\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m changing Congress\u8217\'20\'19: how \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  brought her lived experience to Capitol Hill\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 2,107 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par 12:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Member of \u8216\'20\'18the Squad\u8217\'20\'19 on how her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  experience, sexual assault and front-line fight in Ferguson, Missouri, affected her politics\par \par \uc2 Her new memoir is bracingly, sometimes painfully honest, but there is one passage that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/dec/30/cori-bush-congresswoman-missouri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cori Bush}}}\uc2  seriously considered striking out before publication.\par \par \uc2 She had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when she was 19. Walking into the white-walled room of a reproductive health clinic, Bush writes, she began to have reservations about the procedure. Twice she told a nurse, \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to do this,\u8221\'20\'1D but twice the nurse ignored her objections and carried on.\par \par \uc2 Bush heard \u8220\'20\'1Cthe awful sounds as the vacuum sucked the fetus out of my body \u8230\'20\'26 I remember the intense pain and the feeling of helplessness in that moment. I was furious. That doctor ignored my pleas. I was just another person in his assembly line, just another little Black girl.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the prologue observes, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/678408/the-forerunner-by-cori-bush/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Forerunner}}}\uc2  is not your typical political memoir. Bush, 46, is not your typical politician. She is a registered nurse, ordained pastor, community activist and organiser and single mother. A Democrat from St Louis, she is the first Black woman to represent Missouri in the \uc2 US Congress\uc2 . She is also a survivor and embodiment of resilience.\par \par \uc2 She realises that her frank recollection of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a traumatic experience is politically loaded and could be seized upon by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to further their cause. It comes just a month before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/16/us-midterms-republicans-democrats-major-issues"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 midterm elections}}}\uc2  in which Democrats hope to tap into public anger over the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to torch the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know that many supporters of reproductive rights will be outraged by my decision to share this story,\u8221\'20\'1D the congresswoman writes.\par \par \uc2 Yet in a phone interview, she tells the Guardian she has no regrets about including it. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a difficult position but this memoir is me telling my story,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CTo silence me, to tell me you shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t tell this story because someone else can use it and weaponise it, that\u8217\'20\'19s not an answer. We\u8217\'20\'19ve got to fix the problem. The way to make sure that the problem is highlighted and awareness is placed on the issue is to talk about it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate is often oversimplified, Bush is offering a reminder of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vdonFAfjzSI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the messy, nuanced reality}}}\uc2  that she faced as a young Black woman restrained by white medical providers. She continues: \u8220\'20\'1CSpeaking about what happened wasn\u8217\'20\'19t to condemn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers at all because I work closely with a bunch of providers and reproductive health clinics and I support them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s something that should not have happened to me and it is our work to not only fix certain parts of the system of harm; it is to do the work to fix all of it. But as I also wrote in my book, I was still able to have the services that I needed and the decision was mine to make. In the end, I made the right decision for me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On the day in June that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s rightwing majority overturned its 1973 Roe v Wade ruling, Bush happened to be back at the same clinic where she had undergone that difficult \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (along with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/09/30/politics/cori-bush-sexual-assault-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a previous \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  that resulted from a rape when she was 17). The congresswoman was meeting with providers, advocates and the health secretary, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , when the news came through.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy chief of staff walked up to me during the conversation and showed me his phone and I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t believe it at first. I kept blinking and looking at his phone. Even though we knew that it was most likely going to come any day, it was still hard to see and for that reality to set in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSomeone said it out loud and stopped the conversation: \u8216\'20\'18The supreme court just overturned Roe.\u8217\'20\'19 We all embraced one another. I shed tears, I yelled, I hollered out because I was thinking about the millions of people across this country that will be affected by this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision led to a surge of women registering to vote in some states. In Missouri\u8217\'20\'19s neighbour Kansas, people voted overwhelmingly to continue to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution. But recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/17/upshot/midterms-poll-republicans-lead.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 midterm opinion polls}}}\uc2  suggest that Democratic anger over Roe v Wade could be eclipsed by Republican concerns over inflation and crime.\par \par \uc2 Bush insists, however: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a huge motivator because we have to remember that this is something that had been in place since 1973 \u8211\'20\'13 this was in place when I was born. There are a lot of us that only know a Roe v Wade society and there are people who may not agree with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but they also don\u8217\'20\'19t agree with their rights being stripped away. Those folks are saying that\u8217\'20\'19s going to make me show up to vote for the Democrat because I don\u8217\'20\'19t want my rights being taken away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bush brings lived experience to Capitol Hill in ways unthinkable for a career politician. Having been evicted from her home several times, forcing her to sleep in her car with her children, last year she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/aug/08/cori-bush-interview-eviction-moratorium"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slept on the steps}}}\uc2  of the US Capitol in protest after Congress failed to pass legislation to extend an eviction moratorium (the White House eventually issued a new eviction moratorium).\par \par \uc2 In 2014 she was on the frontlines of the uprising in Ferguson, Missouri, after the police killing of unarmed Black teenager {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/michael-brown-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michael Brown}}}\uc2 . This made her a target for harassment. Her tyres were slashed while her car was parked in front of the complex where she lived. She came home and found her front door had been tampered with.\par \par \uc2 Bush needed to move home. In 2016, a few weeks after losing a Democratic primary election for the Senate, she saw a social media post by a local faith healer advertising a house to rent. When she got in touch and went to see it, the man raped her. She describes the assault in unflinching, unforgiving detail and writes: \u8220\'20\'1CI whimpered through the pain. It seemed like forever. I felt like dying. I wanted to die.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bush explains by phone why she choose to open her memoir with this candid account: \u8220\'20\'1CThat changed everything and I am still in therapy now. I\u8217\'20\'19m still walking out that journey to healing. It affected my life the way that it did because, when I thought about the sexual assaults I had back when I was 18, early adult, I went for the next 20 years blaming myself, like it was because my shorts were really short or my shirt was low or where I was when I met the person. I carried that for all of those years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis time I had just completed my first run for office and lost; I was grieving that but I was a registered nurse now, I was taking care of two kids, I had just come from work, I was in my scrubs uniform, I was going to a place to see a home. That\u8217\'20\'19s what really rocked me. Before, I thought it was because of something I did; I didn\u8217\'20\'19t do any of those things this time; I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t drinking, any of it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo that\u8217\'20\'19s why the book had to start with that, because my life turned upside down but, as I\u8217\'20\'19m working through it, I\u8217\'20\'19ve been able to help so many others and so many others have also helped me to be able to move forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After the rape, Bush was horrified by the lack of discretion she was afforded. She tells how \u8220\'20\'1Ca caravan\u8221\'20\'1D of people followed her to hospital and she was subjected to the humiliation of a rape kit. A forensic exam found that the encounter could have been an assault or, as the rapist had claimed, consensual rough sex. Bush went to court four times to get a protection order against the faith healer but repeatedly lost.\par \par \uc2 As a Christian, has she been able to forgive the man who raped her? She pauses. \u8220\'20\'1CLet me just say I have some forgiveness but is there a blanket all-is-forgiven? I\u8217\'20\'19m still working through that. I\u8217\'20\'19ve always believed that forgiveness is for you, not for the other person, and so usually I\u8217\'20\'19m quick to forgive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut in this instance, because there was some trust there and he hurt me the way that he hurt me and then he lied as a man of God and preacher of the gospel, and he continues on in this lie and continues to evade the system, that has made it difficult for me to just be like, yes, I forgive him and I\u8217\'20\'19m going on with my life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Forerunner also gives Bush\u8217\'20\'19s insider account of joining thousands of activists on the streets in response to the 2014 shooting of Brown in Ferguson. She recalls how the skin on her face and arms burned after police fired teargas. The uprising went on for more than 400 days and, Bush argues, became a pivot point in the centuries-long struggle for Black liberation.\par \par \uc2 It also foreshadowed the nationwide protests for racial justice that followed the police murder of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/george-floyd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 George Floyd}}}\uc2  in Minneapolis in 2020. But calls from Bush and others to \u8220\'20\'1Cdefund the police\u8221\'20\'1D met with a predictable backlash, even from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and other Democratic leaders. Some fear that the momentum of \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  is again being lost.\par \par \uc2 But Bush contends that the working and the organising goes on: \u8220\'20\'1CEven if we\u8217\'20\'19re not on the streets every single day chanting and marching, are we organising folks to get them to the polls? Are we organising groups to be able to teach people what to do if you get pulled over by the police? Are we organising groups to teach white people how to talk about and understand racism? Are we mobilising people to support our immigrant community if they\u8217\'20\'19re under attack? Are we organising for repro rights?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She accuses Republicans of wilfully distorting the central idea of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/how-we-rise/2021/05/19/7-myths-about-defunding-the-police-debunked/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defund the police}}}\uc2 , which means reallocating funding away from police departments to mental health workers, social workers and other government agencies. \u8220\'20\'1CThey would rather scare their people instead of educating them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bush became a leader of the movement seeking police and criminal justice reform in Ferguson and across the St Louis area. She lost a Democratic primary race for a congressional seat in 2018 but, with the backing of progressive group Justice Democrats, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/aug/05/cori-bush-missouri-primary-winner-beats-democratic-incumbent-clay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prevailed in 2020}}}\uc2  over a 20-year incumbent. She was instantly embraced as a member of \u8220\'20\'1Cthe squad\u8221\'20\'1D in the House of Representatives; \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  describes her as a \u8220\'20\'1Csister-in-service\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There have been wins and losses under Biden, a longtime moderate. The president declared racial equity a central plank of his agenda, appointed a diverse cabinet and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/joe-biden/trump-installed-historic-number-judges-biden-outpacing-far-rcna48142"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 far outpaced his predecessors}}}\uc2  in nominating women and people of colour to the federal bench. But legislation on police reform and voting rights stalled in a Congress where Democrats command only narrow majorities. Bush regards the glass as half full.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has absolutely surprised me,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, citing his decisions to commute the sentences of 75 people serving time for nonviolent drug offences, cancel billions of dollars in student loan debt and lift a pandemic-related expulsion policy that effectively closed America\u8217\'20\'19s asylum system at its border with Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Many idealists have arrived in Washington only to find their dreams crushed by compromise. Is Bush changing Congress or is Congress changing her? She laughs. \u8220\'20\'1CCongress is changing me a little bit in the way of helping me to see how, by pushing, our government can work for the regular everyday person. Not understanding the inner workings of Congress before, I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t able to see it but I knew that I needed to go inside of it to push for the change I wanted to see.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut mostly I\u8217\'20\'19m changing Congress because I\u8217\'20\'19ve been there less than two years and we have been able to bring about some change and, if nothing else, my colleagues know where I stand on issues. They don\u8217\'20\'19t have to wonder what\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen if a policing bill comes forward, if we talk about Israel-Palestine, if they relate to equity and inclusion, anything that has to do with incarceration rates. There is no question. People know that I stand on the side of equity and equality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221031eiav001xy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
