\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 The doctors leaving anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  states: \u8216\'20\'18I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t do my job at all\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 2,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par 01:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Four months after Roe v Wade was overturned, some providers have left states restricting abortions or changed careers\par \par \uc2 With Roe v Wade overturned, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now banned in 13 states and subject to restrictions and litigation in more than a dozen others. In some states, courts are embroiled in an on-off battle that can see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  banned in a state one day, unbanned the next, and back off the table two weeks later.\par \par \uc2 Doctors are on the frontlines of this chaotic landscape, fearful of running afoul of ever-changing law, in some cases {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jul/13/texas-abortion-ban-maternal-health-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struggling to provide}}}\uc2  life-saving care. In most cases, doctors have stayed in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -restrictive states, because despite restrictions on their medical practice, they have ties to their patients, their communities, their families.\par \par \uc2 But others have decided to leave. What do they leave behind? In a country where, according to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marchofdimes.org/materials/2020-Maternity-Care-Report.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis from the March of Dimes}}}\uc2 , nearly half of all counties lack a single obstetrician, what care will remain? And what do their predicaments tell us about what it is like to work in reproductive health in much of the US?\par \par \uc2 Here are five of their stories.\par \par \uc2 Alireza Shamshirsaz, 48\par \par \uc2 Alireza Shamshirsaz had a house in Houston, Texas. In the backyard was a small pool, where his kids learned to swim. He met his wife in Texas, built his family there, and felt it was his home. But after Texas passed its six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban late last year, a decision he faced at work would sour things for him.\par \par \uc2 A couple whose pregnancy was not going to be viable came to him for care, but because of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, he had to send them hundreds of miles away.\par \par \uc2 The mother was 21 weeks pregnant with identical twins, which shared one placenta. One of the twins was almost certainly going to die, which would most likely have killed the other twin without intervention.\par \par \uc2 Shamshirsaz wanted to use a selective, lifesaving procedure: by stopping the blood flow to the unhealthy twin, he could save the other\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, he didn\u8217\'20\'19t have that option.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey were just crying, crying in the middle of my office,\u8221\'20\'1D says Shamshirsaz.\par \par \uc2 The worst bit for him was knowing how urgent the situation was.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey needed to get all the way to the east coast or west coast before they would find a doctor who could help them, and they needed to do it in the next 24 or 48 hours. Even in the best scenario I don\u8217\'20\'19t think 99% of people could have made that trip in the time they had,\u8221\'20\'1D he says.\par \par \uc2 Shamshirsaz doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know what happened to the couple, but their case deeply affected him.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost people don\u8217\'20\'19t have the option to travel to other states. The rich people can travel,\u8221\'20\'1D he says, but others can\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 Waiting is the new norm in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -restrictive states: even in cases where foetal survival is an impossibility, doctors often must wait to intervene until the last moment, at huge risk to pregnant people.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we wait to the edge, we have more near misses. In medicine, when you have too many near misses, eventually, you actually miss. That means ending up with a dead mother,\u8221\'20\'1D says Shamshirsaz.\par \par \uc2 Shamshirsaz now works in Boston. Since Roe fell, he is relieved not to be in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -restrictive state surrounded by other restrictive states. But he fears for Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you can\u8217\'20\'19t do a termination, who will support these kids after the birth? Who will pay the bills they will need for medical care? Nobody. We destroy these families,\u8221\'20\'1D he says.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Romanos, 43\par \par \uc2 When Catherine Romanos was 28, she fell in love with the idea being an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. In medical school, she signed up to train as part of a movement to get younger people trained in providing abortions, and after her first job at a Planned Parenthood, she decided to stick with it. She loved the way she was able to build trust with patients, supporting them at an intimate moment in their lives, sometimes soothing their shame.\par \par \uc2 But in June 2022, Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s six-week ban went into effect. Romanos, now 43, went from working in a Dayton office that performed 40 to 50 abortions a day to seeing less than a handful of patients daily.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI went from feeling really needed, to almost three months where I really couldn\u8217\'20\'19t do my job at all. It was very demoralizing,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was worst for people answering the phones. They were taking hundreds of calls a week from people trying to figure out where they could go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The day the Dobbs decision came down, Romanos ordered licenses to practise elsewhere \u8211\'20\'13 in Michigan and Illinois. Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban has since been blocked .\par \par \uc2 Nearby, Indiana\u8217\'20\'19s near-total ban is also on hold due to legal challenges. In November, Michigan will decide whether to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its state constitution or to ban it using a 1931 law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really hard to plan your life when the judge\u8217\'20\'19s orders come in 14-day increments,\u8221\'20\'1D says Romanos.\par \par \uc2 Romanos sees the impact of these seesawing laws on her patients: in late September, she helped a Hoosier patient, who found out she was pregnant on the first day \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal in Indiana. In the time it took the patient to drive to Ohio, then back to Indiana for her mandatory 24-hour waiting period, and back to Ohio again for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ban in Indiana had been temporarily lifted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s so insulting to her,\u8221\'20\'1D says Romanos.\par \par \uc2 Zevidah Vickery, 51\par \par \uc2 Zevidah Vickery was never supposed to be an OB-GYN. She studied feminist theory, and thought she\u8217\'20\'19d be an academic or a sociologist one day. Then she started working as a medical assistant at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Seattle, and everything changed.\par \par \uc2 Her first cases were difficult. She saw patients forced to engage in sex work, leave their children with neighbors, or travel from Alaskan villages without support from their families to find an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s when she decided she on her career. \u8220\'20\'1CI was just blown away by the courage and the desperation of patients,\u8221\'20\'1D says Vickery. \u8220\'20\'1CSuddenly, writing some feminist take on social phenomena seemed less immediately useful.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What followed was 15 years of study to be the kind of provider she dreamed of: Vickery took night classes in math, went to med school, and did another four years of residency and two more years in fellowship to become an expert in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, she landed her dream job in Ohio as a full-time \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider who also taught students. She finally had weekends to herself, which meant she could spend time with her son.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI loved the people. I loved the organization, I loved my boss,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Then, Vickery saw the leaked draft judgment suggesting the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would soon fall. She knew \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans would come quickly in Ohio, an already restrictive state. So she made a decision. After years of moving her son around for her career, she was going to stay put.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m his only family really,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI couldn\u8217\'20\'19t move him any more,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Now, Vickery is a retraining to be an addiction specialist. She describes leaving the job she loved as a long process of mourning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really is exactly like grief. [I\u8217\'20\'19m doing] all of the things you have when you\u8217\'20\'19re grieving \u8211\'20\'13 like suddenly you\u8217\'20\'19re sobbing on your way home,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not married, my career is my husband. I invested everything into getting where I was.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The most difficult thing for Vickery to think about is what her patients have lost.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough of us as it is,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople don\u8217\'20\'19t even want to practice in red states any more. So it\u8217\'20\'19s not just the loss of one person who\u8217\'20\'19s an expert in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. It\u8217\'20\'19s the ripple effect of that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Leilah Zahedi-Spung, 34\par \par \uc2 Leilah Zahedi-Spung has spent her career zigzagging around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -restrictive states. She started out in Georgia, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legis.ga.gov/api/legislation/document/20112012/127778"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just before a 22-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  went into effect in 2019. She moved to Missouri later that year, when the state passed an eight-week ban (which didn\u8217\'20\'19t go into effect as Roe v Wade\u8217\'20\'19s protections still stood). Last year, she moved to Tennessee, where the state recently banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without any exceptions. That was the last straw.\par \par \uc2 Zahedi-Spung is exhausted. She is fed up with sending every patient who comes to her for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state and wondering if she is putting their lives at risk. One patient she sent out of state had to travel in deteriorating condition. After crossing a border five hours away for care, the patient\u8217\'20\'19s kidneys had started to fail. The patient was OK, but Zahedi-Spung wished she could have intervened earlier.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am now complicit in a system that is making me harm people,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a gut punch every time I have to tell someone, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m sorry, your foetus and this child you so desired is not going to survive outside of this pregnancy, because it has some awful anomaly. And there are no exceptions to the law in Tennessee, and you now need to travel out of state to get the care that you deserve.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She is a maternal foetal medicine specialist and is horrified by the number of women who will be forced to give birth \u8211\'20\'13 especially considering the states with the tightest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are those with the worst maternal mortality rates. (Tennessee ranks {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/other/state-indicator/maternal-deaths-and-mortality-rates-per-100000-live-births/?currentTimeframe=0&sortModel=%7B%22colId%22:%22Maternal%20Mortality%20Rate%20per%20100,000%20live%20Births%22,%22sort%22:%22desc%22%7D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fourth worst in the country}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s guilt that I\u8217\'20\'19m leaving patients in a community that I care about deeply,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 But she looks forward to practicing in Colorado, where she will be moving. She feels relief to be starting over in a new state \u8220\'20\'1Cwhere I\u8217\'20\'19m not under a microscope. That someone isn\u8217\'20\'19t gonna turn me in to the cops, or show up at my house and arrest me for doing my job,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Anne Banfield, 44\par \par \uc2 Anne Banfield is glad she left Elkins, West Virginia, in April 2022 to practice as an OB-GYN in Maryland. Shortly after she left, the state brought in a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. She thinks often about the decisions she won\u8217\'20\'19t have to face now she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t live there any more. She doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to worry about whether her patients will be able to access care or whether she can provide appropriate advice to them without running afoul of the law. She doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to worry about them getting needlessly sick or maybe even dying from preventable issues.\par \par \uc2 In 2013, while she was still in West Virginia, she saw a patient in the emergency room who was 17 weeks pregnant when her water broke. The patient\u8217\'20\'19s partner, a US army vet, was out of the country while she grappled with terrible news: without a termination, her baby was unlikely to survive, and if it did, it would probably have significant problems. The patient was at risk of a life-threatening infection, kidney damage, even death.\par \par \uc2 She was able to terminate the pregnancy with Banfield as her doctor. In a post-Roe America, she would have needed to travel out of state or wait until her own condition deteriorated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to tell [people] in a horrible situation, they can\u8217\'20\'19t receive the healthcare they need in their home state, because of the law,\u8221\'20\'1D says Banfield.\par \par \uc2 Having practiced in the state for almost 14 years, she still has friends working in West Virginia under the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Every time one tells her about another patient they had to send across state lines, Banfield is relieved she no longer works there.\par \par \uc2 But she worries about her two nieces and what the future will look like for them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want them ever to be in a situation where they don\u8217\'20\'19t have choice,\u8221\'20\'1D says Banfield.\par \par \uc2 Now, her biggest concern is that people in the state she loves will suffer from avoidable conditions, and die avoidable deaths from ectopic pregnancies or haemorrhaging because they would be scared to get treatment for a miscarriage that may fall under suspicion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re afraid your local emergency department is going to investigate you for a miscarriage, or to turn your information in to the police because of some crazy restriction in your state, then people are going to delay care. That scares me,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221026eiaq001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
