\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  bans create \u8216\'20\'18insurmountable barriers\u8217\'20\'19 for incarcerated women in US\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 1,314 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2022\par 19:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s overturning of Roe will make reproductive healthcare in prisons a lot worse than it already is, experts warn\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decided to strip away constitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in June, it effectively made the situation for many pregnant incarcerated women who are seeking abortions a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 Conditions for reproductive healthcare in many US prison facilities are often abysmal. With many pregnant inmates regularly facing dire circumstances, including being denied abortions or being forced to give birth while shackled, experts warn that the overturn of Roe v Wade will now result in even more severe consequences for an already marginalized community.\par \par \uc2 From 1980 to 2020, the number of incarcerated women across the country increased by more than 475%, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sentencingproject.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/Incarcerated-Women-and-Girls.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  according to}}}\uc2  the Sentencing Project. In 2020, Idaho led the nation in the highest female state imprisonment rate, at 110 per 100,000 female residents, followed by Oklahoma, South Dakota, Arizona, Wyoming, Kentucky and Montana. As of two years ago, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sentencingproject.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/Incarcerated-Women-and-Girls.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  imprisonment rate}}}\uc2  for Black women was 1.7 times the rate of the imprisonment for white women. Meanwhile, Latinx women were imprisoned at 1.3 times the rate of white women.\par \par \uc2 The Prison Policy Initiative {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prisonpolicy.org/blog/2022/05/04/mothers_day/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  found}}}\uc2  that an average of 58,000 people are pregnant each year when they enter local jails or prisons. In many of the states that already have the highest female state imprisonment rates, they also now have strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that ban the procedure almost entirely.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the overturn of Roe v Wade is expected to make the lives of pregnant incarcerated people who are seeking abortions increasingly difficult.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople experiencing incarceration and pregnancy in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been severely restricted or outlawed altogether will likely face new barriers as jails and prisons seek to hide behind the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to avoid their constitutional obligation to provide healthcare (including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to people in custody,\u8221\'20\'1D Alexa Kolbi-Molinas, deputy director of the Reproductive Freedom Project at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven where correctional staff and officials do not deliberately block access to care, the reduced availability of services and need to travel even greater distances to access legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the greater demand for services in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still legal, will only exacerbate all the financial and logistical obstacles that already existed,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.lww.com/greenjournal/Abstract/2021/09000/Abortion_Access_for_Incarcerated_People__Incidence.3.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  study}}}\uc2  led by Carolyn Sufrin, the director of the Advocacy and Research on Reproductive Wellness of Incarcerated People program at \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 , surveyed incarcerated people\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across 22 state prison systems and six county jail systems.\par \par \uc2 The study, which collected policy data for 12 months in 2016 to 2017 and was published in 2021, found that there were already a myriad of obstacles such as self-payment requirements that can prevent a pregnant inmate from obtaining the care. Out of the 19 states that then permitted abortions, two-thirds required the pregnant inmate to pay.\par \par \uc2 Only 11 of the 816 pregnancies in state and federal prisons that ended during the study time period were abortions, or 1.3%. Out of 224 pregnancies that ended at study jails, 33 were abortions, with over half of those happening during the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere were already few abortions in prison settings \u8230\'20\'26 so will [the overturn of Roe] impact \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for an incarcerated individual? Absolutely,\u8221\'20\'1D Sufrin said.\par \par \uc2 For a lot of incarcerated women across the country, many remain behind bars because they are unable to afford bail. As a result, self-payment requirements for those seeking abortions are often very difficult to fulfil.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CState prison systems or jails sometimes would force pregnant people to pay for the procedure, sometimes including even the cost of transport or the time to have prison guards with them, which is problematic because normally if an incarcerated person is going off site for any other medical procedure, they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be charged for the cost of transport or the time for the guards,\u8221\'20\'1D Corene Kendrick, deputy director of the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s National Prison Project, told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrying to expect those people in jails to come up with the money for transport to an offsite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure when they can\u8217\'20\'19t even come up with the money to make bail, to go home to their families, really creates an insurmountable barrier.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2017, Kei\u8217\'20\'19Choura Cathey, a former inmate who discovered she was pregnant in August 2015 while awaiting trial, sued the Maury county sheriff in Tennessee, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newschannel5.com/news/former-inmate-sues-sheriff-for-denying-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  claiming}}}\uc2  that he denied her the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because her pregnancy was not a threat to her health nor the result of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Cathey\u8217\'20\'19s only option at the time was to post bail so she could leave jail to receive the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, her bail was set at a staggering $1m. Eventually, her bond was lowered to $8,000. However, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newschannel5.com/news/former-inmate-sues-sheriff-for-denying-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  lawsuit,}}}\uc2  by the time Cathey was able to post bond, she was already more than six months into her pregnancy, thus making her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal.\par \par \uc2 Experts fear a lot of pregnant incarcerated women seeking abortions in a post-Roe reality may face similar circumstances.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPrisons or jails will argue \u8230\'20\'26 that\u8217\'20\'19s an elective procedure so we are not going to cover it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kendrick, which could force many incarcerated pregnant women who are unable to cover the procedure to carry their pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 For a lot of pregnant inmates, birthing conditions in prison facilities are already dire. Numerous reports in recent years have emerged of inmates either being forced to deliver while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/jan/24/shackled-pregnant-women-prisoners-birth"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  shackled}}}\uc2  to their beds or having to deliver their babies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/allthemoms/2019/05/05/pregnant-florida-woman-forced-to-deliver-own-baby-alone-jail/1111553001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  on their own}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While some states \u8211\'20\'13 and in effect, prison facilities \u8211\'20\'13 have passed outright bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a result of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling in June, others have not overhauled \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections just yet.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://yoming"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wyoming}}}\uc2 , for example, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal but remains restricted, as it can only be performed until fetal \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the Guardian, Wyoming\u8217\'20\'19s department of corrections said that the supreme court ruling on Roe in June has not affected its policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe WDOC has not had any change in policy or care for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  related issues in the WDOC for inmates or offenders. The WDOC does on occasion have female inmates that are pregnant during incarnation and they are cared for at the Wyoming Medium Correctional Institute in Torrington, WY. We rely upon the expertise of expert medical advice in all decisions related to the health and wellness of our inmates.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, according to Sufrin, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere\u8217\'20\'19s tremendous variability in what healthcare service deliveries look like on the ground, and systems are not really set up to provide the full scope of comprehensive pregnancy and postpartum care for people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For pregnant incarcerated people who are sent off-site for abortions, another issue that has emerged since Roe\u8217\'20\'19s overturn is the hesitancy or even outright refusal from external healthcare providers to perform the abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve already seen instances of local hospitals turning people away and not providing medically necessary care because of ambiguities in the law, [such as] there might still be a heartbeat, those sorts of things. Then the carceral facility is left to manage dangerous bleeding or an ectopic pregnancy and they\u8217\'20\'19re just very much ill-equipped to do that and don\u8217\'20\'19t want to and should not,\u8221\'20\'1D Sufrin said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven in the best of circumstances, there\u8217\'20\'19s still a lot of constraints and a lot of trauma that pregnant folks experience. So now after the Dobb\u8217\'20\'19s decision, we anticipate \u8230\'20\'26 that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have more pregnant people in our country and fewer people with access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And I believe that we will see that in incarcerated settings as well,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221021eial0015v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
