\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 Mobile abortions take off \u8216\'20\'18on wheels, at sea\u8217\'20\'19 to fill gaps left by shuttered clinics\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,292 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2022\par 20:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on the seas might be one innovative solution to help women in states where the procedure is illegal\par \par \uc2 When the decades-long campaign to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US started ramping up, Meg Autry, knowing a full assault on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was inevitable, began thinking about the gambling boats she\u8217\'20\'19d seen growing up on the Mississippi River.\par \par \uc2 The huge vessels looked like regular casinos on the inside. Despite the fact that gambling was steadily being prohibited in the US in the 1950s, the boats were a creative solution: because the river\u8217\'20\'19s waters were regulated differently from land, the casinos weren\u8217\'20\'19t illegal.\par \par \uc2 What if Autry, an obstetrician-gynecologist, could use that same logic to skirt \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, she wondered? For the last five years, Autry has pursued the idea of setting up an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic on the seas.\par \par \uc2 She has now raised millions of dollars towards that effort. She hopes to launch the boat by the summer of 2024 in the Gulf of Mexico, providing surgical abortions to people traveling from a swath of states where the procedure is now banned.\par \par \uc2 That is just one of the creative solutions to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access since Roe fell \u8211\'20\'13 ending legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 13 states. In some areas, patients now must travel {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2022-supreme-court-abortion-travel/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 500 miles to obtain a legal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 .}}}\par \par \uc2 To deal with the gap in care, organizations like Autry\u8217\'20\'19s are springing up around the country, offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in international waters, on wheels and through \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills mailed from overseas.\par \par \uc2 Reducing travel times\par \par \uc2 After Roe fell, Planned Parenthood of the St Louis Region and Southwest Missouri saw the number of out-of-state patients increase by 435%. With Illinois now surrounded by bans in neighboring Arkansas, Indiana, Wisconsin, Tennessee and Kentucky, the wait time for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through Planned Parenthood went from four days to two and a half weeks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe estimated that in the first year after Roe was overturned, we would see 14,000 additional patients coming from outside of the state. And that has all come true,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bonyen Lee-Gilmore, vice-president of strategy for Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s St Louis and south-west Missouri region.\par \par \uc2 Patients have also started coming in with more complex needs: making appointments later in pregnancy, and in need of more resources, like travel assistance, lodging and childcare. With only two other clinics in southern Illinois, the strain has been huge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt reaffirms what we\u8217\'20\'19ve always known, which is when you try and ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you push people later into pregnancy. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t eliminate the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it complicates their care,\u8221\'20\'1D says Lee-Gilmore.\par \par \uc2 By the end of the year, Planned Parenthood will launch a new initiative in Illinois: a traveling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic which will drive to meet patients near the borders of restrictive states. Housed in a 37ft RV, with two exam rooms, a lab, a little waiting room and ultrasound machines, the mobile clinic will provide gynecological care and medication abortions, also know as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[We hope it will] relieve the burden on the brick-and-mortar health centers at a time when capacity is so short for providers,\u8221\'20\'1D says Lee-Gilmore.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you could reduce 60 to 100 or 200 miles for a patient, you are reducing travel times, you are reducing gas costs for them,\u8221\'20\'1D she adds.\par \par \uc2 The clinic won\u8217\'20\'19t be a silver bullet. Although nurses and physician\u8217\'20\'19s assistants in many other states can provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, in Illinois they can\u8217\'20\'19t, putting further strain on a limited pool of medical professionals.\par \par \uc2 And Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s mobile clinic won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to assist with the surge in demand for procedural abortions, sometimes called surgical abortions, at a time when more patients are coming to them for abortions after 14 weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 at which point medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer recommended.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIs one mobile \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic going to solve a problem for the entire state of Illinois? Absolutely not,\u8221\'20\'1D says Lee-Gilmore.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no substitute for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care that is near somebody\u8217\'20\'19s home,\u8221\'20\'1D says \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , principal policy associate for state issues at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAccess close to home is critical, because there are huge financial and logistical hurdles people have to overcome to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including travel, taking time off from work, and arranging childcare. Low-income people and people of color make up a disproportionate share of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients, and for many of them overcoming those barriers to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be extremely difficult, if not impossible,\u8221\'20\'1D says Nash.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Doing what it takes\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Just the Pill has been providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  support on wheels for years. Initially that was to help patients in rural areas, because even before Roe was overturned, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access was largely dependent on geography \u8211\'20\'13 with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/abortion-laws-by-state-map-clinics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hundreds of thousands of people}}}\uc2  traveling hundreds of miles to get to a clinic.\par \par \uc2 Just the Pill\u8217\'20\'19s project, called \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Delivered, operated mobile clinics in Montana, Minnesota and Wyoming until 2021, and after Roe fell, they set up a new mobile clinic in Colorado to address the influx of patients coming from states like Texas.\par \par \uc2 They plan to open a mobile procedural clinic this fall, which would be the first in the country. That clinic will allow them to provide first trimester vacuum removals, and deal with any complications that arise from medication abortions.\par \par \uc2 Julie Amaon, Just the Pill\u8217\'20\'19s medical director, also emphasizes that such provisions can\u8217\'20\'19t replace legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across states.\par \par \uc2 She mentions patients who have booked appointments and even bought plane tickets, only to have to cancel because their babysitter didn\u8217\'20\'19t show up or they didn\u8217\'20\'19t have the right documents.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been pretty eye-opening,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CEven knowing the hoops that people had to jump through before \u8211\'20\'13 now, it\u8217\'20\'19s expanded twentyfold.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut we want to push the envelope because we think this is essential medical care that everybody should be able to access,\u8221\'20\'1D says Amaon.\par \par \uc2 Autry, who is setting up the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  boat, hopes to provide a wide range of care at a larger scale than a mobile clinic on wheels. Her boat will be big enough to house a helipad in case of emergencies, and she hopes it will ease the needs of patients in the south, with states like Alabama, Georgia, Louisiana and Texas all bordering the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy proposal increases access to a different group of people because it\u8217\'20\'19s surgical terminations past the point where you can get medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D says Autry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of people that live in these areas are very poor. And for them to take time off for work or time away from their family, it\u8217\'20\'19s too burdensome. So to have an option where they could go on a boat in half a day, or a day at most, is a benefit,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 She has identified a vessel which she plans to retrofit to meet clinic specifications. Their services are going to be free or on a sliding scale. Hiring a crew and covering maintenance, fuel and medical supplies will cost another five to 10 million dollars (\u163\'00\'A34.4m to \u163\'00\'A38.8m) \u8211\'20\'13 which she says is not as much as it sounds, when it comes to big philanthropic funders. She did not disclose information about donors who have already signed on.\par \par \uc2 She hopes that providing abortions at sea isn\u8217\'20\'19t a long-term solution for America.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople need to understand that patients and physicians and the medical community are going to do what it takes to make things right until our country comes around. But I hope they do come around,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221019eiaj000pb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
