\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 Phyllis Nagy: \u8216\'20\'18Knowing Patricia Highsmith changed my thinking about how a female writer could live\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Sarah Crompton \par \uc2 1,213 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2022\par 08:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The American director and screenwriter on her new film about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in the 60s, Roe v Wade being overturned, and her ideal next Bond\par \par \uc2 Phyllis Nagy is a director, screenwriter and playwright. Born in New York in 1962, she began her career at the Royal Court in London, where she was writer in residence in the 1990s, with plays such as Butterfly Kiss. She moved back to Los Angeles a decade ago. In 2006, she was nominated for an Emmy for writing and directing Mrs Harris, her screen debut. In 2016 she was nominated for an Oscar for her screenplay for Carol, based on a novel by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2021/nov/21/diaries-and-notebooks-by-patricia-highsmith-review-sex-booze-and-cold-blooded-murders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Patricia Highsmith}}}\uc2 , who she knew. Now she is directing Call Jane, her feature debut starring Elizabeth Banks, based on the true story of the \u8220\'20\'1CJanes\u8221\'20\'1D, a group of suburban women who organised a network to provide safe, affordable abortions in the States in the late 1960s, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal.\par \par \uc2 How did you get involved in\par \par \uc2 Call Jane\par \par \uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 When I was sent the script, I am embarrassed to say I had never heard of the Janes. It got me thinking about how women and minorities don\u8217\'20\'19t learn anything about our heroes. Those are lessons that are not taught.\par \par \uc2 How is it different from other films dealing with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 I realise that there is a genre of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  films, often inspired by true stories, which focus on the trauma of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What seemed important here was to treat \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as what it is \u8211\'20\'13 a normal medical procedure that is part of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare in some cases. Artists and the media often deal with the horror stories, the exceptions rather than the rule. This film is not interested in repeating those tropes.\par \par \uc2 Were you conscious that\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\par \par \uc2 , which conferred the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was under threat when you first began the movie?\par \par \uc2 Once Trump was elected and changed the composition of the supreme court, [the overrule] was coming. What I think could not be anticipated was the speed with which they decapitated it.\par \par \uc2 How did you feel when you heard the news?\par \par \uc2 To know it\u8217\'20\'19s coming and to have it happen are two entirely different things. I thought, well, are they going to come for everything else now? Are they going to go after voting rights, gay marriage, all these things people take for granted now? I was proud of the dissenting opinions, that\u8217\'20\'19s what gave me hope.\par \par \uc2 I miss the Bond from the novels, who was rather a shit\par \par \uc2 Why did you preview the film for people you knew voted\par \par \uc2 Republican\par \par \uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 I wanted to avoid the film having a sense of preaching to the converted, or a sort of snotty quality. Having [those] people watch it and get what their reactions were confirmed that hunch for me. Basically, they felt it didn\u8217\'20\'19t tell them they were wrong. Did it change their minds? As if. But perhaps it shifted them. The film allowed them to see some things about the issue in a different light. I think that\u8217\'20\'19s the best you can hope for.\par \par \uc2 I was surprised by how funny the film was \u8211\'20\'13 and how people didn\u8217\'20\'19t always behave in the way you expected\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Then it did its job. I do not think serious and enjoyable are mutually exclusive. We have too many pompous politicians [and] probably artists who make the mistake of equating humour with a lack of seriousness.\par \par \uc2 I first knew you as a playwright. Why have you moved into films?\par \par \uc2 They are two very distinct forms, [something] that people don\u8217\'20\'19t credit enough. There is a lot of playwriting on film, which is not good, and a lot of screenwriting on stage, which is equally not good. I have always wanted to do film, but not to the exclusion of theatre. It\u8217\'20\'19s just once you get into film production you never have the time. Also, I go to see theatre and I feel old, because there are lots of video screens, and to me it isn\u8217\'20\'19t theatrical. There\u8217\'20\'19s a wish for constant deflection, entertainment. What happened to the idea of a Beckett play being theatrical? I\u8217\'20\'19m not sure I would be successful as a playwright any more.\par \par \uc2 What was your first introduction to film?\par \par \uc2 As a kid in New York, the only culture I had was movies. I would go to the repertory cinema on the corner, and I would watch three films in an afternoon, anything from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2019/may/24/midnight-cowboy-50th-anniversary-x-rated-best-picture-winner"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Midnight Cowbo}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2019/may/24/midnight-cowboy-50th-anniversary-x-rated-best-picture-winner"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 y}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2014/feb/02/wings-classic-dvd-philip-french"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wings}}}\uc2 , not at all age appropriate. I knew this was something I wanted to do, but I also wanted to wait for the right moment. I\u8217\'20\'19ve never been a very ambitious person. It\u8217\'20\'19s probably either supremely arrogant of me to have no ambition, or it\u8217\'20\'19s the thing that keeps you sane. I tell young writers that ambition will kill you \u8211\'20\'13 you have to love the actual act of writing. But it is such a different time. Ambition is one of the things you are taught to have, even if it\u8217\'20\'19s just as a TikTok star or a social media influencer.\par \par \uc2 You are a naturalised UK citizen and lived here for 20 years\par \par \uc2 before moving to Los Angeles\par \par \uc2 . What do you think of the current state of UK politics?\par \par \uc2 Jesus God almighty! Has a budget ever been walked back like this?\par \par \uc2 How does it compare with the US?\par \par \uc2 I guess it\u8217\'20\'19s slightly less scary here [in the UK]. Trump is being revealed now as an absolute fascist. I tend to be quite pessimistic about these things, having grown up in a very conservative, poor family in New York. There\u8217\'20\'19s always that false notion that conservatives are more interested in helping you keep your money than others, despite all evidence to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 Has culture improved in its depiction of lesbian relationships since you wrote\par \par \uc2 Carol\par \par \uc2 in\par \par \uc2 2015\par \par \uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Well, let\u8217\'20\'19s see. What have we had since then? We\u8217\'20\'19ve had a few period pieces \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s always the period pieces \u8211\'20\'13 and some of those have been quite wonderful. I liked C\u233\'00\'E9line Sciamma\u8217\'20\'19s film [ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/mar/01/portrait-of-a-lady-on-fire-review-celine-sciamma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Portrait of a Lady on Fire}}}\uc2  ], but broadly I probably do think that lesbians are underrepresented.\par \par \uc2 You put a picture of\par \par \uc2 Richard Madden\par \par \uc2 on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  recently. Is he your choice for the next James Bond?\par \par \uc2 Yes, because he seems to perfectly encapsulate Bond. I miss the Bond from the novels, who was rather a shit. I don\u8217\'20\'19t need to know his backstory, his trauma, his pain, I want him to save the world.\par \par \uc2 How much did meeting Patricia Highsmith change things for you?\par \par \uc2 She was one of the first real writers that I met and knowing her over the course of almost a decade before she died changed my thinking about how a female writer could live life. I thought, yes, you can live alone, yes, you can produce vital, brilliant work when you are over 40, which I think a lot of young people don\u8217\'20\'19t think. There\u8217\'20\'19s time.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Call Jane will be released in cinemas in the US from 28 October, and in the UK from 4 November\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221026eian002nx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
