\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Are Democrats messing up their midterm messaging? Our panel responds\b0\par\par\uc2 Cas Mudde, Ilyse Hogue, David Sirota, LaTosha Brown and Liza Featherstone \par \uc2 1,661 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2022\par 20:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats have come under fire from critics like Bernie Sanders, who say the party isn\u8217\'20\'19t focusing enough on the economy. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 Cas Mudde: Democrats are right not to focus on the economy\par \par \uc2 It is true that the economy and inflation are seen as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/opinion/polling-matters/401792/public-opinion-election-economy.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 top problems}}}\uc2  by most Americans, but that does not necessarily mean that Democrats should run their midterm campaigns on economic issues. The country has been dealing with continued inflation, the possibility of a recession, and the prospect of an even worse energy crisis for months now.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/opinion/polling-matters/401792/public-opinion-election-economy.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vast majority of Americans}}}\uc2  say they personally feel the pain of inflation and believe that the US economy is getting worse, not better. Running on the economy will make these feelings even more salient, while centering \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , whose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://graphics.reuters.com/USA-BIDEN/POLL/nmopagnqapa/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approval ratings}}}\uc2  are near their lowest level during his presidency, and the Republican party, which is still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/402293/gop-leads-image-handling-terrorism-nation-prosperity.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more trusted}}}\uc2  on the economy than the Democratic party.\par \par \uc2 So how about running on the economic accomplishments of the Biden administration, like the Inflation Reduction Act (IRA)?\par \par \uc2 Turns out, while the individual policies of the bill are popular, only about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/opinion/polling-matters/401792/public-opinion-election-economy.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one-third of Americans}}}\uc2  have heard of the IRA. That\u8217\'20\'19s why, in this particular economic and political context, running primarily on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the extremity and incompetence of Republican candidates like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  makes more sense.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.majorityleader.gov/content/americans-oppose-supreme-court%E2%80%99s-decision-overturn-roe-v-wade-support-access-reproductive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Clear majorities}}}\uc2  of Americans, including independents, oppose the overturning of Roe v Wade, while there has been a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.teenvogue.com/story/women-registering-to-vote-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surge}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D in voter registration of women, including in battleground states like Arizona and Georgia. Although they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/09/20/roe-abortion-dems-voter-registration-00057618"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mainly offset}}}\uc2  earlier increases in registration of Republicans, it is crucial for Democrats to get these newly mobilized voters to the voting booths in November. Will it be enough? I doubt it. But I am pretty certain it gives Democrats a better chance than a mainly socioeconomic campaign.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Cas Mudde is a Guardian US columnist and the Stanley Wade Shelton UGAF professor in the School of Public and International Affairs at the University of Georgia\par \par \uc2 David Sirota: \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s the economy, stupid\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Eight years ago amid the wreckage of the financial crisis, Democrats lost a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.levernews.com/deferring-to-donors-dems-ignore-their-cautionary-tale/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 key US Senate race in Colorado}}}\uc2  when their candidate focused almost exclusively on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and did not have a compelling economic message. The episode served as a cautionary tale warning Democrats that they may lose winnable elections if they are not willing to break with their big donors and offer a populist economic message. Without that kind of message, they may lose even if they\u8217\'20\'19re right about conservatives\u8217\'20\'19 social-issue extremism.\par \par \uc2 And yet, that 2014 cautionary tale now seems like a preview of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 2022 election strategy. Polls show voters\u8217\'20\'19 top two issues in the election are the economy and inflation \u8211\'20\'13 and yet Democrats have decided to spend much of their advertising resources on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related messages, not inflation-related messages.\par \par \uc2 There is still time for a turnaround \u8211\'20\'13 and there is plenty for Democrats to say. They could be blaming Republicans for blocking the extension of the expanded child tax credit. They could pledge more direct economic help if they win the midterms. They could hammer Republicans for protecting their oil donors from price-gouging legislation.\par \par \uc2 In short, Democrats could be vilifying the entire plague of greedflation, whereby corporate oligopolies are using their market power to hike prices.\par \par \uc2 Doing that would require Democratic leaders to do the one thing they always seem most reluctant to do: offend their big donors.\par \par \uc2 But if they don\u8217\'20\'19t do that \u8211\'20\'13 if they don\u8217\'20\'19t remember their own \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s the economy, stupid\u8221\'20\'1D mantra \u8211\'20\'13 then the national election could turn out like the Colorado election of 2014, and everything they purport to stand for could be lost: reproductive rights, democracy, everything.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 David Sirota is a Guardian US columnist and an award-winning investigative journalist. He is an editor-at-large at Jacobin, and the founder of the Lever\par \par \uc2 LaTosha Brown: \u8216\'20\'18The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  message needs to be layered with other messages\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 For Democrats to win, it\u8217\'20\'19s all about three things: money, message and mobilization. And when it comes to the message, the message needs to be more layered.\par \par \uc2 Democrats should lead with a trifecta of messages focused on the economy, voting rights and racial justice.\par \par \uc2 They need to speak about the economy from a worker\u8217\'20\'19s perspective. They need to humanize the issue and focus on the struggles of ordinary people, like wage stagnation and rising costs. They need to ditch the jargon. They need to lay the blame for the mess we\u8217\'20\'19re in on Republicans and their corporate tax cuts, which only benefit the ultra-wealthy.\par \par \uc2 We also need to hear more on white nationalism, white supremacy and the continuing attack on voting rights. Democrats are side-stepping one of the biggest threats to our democracy, which voters, especially voters of color, want to hear about.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans are using racial fear as weapon, we need to lean into racial justice as a tool. There is an organizing opportunity in racial justice. That\u8217\'20\'19s what will activate young people and voters of color, who are the fastest-growing electorate in the country.\par \par \uc2 We have to stop repeating this mantra that Republicans are good on message. It\u8217\'20\'19s easier to have an elite message than an inclusive message. Republicans are just focused on the consolidation of white power and control by any means. The Democrats have a much more diverse and nuanced base, so the message needs to be layered.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Democrats should stay on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  message. Democrats didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a fighting chance to win in the midterms, and that only changed thanks to Roe v Wade being overturned. It\u8217\'20\'19s a message that\u8217\'20\'19s important, but it needs to be layered with other messages in order to have most impact.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 LaTosha Brown is the co-founder of Black Voters Matter\par \par \uc2 Ilyse Hogue: \u8216\'20\'18Democrats need to go on the offensive\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Democrats have solid policy wins under their belts and even better ones in the pipeline if they can expand their margins in the Senate and hold the House. So why aren\u8217\'20\'19t they polling better?\par \par \uc2 Simply put, because the national information landscape is a nightmare. Die-hard Republicans have a monolithic information system in Fox News. This machine puts Democratic candidates at a disadvantage out of the gate, which is too often compounded by an instinct to let the right trip themselves up instead of forcing the questions in the eyes of the voters. Only one party wants to cut social security, keep drug prices high, and criminalize pregnancy. Don\u8217\'20\'19t make the voters guess. Lean into it.\par \par \uc2 Polls are like snapshots of moods on any given day, but in aggregate, the polls show that women are motivated right now and being pulled in many directions.\par \par \uc2 They are deeply affected by the fall of Roe and understand the Republican position on criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a cascading crisis of autonomy, economic viability and health care access \u8211\'20\'13 all issues on which the Republican party has abandoned the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 Women \u8211\'20\'13 especially women of color \u8211\'20\'13 who bear the brunt of these compounding crises, are highly energized to vote, as is evidenced by voter registration numbers around the country.\par \par \uc2 The trick for Dems in the closing weeks of this consequential election is a one-two punch: go on offense and lift up the messengers that already have social currency in your electoral coalition. Invest in your community leaders, your PTA chairs, your TikTok influencers, your clergy, your warehouse workers.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s so much to tout: life-changing money to replace lead pipes in the infrastructure bill? Check! Get that to parents in unserved parts of Michigan. Incentives to bring clean-energy, high wage manufacturing jobs in the Inflation Reduction Act? Check! Get that to economically distressed families in the south-east. One more Senate seat and Biden promises to suspend the filibuster and codify Roe? Check! Get that to, well, women everywhere.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ilye Hogue is the president of Purpose. She was formerly the president of Naral\par \par \uc2 Liza Featherstone: \u8216\'20\'18\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone can\u8217\'20\'19t deliver the midterms\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, a yard sign appeared around the corner from my home: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is what happens when you don\u8217\'20\'19t vote.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By the next day, someone had fixed it, using a Sharpie to cross out the word \u8220\'20\'1Cdon\u8217\'20\'19t\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The quiet dispute in a reliably blue neighborhood seemed to underscore the perils of assuming that attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will scare voters into voting Democratic.\par \par \uc2 Granted, most Americans disagree with the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, and many are more motivated to vote this year as a result. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone can\u8217\'20\'19t deliver the midterms to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Some wonder, with my Sharpie-wielding neighbor, how voting for Democrats has helped protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights lately. Besides, inflation is an even higher priority for more than eight out of 10 voters, and only 30% approve of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s leadership on that issue.\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders have sometimes seemed dismissive of inflation. The White House has repeatedly shrugged it off, and this summer, the Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  mocked a campaign video featuring his opponent in a grocery store discussing high food prices.\par \par \uc2 Worse, national Democratic leaders have been partying with wealthy donors, from the billionaire James Murdoch in Manhattan to real estate moguls in Los Angeles. No wonder they seem out of touch with people visiting food pantries or struggling to make rent.\par \par \uc2 If the Democrats can\u8217\'20\'19t deliver a better economic message, many Americans may conclude that like the repeal of Roe v Wade, inflation is what happens when you vote.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Liza Featherstone is a columnist for Jacobin and the New Republic. She is the author of Divining Desire: Focus Groups and the Culture of Consultation (OR Books), among other titles\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221019eiaj000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
