\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Divided midterms: parties play up different issues as US elections loom\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,630 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2022\par 09:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats and Republicans are largely talking past each other as they campaign, with little to no overlap on what they consider major issues\par \par \uc2 Ron Johnson\u8217\'20\'19s priorities were clear. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a huge problem with skyrocketing crime,\u8221\'20\'1D insisted the US senator for Wisconsin, accusing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.c-span.org/video/?523242-1/wisconsin-us-senate-debate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his debate opponent}}}\uc2 , Mandela Barnes, of pushing to reduce cash bail, release violent criminals and slash police funding.\par \par \uc2 Minutes later it was Barnes\u8217\'20\'19s turn to go on the offensive. Johnson, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://evers.wi.gov/ltgov/Pages/About_LG.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the lieutenant governor}}}\uc2  said, had described the end of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a \u8220\'20\'1Cvictory\u8221\'20\'1D and shown himself to be \u8220\'20\'1Ccallous\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cout of touch\u8221\'20\'1D. Barnes promised to codify that right into law if elected.\par \par \uc2 A casual observer could have been forgiven for thinking the two candidates were fighting two different elections. For in split-screen America, Democrats and Republicans are largely talking past each other as they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/08/biden-trump-midterms-support-fundraising-campaign-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign for midterms}}}\uc2  that will decide control of Congress on 8 November.\par \par \uc2 Each party is playing to its perceived strengths. A recent poll by the political research firm {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pos.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Public Opinion Strategies}}}\uc2  for NBC News found that 90% of voters would prefer Republican control of Congress for the issue of immigration and the border, 65% favour Republicans on crime and 60% want Republicans to handle jobs and the economy.\par \par \uc2 The same survey showed that 86% of voters would prefer Democratic control of Congress to address climate change, 74% favor Democrats on guns, 71% prefer Democrats on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and 67% choose Democrats to cope with threats to democracy. The divide is unusually stark.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn past campaigns, the top one or two issues in the election were closely contested and divided between the two political parties,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/pollsterguy?lang=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bill McInturff}}}\uc2 , a partner of Public Opinion Strategies. \u8220\'20\'1CThis election is different. Each party holds a wide marginal advantage on a distinct set of issues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McInturff added: \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica is more polarised than at any point in the last 40-plus years. Partisans have retreated to their own corners, with limited engagement between partisans, and very little to no overlap of agreement on any major issue.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the most vivid illustration of America\u8217\'20\'19s divide is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In June the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s rightwing majority overturned the landmark Roe v Wade ruling that had enshrined it as a constitutional right for nearly half a century. The political consequences could be profound.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision prompted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/08/25/upshot/female-voters-dobbs.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a surge among women}}}\uc2  registering to vote in some states. In conservative Kansas, people overwhelmingly voted to continue to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 The upshot is a Democratic campaign intensely focused on mobilising women and young people around reproductive rights. Citing data from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bpimedia.com/who-we-are"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bully Pulpit Interactive}}}\uc2 , the Axios website reported that Democrats have spent hundreds of thousands of dollars on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  ads about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past three months.\par \par \uc2 They were handed more ammunition when \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , a Republican senator for South Carolina, proposed a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at 15 weeks, and when \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who supports an absolute national ban and is running for a Senate seat in Georgia, was reported to have encouraged and paid for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/oct/08/herschel-walker-urged-second-abortion-new-york-times"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  in 2009}}}\uc2  for a woman with whom he later fathered a child.\par \par \uc2 Just as Democrats are eager to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 who spent decades promoting it as a hot button topic \u8211\'20\'13 are now equally eager to avoid the topic. Candidates are waffling around it in debates and interviews, deleting hardline positions from their campaign websites and seeking to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/12/us/politics/abortion-republicans.html?campaign_id=56&emc=edit_cn_20221012&instance_id=74448&nl=on-politics&regi_id=73407093&segment_id=109783&te=1&user_id=757069e6d408ae27eaeb8ed46f31e703"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 change the subject}}}\uc2  whenever they can.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats can take nothing for granted. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/commentisfree/2022/oct/10/democrats-midterms-victory-economy-bernie-sanders"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Writing in the Guardian}}}\uc2  this week, Bernie Sanders, a senator for Vermont, said he was \u8220\'20\'1Calarmed\u8221\'20\'1D to hear that Democratic candidates are being advised that their closing arguments should focus only on the right to choose. \u8220\'20\'1CIn my view, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue must remain on the front burner, it would be political malpractice for Democrats to ignore the state of the economy and allow Republican lies and distortions to go unanswered,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders wrote.\par \par \uc2 James Carville, a veteran Democratic strategist, also struck a note of caution, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-2022-midterm-elections-inflation-crime-nevada-7f2356f8d2be07e61389d70c6001e696"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 telling the Associated Press}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of these consultants think if all we do is run \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  spots that will win for us. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think so. It\u8217\'20\'19s a good issue. But if you just sit there and they\u8217\'20\'19re pummeling you on crime and pummeling you on the cost of living, you\u8217\'20\'19ve got to be more aggressive than just yelling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  every other word.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democrats have other cards to play: the climate crisis, gun safety and threats to democracy. While the last of these may be complex and abstract to some voters, former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s continued scandals and campaign rallies are helping to keep it front and centre.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans, meanwhile, made their priorities clear with last month\u8217\'20\'19s launch of a \u8220\'20\'1CCommitment to America\u8221\'20\'1D policy agenda, emphasising the economy, crime, freedom of choice and government accountability. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.republicanleader.gov/commitment/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 They claim that}}}\uc2  Democratic rule has produced 40-year high inflation, 12 cities with record murder rates, a 60% increase in petrol prices, 3.5m illegal border crossings under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and record-high drug overdoses.\par \par \uc2 Some of the attacks could be touching a nerve. Only 36% of Americans say they approve of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s handling of the economy, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/inflation-biden-health-economy-only-on-ap-65540cfa0f493ca94afd1fefd69df33e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to a poll}}}\uc2  by the Associated Press-Norc Center for Public Affairs Research, while 63% disapprove. Although the president has blamed rising energy and food prices on Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine in February, critics argue that his $1.9tn coronavirus relief package last year was excessive.\par \par \uc2 In a mirror of Republicans\u8217\'20\'19s dissembling around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Democrats are being forced on the defensive despite low unemployment. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/10/11/politics/joe-biden-interview-cnntv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden told CNN}}}\uc2  this week: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think there will be a recession. If it is, it\u8217\'20\'19ll be a very slight recession. That is, we\u8217\'20\'19ll move down slightly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 With Democrats similarly reluctant to discuss border security, Republicans believe that they hold the winning hand. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/people/ed-rogers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ed Rogers}}}\uc2 , a political consultant who worked in the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and George H W Bush administrations, said: \u8220\'20\'1C Both parties are talking about what they can talk about. Republicans have the big four: inflation/economy; crime/lawlessness; public education, underreported in the US; the border/immigration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Democrats have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and name-calling \u8211\'20\'13 you\u8217\'20\'19re a fascist, you\u8217\'20\'19re a Nazi, you\u8217\'20\'19re an extremist. That\u8217\'20\'19s all they have. I can\u8217\'20\'19t believe how the Democrats have abandoned the big four. They just don\u8217\'20\'19t talk about them. They\u8217\'20\'19re in a swirl of denial, of obfuscation, of defeat.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey just abandoned the ground. They\u8217\'20\'19re not looking at the polling and selfishly saying, \u8216\'20\'18Hey, gang, look at what people care about. We should have a well-crafted, affirmative message about these things.\u8217\'20\'19 They didn\u8217\'20\'19t have that meeting this cycle for some reason. It\u8217\'20\'19s inexplicable to me .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For voters, it can often seem like two campaigns running in parallel with little overlap. Instead of coming at the same issue from different sides, now the issues themselves are different. One team is playing baseball, the other cricket. Longtime political observers say it used not to be this way.\par \par \uc2 This is an election about different realities\par \par \uc2 John Zogby\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://johnzogbystrategies.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Zogby}}}\uc2 , an author and pollster, said: \u8220\'20\'1CUsually there\u8217\'20\'19s one set of issues. Pro, con, yay, nay. Not always but generally the economy. In the mid-90s crime was a big issue and there was no changing the subject. \u8216\'20\'18Here\u8217\'20\'19s our approach, here\u8217\'20\'19s their approach.\u8217\'20\'19 There was a common set of issues with different approaches. This is an election about different realities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CWe talked politics even when I was a little kid and there were always disagreements but what makes this so unique is, as [former White House counselor] Kellyanne Conway pointed out, there\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/storyline/meet-the-press-70-years/wh-spokesman-gave-alternative-facts-inauguration-crowd-n710466"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alternative facts}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19 that are involved.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The parties\u8217\'20\'19 supporters do agree on one issue: last month a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://poll.qu.edu/poll-release?releaseid=3854"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Quinnipiac University poll}}}\uc2  found that 69% of Democrats and 69% of Republicans say that democracy is \u8220\'20\'1Cin danger of collapse\u8221\'20\'1D. But they have fundamentally divergent explanations: Democrats blame Trump and \u8220\'20\'1Cultra-Maga Republicans\u8221\'20\'1D, Republicans condemn Biden and socialism.\par \par \uc2 Biden ran for election as a moderate promising to heal divisions and he still peppers his speeches with an emphasis on the \u8220\'20\'1C United States of America\u8221\'20\'1D. But the past two years have seen continued acrimony and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/09/is-the-us-really-heading-for-a-second-civil-war"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 even talk of civil war}}}\uc2  as Trump continues to dominate an extremist Republican party.\par \par \uc2 Not everyone believes that the trend is irreversible, however. The Common Ground Committee, a non-partisan organisation, has launched a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://commongroundscorecard.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 score card}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D to assess the degree to which elected public officials and candidates for office seek points of agreement. Bruce Bond, co-founder and chief executive of the committee, argues that bipartisanship can actually be a selling point in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 He said via Zoom: \u8220\'20\'1C Because politics have become national and if I\u8217\'20\'19m a Republican I\u8217\'20\'19m going talk about inflation, and if I\u8217\'20\'19m a Democrat I\u8217\'20\'19m going to talk about the Dobbs decision [by the supreme court on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ], there\u8217\'20\'19s now a little bit more interest in saying, \u8216\'20\'18I have this strong position on this issue and I agree with my base but I also can work with people from the other side\u8217\'20\'19 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bond pointed to the example of \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , a Democratic candidate for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/25/jd-vance-ohio-senate-race-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senate in Ohio}}}\uc2 , who has said he wants to represent \u8220\'20\'1Cthe exhausted majority\u8221\'20\'1D and work across the aisle. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing is that the candidates recognise that people are tired of the political divide. If you\u8217\'20\'19re going to talk about the issues that you know your guys care about, that\u8217\'20\'19s not going to be enough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221019eiag0025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
