\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  is key motivator for Democratic-leaning voters in US midterms, poll finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Samira Asma-Sadeque \par \uc2 419 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2022\par 22:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than half claim they are more motivated to vote than in previous elections, with 50% of those people citing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 More than half of Democratic-leaning voters say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a crucial motivation for them to vote in next month\u8217\'20\'19s US midterm elections, according to a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/poll-finding/kff-health-tracking-poll-october-2022/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poll}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A survey by the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  (KFF) conducted in September reveals that June\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court decision to overturn Roe v Wade has put a fire under Democratic voters, with more than half claiming they are more motivated to vote than in previous elections, and 50% of those citing as their prime reason the ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is a steep increase since May, when the ruling on Roe v Wade was the primary motivating factor to vote among 30% of the voters, and 43% in July.\par \par \uc2 In May, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/supreme-court-abortion-law-leak-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked draft}}}\uc2  exposed that the supreme court was preparing to overturn Roe v Wade, sparking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/us-supreme-court-abortion-protesters-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protests}}}\uc2  across the country. The next month the court followed through in the case of Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 Among Republicans, the primary motivating factor to vote was the economy.\par \par \uc2 In its September survey of slightly more than 1,500 American adults, KFF collected strong opinions among both parties about why they were most motivated to vote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause women\u8217\'20\'19s rights are being threatened now more than ever and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be damned if I stand by and let it happen,\u8221\'20\'1D said a 29-year-old woman voting Democratic from Alabama, while a 48-year-old Republican man in New York mentioned a wider range of issues: \u8220\'20\'1CPersonal investments have plummeted, my grocery bill is astronomical, fuel cost is out of control, bail reform in NYS and criminals across the country are not being punished!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Voters also told the poll they wanted candidates to talk more about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains an important and growing issue among women: while a little less than 50% of women said they were more motivated to vote this year than previously, almost 60% of those said it was because of the overturning of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 The poll found the racial group most motivated to vote was white voters, making up a little more than half of that pool; Latino voters were the least motivated.\par \par \uc2 Between parties, Republicans were more motivated to vote in these midterms than Democrats in general, although those numbers are quite close.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221012eiac002jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
