\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats see hope in \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  (again) in a crucial US election \u8211\'20\'13 if she can get voters to show up\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Atlanta \par \uc2 2,239 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2022\par 15:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Georgia in focus: Despite being hailed as architect of Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s political transformation, Abrams is still an underdog in her rematch with \uc2 Brian Kemp\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  was a high school senior the first time she was invited to the Georgia governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion. It was for a ceremony honoring the state\u8217\'20\'19s class valedictorians, and Abrams was her school\u8217\'20\'19s top academic achiever. At the time, her family did not own a car, so Abrams and her parents rode the bus from their working-class suburb to the stately mansion in downtown Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 When they arrived, Abrams recalls a guard emerging from the security booth. Eyeing the bus, he told them: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a private event. You don\u8217\'20\'19t belong here.\u8221\'20\'1D Never mind that her invitation was tucked into her mother\u8217\'20\'19s handbag or that her name was second on the list of invitees.\par \par \uc2 A terse exchange ensued between her father and the guard, who grudgingly checked the guest list and let them in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe thing of it is,\u8221\'20\'1D Abrams said at a recent campaign stop in Atlanta, \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t remember meeting the governor of Georgia. I don\u8217\'20\'19t remember meeting my fellow valedictorians from 180 school districts \u8230\'20\'26 All I remember is a man standing in front of the most powerful place in Georgia, looking at me, telling me I don\u8217\'20\'19t belong.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the story doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to end there, Abrams tells supporters as she campaigns to become the first Black female governor in American history. With their help this November, she promises, they will \u8220\'20\'1Copen those gates wide\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cwin the future for Georgia\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Four years ago, Abrams came within a hair of it. She lost the Georgia governorship to Republican \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  by fewer than 55,000 votes, in a race {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/nov/10/georgia-election-recount-stacey-abrams-brian-kemp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dominated}}}\uc2  by allegations of voter suppression, which Kemp, then the secretary of state overseeing the election, denied.\par \par \uc2 In her near-miss, national Democrats saw a promising leader \u8211\'20\'13 and the potential to reclaim the southern state that had long ago slipped away. Successive Democratic wins in the years that followed validated her work expanding the electorate, a decade-long project aimed at mobilizing the disillusioned and the marginalized.\par \par \uc2 Abrams was even considered a potential running mate for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.elle.com/culture/career-politics/a32132819/stacey-abrams-on-voting-rights-covid-19-and-being-vice-president/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prospect she welcomed}}}\uc2 . But she always kept her sights on the governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansion, declining pleas to run for the Senate. Now, her second chance has arrived.\par \par \uc2 I can\u8217\'20\'19t get this job if you all don\u8217\'20\'19t show up. I can get this job if you all do what you did in 2018\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams\par \par \uc2 Yet Abrams, hailed by Democrats as the architect of Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s political transformation, enters the final weeks of her rematch with Kemp {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.realclearpolitics.com/epolls/2022/governor/ga/georgia_governor_kemp_vs_abrams-7538.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an underdog}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Polls consistently show the 48-year-old Democrat trailing Kemp, now a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/news/georgia-polls/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 relatively popular}}}\uc2  governor with the advantage of incumbency. The latest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/politics/ajc-poll-gives-republicans-the-edge-in-most-races/CPN7VPBV5VAADJZ77I7VBPPRGY/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Atlanta Journal-Constitution}}}\uc2  (AJC) poll found that the governor had significantly expanded his lead over Abrams, 50% to 42%. And while Abrams has stronger support among her base than Kemp, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.monmouth.edu/polling-institute/reports/monmouthpoll_ga_092222/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to a Monmouth University survey}}}\uc2 , it concluded that her path to victory was \u8220\'20\'1Cmuch narrower\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But Abrams is refusing to be counted out. A Yale-educated tax attorney, she says she trusts her math better than the polls. In the fast-growing and diversifying battleground state, she notes that as many as 1.6 million new voters have been added to the rolls since 2018, many times Kemp\u8217\'20\'19s margin of victory that year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery success I\u8217\'20\'19ve ever had in politics has been about building the electorate I need \u8211\'20\'13 building the electorate we should have, which is an electorate that\u8217\'20\'19s much more reflective of the state,\u8221\'20\'1D Abrams said during an interview at a coffee shop in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Georgia is nearly evenly divided between the parties, and in many ways, Abrams and Kemp embody the dueling factions of the state\u8217\'20\'19s polarized electorate. Abrams, a former state house minority leader and prominent voting rights advocate, is working to mobilize Black, Latino and Asian American voters along with young people in Atlanta and its sprawling suburbs. While Kemp, a staunch conservative who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/24/georgia-primary-elections-kemp-carr-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 easily defeated}}}\uc2  a Trump-backed primary challenge earlier this year, draws overwhelming support from white voters in the rural and exurban parts of the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is 100% the battle of the bases,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ns\u233\'00\'E9 Ufot, leader of the New Georgia Project, a group founded by Abrams to register and engage young people and voters of color. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it\u8217\'20\'19s 100% going to be determined by who shows up to vote and whose votes get counted.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Canvassers with the New Georgia Project are pounding the pavement to register and turn out voters this cycle. Their goal is to knock on at least 2m doors by election day.\par \par \uc2 Though some Democrats {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/07/us/politics/stacey-abrams-georgia-governor.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  have expressed doubts}}}\uc2  about Abrams\u8217\'20\'19 expansion strategy, Ufot said it has already proven effective by paving the way for Biden\u8217\'20\'19s victory in 2020 and the election of two Democratic senators in 2021, which delivered the party control of the chamber.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow is the time to double down, not to second guess ourselves,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Despite a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.monmouth.edu/polling-institute/reports/monmouthpoll_ga_092222/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deeply loyal base}}}\uc2 , surveys suggest Abrams has become a more polarizing figure since her last campaign. Supporters say it is not surprising, after four years of being vilified by conservatives as a far-left extremist who views the governorship only as a stepping stone to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 But it may be making it harder for Abrams to attract the vanishingly thin slice of independent and moderate Republican voters whose discomfort with Trump pushed them toward Democrats in recent elections. A Marist Poll {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://maristpoll.marist.edu/polls/the-2022-elections-in-georgia/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that 11% of Georgians who voted for Biden in 2020 plan to back Kemp for governor, while just 5% of Trump voters favor Abrams.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Abrams looks to be more than a contender and she wants to win,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andra Gillespie, a political scientist at \uc2 Emory University\uc2  in Atlanta, \u8220\'20\'1Cshe really is going to have to shake the tree and find a few more Democratic voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Trump loyalists who were once wary of Kemp have largely aligned behind him, persuaded by his conservative record and their fear of an Abrams victory.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe definitely cannot have a \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  governorship in Georgia,\u8221\'20\'1D said Salleigh Grubbs, chair of the Cobb County Republican party, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/politics/politics-blog/the-jolt-cobb-gop-infighting-spells-trouble-for-state-republicans/4VNFKQWZCVAB5NV3SFCHLHXLRQ/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 censured}}}\uc2  Kemp in 2021. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a very scary proposition.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kemp\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/trump-kemp-call-georgia/2020/12/05/fd8d677c-3721-11eb-8d38-6aea1adb3839_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 refusal to overturn }}}\uc2  Biden\u8217\'20\'19s victory in Georgia during the turbulent weeks after the 2020 election infuriated Trump. In the months that followed, he made Kemp the target of a vengeance campaign, even once {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/videos/politics/2021/09/26/trump-stacey-abrams-kemp-georgia-governor-rally-sot-analysis-bts-ip-vpx.cnn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 musing}}}\uc2  that Abrams would make a better governor.\par \par \uc2 But since Kemp\u8217\'20\'19s strong primary showing, Trump has mostly stayed away from the governor\u8217\'20\'19s race. And Republican allies say Kemp\u8217\'20\'19s independence will probably help him win back disaffected suburban voters.\par \par \uc2 Abrams is vocal in her view that Kemp deserves no credit for withstanding pressure to subvert a free and fair election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile I\u8217\'20\'19m glad that he didn\u8217\'20\'19t commit treason, that is not a reason to lionize him,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in the interview. \u8220\'20\'1CHe simply did not do one thing and he has used that to cloak every other bad behavior.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kemp, Abrams argues, has been relatively silent on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to overturn the election, even as the former president\u8217\'20\'19s stolen-election myth {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.monmouth.edu/polling-institute/reports/monmouthpoll_ga_092222/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continues to resonate}}}\uc2  deeply with conservatives in the state. He also backed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/25/georgia-voting-restrictions-law-passed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overhaul of the state\u8217\'20\'19s voting laws}}}\uc2  that critics said was rooted in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s groundless claims of widespread fraud.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKemp is a Maga Republican who has done everything in his power to align himself with not only Trump\u8217\'20\'19s values but Trump\u8217\'20\'19s behavior,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CHe has just done it in a more subtle way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, like elsewhere, Democrats face a challenging political environment. Voters have soured on the president amid widespread economic malaise and anxiety about the rising cost of living.\par \par \uc2 With the economy top of mind for voters, Kemp has sought to tie Abrams to Biden and warned that her economic plans would deepen inflation. At the same time, he is campaigning as a steward of Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s bustling economy, which includes record-low unemployment and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/georgia-atlanta-economy-stacey-abrams-0387fcf02fc001a213e6c23649c14e97"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record $21.2bn in state-tracked business investments}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/politics/ajc-poll-gives-republicans-the-edge-in-most-races/CPN7VPBV5VAADJZ77I7VBPPRGY/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AJC poll}}}\uc2 , Georgians were significantly more pessimistic about the direction of the country than the direction of their state. On the campaign trail, Kemp attributes the rosier outlook to his decision to reopen businesses after they closed during the earliest months of the pandemic. He also approved of popular policies that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wabe.org/abrams-proposes-big-boost-in-georgia-teacher-pay/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 boosted teacher pay}}}\uc2 , provided {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox5atlanta.com/news/georgia-kemp-unveils-tax-break-proposals-abrams-critical"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tax rebates }}}\uc2  to families and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wabe.org/governor-again-suspends-georgia-gas-taxes-into-mid-october/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suspended the state\u8217\'20\'19s gas tax}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe courage that we have seen from Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has been extraordinary,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nikki Haley, the former Republican governor of South Carolina, during a campaign appearance with Kemp at a burger joint in Atlanta. \u8220\'20\'1CFirst state in the country to open up after Covid \u8211\'20\'13 he was vilified for it, and it turns out that he\u8217\'20\'19s the one that saved the economy, saved our businesses \u8230\'20\'26 and allowed people to get back to work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Abrams has sought to paint a starkly different picture of the economy under Kemp, one in which the wealthiest have profited while the poor have been left behind. At the center of her economic agenda is a plan to fully expand Medicaid, which she argues is critical to stopping a wave of hospital closures across the state, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-09-02/abrams-blames-surprise-atlanta-hospital-closing-on-governor-kemp#xj4y7vzkg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including a major trauma center in Atlanta}}}\uc2  that has become a flashpoint in the campaign.\par \par \uc2 But it is a brewing national backlash to the supreme court decision overturning the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Abrams and Democrats believe could change the tide. In ads and on the campaign trail, Abrams has lashed Kemp for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/may/07/georgia-governor-signs-bill-to-ban-abortion-after-six-weeks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signing a 2019 law }}}\uc2  that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as early as six weeks in Georgia, before many women know they are pregnant. The law was allowed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/20/us/georgia-abortion-ban.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to take effect}}}\uc2  in the aftermath of the high court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling.\par \par \uc2 According to recent polling, most voters in Georgia {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/news/georgia-polls/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disagree}}}\uc2  with the supreme court decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.monmouth.edu/polling-institute/reports/monmouthpoll_ga_092222/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than half }}}\uc2  say the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are too strict.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe No 1 issue that people talk about when I\u8217\'20\'19m on the campaign trail is \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nabilah Islam, the Democratic nominee for a competitive state senate seat in Gwinnett county.\par \par \uc2 Islam, who has put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access at the center of her campaign, pointed to the rise in voter registration among women since the Dobbs decision. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a feeling of helplessness,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut also hope because people are so angry that they\u8217\'20\'19re organizing at levels unseen before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Abrams is also working to shore up her base. Black voters are the cornerstone of the Democrats coalition in Georgia. While they still overwhelmingly prefer Democrats, there are some signs the party is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/politics/ajc-poll-gives-republicans-the-edge-in-most-races/CPN7VPBV5VAADJZ77I7VBPPRGY/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struggling to motivate}}}\uc2  Black voters at the levels needed to win in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 The trend is particularly pronounced among Black men, who have edged toward Republicans in recent years. In several polls, Kemp has notably improved his standing among Black voters from 2018.\par \par \uc2 Abrams says she is taking no vote for granted. As part of her campaign\u8217\'20\'19s outreach to Black men, she has hosted a series of conversations called \u8220\'20\'1CStacey and the Fellas\u8221\'20\'1D to discuss how her initiatives on issues like healthcare and housing will benefit their communities.\par \par \uc2 At one such event over the summer, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://v"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she was blunt}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CIf Black men vote for me, I will win Georgia.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Andrekay Askew is among the roughly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/politics/stacey-abrams-has-a-surprising-problem-with-black-voters/OH4BFMQIDZCUVAKJIR32UZXCTA/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in 10}}}\uc2  Black voters who remain undecided in the state. The 27-year-old said he is skeptical of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 economic policies but was open to learning more about Abrams\u8217\'20\'19s platform.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, he said, his decision would be guided by: \u8220\'20\'1CWho is better with the money?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Listening from the porch, his mother shook her head in disagreement. Laqua Askew, who works in special education, is a lifelong Democrat who voted for Abrams in 2018 and plans to do so again this year. She is worried about a lack of public school funding, as well as gun violence and crime, all of which she said takes a heavy toll on the low-income students she works with.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKemp had the opportunity to make a change but he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got to try something else.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a state as closely divided as Georgia, much could still change before election day. Kemp and Abrams will face off in a public debate that could help sway the critical few undecided voters. And if neither candidate wins a majority of the vote, the contest proceeds to a runoff election.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  Labor Day picnic last month, Abrams asked supporters to spend the final weeks of the campaign focused on the \u8220\'20\'1Cunfinished business\u8221\'20\'1D before them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t get this job if you all don\u8217\'20\'19t show up,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI can get this job if you all do what you did in 2018.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/joan-greve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joan E Greve}}}\uc2  contributed to this report from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 The headline of this article was amended on 4 October 2022 because a previous version was inconsistent with our editorial standards.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221012eiac0032d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
