\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 More  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  votes across US \u8211\'20\'13 can Kansas inspire another pro-choice win?\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,090 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par 17:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Campaigners on both sides scramble ahead of referendums in Kentucky, Montana, California, Vermont and Michigan\par \par \uc2 When Kansans voted overwhelmingly to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this summer, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sos.ks.gov/elections/22elec/2022-Primary-Official-Vote-Totals.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 59-41 referendum margin}}}\uc2  in the deep-red state sent shockwaves through the country, inspiring pro-choice advocates and sending anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners scrambling for an unexpected political dogfight as five similar ballot initiatives approach in November.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums \u8211\'20\'13 in Kentucky, Montana, California, Vermont and Michigan \u8211\'20\'13 have seen both sides organize extensive campaigns.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky and Montana, red like Kansas, it was Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates who brought the initiatives with the aim of removing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections from state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 They say the Kansas loss was an outlier because the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2022/aug/02/kansas-abortion-ballot-language"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 language on the ballot in }}}\uc2  that referendum was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thedispatch.com/p/kentucky-pro-life-groups-hope-to"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 too confusing }}}\uc2  for the result to be reflective of Kansans\u8217\'20\'19 will. Hoping to avoid the same fate, the Kentucky ballot will ask voters to agree with the statement: \u8220\'20\'1CNothing in this constitution shall be construed to secure or protect a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or require the funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Although Kentucky is reliably Republican, its supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 which will decide, a week after the referendum, whether the state\u8217\'20\'19s current \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is viable \u8211\'20\'13 does not yet clearly lean one way on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe stakes are kind of like they were in Kansas,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, the dean of \uc2 Temple University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s school of law. \u8220\'20\'1CYou have a red state with a history of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation \u8211\'20\'13 and if the electorate says no to this ballot, it will send a clear signal that there\u8217\'20\'19s a disconnect between the voters and the state and the legislators, who would otherwise pass anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She cautioned, however, that it was not clear Kentuckians will turn out to vote in the same way Kansans did. Kentucky\u8217\'20\'19s electorate also skews more conservative on reproductive rights, with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/04/upshot/polling-abortion-states.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-point margin}}}\uc2  between those who want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be mostly legal and mostly illegal, according to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  (though that analysis appeared to undercount the support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kansas, predicting a neck-and-neck race that was far from the 18-point margin that materialized in August).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is the general sense that [Kentucky] is a harder fight. People suspect the ballot initiative will be successful, or if it fails, it won\u8217\'20\'19t be by as wide a margin as in Kansas,\u8221\'20\'1D says Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, Republicans are pushing a more confusingly worded measure, stating that \u8220\'20\'1Cinfants born alive after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are legal persons\u8221\'20\'1D, and threatening medical providers with up to 20 years in prison and fines of up to $50,000.\par \par \uc2 Montana\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court has ruled that the state constitution\u8217\'20\'19s right to privacy protects the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so a yes vote for this ballot would not explicitly ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But experts worry the wording of the ballot could confuse people in a red state where the electorate, like in Kansas, is nonetheless largely in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe bill is intended to incrementally change the narrative \u8211\'20\'13 to recognize fetal personhood and to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it demands doctors provide care to \u8216\'20\'18infants born alive after an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D said legal historian Mary Ziegler from \uc2 UC Davis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis wording is intended to make pro-choice groups look extreme if they campaign against the bill.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballots where a pro-choice result seems almost certain are in California and Vermont, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are hoping not just to protect, but to expand pre-existing protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 California\u8217\'20\'19s ballot proposal asks for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections to be written into the state constitution, shielding rights from the whims of changing governments, and also covers a right to contraception \u8211\'20\'13 something pro-choice advocates are worried about since the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Considering the political makeup of California, where Democrats outnumber Republicans three to one in the assembly and the senate, most observers are expecting a win for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. A recent poll showed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2022/08/focus-on-abortion-rights-can-help-blue-state-democrats-too.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 71-18 margin}}}\uc2  for those in favor of the amendment among registered voters in California. Among them, 35% of Republicans backed the referendum, too.\par \par \uc2 Similar to California, Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s measure looks to shore up pre-existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution, with its ballot proposal asking to add a line to the constitution: \u8220\'20\'1CThat an individual\u8217\'20\'19s right to personal reproductive autonomy is central to the liberty and dignity to determine one\u8217\'20\'19s own life course and shall not be denied or infringed unless justified by a compelling state interest achieved by the least restrictive means.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It, too, looks to be an easy win in a state so pro-choice even the Republican governor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/blue-states-protecting-abortion-rights-end-of-roe.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supports the amendment}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But it is Michigan where the stakes feel highest this November. Its political battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights centers around a 1931 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban law that was written pre-Roe, and whether it can spring back into action.\par \par \uc2 Three-quarters of a million Michiganders brought the ballot initiative, hoping to persuade voters to make the old ban unviable by enshrining protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, perceiving a threat, already tried to block the referendum by claiming the signatures collected were incorrectly spaced, a decision quickly dismissed by the state supreme court. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobby knows 64% of Michiganders in a recent poll specifically said they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.freep.com/restricted/?return=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.freep.com%2Fstory%2Fnews%2Fpolitics%2Felections%2F2022%2F09%2F22%2Fmichigan-abortion-proposal-has-strong-support-poll-shows%2F69507400007%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support}}}\uc2  adding a constitutional protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the ballot initiative \u8211\'20\'13 and more general polling has also shown broad support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/04/upshot/polling-abortion-states.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 55% of Michiganders}}}\uc2  believing it should be mostly legal versus 39% who believe it should be mostly illegal, according to the same New York Times study.\par \par \uc2 That 16-point margin would \u8220\'20\'1Cput Michigan in the company of states like Illinois, Minnesota and California when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\u8221\'20\'1D, Ziegler said.\par \par \uc2 Still, pro-choice campaigners are hesitant to view the Michigan ballot as a clear win, especially with huge amounts of money pouring into the campaign on both sides in a state that is deeply purple \u8211\'20\'13 tending to elect Democrats to the Senate and the presidency but Republicans for statewide office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  support] is not necessarily partisan or evident through polls \u8211\'20\'13 and ultimately, without deep communication and education we can\u8217\'20\'19t pre-determine success or failure,\u8221\'20\'1D said Chris Melody Fields Figueredo, executive director of the Ballot Initiative Strategy Center.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Kansas showed us anything, it\u8217\'20\'19s that we don\u8217\'20\'19t know how voters will go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221007eia7000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
