\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 In Idaho, we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing how freedom of speech is being curtailed around  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2022\par 14:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In red states, women\u8217\'20\'19s options and speech are being dramatically constrained by the misogynist laws that have sprung into effect since the reversal of Roe v Wade\par \par \uc2 On 23 September, staff and faculty at the University of Idaho received a strange missive from their employer. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/22927177-university-of-idaho-guidance-on-abortion-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an email}}}\uc2  sent out by university lawyers, professors were told that in accordance with the state\u8217\'20\'19s new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, they are now prohibited from a large swath of activities. They are no longer allowed to \u8220\'20\'1Cpromote \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. They are no longer allowed to \u8220\'20\'1Ccounsel in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. They are no longer allowed to tell students, or anyone else, where to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; they are no longer allowed to dispense emergency contraception, like Plan B. State law now forbids any state resources or state employees\u8217\'20\'19 time from being spent \u8220\'20\'1Cpromoting\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cadvertising\u8221\'20\'1D either \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \u8220\'20\'1Cservices for the prevention of conception\u8221\'20\'1D, so the university won\u8217\'20\'19t be dispensing birth control any more, either.\par \par \uc2 The email said that the university can continue to provide condoms, so long as those condoms are distributed for the purposes of preventing disease transmission only, not for birth control. Since when used correctly condoms prevent both pregnancy and disease transmission, how this change will affect condom availability on campus remains unclear. Those university employees who are found to violate this new gag rule, either by \u8220\'20\'1Cpromoting\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or by dispensing contraception, risk felony convictions, prison time, fines, firing and permanent bans from all state employment.\par \par \uc2 University of Idaho students will still be able to obtain contraception, but not from any university employees \u8211\'20\'13 prescriptions will only be available at the student health centers that are operated by a private contractor, Moscow Family Medicine. But those students can\u8217\'20\'19t get that medicine from the staff and faculty that they trust, and they can\u8217\'20\'19t have frank conversations \u8211\'20\'13 either in the classroom or outside of it \u8211\'20\'13 with their teachers about the reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or contraception, about the political and moral implications of them, about their link to women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom, to better {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iwpr.org/iwpr-issues/reproductive-health/the-economic-effects-of-abortion-access-a-review-of-the-evidence/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 economic}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.apa.org/monitor/2022/09/news-facts-abortion-mental-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 psychological}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/news/features/abortion-restrictions-health-implications/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medical}}}\uc2  outcomes, or about how the rollback of these rights are connected to the rise of authoritarianism globally. In short, students at the University of Idaho may still be able to access contraception \u8211\'20\'13 if they\u8217\'20\'19re especially capable and well-informed, and can figure out where to go. But their teachers can no longer tell them where to get contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they can\u8217\'20\'19t tell them the whole truth about them, either. To do so would risk a felony.\par \par \uc2 The sweeping new guidance from University of Idaho administrators represents a brutal new reality in red states, where women\u8217\'20\'19s options, speech and academic freedoms are now being dramatically constrained by the misogynist laws that have sprung into effect since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s reversal of Roe v Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, the guidance is meant to keep professors in line with two laws: Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.idaho.gov/statutesrules/idstat/Title18/T18CH6/SECT18-622/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2020 trigger ban}}}\uc2 , a total, criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that went into effect on 25 August, and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.idaho.gov/statutesrules/idstat/title18/t18ch6/sect18-603/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long-dormant 1972 law}}}\uc2  that makes \u8220\'20\'1Cadvertising\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or contraception a felony. According to that law, only licensed doctors may legally provide contraception to people who ask for it and are deemed \u8220\'20\'1Csufficiently intelligent and mature\u8221\'20\'1D. But the gag rules now in effect there are typical of many red state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, which now frequently prohibit not just performing abortions, but vocally supporting abortions, providing information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or helping to provide funds and other material support for abortions \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cpromoting\u8221\'20\'1D them, in Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s parlance.\par \par \uc2 This prohibition on \u8220\'20\'1Cpromotion\u8221\'20\'1D of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is capacious and ill-defined. In Texas, a similar rule banning the \u8220\'20\'1Caiding and abetting\u8221\'20\'1D of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised first amendment concerns. A group of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds have had to stop their services providing money to women travelling out of state for abortions. They have sued to have their right to public participation and pro-choice speech restored, but their legal action is being stymied. When a process server for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds tried to subpoena the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , he fled, sending his wife, a state senator, to start their truck and running into it as the process server tried to hand him the papers. Meanwhile, Texans still don\u8217\'20\'19t have a right to materially support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a state of affairs that means a specific viewpoint \u8211\'20\'13 a feminist viewpoint \u8211\'20\'13 is barred from expression by law.\par \par \uc2 At the University of Idaho, the memo from the administration says that the new state law is \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.idahopress.com/news/local/u-of-i-memo-on-abortion-laws-draws-national-attention-1st-amendment-concerns/article_d7f3e458-3de8-11ed-8d34-173525bf7e95.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not a model of clarity}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, and that the extreme official guidance is meant to shield professors, and the school itself, from liability. This lack of clarity, leading to extreme caution, is not an accident. The wording of these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  gag rules in states like Texas and Idaho is deliberately vague, designed to chill as much conduct \u8211\'20\'13 and as much speech \u8211\'20\'13 as possible. They are designed to instill fear, distrust, and chastening silence among those who would support women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom \u8211\'20\'13 if not by prosecuting these people outright, then by threatening them with the possibility of indictment.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, for instance, it\u8217\'20\'19s clear that professors are not allowed to provide their students with moral or material support in seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it\u8217\'20\'19s not clear whether they are able to teach about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a frank way, teach materials that address \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , publish academic articles that are supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or provide accurate historical, legal or medical information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It\u8217\'20\'19s not clear whether a university press can publish a pro-choice book; it\u8217\'20\'19s not clear what consequences an Idaho professor might face if they make a pro-choice presentation at an out-of-state academic conference. The University says that faculty are allowed to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception so long as they only discuss these issues when relevant, and in \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/09/26/u-idaho-may-stop-providing-birth-control-under-new-abortion-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 neutral}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D terms. \u8220\'20\'1CNeutral\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Crelevant\u8221\'20\'1D are not defined \u8211\'20\'13 presumably, these are judgments that would be left to prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a particular cruelty the University of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s prohibition on counseling students. The law, and the University\u8217\'20\'19s maximalist interpretation of it, inserts itself between vulnerable students and the professors they most trust. When a young student at the University of Idaho, becomes pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 when she is faced with either having a child she has not planned for, or continuing her education, pursuing her dreams and living life on her own terms \u8211\'20\'13 she cannot turn to her teachers for guidance; they are forbidden from giving it to her.\par \par \uc2 Because it is overwhelmingly women, both in academia and outside of it, who study \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , advocate for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and promote both \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception as moral goods that ensure women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom, this guidance is an attack specifically on women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom of inquiry and freedom of speech. Because it is women who have abortions, and other women who they seek advice from in moments of gendered peril, the guidance also creates punishment for women who engage in that most intimate and politically vital of feminist political activities: the frank discussion between women of how to survive in a sexist world.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221003eia3001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
