\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Australian woman deported from US says border agency told her questions about  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  were policy\b0\par\par\uc2 Caitlin Cassidy \par \uc2 621 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2022\par 04:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Madolline Gourley says a special agent in the office of professional responsibility told her the questions she was asked were in line with Customs and Border Protection procedures\par \par \uc2 An Australian woman says the US border agency told her that asking travellers about terminating a pregnancy is in line with their policies after she was detained at an airport and then deported.\par \par \uc2 Madolline Gourley says she was asked whether she\u8217\'20\'19d had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while detained at Los Angeles airport in June. It came days after Roe v Wade \u8211\'20\'13 the landmark court case that legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 was overturned in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Gourley says she was en route to Canada when she was held in a detention room, interrogated twice, patted down, fingerprinted, photographed and then deported during a stopover in the US.\par \par \uc2 She was detained over suspicions regarding her intention to house and cat sit during her holiday.\par \par \uc2 Following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/jul/13/have-you-recently-had-an-abortion-australian-transiting-through-us-questioned-then-deported"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Guardian Australia report on her experience}}}\uc2 , Customs and Border Protection (CBP) reportedly launched an investigation into the matter. Two months later, Gourley was told asking travellers about \u8220\'20\'1Ctermination of pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D was part of their official policies.\par \par \uc2 She says a special agent within the office of professional responsibility told her a week ago the questions she was asked were in line with the policies and procedures related to travellers in CBP custody.\par \par \uc2 According to the CBP\u8217\'20\'19s publicly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbp.gov/sites/default/files/assets/documents/2022-Jul/2022-Policy%20Statement-%20and-Required-Action-Pregnant-Postpartum-Nursing-Individuals-and-Infants-%20%28signed%29_0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 available policy}}}\uc2 , termination is not mentioned in reference to women of child-rearing age. Pregnancy loss is defined as \u8220\'20\'1Cstillbirths and miscarriages\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI looked at the policy and the form they provided and termination of pregnancy is not listed on the form or the policy,\u8221\'20\'1D the Brisbane resident said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m pretty pissed off, it took them about two months to come back with that and less than two hours to investigate what I was saying and send me home.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m annoyed but having said that I actually thought they would say I made the whole thing up \u8230\'20\'26 in a sense I was surprised they said yes, we do ask those questions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gourley said she was not asked about her medical history outside of whether she\u8217\'20\'19d had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until two hours into the interview, when a different officer asked if she was taking medication.\par \par \uc2 Prior, a US border official asked Gourley, who was wearing a loose-fitting dress, whether she was pregnant. The same question was repeated as she was moved between rooms. When she again told the US officials she was not pregnant, Gourley says she was asked whether she had had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Customs and Border Protection said they could not comment \u8220\'20\'1Con a private conservation between a traveller and a CBP official\u8221\'20\'1D but \u8220\'20\'1Cthe correct terminology is pregnancy loss\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Customs and Border Protection said in a statement: \u8220\'20\'1CThe health and well-being of those under our custody is a top priority for CBP. Under current guidelines, CBP officials are required to collect information regarding potential medical issues of concern, including pregnancy, postpartum and pregnancy loss to childbearing females in CBP custody.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis guideline ensures that all humanitarian and public health needs of childbearing females in temporary detention are addressed appropriately.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCBP officers ask overall medical concern questions to all travellers found inadmissible (males and females) under temporary detention. For childbearing age females there are additional questions regarding pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gourley said: \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ve changed their story [to the media] three or four times in less than two weeks \u8230\'20\'26 and gone from that I was asked about termination to loss of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re trying to cover their own ass. I\u8217\'20\'19d like them to be held accountable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020221001eia10005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
